<div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>1914 Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>1914 Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing Empress <a href="bword://D&#x27;nadia">D'nadia</a> and receiving the <a href="bword://Gavrilo Princip Achievement">Gavrilo Princip Achievement</a>. The both receive a Legendary version of the box. The name is a reference to the Assassination of Archduke Franz <a href="bword://Ferdinand">Ferdinand</a>, which took place on 28 June 1914, and was done by Gavrilo Princip, who the achievement granting this box is named after.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gavrilo Princip Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: 1914 Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/1914_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>1965 Harley Davidson Motorcycle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>When <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> was a kid, Carl's <a href="bword://Father">Father</a>'s most prized possession was a red and beige <b>1965 Harley Davidson FLH Panhead Electra Glide</b> motorcycle. The small dent on the gas tank was from when Carl knocked it over as a kid.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Earth</li></ul><p class="source">Source: 1965 Harley Davidson Motorcycle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/1965_Harley_Davidson_Motorcycle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>201st Security Group Militia</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The 201st Security Group</b> is a cult of City Elves on the Third Floor. They worship the goddess <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a>, and the city elves are a "batshit crazy, doomsday cult of banished elves" who traveled from the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds to the Over City, joined the uniformed <b>201st Security Group Militia</b>, and swore to protect the <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> from flightless creatures and the Oak Fell.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: 201st Security Group Militia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/201st_Security_Group_Militia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>abandon Faction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic Collective has abdicated their spot and indicated their intention to abandon Faction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>abandon Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic Collective has abdicated their spot and indicated their intention to abandon Faction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ability to loot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Vengeance of the Daughter quest had said the reward would be the ability to loot the High Elf castle, but we’d zapped the place down to the ninth floor, and it had likely landed someplace.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Able to attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The “idolized” turkeys weren’t totems, which meant they’d be able to attack Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>About Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had to do it quickly.” “About Mordecai my ass,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>About the Author Mailing List</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Peeling of Larracos Several days earlier Rosetta Chapter 69 Chapter 70 Chapter 71 Chapter 72 Chapter 73 Chapter 74 Chapter 75 Chapter 76 Chapter 77 Chapter 78 Chapter 79 Chapter 80 Chapter 81 Chapter 82.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>About the Author Matt Dinniman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, About the Author Matt Dinniman is described as a writer and artist from Gig Harbor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Absolutely</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Only a rare few crawlers manage to train one or two skills above 15.” “Is it worth it, to get a skill above 15?” “Absolutely,” he.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Access</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Access to the club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: First off, people are all over the Desperado Club, including the Cosmic Lounge in the basement, and while there’s lots of places to hide, there isn’t anywhere comfortable, and there’s been no indication of any.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>access to the Desperado Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were also bubbles where the people inside had no access to the Desperado Club or Club Vanquisher—which was still closed for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was our only opportunity to deal with this problem before it was too late According to both Mordecai and Rosetta, this particular drug—Glory Bound—was based on a real-life, outside-the-inner-system.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Elle’s manager, Mistress Tiatha, a regular NPC’s constitution plus charisma had to be lower than a crawler’s charisma in order for the crawler to able to successfully get the NPC to fawn over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Imani</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Imani’s efforts, we had a pretty good chart of what our possible matchups.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Juice Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Juice Box, the Nagas had deliberately built their castle on rocky ground to stop anyone from tunneling in from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Kibben</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Kibben, she was as gentle of a mount as they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My stats were: Strength: 6 Intelligence: 3 Constitution: 5 Dexterity: 5 Charisma: 4 According to Mordecai, I couldn’t directly adjust these.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Rosetta, most of the action took place in the actual halls, which were placed on a green, fertile hill overlooking miles and miles of fields of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>According to Tipid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Tipid, this was something that had been pointed out years and years ago, but it had never been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Accounts Tab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Accounts Tab</b> is a tab in a Crawler's Heads-Up Display (HUD). The tab is specifically related to new items created in-dungeon by a crawler. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gains access to this tab when he creates his <a href="bword://Carl&#x27;s Jug O&#x27;Boom">Carl's Jug O'Boom</a>, which is a brand new, never before seen creation in any dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Accounts Tab on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Accounts_Tab</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Achievements</b> are a type of crawler notification generated and distributed by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> to reward, mock, or warn crawlers for performing specific actions within the Dungeon. Most achievements reward crawlers for playing the game with <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a>, but some serve to warn crawlers of impending danger, notify crawlers that they're on the wrong track, or even nudge crawlers to remember particular tactics or information.</li><li><b>Source:</b> System AI</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Achievements and boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had literally dozens of achievements and boxes, including a Legendary boss box and two different quest boxes for the Vengeance of the Daughter and the Recital.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Across</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Did you notice his lisp was gone when he’d turned into a ghost?” Across the way, the chains were doing the same thing to the ghost form of the fallen mantaurs, all of whom panicked and screamed as they were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Action item proposed by Co-Warlord Princess Donut of the Princess Posse: Change the name of Crown Prince Stalwart to “Captain Enormous.” ***Item rejected by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Action item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Because them alien cunts on other floors are being little bitches and cheating, we propose that nobody in the dungeon, including those pricks partying it up in that centipede’s asshole, be allowed to watch.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Action items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Action items is described as suddenly decided, mostly involving building materials and cost.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Action Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Activate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let me show you.” I mentally clicked Activate on both the torches, stepped to the side to the room’s entrance, and I tossed both jugs in at the group of 40 monsters stupidly staring back at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>activated Talon Strike</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This one was still alive, and I activated Talon Strike and kicked him in the head, finishing him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Active crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: You guys want to help me crowdsource a solution?” “This is why we should never use active crawlers for this,” Circe hissed as the room went.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Actor class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’re technically a mercenary.” “Actually,” Mordecai said, “I suspect if we make it to the eleventh floor, Donut’s Former Child Actor class might come with mercenary accessories, too.” Donut kept going.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Actor skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: When Donut had used her Character Actor skill to choose the Artist Alley Merchant class as the one to emulate on this floor, it had come with multiple skills and benefits, including a huge discount for buying.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Actual</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Actual body armor worked much better than this personal shield, but it was cumbersome and custom fitted to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Actually</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’re technically a mercenary.” “Actually,” Mordecai said, “I suspect if we make it to the eleventh floor, Donut’s Former Child Actor class might come with mercenary accessories, too.” Donut kept going.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Acute Ears</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The skill potion gave her level three in something called Acute Ears, which didn’t just improve her hearing, it vastly increased the information that appeared on her minimap regarding not-yet-identified.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Adad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon lore, <b>Adad</b> is a mountainous storm god capable of summoning clouds of lightning-shooting bugs. During <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a>'s crawl, he used a Summoning Box to summon <a href="bword://Dodola">Dodola</a> and Adad on the Tenth Floor. Adad was AI controlled.</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Prologue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Adad on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Adad</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Additional</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Additional tendrils of golden light whipped about the gem, flying in all directions, as if it was seeking further items to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Additional rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There are additional rules if there’s only one saferoom in an area, but it’s pretty complicated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Additional skills</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Well, I Have a Face-Melting Bazooka Apocalyptic Plague Resurrect Totem +25 additional skills and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Additional skills and spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Well, I Have a Face-Melting Bazooka Apocalyptic Plague Resurrect Totem +25 additional skills and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Adept's Fountain Pen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Adept's Fountain Pen</b> is an item that allows a user to do calligraphy. The description of "<i>Or will you literally stab someone in the neck with it again?</i>" is in reference to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> stabbing a <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> journalist named Nihit in the neck with a sharpie at CrawlCon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Adept's Fountain Pen on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Adept%27s_Fountain_Pen</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Adjutant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Florin had actually named the Sergeant rank “Adjutant” at first, but we made him change it because it would be confusing with the existing Adjutant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Admin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mukta (Admin): Administrator Zev had you scheduled on a program called Dungeon Crawl Tactics.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Admin Loita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: That process was greatly enhanced when you placed the toy within range of Admin Loita’s rebreather.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Admin Message</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What is going on?” Admin.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Admin Note</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then you go kill her.” Admin Note: Boss Battles that arise concurrently with secondary productions or as parts of a Quest may present themselves.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Admin Notice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Admin Notice: Three items in your inventory have been placed under an administrative lock while an appeal in the Syndicate court determines if these items are… The message appeared and disappeared in a flash.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Administrative building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: A group of several dozen crawlers were holed up in that burnt-out administrative building fighting off the waves of ghouls and the mimic’s minions, which were apparently nothing more than giant, car-sized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Administrator Loita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: And even though I can’t find legal cause to place Administrator Loita’s death upon you, if it were up to me, I’d have you removed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Administrator Zev</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Administrator Zev, you may inform the show runner we are prepared.” “I’m pinging Rosetta right now,” Zev.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Advance Reader Copy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is an Advance Reader.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Adventurer Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Adventurer Boxes</b> are common <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> containing standard adventuring gear. Bronze and Silver Adventurer Boxes are liberally distributed on the first two floors, and typically contain potions and bandages.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Adventurer Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Adventurer_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Adventurer boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first dozen were bronze and gold adventurer boxes, filled with good healing and mana potions along with a few potions of Phase Through Wall , which Mordecai was pretty excited about, along with dozens of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>African</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The other three groups were larger, including one that comprised of 150 African soldiers armed with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Afterward</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Afterward, the wriggling leeches would be filled with blood and other fluids from the dead mobs, and they could be eaten, giving a wide array of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Agatha</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Agatha</b> is a Crawler who appears to be a homeless human woman pushing a loaded-down shopping cart through the Dungeon. Agatha keeps her own company. She refuses the Tutorial, declines to join the newly formed <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party, and, never having passed through a guild, has no <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a>, which is why she pushes her belongings ahead of her rather than storing them. She cackles at moments other crawlers find terrifying, mutters to herself, refers to herself in the third person, and huffs the metallic silver spray paint she uses to tag dungeon walls with happy faces.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Agent 22</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Agatha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Agatha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Agent Provocateur</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: My Agent Provocateur class added an additional +1 to intelligence, and thanks to the constitution bump from the ring, I figured it was in my best interest to utilize my special ability to double my level-up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Agreed</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s not an active skill, but it’s what I would choose.” “Agreed,” Katia said as she sewed the arrow patch next to the planet earth on my jacket’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ahead</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Why are you gathering people.” Ahead, the bushes rustled again, and the six dots of the were-warriors spread away from the caterpillar, chasing after something.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ahh</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you refuse to re-institute the holy shield of piety and order, then you’ll have to go out there and do something about the dinosaur menace.” “Ahh, shit,” Mordecai mumbled the moment the cleric guy finished.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ain’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Ain’t never felt a buff like that before.” “I was buffing people down the line,” Imani.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Air</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It hovered on Sea , looked like it was going to settle, and it ticked over one more spot, landing on Air.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Air Battery</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trap Message : Your Silent Alarm trap labeled “Home base Anti-Air Battery” has been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Air gun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We were having her buy a third, mobile anti-air gun since several of the spider guns were wrecked, and we had an armor slot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Air guns</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Air guns is described as equal to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Air Quadrant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: All crawlers who originated in the Air Quadrant may now freely travel to the other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ajib</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ajib</b> is an Earth crawler and presumably a dexterity-themed fighter. He lost the bottom half of his left leg jumping through the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a> on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ajib on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ajib</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Akland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Akland</b> was an accountant and a hunter on the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds. As an accountant for the <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>, Akland worked with soy crop shipping manifests. When the Dream rented out the <a href="bword://Fantasy Hunter Camp">Fantasy Hunter Camp</a> as a sort of employee retreat, he performed well and won a spot in the Dream's Faction Wars army. The Dream offered him money to participate, and more money to travel down to the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds and hunt <a href="bword://Louis">Louis</a>. Akland claimed to have a family.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Moon Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Akland on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Akland</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Akula</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Akula ’s control center was a combination between the Enterprise bridge from Star Trek and the interior of a Victorian-era-themed restaurant that was so expensive, the prices weren’t on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Akuma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Akuma</b> is a <a href="bword://War Mage">War Mage</a> and the leader of the War Mage Rebellion. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> describes Akuma as a "a long-haired elf, old and wizened with an angry sneer on his sagging face.”</li><li><b>Race:</b> War Mage</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Akuma on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Akuma</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alarm Module</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: One was called a Spike Module and the other an Alarm.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alchemy Material</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The system called them Mairmei Mushroom – Alchemy Material.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alchemy Table</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Alchemy Table</b> is a crafting workbench that allows crawlers to transform various Dungeon loot such as organs and skins into potions. Although the table includes tools and resources, it does not necessarily include recipes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Alchemy Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Alchemy_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alexandro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Alexandro</b> is a <a href="bword://Residual">Residual</a> seeking to free the Eulogist and wipe out biological life in the galaxy. Agent 21 peeled away from the collective and traveled to Earth with <a href="bword://Agatha">Agatha</a> ten years ago. Like Agatha, they killed an <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a> residual already on earth and assumed control of its body to secure access to the Apothecary's information feeds and equipment. For years now, they have gone by their human name "Parvati."</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Agent 21, Parvati</li><li><b>Race:</b> Residual (assumed)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Alexandro on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Alexandro</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Algos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In the Dungeon, <b>Algos</b> is the god of pain. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> notes that Algos is "a good god to choose if you want your target to suffer." The <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> sent <a href="bword://Maggie My">Maggie My</a> a <a href="bword://Benefactor Box">Benefactor Box</a> containing a scroll that allows her to choose a god to worship, and <a href="bword://Chris Andrews">Chris Andrews</a> a benefactor box containing a picture of Algos. After choosing to worship Algos and arriving on the Fifth Floor, Maggie My was approached by a mysterious person in a safe room and given a <a href="bword://Celestial Grenade">Celestial Grenade</a>. She was supposed to detonate the grenade near <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> to summon Algos.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Algos on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Algos</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alien race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They are a hyperspatial, collective-mind alien race without a home, so they are content to move into the homes of others, to blend.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>All Back</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: And that wasn’t including the Pawna’s Cries potion I’d received from the Open Intellect Pacifist Network or the I Take it All Back potion given to Prepotente, both also intended to be given to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>All Crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: All Crawlers currently within the 45 square kilometer blast radius will receive this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>All Eyes on Me</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut was casting the spell All Eyes on Me , which under normal circumstances required a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>All Eyes on Me Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>"All Eyes on Me"</b> is a minor charm bard spell which causes those hearing it to focus attention on a subject of the caster's choice. As a charm bard spell, the caster only needs to sing the song in tune to affect everyone in line of sight. The better the caster sings, the more effective it will be. It does not cost mana to cast.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: All Eyes on Me Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/All_Eyes_on_Me_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>All Tree</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This usually happens here in the dungeon, but only upon the battlefields outside Larracos, where the Semeru Dwarves built their castle along the roots of the All Tree, seeking the very gods who disdain.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alley Mogul</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were: Artist Alley Mogul NecroBard And Former Child Actor I said a silent thank you to the game gods that they hadn’t named the roller derby one “Derby Queen.” She probably would’ve insisted upon picking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Allister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Allister</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (13th Edition)</i>. For Allister, the cookbook took the form of T'Ghee Cards. As T'Ghee was part of his meditation-based religion, he was able spend hours studying the cookbook's contents. He wrote a little bit in the cookbook about how T'Ghee works.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Allister on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Allister</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Allows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Offered only to those who have defeated the keymaster, the champion pack contains the following items: Backup slot: Allows space for two extra totems to be kept on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Almost</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Almost all of those guys in his group are from other countries.” I didn’t actually care where anybody was from as long as they were from Earth, though the story about Vadim did worry me.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alone in the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The show ended shortly thereafter, and the virtual studio faded away, leaving me and Donut alone in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Along</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Along the highway, cars still came from the west, but the eastern lanes were mostly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alpha Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was also casting spell after spell, but it was clear that because she was a totem, she couldn’t hurt the actual hydra body, not until the last two totems—the staff and Alpha Carl—were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alpha Male</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: One said “#1 Son,” and the other was “Brother of the Century.” Alpha Male.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Alpha Male Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Alpha Male Carl</b> is a T'Ghee totem belonging to the <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a>. Alpha Male Carl is a masculine caricature of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>. He has gelled-back hair that <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> says is better than Carl's, and a prominent bulge in his star-patterned boxers. His War Gauntlet is bone-white rather than black. His patch jacket has two buttons: "#1 Son" and "Brother of the Century." He speaks in Carl's <a href="bword://Father">Father</a>'s voice.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Alpha Male Carl on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Alpha_Male_Carl</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Already</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: At level five, this skill imbues both the Extra Steady Balance skill and the Walk on Air Skill (already obtained.) “Already obtained?” I asked out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Also by Matt Dinniman Kaiju</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also by Matt Dinniman Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!) Trailer Park Fairy Tales (Short.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Also by Matt Dinniman The</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also by Matt Dinniman The page where we tout Facebook groups so they let us spam them about this book.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Always</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Always read the descriptions before you activate or wear.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Amayon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Amayon</b> is a major demon lord and one of the four crown princes of Sheol. Expelled from Sheol by one of his brothers, he was stranded on the Eighth Floor, where the portal he cast to return home carried keyless crawlers into the stairwells to the next floor.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Sheol</li><li><b>Race:</b> Major Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 58</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Amayon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Amayon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Amazon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Two were unopened boxes that appeared to be from Amazon.com: a scented candle and a large-sized cat tree with multiple scratching posts and platforms and a round cat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Amazonian</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Amazonian</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. The Amazonian race makes the crawler a foot taller and wider, enhancing their muscles until they look like a body builder. It most likely includes an increase in strength stats.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Amazonian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Amazonian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>American</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Commercial buildings surrounded the town square, giving the impression of a downtown area of a small, American town from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Amour</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d traded away all of my Louis L’Amour books, and I’d suddenly realized how much I’d gotten used to reading, even if it was only for a few minutes before I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>An Odious Creeper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The delivery system is too big to tape to your head.” System Message: An Odious Creeper has.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anaconda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Anaconda</b> is a level 75 Quarter <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> NPC who works as a lead exotic dancer at the Penis Parade strip club located within the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. He is a highly favored performer of Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and has deep, secretive family ties to the club's management.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Quarter Naga</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3 (mentioned); Book 6, Chapter 23 (in person)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Anaconda on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Anaconda</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anaconda and Damascus Steel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were just in the Desperado Club, looking for some sign of the two missing strippers, Anaconda and Damascus.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anacortes Boys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then I was shipped off to a place called the Anacortes Boys’ Ranch, where I lived until my 18 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anarchist’s Cookbook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drakea was the author of the 22 nd edition of the Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook , and he (or she, I wasn’t really sure) was also a crawler during the final naga-controlled season, which had suffered some sort.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>and Bucket Boy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He and Bucket Boy grabbed the still-unconscious unicorn and lugged him back toward his garage before the bartender could gather some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>and Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She and Chris along with their two new mercenaries were going to head out on their own after her daily.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, and Donut is described as going to purchase the store interface and kitchen.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>And Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: But after that part, the most important thing that happened is that Louis broke up with Juice Box so we can have our torrid affair.” “And Katia left,” Tipid.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Andre Norton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Andre Norton is described as a woman,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Angel</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Angel</b> was a female cocker spaniel living with an unidentified female owner in the Seattle, WA apartment next door to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, Bea, and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cocker Spaniel Dog</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Angel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Angel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Angels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Angry god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Especially for a creature who is probably going to get us murdered by an angry god at any moment.” “I’m going to kill your mother.” Mordecai : I know I’m going to regret this, but I have an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Animal potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Mordecai, how long will it take for you to make that charm animal potion?” “Fifteen minutes once I have the ingredients.” “What about the knockout potion?” Both Donut and Mongo screeched with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Animal Test Subject</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Animal Test Subject is described as a mage that specialized in using.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anna Nardini</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We flipped a coin for the right to name it, but Samantha was the one to do the coin flip, and I think she cheated.” “Anna Nardini?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anne Chonk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Annie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Annie</b> is the child that <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> tried to adopt before the Transformation.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Annie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Annie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anser</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Anser</b> is the long-dead emperor entombed within the Necropolis of Anser on the Fourth Floor, a conqueror who subjugated the local <a href="bword://Dromedarian">Dromedarian</a>, <a href="bword://Bactrian">Bactrian</a>, and Glaber peoples and brought his own people through a magical gate from elsewhere. His legend looms over the entire floor, though his true nature is only revealed once his tomb is finally breached.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Anser on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Anser</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anti</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Two of the trebuchet positions were circled with “Anti-air?” written on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Antidote potions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The bronze assassin box contained a pair of Quiet Slippers , which I wouldn’t be able to wear plus a mess of antidote potions I didn’t need unless I wasn’t wearing my Nightgaunt.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The last time they’d tried this, the seal had overwhelmed Paz’s defenses too quickly, making it so Anton had to take it out with a “one-shot.” I wasn’t certain what that was, but I assumed it was a powerful.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anton and Paz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anton and Paz ignored my calls to repair Yemaya’s shrine and were hard at work on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anton Lopez</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Anton Lopez</b> is an Earth Crawler from Cuba. While originally a prison guard before the collapse, he is now part of a squad with <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a> and his cousin <a href="bword://Paz Lo">Paz Lo</a>. He briefly allies with the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 5, Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Anton Lopez on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Anton_Lopez</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anybody</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anybody who wants to take that castle has gotta learn how to swim.” We moved to the ramp, but I paused, seeing the traps glowing ominously at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Anyway</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the zombie event had started to disrupt Sister Ines’s charm poem, but since her sponsor and patron, the Nebulars, helped her raise that Word Weaver spell to level 15, she was able to fully control both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apartment building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I swear to the gods old and new,” Pontiff rumbled as we eased into the crowded garage, “it’s a miracle any of us are still alive.” Nester was turned around in her chair, looking toward the apartment building.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apex Predator Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Apex Predator</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing the very last hunter on the Sixth Floor, thus fulfilling the promise he made to the hunters at the end of the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apex Predator Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apex_Predator_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apito</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Apito</b>, the <b>Oak <a href="bword://Mother">Mother</a></b>, is a goddess of the Dungeon's in-game pantheon, revered as the mother of all gods and worshipped by elves, most fervently by the doomsday cult known as the 201st Security Group.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Oak Mother; Blessed Oak Mother; Oak Goddess; Tree Goddess</li><li><b>Race:</b> Goddess</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 17 (mentioned)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apito on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apito</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apito Education Center</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Wand Bearers are the warm and fuzzy faces of the Apito Education Center, which is the outreach department of the 201st Security Group.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apollon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Areson, Apollon, and Herman are ogres, yes, but they were born the day their progenitor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apollon the Mighty</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Apollon the Mighty</b> is an Elite created for the <i>Vengeance of the Daughter</i>. He and his brothers, <a href="bword://Areson the Wise">Areson the Wise</a> and Herman, were once the nodling tattooed on <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a>'s skin.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Sensation Entertainment Inc</li><li><b>Race:</b> Nodling | Ogre</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apollon the Mighty on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apollon_the_Mighty</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apostate Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Apostate Box</b> is a rare Loot Box awarded for angering gods other than the god that you worship. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this box on the Sixth Floor from the <a href="bword://Temple Defiler Achievement">Temple Defiler Achievement</a> for falling through the roof of <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a>'s desecrated church and landing on the altar, breaking it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements related to pissing off Gods; Marked for Life Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apostate Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apostate_Box</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apothecary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Apothecary</b> is a galaxy-spanning collective mind and the entity the Dungeon caricatures as the <a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a>. "Apothecary" is the truer translation of her name, taken from her ability to synthesize elements; the dungeon's version bends that translation into an insult.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Krakaren; The Collective; The Apothecary Lady</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apothecary on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apothecary</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The Krakaren are a hive mind that runs the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a> and is financing the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Apothecary</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apothecary Beanie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Apothecary Beanie</b> appears to be a small, knitted beanie hat. It boosts all of the wearer's base stats by 3% and allows the wearer to cast the <a href="bword://I Need My Personal Space Spell">I Need My Personal Space Spell</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apothecary Beanie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apothecary_Beanie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apparel Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i>Apparel Boxes</i> are a Loot Box distributed on the First Floor. The Gold Apparel Box glows with a golden light and contains two items. A Gold Apparel Box may be rewarded by an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> or by cashing in a <a href="bword://PVP Coupon">PVP Coupon</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apparel Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apparel_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apparently</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I clicked on it, and the list took me aback: Ratings Views: 0 Followers: 0 Favorites: 0 Patrons: 0 Apparently the first level of the dungeon was off-limits to live viewers, so these stats wouldn’t move until I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Appear on your map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The safe areas, I mean?” “The safe rooms will always appear on your map, even if they’re within the fog of war.” “Fog of war?” “If you haven’t visited an area, it won’t show the specifics of the hallways, even.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Appearance in the realm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s pulsing red.” Warning: The God Diwata has made an appearance in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arachnid</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Arachnid</b> is a half-human half-tarantula race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Arachnid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Arachnid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arby</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was sandwiched by a pair of training guilds—one for longbow and one called “Druids only.” Next to the druid guild was an Arby’s, which would be the saferoom, and then another store specializing in clothes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Archie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hanging on the wall was a wedding photo showing the woman and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Architect Houston</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Incidentally, Architect Houston, who is currently being operated on by his own machine, is the prime of the Viceroys not because he was elected so, or because of some innate ability to lead his people, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Architect Houston’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It had happened right when Yarilo stepped out of the Nothing.” We had used the Gate of the Feral Gods to teleport to Architect Houston’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Area</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were based in Shanty Town where they could run to Donut’s aid, but the actual missile batteries they were controlling were mostly fired from Area 52 in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Area attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dodola was immune to the lightning bolt itself, but not the area attack it inflicted at the end of its.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Area Message</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Area Message: Warning: Cuba is currently experiencing a Demon Eviction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Area of effect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Area of effect is described as relatively low, the synergy tower’s ability to upgrade Atrocity spells and allow them to be cast over a wide area made War Crime a true spell of mass.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Aren’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The announcement stated the whole thing is scheduled to last about three hours.” “Aren’t these things supposed to last like weeks and weeks?” Elle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ares Warrior Cleric Middle Priest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ares Warrior Cleric Middle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Areson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The were-castors and Areson and the rest of Signet’s crew would be there to help them make their way through the lower tunnels and into the secret, dungeon-level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Areson the Wise</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Brother to Apollon and Herman, the three of them used to be nodlings.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Ogre</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 13, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Areson the Wise on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Areson_the_Wise</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arief</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Arief</b> is a Level 80 Semeru <a href="bword://Dwarf">Dwarf</a> and the War Leader commanding the Larracos Defense Force Infantry, serving under <a href="bword://Juice Box">Juice Box</a> as part of <a href="bword://Team Retribution">Team Retribution</a> during the Ninth Floor Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Arief on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Arief</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arm shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It hurts!” Just when I thought he was done, about to die, he twisted, his giant arm shot up, fast as a snake strike, and it grasped my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Armita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Armita</b> is a former crawler who was part of <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a>'s party. She entered the dungeon with Odette and got deep enougth that she managed to become a demigod attendant on the twelth floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human (non Earth)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Prologue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Armita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Armita</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Armor and weapons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: My primal race allowed me to train past 15 in skills and spells, but armor and weapons-based enhancements lost effectiveness past 15, which meant certain benefits, such as the +1 to Powerful Strike from my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Armor spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I activated my Wisp Armor spell, which was supposed to protect me from mind.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Armorer's Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Armorer's Workshop</b> is a Crafting Crafting Workbench that can be purchased from a Safe Room Marketplace and installed in a crawler's Personal Space.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Marketplace</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Armorer's Workshop on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Armorer%27s_Workshop</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Although it looks deceptively like a normal arrow, the <b>Arrow of Enthusiastic Gonorrhea</b> is tipped with a poison that inflicts Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea.</li><li><b>Source:</b> The Dream Hunters</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Arrow_of_Enthusiastic_Double_Gonorrhea</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Artist Alley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few people have expressed confusion as to why Donut chose Artist Alley as this floor’s selection, but generally people like the idea of the Former Child Actor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Artist Alley Mogul</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were: Artist Alley Mogul NecroBard And Former Child Actor I said a silent thank you to the game gods that they hadn’t named the roller derby one “Derby Queen.” She probably would’ve insisted upon picking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Arwick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Arwick</b> was a member of the <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a> and a hunter on the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds. Bald and exceptionally pale, Arwick carried a quiver of unenchanted arrows but no bow. He wore a flowing, enchanted robe that allowed him to move quickly and gave him a small constitution buff.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Arwick on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Arwick</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>As Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Don’t even get me started on your team’s namesake, ‘One Fine Pig.’ That’s Wilbur, who we’re supposed to think is a friend to this spider lady, but that’s only because...” As Donut droned on, I went to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ascendency</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That while there was oftentimes violence and chaos during the final days of the Ascendency, the NPCs and attendants were AI-protected up until the games officially started, and once the games did start, most.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ascendency game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We believe that a surgical crawler kill will destabilize the whole group, causing a systematic collapse, which will cause the Ascendency game to kick off early and allow our other assets to quickly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ash</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Yes, it is.” * * * I moved to Ash’s room while my stepmother injected my father with a lethal dose of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Asher</b> is <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s half-brother, whom Carl did not know existed until encountering a simulation of his father's home on the Eighth Floor. Asher was the son of Carl's father and Carl's stepmother, <a href="bword://Tami-Lynn">Tami-Lynn</a>. Carl was completely unaware of his existence, as his ex-girlfriend <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a> and her mother had secretly known about the boy but deliberately hid the information from Carl. He liked to draw, as evidenced by the artwork Carl found in his childhood bedroom.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human (Simulated as a Hydra Head)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 45</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Asher on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Asher</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The look on his face makes this suicide mission all worth it.” It was Imani, Katia, Bautista, Louis, Britney, Tran, Li Jun, Li Na, Zhang, Chris, Elle, a few Meadowlark crawlers I didn’t really know, and a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ask</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: He says now that the brain worms have taken over, you’re going to die any day now.” “Ask your dad why that other guy is always coming over when he’s not home,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ask Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ask Mordecai how that all works.” She.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asojano</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Asojano</b> is the Lord of Smallpox. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a>, Anton and Paz get a quest in which they get the option to repair his shrine. The description notes that repairing his shrine will not cause him to ascend.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Asojano on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Asojano</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asojano and Uzi Jesus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Asojano and Uzi Jesus is described as both good, powerful cards, but if they were going to be slow to comply, they were almost.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ass Magnet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It involved the Big-Ass Magnet spell Donut had just eaten from her Feast.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ass Worgo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Not you,” Justice said to Sick-Ass Worgo, the level 53 slug at the end of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assassin of Sekhmet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “My Smoke Form spell that comes with my Assassin of Sekhmet class is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assassin! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Assassin! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after he kills <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Assassin! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Assassin%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assassin's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Assassin's Box</b> is a Loot Box awarded for successfully assassinating <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this box for the <a href="bword://Assassin! Achievement">Assassin! Achievement</a> for killing <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a> on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Assassin! Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Assassin's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Assassin%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assassin's Veil</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Assassin's Veil</b> is a unique item created for <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> with her choosing the Assassin of Sekhmet class on the Tenth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Donut when choosing the Assassin of Sekhmet Class</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Assassin's Veil on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Assassin%27s_Veil</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assault team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the alarm went off, signifying an invasion at the front door, all the poorly-trained guards would move in that direction, leaving the rear, air area of the castle undefended while Signet’s amphibious.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asshole Boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I got the exact same items from the two Asshole Boxes, though my tattoo appeared on my goddamned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asshole's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Asshole's Boxes</b> are <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> awarded for particularly ruthless actions. Unless a Crawler is already affiliated with a club, they will most likely receive a <a href="bword://Desperado Pass Tattoo">Desperado Pass Tattoo</a> in their first Asshole's Box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Asshole's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Asshole%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Asshole’s Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: When my next box opened, the Platinum Asshole’s Box, I realized what it was the moment the symbol appeared in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assistant Manager</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kill Astrid, level 125 Bloodlust Sprite, Assistant Manager of the Desperado.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Associated Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drawing: An amateur picture of a bowling alley Associated Spell: Gutter Bumper.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Assuming</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Assuming whatever ends up happening at 24 is going to be awful, The jikininki and the blisters are probably the easiest to kill, but they both have generators, which means they continuously.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Astral Paw</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Astral Paw appeared, swiping through the woods ahead of us, sweeping all the cannibals on the bridge away like she was sweeping pieces off a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Astral Paw Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Astral Paw Spell</b> is a telekinesis spell that mimics the paw of an animal. At higher levels it becomes a potent attack spell. Obtained by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on Floor 5 through her membership to the <a href="bword://Dungeon Book of the Floor Club">Dungeon Book of the Floor Club</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Astral Paw Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Astral_Paw_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Astrid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Astrid</b> is the Assistant Manager of the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Her sons are <a href="bword://Damascus Steel">Damascus Steel</a> and <a href="bword://Anaconda">Anaconda</a>. Astrid is a hand-sized fairy with buzzing wings; red sparkles follow her when she flies. She wears a tuxedo, and her raven hair is tied up in severe bun. She has a deep, serious voice that is not at odds with her appearance.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bloodlust Sprite (Fairy)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 9, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Astrid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Astrid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Atoll</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Atoll</b> are a tall, thin race with four arms and only three fingers on each hand, they have thin shovel like faces with high set eyes, all set into wrinkle ape like skin. They have no mouths but they do have about a dozen nostril holes. They speak using sign language.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Atoll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Atoll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Atop the building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Behind me atop the building, Donut was now temporarily unfettered, which would allow her to cast her spells and use her inventory while I had control of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Atrocity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And while the area of effect was relatively low, the synergy tower’s ability to upgrade Atrocity spells and allow them to be cast over a wide area made War Crime a true spell of mass.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Attending cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Attending cat shows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Aura effects</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The other aura effects, like some of her more specialized healing and protection spells, were called Blessings, and they could be turned on at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Australian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We later found out that there’s something weird going on with Lucia Mar that I honestly just don’t understand, but Florin, our crocodilian friend who says he’s from France but was in Africa and has an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Author</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Author reserves the right to call you names to his mother if said reviews are not.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Author Mailing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Butchering Chapter 56 Chapter 57 Chapter 58 Chapter 59 Chapter 60 Chapter 61 Chapter 62 Chapter 63 Chapter 64 Chapter 65 Chapter 66 Chapter 67 Chapter 68 Chapter 69 Chapter 70 Chapter 71 Chapter 72 Chapter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Author Mailing List</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Butchering Chapter 56 Chapter 57 Chapter 58 Chapter 59 Chapter 60 Chapter 61 Chapter 62 Chapter 63 Chapter 64 Chapter 65 Chapter 66 Chapter 67 Chapter 68 Chapter 69 Chapter 70 Chapter 71 Chapter 72 Chapter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Author Steve</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of them—Author Steve Rowland—was slow dancing with Britney despite the music, which was currently “Grandma Got Run Over by a Reindeer.” The song was being played on an old, early-generation iPod, which was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Author Steve Rowland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Author Steve Rowland</b> is a Level 50 Forest Troll NPC who initially works as a male exotic dancer at the Penis Parade, a strip club located on the top floor of the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Following the destruction of the club, he is hired as a mercenary by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and joins his entourage as an allied fighter, remaining with the crawlers until the climactic battle of the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Stevie</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Forest Troll</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Author Steve Rowland on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Author_Steve_Rowland</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Auto</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Take on meeeee!” Donut echoed, singing her part, her Auto-Tuned voice high and, thankfully, in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Automaton Table</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Automaton Table</b> is a crafting table where crawlers can create automaton robots.</li><li><b>Source:</b> (bribery)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Automaton Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Automaton_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Auxiliary Tracks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: This first one was labeled “Auxiliary.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Avernus Creeper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Biological life is much like an Avernus.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>awarded to the Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: System Message: All assets of the Madness have been awarded to the Princess Posse.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Away</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Away,” Donut called, dropping another boom.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Away from the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d chucked the dynamite a pretty good distance into the large room, but I was only a few steps away from the door when it went.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Azin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Azin</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (17th Edition)</i>. On Azin's homeworld, spiders only existed in myths. &lt;Note added by Crawler Azin. 17th Edition&gt; - Responding to <a href="bword://Sinjin">Sinjin</a>'s Final Entry</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 26</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Azin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Azin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Azra and Yaz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And if by some chance you can’t save those of us who took the deals, please find my Azra and Yaz and tell them that their father fought to save them and that he’s sorry he’s not there anymore, but that he will.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>B'sieging Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>B'sieging Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for using the Venomous Elven Rock Chucker Trebuchet Device against an Odious Creeper.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: B'sieging Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/B%27sieging_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Babababoon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Babababoons</b> are mobs specifically created for <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. They are introduced on the Fourth Floor, where they are ~ Level 17.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Babababoon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Babababoon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bachelorette party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It smells like someone dumped a barge full of mulch over a succubus’s bachelorette party in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Back</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Wait, wait, this is the best part!” Back in the treehouse, Cici finally discovered the round hole between her legs and peered down through.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bactrian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bactrian</b> are A race of two humped camels.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bactrian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bactrian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bactrian town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I talked them into holding off for a bit and to start stepping out of town to train themselves up while we walked over to the Bactrian town, which was about three miles away on the other side of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bactrians</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trapped atop the Necropolis of Anser, these local Dromedarians are locked in a three-way stalemate with the Bactrians and the Dirigible.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bad Attitude</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Bad Attitude is described as like my Fear spell, but it lowered resistances and temporarily drained intelligence, making it so the targets couldn’t cast their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bad Attitude Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bad Attitude Spell</b> is a spell that is similar to the <a href="bword://Fear Spell">Fear Spell</a> but it effects intelligence instead of dexterity and lowers resistance.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bad Attitude Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bad_Attitude_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bad Llama</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters low level, lava-spitting, meth-dealing <b>bad llamas</b> on the first floor. With giant yellow buckteeth and tan fur, Bad Llamas are approximately the size of a horse. According to the Denver Zoo, regular llamas are characterized by a small head, a split upper lip, large ears, and short tails.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bad Llama on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bad_Llama</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bad Santa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Normally the screens remained turned off for this part, but the screen in the room blinked and the Bad Santa movie switched off and was replaced by two people sitting at a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Badger bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was shouting orders at them, and they rushed off, out a second door, this one leading toward the badger bar, presumably also headed toward that back, secret.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bag Damnation Lamp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Baggie of Trailer-Park-Grade Meth</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Baggie of Trailer-Park-Grade Meth</b> is a pharmaceutical item that is received as a loot drop after killing Bad Llamas. The system AI automatically adds these to the Pharmaceutical Items tab when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> adds them to his inventory.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bad Llama</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Baggie of Trailer-Park-Grade Meth on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Baggie_of_Trailer-Park-Grade_Meth</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bahamas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “…And they thought Carl was doing some Breaking Bad chemistry stuff, and I told them that was impossible because Carl used to pull his pants down when he wanted relations from Miss Beatrice, and that’s when.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bait shop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: When we exited the guild saferoom and went into the FUPA via the bait shop, the situational space had turned into a long corridor consisting of multiple doors, all one after another in a long.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Balance skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Mind Balance skill came from my new toe ring, though now it was trained up so much, I’d still have the skill, minus five levels, even if I took the ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bald elf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The third was an older, heavily-scarred, albino, bald elf that I immediately recognized as one of those Dream.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ball of Swine</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ball of Swine</b>, also known as the Porkchop Express, is a level 15 Borough Boss fought on the First Floor of the World Dungeon. It is not one creature but a rolling battle formation of dozens of <a href="bword://Tuskling">Tuskling</a> aristocrats fused into a single sphere of flesh, and it kills by steamrolling crawlers along concentric tracks governed by shifting dungeon walls.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ball of Swine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ball_of_Swine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a level-70 Belly Acher named Bam-Bam.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bam Bam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bam Bam</b> is a level 70 <a href="bword://Tummy Acher">Tummy Acher</a> T'Ghee card that was designed to eat mongo. He was a T'Ghee card owned by the <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a> that never got to be played.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bam Bam on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bam_Bam</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bandages</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bandages</b> can be used to stop Bleed effects. They are frequently and abundantly found in low-tier <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> (especially Adventurer Boxes) on the First and Second floors.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bandages on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bandages</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bandit Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bandit Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to all crawlers who survived the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest. However due to a veto filed by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>, only <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a> got to keep the reward. All other participants get the <a href="bword://Cuck Aquaman Achievement">Cuck Aquaman Achievement</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bandit Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bandit_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bang Bro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I also spent the time training my neglected Fear spell and casting the fire and electric-enhancing Bang Bro on my gauntlet, though I didn’t get to actually use.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bang Bro Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bang Bro</b> is an enchantment Spell that adds fire and electrical damage to an equipped item.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bang Bro Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bang_Bro_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Banger Spheres</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I called one of my new “Banger Spheres” into my hand, loading it into the curved.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Banquet Fit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Every single page from Carl’s Book of Boom , Donut’s A Banquet Fit for a Princess , and the original Book of Voodoo that offered lightning magic had now been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Banquet Fit for a Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Every single page from Carl’s Book of Boom , Donut’s A Banquet Fit for a Princess , and the original Book of Voodoo that offered lightning magic had now been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Banshee Station</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: These were monsters from Banshee Station, who’d just gotten on moments.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bard's Golden Throat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bard's Golden Throat</b> is a microphone that automatically tunes songs to within one and a half steps of the correct note, assists in the proper formation of bard based songs and amplifies noise to twice normal volume, this can be increased to five times normal volume. It also provides +5% charisma.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bard's Golden Throat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bard%27s_Golden_Throat</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bare Knuckle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I really needed to work on my Powerful Strike, which was a more general, catch-all fighting skill than the punching-only Bare Knuckle, but since I’d practiced it the day before, I wanted to see if training it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bare Knuckles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Still, I could select multiple items, like Powerful Strike or Bare.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Barely</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Barely surviving battles is great for your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Barn doors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: One kobold, trailing blood, dragged itself to the wall and pressed a button recessed against the wall near the barn.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Baroness Eclair Exquisite</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A cat and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s cousin, she got run over by a tractor after running into a field.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Baroness Eclair Exquisite on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Baroness_Eclair_Exquisite</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Baroness Victory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were no circumstances in which Baroness Victory would end up in charge of the Skull Empire, not unless she first killed about 1,000 other nobles spread across the galaxy, so her motivations in helping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Barracks building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 31 ] We’d deliberately placed the barracks building on the lip of the hill so the enemy wouldn’t have a straight shot at the front door of the castle, but it also blocked our own line-of-sight to the bottom.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Barry</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bartholomew Eli McGibbons</b>, known to everyone as <b>Barry</b>, was the late husband of <a href="bword://Elle McGibbons">Elle McGibbons</a>. He never appears in the Dungeon himself, having died decades before the crawl began, but he surfaces constantly in Elle's memories and stories, and is formally eulogized by her on the Tenth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Bartholomew Eli McGibbons</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 31</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Barry on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Barry</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bart Simpson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I was gonna get it on my chest.” “Who is Bart Simpson?” Britney asked as I picked up my two new patches to examine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Based on what Prepotente said and what he received, I took an existing weedkiller designed to take out an odious bloom and then added multiple anti-undead boosts to it, similar to the holy grenade I’d made.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had multiple protections against poisons and cloud-based attacks, but I wasn’t certain if any of that would protect me against a skunk’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Chuluuna was also a human, but he had a strength-based class that allowed him to turn his skin to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based classes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “There are a few additional races available that still give you access to the exclusive earth-based.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That means this skill will be any combat-based skill from any Sheol-level mob level 200 or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based skills</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My IED and Explosives Handling and several other explosives-based skills had leaped to seven thanks to my creation of the Carl’s Jug O’.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, any energy-based spell that originates from the ocular region is caught and focused by these goggles, which allows for multiple combat and targeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Based spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Yeah, so my Pest Control class makes it so cloud-based spells or something have faster.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Basically</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Basically, if she saw a red dot on her map, it now told her how large the mob was, and if it was a type of mob we’d already run across, it would tell us with “somewhat-reliable”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Basilica Guards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Basilica Guards is described as the ogres who guard, you guessed it, the Basilica Entrance of Club Vanquisher on the 12 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bastard box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The silver lucky bastard box contained a few invisibility potions and a useless cloak that I wouldn’t be able to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Batbilge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Batbilge</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (12th Edition)</i>. &lt;Note added by Crawler Batbilge, 12th Edition&gt;</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Batbilge on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Batbilge</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bathin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bathin</b> is one of the chief engineers of the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a> Tunnel Project, the galaxy-spanning tunnel network built by the caprid Plenty. Bathin has a distinctive jowly face and is present on the caprid talk show hosted by <a href="bword://Botis">Botis</a> as a panelist. His reactions swing between joyful and panicked in a way <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> can't quite parse, culminating in a humanlike wail once the herd's mood turns.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bathin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bathin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battery power</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The engine had a connector that allowed it to run off the third rail power or it could run on battery.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battle Begins in Ten Seconds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Battle Begins in Ten.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battle Formation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But when the Battle Formation spell was dispelled, they fled into.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battle of Beijing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I thought of the stories from the Battle of Beijing, only rumors, of the things she’d done with her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battle Royale</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was of age last season, but the stupid Squim Conglomerate always plays Battle Royale, which doesn’t have a Faction Wars or Celestial Ascendancy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battlefield Construction Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Battlefield Construction!</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers the first time they build a fortification or structure and then assemble it in a fight.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Battlefield Construction Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Battlefield_Construction_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Battlefield Surgeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bautista</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Daniel Bautista</b> is an Earth Crawler and the sole survivor of a large extended family that entered the Dungeon together. A <a href="bword://Tigran">Tigran</a> Swashbuckler who fights with a curved sword and an army of summonable plush figures, he becomes a long-term ally of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> after they avenge his family on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Bautista</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Tigran</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bautista on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bautista</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grace Bautista 3</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grace <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a></b> is Daniel Bautista's sister. She was killed by a Circus Lemur in the Overcity. The pile of corpses that includes Grace Bautista includes two <a href="bword://Human">Human</a> corpses, and the corpse of a large, humanoid, tree-like race. The humans appear to be "Asian, perhaps Filipino". Grace is implied to be the tree-like corpse. The body is humanoid, but badly mishapen ("closer to an Ent from Lord of the Rings than Groot from Guardians of the Galaxy”), with her facial features resembling a human but her back is hunched, with an extra set of limbs emerging from her back.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Large, tree-like creature</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grace Bautista 3 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grace_Bautista_3</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bautista and Tran</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most were human, but there was a mushroom guy I’d only seen in passing who was afraid of Elle for some unknown reason and a badger-headed woman who was also a Swashbuckler, the same class as Bautista and Tran.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bautista’s crew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So when Bautista’s crew only saw one cart—this one playing “Rock of Ages” by Def Leppard—it was actually the cart they needed to jump in front of, but they had no way of knowing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: More specifically, the room was devoted to Donut’s Best-in-Show ribbons, her Best-in-Breed ribbons, and the countless trophies and framed photographs of her sitting on a table, looking all fuzzy and pissed off.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bea and Brad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her candle was sitting there, right by the screen, along with the photograph of Bea and Brad, which she’d never put.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bea Arthur</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Is that where you keep the bucket of glitter and the Bea Arthur condom and the sushi-making kit?” “Amongst other things, yes.” The most intriguing spell, at least to me, was called Map the All Tree , which.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bea's Mother</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bea's Mom</b> is portrayed as having been a mean spirited woman and a bad influence on <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. She was highly religious and had had plastic surgery. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> describes her as having "...perfect, tight, face-lifted cheeks."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: USA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bea's Mother on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bea%27s_Mother</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beanie Babies</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The corpse’s legs, still stiff with rigor, upset the line of Beanie Babies, tumbling them off the edge of the desk one by one, like a line of synchronized swimmers diving into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bear Witness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What the hell is Bear Witness ?” I asked, but all around me, everyone had frozen, like at the beginning of a boss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bear Witness Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bear Witness Spell</b> is a spell. Spell can be negated by a high enough Mind Balance Skill. <a href="bword://Eris">Eris</a> (Goddess of Chaos) casts this at the start of the Open Hostilities section of Faction Wars (game).</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bear Witness Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bear_Witness_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bearded Sage Caterpillar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Bearded Sage Caterpillar is described as an enormous, furry, tree-dwelling insect who is normally content to sit atop trees and munch on leaves until the day they get the notion to form a chrysalis and turn into.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beat Poetry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her smash-hit book, having just passed a trillion copies sold, is entitled A Petite Chronicle of the Crawl: One Lady’s Journey into Enlightenment Through Knowledge and Scholarship and Three-Beat Poetry.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beatrice</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Beatrice</b>, usually called <b>Bea</b>, is an Earth character, the ex-girlfriend of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, and the owner of the Persian show cat who becomes <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. She never enters the Dungeon, but is referenced frequently by both protagonists.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Bea, Miss Beatrice</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Beatrice on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Beatrice</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beatrice’s mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She’s really into the idea of killing undead creatures, but she’s terrified of the idea of other gods.” “Well, it reminds me of Miss Beatrice’s mother, and I don’t like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beautiful Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: But that last symbol—the Hag, the Unwashed, the Stalker, the Beautiful Place, and so many other names—was a symbol for something.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bedlam Bride</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This would supposedly protect from charm spells and things like the Eye of the Bedlam Bride, should he have something like that hidden upon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beefy Boys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The head of the Beefy Boys, which he’d named as a joke after someone said he had to “Beef up the wall defenses.” The one in charge of shoring up the interior castle walls with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beguiler Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Beguiler Box</b> is a Loot Box awarded for charming mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives this after charming the goblins on the First Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Beguiler Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Beguiler_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Behind</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew Louis, Imani, and Zhang—who was riding behind Bodi—were all on a track called the “Infected Gravity Well.” Florin was on a track called “There’s Something Wrong with the Zoo.” Behind us, Dong, Porky.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Behind Leon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It will require me to touch him and a random sampling of your men.” Behind Leon, the sniper gnome leaned even further.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Believe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Believe me, when I was the number one, it was a terrible burden, yes, but my physical appearance was a priority.” “You were number one for like five minutes.” “I was number one for the entirety of the seventh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Belt of Buoyancy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Belt of Buoyancy</b> is a simple belt that, when equipped, prevents the wearer from sinking. It only works in water. It can be equipped on quadrupeds by wrapping it around their stomach.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver I'm Wet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Belt of Buoyancy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Belt_of_Buoyancy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Benefactor Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Benefactor Boxes</b>, also called <b>sponsor boxes</b>, are a class of loot box purchased for a crawler by their sponsors. They use the same six quality tiers as every other box, but rank for rank they outclass ordinary loot: a bronze benefactor box beats a gold adventurer box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Purchased and sent by one of a crawler's sponsors; Priority delivery available for an emergency fee</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Benefactor Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Benefactor_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Benefactor Boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: That said, patrons are the only ones who can send what are called Benefactor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Benevolent Champion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This item imbues the following effects: This item’s abilities are identical to the abilities of the Cloak of the Benevolent.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Benjamin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ricky Joe sat cross-legged on the roof, sullenly clutching onto the remains of his severed limb and the twice-eaten corpse of his pet salamander.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Berserking potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This one was filled with my last potion of Bloodlust, the same berserking potion I’d used to save us against the baby.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Besides</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Besides, if the AI thinks redheads are the spiciest, he’s clearly never met a Latina.” “I can’t help but notice you’ve been referring to the AI as a ‘he’ a lot more recently, Carl.” Olga slumped over in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Best</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: More specifically, the room was devoted to Donut’s Best-in-Show ribbons, her Best-in-Breed ribbons, and the countless trophies and framed photographs of her sitting on a table, looking all fuzzy and pissed off.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Best in Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Best in Dungeon Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Best loot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This orcish warrior was known for his uncanny ability to swoop in at the last moment and steal glory from other crawlers, gaining the best loot and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Best Sex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Gold Makeup Sex is the Best Sex.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Betrayal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re spot number three, and our theme is “Betrayal.” Donut: WE HAVE VENGEANCE, AND CARL SAYS THAT ISN’T THE SAME THING AS.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Better</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Better go out there.” The woman Mackenzie was now standing in the bedroom, screaming, sounding confused, tearing at her own.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beware</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You know how they love to plot twist us with these back stories.” “It doesn’t matter.” My eyes read that Beware her lies line.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Beyond</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: You will never be able to return to that particular village.” Beyond the gate were more buildings, but they were decrepit and abandoned, made of crumbling, moss-covered bricks slumping in on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bianca</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bianca Del Ciao</b> was originally a Boer goat and dungeon familiar to <a href="bword://Miriam Dom">Miriam Dom</a>. She is now a <a href="bword://Hellspawn Familiar">Hellspawn Familiar</a> to <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>. Bianca was one of fifteen brown-and-white Boer goats that Miriam Dom shepherded into the Dungeon. She enjoyed butt scratches and grapes.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Italy</li><li><b>Race:</b> Boer Goat | Hellspawn Familiar</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bianca on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bianca</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bidding</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bidding today is sponsored by Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited, because of course it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Boy Blue</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Big Boy Blue</b> are a white-tagged (nonhostile) race found in Water Quadrants on the Fifth Floor. A smaller white variant of the Big Boy Blue amplifies pain.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Big Boy Blue on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Big_Boy_Blue</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Daddy Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Big Daddy Box</b> is a rare Loot Box awarded for the sort of crazy feats that cause the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> to say, "Holy shit. Good job."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Three Cheers for Slaughter Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Big Daddy Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Big_Daddy_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Shot Chicken</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d never heard of the restaurant chain before we’d entered the dungeon, but Donut and I had spent some time stuck in a Big Shot Chicken safe room on the second floor while we’d been trapped by the rage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Six</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re still in their early stages of planning, but the Shadow Mimics who are loose in my dungeon are one part of the Big Six groups left in the dungeon trying to either take over or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Things</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Big Things is described as also happening to the little things in the world.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Tina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Big Tina</b> is a level 80 allosaurus city boss. She was born an ursine, but was transformed into an allosaurus after <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s <a href="bword://Nine-Tier Attack">Nine-Tier Attack</a>. She contracted Vampirism from <a href="bword://Miriam Dom">Miriam Dom</a>, but was cured of the curse after Miriam died.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Allosaurus</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Big Tina on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Big_Tina</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Big Top Ticket</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Big Top Ticket</b> is an item looted from <a href="bword://Terror the Clown">Terror the Clown</a> on the Third Floor. It allows entry into <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a Big Top Ticket from Terror the Clown on the Third Floor. It allows entry into Grimaldi's Traveling Circus. He uses the ticket to come into the circus when he and <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a> attack the following night.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Terror the Clown</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Big Top Ticket on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Big_Top_Ticket</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bigs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bigs here was talking to another slug, and that one said that Justice Light taught them that if a mount utilizes a teleport trap, the entire mount and all its riders make it through.” Dong turned his head to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bigs and Jamal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew the system considered the sluggalos and the former tattoos as a different kind of NPC, which was why Bigs and Jamal hadn’t been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bijanbi Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bijanbi Spell</b> is a spell that summons an enemy to your location. Bijanbi is never clearly described, but is instead described in relation to other spells. The Hamzo Spell stuns and summons an enemy to your location. The Dinner Bell Spell does a similar effect but over an area of affect to multiple enemies. It doesn't stun the enemies, but you can designate a location. Binjanbi is something in that similar range of spells.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bijanbi Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bijanbi_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bijou</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: After a new adherent pledges themselves, a tiny, non-corporeal fairy mob called a Bijou grabs a tendril of light from the god and drags it to the new worshiper and attaches it to the adherent’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s meaner than Bill.” I didn’t know who Bill was, nor did I care at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Billboard Hot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you do not or are unable to pick an alarm tone, a random, culturally-important song from the past US Billboard Hot 100 will be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Billion Favorites</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: 41 Time to Level Collapse: 4 Days, 6 hours Views: 1.4 Trillion Followers: 352.2 Billion Favorites: 21.4 Billion I still had plenty of moonshine left, and Donut and I spent the hour torching all the pupae in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Billion Followers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: 32 Time to Collapse: Five days, ten hours Views: 69 Billion Followers: 635 Million Favorites: 149 Million As we approached the quadrant with the stairwell, we could now see that there was a tutorial guild and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Billy Maloney</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Billy Maloney</b> was one of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s friends in Seattle, WA. One week before the transformation, Billy stumbled out of a bar and peed on a cop's bicycle... while the same cop was writing up someone else for drunk and disorderly conduct. Billy already had an outstanding warrant for failing to appear in court a previous charge, and was still in jail during the collapse.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Billy Maloney on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Billy_Maloney</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bin</b> is a <a href="bword://Nullian">Nullian</a> producer of Planet Beautiful, produced by the <a href="bword://Titan Conglomerate">Titan Conglomerate</a>. He looks tired and irritated, annoyed that his show has gotten popular enough that he is being micromanaged.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Nullian</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Birth Race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Birth Race: Sward.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Biscuit Packs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We need to make sure they can reach their gate!” I pulled myself from my seat, activated Sticky Feet to make certain I wouldn’t plummet, and I started hurling Biscuit Packs at the bugs if they flew too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bisected Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bisected Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for teleporting into a door when he gets teleported back from CrawlCon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bisected Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bisected_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I could crunch on your bones and glory in the sound that they made when they splintered.” “Bitch, what?” Imani demanded, her demeanor changing on a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitch of a mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few hundred meters away, Gustavo 3 sat on the ground, oblivious, happily gnawing on Samantha’s head while she wailed that she was going to kill Gustavo’s bitch of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitch, What? Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bitch, What? Spell</b> is a spell granted to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> by the <a href="bword://Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Superior Fire Demon’s Hand Maiden">Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Superior Fire Demon’s Hand Maiden</a>. Donut receives a legendary Beguiler's Box for having charmed <a href="bword://Ferdinand">Ferdinand</a> during the Butcher's Masquerade on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bitch, What? Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bitch%2C_What%3F_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitches</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’ll explain that later.” I watched as a group of three human crawlers were led away into the Bitches room by an elephant-headed elite who stood about seven-feet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitches and Penis Parade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We should go into the Bitches and Penis Parade strip clubs to see if anybody is in there,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitches room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’ll explain that later.” I watched as a group of three human crawlers were led away into the Bitches room by an elephant-headed elite who stood about seven-feet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bitchmeat! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bitchmeat!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler is attacked inside a safe room by another crawler. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> both receive this achievement after <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a> and <a href="bword://Maggie My">Maggie My</a> attempt to assault them inside a safe room on the First Floor. It's the first <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> notification that Carl laughs at, marking an important moment in their relationship.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bitchmeat! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bitchmeat%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Black</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai : Black bears are the smallest of the bears, but they are faster and stronger than they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Black Friday</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They are nothing better than selfish, wild animals, reminiscent of those creatures who crawl over each other to purchase televisions on Black Friday each.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Black Inquisitor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A cleric, magic-user, warrior combo called a Black Inquisitor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Black Inquisitor General</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A cleric, magic-user, warrior combo called a Black Inquisitor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Black Nimbus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Black Nimbus</b> is a spell <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets from the <a href="bword://Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler">Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Black Nimbus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Black_Nimbus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blade Runner</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even though the whole town had this aesthetic from the 1980s film Blade Runner , the restaurant gave the 1950s, at least at first.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blades Series</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bleak Congregation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente was in the middle of raiding the bar while Dong, the skeletons from the Bleak Congregation, and a few of the other regulars finished cleaning up the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blender Fiend</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Blender Fiends</b> are an unknown mob type that initially populated the First Floor, but were relocated to a lower level some 62 hours after the Dungeon opened due to concerns that they may wipe out the crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Blender Fiend on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Blender_Fiend</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blister Ghoul</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Blister Ghoul</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that congregates at the stairwell location at station 72 on the Iron Tangle. Blister Ghouls look like almost-human heads with green, rotting skin. Some of them have torsos attached.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Blister Ghoul on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Blister_Ghoul</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blitz Sticks</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Blitz Sticks</b> are a common but highly addictive magical hallucinogen. Blitz sticks look a bit like licorice sticks and, when lit, have a distinct tangy smell like patchouli. While smoking a blitz stick, saying <i>"show me..."</i> can produce a vivid non-existent memory or create a false escapist dream. The hallucinations are shorter each time the drug is used.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon PharmacistsLoot</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Blitz Sticks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Blitz_Sticks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blizzardmancer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle had transformed from a Blizzardmancer to a Tundra Princess, but the fairy woman didn’t look any.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bloc</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blocking the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl, do you remember when the police were at the apartment like three days in a row, blocking the door, and Miss Beatrice got mad because they stopped her from going.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were supposed to show flashing words like “Death” and “Blood,” but I knew they hadn’t been able to get it to work because they were just showing the same thing everybody else was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood and Ink Elemental</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Blood and Ink Elemental</b> are a type of summoned minion. <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a> summons Blood and Ink Elementals with her <a href="bword://Ink Marauder Spell">Ink Marauder Spell</a>. When the Ink Marauder Spell spell is cast, the tattoos of the caster become huge, fearsome monsters, but remain in the form of drawings. They barely have 3D shape, like a cutout paper monster.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Blood and Ink Elemental on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Blood_and_Ink_Elemental</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood Bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Blood Bar is described as now in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood Hunter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the festivities begin, I have a very specific score to settle with the lead producer of the Blood Hunter program, and after that is complete, only then will I come to your aid and join in your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood Sultanate</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Blood Sultanate of the <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a></b> is the royal house of the Naga in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space and a permanent participant in the Ninth Floor Faction Wars. Its line of succession is bound to a piece of dungeon loot, which makes it the single largest obstacle between the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> and the Tenth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Sultanate</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Blood Sultanate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Blood_Sultanate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blood Trail</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Blood Trail A favorite of game hunters and medical debt collectors chasing after deadbeats, this magical, bleeding debuff is usually applied to ranged.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bloodlust Sprite</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was a Bloodlust Sprite , an adept in the dreaded cardiovascular.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bloody Leeches</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Bloody Leeches is described as settled back in their trenches at the southern border after they’d filled the existing enemy positions with Fill Trench.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bloom</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Bloom</b>, or the <b>Party</b>, is the ultranationalist ruling political party of the <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a>-dominated <a href="bword://Borant System">Borant System</a>. The Bloom rose to power in the Borant System in the last several hundred cycles. The party promotes the concepts of "system strength," True Unity, and the Great Consensus.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Party</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bloom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bloom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Blue Stuff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bobby</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bobby D.J.</b> was a crawler from Earth. His party occupied Bubble #543's Subterranean Quadrant on the Fifth Floor; <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> calls them the "tomb raiders."</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bobby on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bobby</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bodi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bodi</b> is a former crawler whose season was Land War. He comes to help the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> during Faction Wars (game). He is selected to be a battalion commander of the 103rd based on his experience inside and outside the dungeon. He is known as someone famous from outside the dungeon.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bodi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bodi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Body armor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Florin had taken out two Goriffs—blue, humanoid aliens with flapping antennae that looked oddly like twin elephant trunks upon their heads—and each had pulse rifles and electric body.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bogdon Ro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bogdon Ro</b> is a crawler in the dungeon. He spends time on/off the top 10 list. He has a lot of player killer skulls. He is killed by a hunter on the 6th floor and <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> sports his head attached to her back while fighting.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bogdon Ro on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bogdon_Ro</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boiler Breach Imminent</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Goblin Copper Chopper – Boiler Breach.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bolt of Ophiotaurus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Bolt of Ophiotaurus</b> is a crossbow bolt that, if shot into a deity's eye, can strip the deity of its invulnerability for fifteen seconds. There are only (100) Bolts of Ophiotaurus available.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bolt of Ophiotaurus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bolt_of_Ophiotaurus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bolt of Petrify Rock Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Bolt of Petrify Rock Class</b> is a crossbow bolt that imbues a rock-class target with the "Petrified" status condition. The bolt vanishes in a puff of smoke after impact. This type of bolt is more expensive than bolts designed to injure rock-class creatures.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bolt of Petrify Rock Class on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bolt_of_Petrify_Rock_Class</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bolt shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A moment later, a green bolt shot from one of the red dots, and it crashed right into the torch, snuffing it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bolt-Thrower's Workshop</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Bolt-Thrower's Workshop</b> is a crafting workbench that allows for the mass production of explosive and other magical crossbow bolts. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> buys the Bolt-Thrower's Workshop from the marketplace on the Fourth Floor. The bolts pair with <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>'s <a href="bword://Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers">Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Marketplace</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bolt-Thrower's Workshop on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bolt-Thrower%27s_Workshop</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Bard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re the Hobgoblin equivalent of a Bomb Bard.” We exited the market and hurried through the dance arena, which was now showing the recap.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Bards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Did they have more?” “I’ll tell you where to get more in exchange for that machine—one that won’t blow up on us—plus coal or whatever you need to run it, and some of those grenade things your Bomb Bards.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Chicken</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s a play on words.” “Well, I’m going to call it the Bomb Chicken whether you like it or not,” Brandon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Maker’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The advanced Trap Maker table was required to build some of the best, magical-based traps, but the advanced Bomb Maker’s Workshop would allow me to precisely tune my explosives and to eventually make much more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Maker’s Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The advanced Trap Maker table was required to build some of the best, magical-based traps, but the advanced Bomb Maker’s Workshop would allow me to precisely tune my explosives and to eventually make much more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Squad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Many were similar to the Bomb Squad one, like Riot Forces Support and Trebuchet Commander.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Surgeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bomb Surgeon, which came with a few of my choices allowed me to basically take a stick of dynamite and cut it into pieces, making smaller, less potent.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Bomb Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Sixth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Spicy Box">Spicy Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://This Little Piggy Made a Boom Boom! Achievement">This Little Piggy Made a Boom Boom! Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Spicy Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bomb Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bomb_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomb's Away Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Bomb's Away Achievement</b> is an achievement you get by dropping a lot of ordnance while flying. A lot. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> did that after fleeing Larracos, and opens this box in a safe room on the Tenth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bomb's Away Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bomb%27s_Away_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bombs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Bombs away!” Louis called, and several spots on the ship’s floor slid open at the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bomo</b> is a <a href="bword://Cretin">Cretin</a> NPC employed as a body guard at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. First appears as private security for Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> in the Desperado Club. He is good friends with the <a href="bword://Sledge">Sledge</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cretin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bomo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bomo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomo and Sledge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bomo and Sledge along with a few strippers were in the process of moving boxes of supplies from the various crafting rooms and out the door and into the castle while we still had the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bomo and The Sledge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: One was named Bomo, and the other was “The Sledge.” They seemed about as intelligent as one would expect from someone named Bomo and The Sledge, but the 7-foot-tall, tuxedoed monsters followed us at a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bone Clan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first pie piece in spot number one was owned by the Bone Clan of the Skull Empire, and their castle was called “The King’s Point.” It was the only of nine pie pieces that came with a pre-built castle, and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bone key</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I will use that bone key if it means I don’t have to fight either of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boned Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <i><b>Boned</b></i> spell reanimates skeletons, apparently creating Skellies. This is the only spell that Fleshers know.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Boned Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Boned_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bonnie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bonnie</b> is a juvenile <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> NPC originating from the Fifth Floor of the World Dungeon. Initially rescued by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> after the tragic death of her father, <a href="bword://Commandant Kane">Commandant Kane</a>, Bonnie is taken under the wing of the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> and its allies.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Fifth Floor, Air Quadrant)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 16</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bonnie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bonnie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bonnie and Skarn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d ordered Louis to kick Bonnie and Skarn off Party Planner once we’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book Burner Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Book Burner <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a></b> is an Upgrade Patch given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Talk of the Town Box">Talk of the Town Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://LBC Achievement">LBC Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Talk of the Town Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Book Burner Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Book_Burner_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book Burner Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Book Burner Spell</b> is a spell <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets as part of his <a href="bword://Book Burner Patch">Book Burner Patch</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Book Burner Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Book_Burner_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book of Lore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was called Book of Lore – Commonwealth of the Bahamas.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book of Voodoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: These crawlers who were leaving were tearing apart their vehicles and trading upgrades, and they were all using Milk’s method to add a spell into the Book of Voodoo before they disappeared, leaving the dungeon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Books Group</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s the super-awesome extreme LitRPG Books.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Created by an unstable, pantsless man who talks to a cat, Carl’s Jug O’ Boom takes the bigger-is-better approach when it comes to hobgoblin fire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boom! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Boom!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler causes a significant explosion inside the Dungeon. It is a prerequisite to selecting the Bomb Squad Tech Class on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Boom! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Boom%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boomer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Boomer</b> is a former crawler who bowed out on the 10th floor. He eventually came back to help <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s army in their Faction Wars. The old man had been an outspoken critic of the crawl for a very long time.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7 chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Boomer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Boomer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bopca</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bopca</b> are an alien race, but often work as non-combatant <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> inside the Dungeon. Bopca are a stout, green-tinted, hairy dwarfish race that smell vaguely like wet moss. The females and males are equally shaggy. In general, Bopca are good cooks but known for having "a miserly streak"; giving discounts goes against their better nature.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bopca on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bopca</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bopca Protector</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut and I had sat in the Taco Bell watching it on the screen while the proprietor, another Bopca Protector named Sebastian, sat next to us and brushed Donut’s hair with one of his own brushes, which he’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bopca Protectors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The driver was clearly a gnome, similar in size and shape to the Bopca Protectors, but maybe a little smaller and with even more bulbous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bopcas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 71 &lt;Note added by Crawler Carl, 25 th Edition&gt; From page four of our planning notes: There’s a big difference between Donut enchanting someone, like she used to do to the Bopcas in the saferooms, and her.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant Corporation</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a> megacorporation operating within the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, the <b>Borant Corporation</b> (aka <b>Borant</b>) is the current planetary regent of Earth and the producer of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Borant</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borant Corporation on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borant_Corporation</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant System</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Borant System</b> is a government and <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> System largely inhabited by <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a>. The Borant System was formerly a democracy, republic, or other vote-based government with multiple political parties. In the last several hundred cycles, an ultranationalist party called the <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a> rose to power. The Bloom introduced a totalitarian regime and strict social hierarchy in which Party members enjoy special treatment within the System and Bloom-controlled companies (e.g.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-Tin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borant System on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borant_System</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant Corporation</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a> megacorporation operating within the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, the <b>Borant Corporation</b> (aka <b>Borant</b>) is the current planetary regent of Earth and the producer of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Borant</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borant Corporation on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borant_Corporation</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant Emancipation Front</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The Borant Emancipation Front is a rebel group fighting for control of the <a href="bword://Borant System">Borant System</a>. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and Zev helped smuggle Valtay secret agents to them.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borant Emancipation Front on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borant_Emancipation_Front</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant System</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Borant System</b> is a government and <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> System largely inhabited by <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a>. The Borant System was formerly a democracy, republic, or other vote-based government with multiple political parties. In the last several hundred cycles, an ultranationalist party called the <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a> rose to power. The Bloom introduced a totalitarian regime and strict social hierarchy in which Party members enjoy special treatment within the System and Bloom-controlled companies (e.g.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-Tin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borant System on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borant_System</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borant System Government</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, the Borant System Government no longer has any stake in either the planet, the resources, or the production of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth , and regency has been granted solely to the Borant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boring Ol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This woman was a level-52 Boring Ol’ Mage named Zeynep.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borough Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: And since that one earlier was a Neighborhood Boss, I’d bet my left nipple this one is a Borough.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borough Boss! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Borough Boss!</b> is an achievement distributed the first time a crawler encounters a Borough Boss and/or enters their boss chamber. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>, <a href="bword://Chris Andrews">Chris Andrews</a>, <a href="bword://Brandon Andrews">Brandon Andrews</a>, <a href="bword://Yolanda Martinez">Yolanda Martinez</a>, and <a href="bword://Agatha">Agatha</a> receive this achievement after defeating the <a href="bword://Ball of Swine">Ball of Swine</a> on the First Floor. Carl and Donut immediately descend the stairs, and do not open the achievement until after finding a Safe Room in the <a href="bword://Mind Horror">Mind Horror</a> neighborhood on the Second Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borough Boss! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borough_Boss%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Borough Field Guide</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Borough Field Guide</b> is a persistent loot item dropped by slain Borough Bosses. Once a crawler picks up the item, the boss's borough will be outlined on the crawler's minimap. The individual rooms and hallways are obscured by the "fog of war" but, when a crawler zooms in on an area, a tooltip pops up showing the mob types and levels there as (for example) <b>Mobs Level 2-5. Axebeak.</b></li><li><b>Source:</b> Borough Boss</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Borough Field Guide on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Borough_Field_Guide</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss Babe Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Boss Babe</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler deals damage to a boss. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the First Floor after lobbing <a href="bword://Goblin Dynamite">Goblin Dynamite</a> into the <a href="bword://Hoarder">Hoarder</a>'s lair.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Boss Babe Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Boss_Babe_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss Battle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: B-b-b-b Boss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Boss Box</b> is a Loot Box awarded to Crawlers who kill a Boss monster. The box's tier, and the quality of the loot inside, scales with the difficulty of the boss defeated.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Defeating a Boss</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Boss Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Boss_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: On the first two floors, the bronze and silver boss boxes held great loot, but the boxes were now shit compared to how they’d been on the earlier.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss monster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, I was not protected from the bloating corpse of the boss monster, which in turn blew like a goddamn potato in the microwave, either from me falling into it, or from the shockwave of the dual.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss Monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, if you brush your teeth using this enchanted, cherry-flavored toothpaste, you are imbued with the following buffs for 30 hours: +Three Times Damage to all Boss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boss room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had no idea what was behind that door, but the support creatures of this area were significantly stronger than the ones guarding the last boss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Anton and Paz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Both Anton and Paz is described as on the ground, hunched over in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Bomo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both Bomo and the Sledge sat on the ground, enraptured by the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both Donut and I were immune to poison, but not paralysis, as we’d both learned the hard way when Signet had captured us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Donut and Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Mordecai, how long will it take for you to make that charm animal potion?” “Fifteen minutes once I have the ingredients.” “What about the knockout potion?” Both Donut and Mongo screeched with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Jesus and Asojano</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Both Jesus and Asojano is described as undead, but he’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both Mongo and the meatball scrambled toward the bowl, but a shout from Donut caused Mongo to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Both Mongo and Kiwi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both Mongo and Kiwi had stopped growling and stood right next to me, sniffing at the air as if they, too, were surprised by the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Botis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Botis</b> is a caprid goat and the magistrate/host of a live trial-style talk show, <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a> of Plenty, run by the Plenty, the goat-people who run the galaxy's tunnel network.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Botis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Botis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bottom floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They worked on anything that was “a solid and unnatural non-biological with empty space on both sides,” so if we were on the second floor of a building, we’d plummet away and would only stop once we hit the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bounce House</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Just below where the ball had stopped should be a wide-area spell called Bounce House that was going to catch them all, giving them a moment to recover while they received.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bounty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bounty Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bounty Boxes</b> are rare <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> awarded to players who kill one of the Top Ten Crawlers on the Leaderboard. The Bounty Box contains the bounty for the slain crawler.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Claim a Crawler's Bounty</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bounty Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bounty_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The platinum Here’s-Some-Good-Shit-Because-You’re-Gonna-Need-It-Box also contained the same thing Donut had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Box upon choosing this class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boxes and achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imani and Elle are already out there in the town.” I was putting off opening my own boxes and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: (x5) Boy, I hope you’re up on your knowledge of the fighting techniques of the first world.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boy Snick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: This gripe you have with Prince Maestro is getting much more attention, especially with that Pork Boy Snick that just showed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boys Employment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Boys Employment Agency</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brad</b> is <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a>'s ex-boyfriend and presumed lover. He persuaded her to post a picture of the two of them on instagram that resulted in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> breaking up with her.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brad on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brad</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brain Boiler</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brain Boilers</b> are a Third Floor mob that specializes in psionic attacks. They are a more advanced form of the Mind Horrors from the Second Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brain Boiler on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brain_Boiler</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brain Freeze</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I dropped a Fear-inducing smoke curtain into the cargo just as the still-dangling Donut cast her health-draining, resistance-lowering Brain Freeze on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brain Freeze Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brain Freeze Spell</b> is a spell that targets one mob and drains their health. The spell targets one mob and drains their health. At level 10 it makes them temporarily blind.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brain Freeze Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brain_Freeze_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brain Trust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s Second Chance spell was currently at level 11, which brought it up to level 12 with her Brain Trust.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The pieces were large and unwieldy, and heavy as shit, but when placed down in the correct order—Imani, then Brandon, then Imani, then me with the largest piece, and then finally Brandon—the multiple pieces.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandon An</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Brandon An : Loud and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandon and Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had very little space to test this in, but thanks to the know-how from Brandon and Chris, I was confident that the babies would work as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandon and crew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: It is completely random, so you will now be mixed in with crawlers from around the world.” “So Brandon and crew could be anywhere?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandon Andrews</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brandon Andrews</b> is a crawler from Wenatchee, WA, and a member of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party. He and his older brother <a href="bword://Chris Andrews">Chris Andrews</a> entered the Dungeon alongside the elderly residents they were paid to look after, and are the first friendly crawlers <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> meet.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brandon Andrews on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brandon_Andrews</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brandy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Brandy made a face like she was going to protest, but then another baby came, and I took the opportunity to slam the hot door while her face was scrunched up in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bravvo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bravvo</b> is the thirty-sixth Hunter killed by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>. Dropped black leather boots, enchanted against magical bolts; 30 Good Health Potions; 30 Good Mana Refills; magical longsword (+2 CON), 9 potions of Levitation.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Yenk</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bravvo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bravvo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brawler's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brawler's Boxes</b> are a Loot Box awarded for... brawling. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a Gold Brawler's Box for the <a href="bword://Two Chicks at the Same Time Achievement">Two Chicks at the Same Time Achievement</a> during the fight with the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Two Chicks at the Same Time Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brawler's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brawler%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Breaking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Breaking the rules can result in severe punishment, including but not limited to additional time added to your indentureship, reassignment to a combat role, or the worst.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Breaking Bad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “…And they thought Carl was doing some Breaking Bad chemistry stuff, and I told them that was impossible because Carl used to pull his pants down when he wanted relations from Miss Beatrice, and that’s when.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ring of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a <b>Ring of Water Breathing</b> on the Fifth Floor. A glowing blue ring that twinkles with little sparkles.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold I'm Wet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ring of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ring_of_Water_Breathing</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b> is a scroll that allows a crawler to breathe underwater for a limited amount of time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Water_Breathing</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Breathing scroll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’s not going to like it, but it’ll work.” Mordecai cast a Water Breathing scroll on Louis—we had to use the scroll version because he now had an extra-long potion cooldown—and as the gills formed, he quickly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Breathing scrolls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: From what little we know, it sounds like it’s underwater.” “Yeah, we-we keep getting water breathing scrolls and scrolls of d-d-disarm trap, which are useless unless you know for sure a trap is right there,”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brin Root</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brin Root</b> is a rare alchemical ingredient, but less stable than Bujold Sap. When <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> purchases a <a href="bword://Pharmaceutical Starter Kit">Pharmaceutical Starter Kit</a> from <a href="bword://Quint">Quint</a> in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> on the Third Floor, he notes that it does not include brin root. Quint explains that it's hard to come by, and he has substituted two vials of bujold sap instead.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quint</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brin Root on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brin_Root</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brindle Grub</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brindle Grubs</b> are the janitor mob of the Second Floor. One to fifteen Brindle Grubs are spawned every time a corpse is created until their numbers cap out at 5,000 per quadrant. Destroying corpses prevents Brindle Grubs from leveling up but not from spawning, and eventually causes hoards of the mob to swarm crawlers, thus chasing crawlers out of otherwise secured neighborhoods.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brindle Grub on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brindle_Grub</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brindle Grubs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: After the incident with the Brindle Grubs on the second floor, it was something all three looked for in their respective.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brindled Vespa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It now showed Lucia Mar smashing a Brindled Vespa into pieces with her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brindled Vespas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Brindled Vespas is described as too aggressive, and we have halved the number of these mobs currently in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bring a sling, some rope, a few extra parts, and you have a device that can hurl 300 kilos worth of “Do you hear something?” in an enemy’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bring Forth the Witnesses</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: The effects of a level-20 Bring Forth the Witnesses has caused a magical backlash over the entire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>British</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: He had an unexpectedly deep and growly voice, like he was a British street kid trying too hard to sound like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Britney</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Britney Proskurina</b> is an Earth Crawler from Ukraine and one of the three crawlers to come out alive from the water quadrant of Bubble 543 on the Fifth Floor. She travels with the loose group of survivors that forms around <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Ukraine, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 25</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Britney on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Britney</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Broken door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We rushed across the street and moved up the stairs moving toward the broken.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze, then Silver, then Gold, then Platinum, then Legendary, then Celestial.” “Yeah,” I said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Adventurer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have forty boxes, but almost all are just Bronze Adventurer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Adventurer Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Bronze Adventurer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Asshole’s Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Bronze Asshole’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Benefactor Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have received a Bronze Benefactor Box from the Valtay.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Boss Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Condom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bronze Condom</b> is an item <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives as a result of the <a href="bword://It itches when I pee Achievement">It itches when I pee Achievement</a>. An unenchanted condom in bronze colored packaging. It has a sketch of an older woman (possibly Bea Arthur) and a sheep on the other.</li><li><b>Source:</b> It itches when I pee Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bronze Condom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bronze_Condom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Daddy’s Little Secret Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Bronze Daddy’s Little Secret Box contained a group of five potions Mordecai had mentioned in passing once.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Pet Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Legendary Pet Box had the same symbol etched on it as the Bronze Pet Box, but this thing was three times the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Vanessa Williams Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Bronze Vanessa Williams Box contained an unenchanted spray paint can of white paint for some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bronze Weapon Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: After that was Bronze Weapon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brooks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rust, still laughing, had stepped back through the portal, lassoed Brooks, and pulled him into the glass, which had cut him right in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Brother, what are you doing?” Damascus turned and put his hand on the other stripper’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bruce Lee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The monk seal let out a stream of Bruce Lee noises and flipped through the air, aiming toward the swirling cluster of hydra.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bruna</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before Bruna, on the other side of Dwight, had even managed to let out a bellow from the point-blank blast, Dwight’s head had been removed from his body in a single swipe of Donut’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brynhild's Daughters</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Brynhild's Daughters</b> is an all-female party of crawlers founded and led by <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a> and themed after Norse shieldmaiden imagery. The party operated separately from the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Daughters, Hekla's team</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Brynhild's Daughters on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Brynhild%27s_Daughters</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Brynhild’s Daughters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The outreach associates represent the whole party, and since Katia was in Brynhild’s Daughters, she’s making noise that she should be the one in charge of you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bubba Lane</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bubba Lane says it’s because women are.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bubble Buddy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the end, we went with the Bubble Buddy upgrade, a heat sink in case we lost our containment, and the regular version of the same booster rockets that team Sparkles had, which, when combined with the bubble.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bubble level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl, do you remember that turkey Prepotente and Miriam Dom killed at the end of the bubble level?” “I remember.” We’d never actually seen the feral god, but that one had been the size of the entire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bubble Notification</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bubble.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bucket Boy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bucket Boy</b> is a young <a href="bword://Crocodilian">Crocodilian</a> NPC who works as a tip collector at the Penis Parade strip club within the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. He is later hired as a mercenary for the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Crocodilian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bucket Boy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bucket_Boy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Buff potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Those aren’t the same type of potion anyway, so they aren’t restricted to the same limits.” Donut drank the first buff.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bug helmet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: With her bug helmet, her face was expressionless, but I could hear the grin in her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bugaboo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bugaboos</b> are a type of mob found on the First, Second, and Third Floors. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> is a bugaboo on the second floor. Approximately 7 ft tall, Bugaboos look both "terrifying and cartoonish." They take the general shape of a bear with no neck, enormous owl-like eyes, "comically skinny legs," and long but "absurdly thin" arms. They are covered in obsidian-colored hair.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bugaboo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bugaboo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bugbear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bugbear</b> are a race on the Fifth Floor. Unknown, but references are made to bugbear paste (as an explosive) and bugbear metal (found in the submarine of Bubble #543) by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Tran">Tran</a>. Also, <a href="bword://Priestly">Priestly</a> marched on Larracos with the bugbear army on his ninth floor, which drove him insane.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bugbear on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bugbear</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bugbear Paste</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bugbear Paste</b> is a paste capable of creating powerful explosions. <a href="bword://Coolie">Coolie</a> secretes bugbear paste into his boots with the intention of detonating it some time later and the expectation that the small quantity will destroy a spaceship, suggesting that it is relatively stable and extremely powerful.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bugbear Paste on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bugbear_Paste</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Build Trench</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Build Trench scrolls, despite being handed out like candy to us crawlers during the last floor, were rarely used by the Faction Wars participants because they were expensive, and as such, possible exploits.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Build Trench scrolls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Build Trench scrolls, despite being handed out like candy to us crawlers during the last floor, were rarely used by the Faction Wars participants because they were expensive, and as such, possible exploits.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bullshit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you refuse, we are authorized to drop you into this ocean, drowning you both.” “Bullshit,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bully and a Thief Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bully <i>and</i> a Thief!</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers who seize a lower level crawler's property. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement for taking <a href="bword://Agatha">Agatha</a>'s shopping cart into his inventory during the battle against the <a href="bword://Ball of Swine">Ball of Swine</a> on the First Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bully and a Thief Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bully_and_a_Thief_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bulwark Battalion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tran and his 102 nd Bulwark Battalion, plus Boomer and the 106 th Bloody Leeches were settled back in their trenches at the southern border after they’d filled the existing enemy positions with Fill Trench.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bumper spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s talking to her now, telling her that you used a spell that our father gave you to win your races.” “Wait,” I said, remembering the note from the Book of Boom that I’d eaten to get the Bumper.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bune</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bunes</b> are a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race, and inhabited at least one seeded planet featured in a previous season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World.</i> A slight, dragon-like people, Bunes are naturally peaceful.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bune on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bune</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burcu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Burcu</b> is a badger-headed <a href="bword://Porsuk">Porsuk</a> Swashbuckler crawler who spent much of her crawl floating near the bottom of the top ten leaderboard, and who came to blame <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for her father's death.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Turkey</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Porsuk</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Burcu on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Burcu</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burgundy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Burgundy</b> is a human assistant working in the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement on the Third Floor who is secretly cursed to transform into a <a href="bword://Krasue">Krasue</a> each night.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Burgundy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Burgundy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d been immune to Burn, which also came with some fire resistance, and it kept fire damage from compounding, which is what had happened to the.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book Burner Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Book Burner <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a></b> is an Upgrade Patch given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Talk of the Town Box">Talk of the Town Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://LBC Achievement">LBC Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Talk of the Town Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Book Burner Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Book_Burner_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Book Burner Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Book Burner Spell</b> is a spell <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets as part of his <a href="bword://Book Burner Patch">Book Burner Patch</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Book Burner Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Book_Burner_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burning building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The remaining lemurs screamed as they flew, brandishing shining knives as more knives flew at us from the windows of the now-burning.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burrowers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Burrowers</b> are the <a href="bword://Mantis">Mantis</a> royal family and governmental entity in charge of the Hive Home System, one of several Mantis-occupied solar systems in a cluster of stars. They are the driving force behind the development of Macro AIs, and a regular participant in Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Burrowers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Burrowers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Burst into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Everything was shattered a moment later as Shi Maria, confused and screaming, burst into the room, jump scaring us both as Katia’s dream and my dream finally collided with one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Busch Light</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was an ice-cold Busch Light, peach.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bush Elf</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bush Elves</b> are a subrace of Elves. Crawlers may choose the "Bush Elf" race on the Third Floor, or may encounter Bush Elf <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> on the Dungeon's Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bush Elf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bush_Elf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bush Elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of the few races who managed to not get fully wiped out by Scolopendra’s nine-tier attack, the bugbears are now one of the most common races one might find in the Kapok district of the Hunting Grounds.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>But Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “But Donut’s charisma is 76, and she has a merchant class, which means we’ll be buying it at a quarter of the price you sell it for.” Mordecai, ever the teacher, turned to me and said, “Charisma bonuses don’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>But Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, But Katia is described as okay.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Butcher's Masquerade Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Butcher's Masquerade</b> is a divine ritual spell and one of the holiest sacraments of <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a>, the Blessed Oak <a href="bword://Mother">Mother</a>. It cannot be learned or cast by ordinary crawlers; the right belongs to the goddess's clerics, and only to the most divine among them, who may use it once in their lives to convene a protected gathering of mortal enemies. No caster has ever completed one successfully.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Butcher's Masquerade Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Butcher%27s_Masquerade_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Butcher’s Masquerade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “This is us, taking the matter up privately.” * * * 62 Hours until the Butcher’s Masquerade “I appreciate what you’ve done for us, but that don’t mean we gonna just blindly trust you,” the changeling said as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bye</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Madison, are you sure?” “Rod will come.” “Bye, Madison,” I said, turning.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cabaret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente and Mordecai were going to try to figure out this shop-interface exploit, and I was going to task some of the strippers with returning to the Desperado Club to try to figure out the other method of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Call</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are too.” “Call him anyway.” “Gus,” Lucia shouted, her voice surprisingly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Called a goblin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Calm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Calm down little dude.” Mongo screeched in rage and jumped again, leaping astonishingly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Came into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What the heck is that noise?” Katia asked as she came into the room from the common.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Camouflage Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Camouflage</b> is a spell that conceals its caster's dot on the Minimap as long as the caster stays in place. <i>Camouflage</i> is included with the Guerilla Class Upgrade.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Camouflage Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Camouflage_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Canada</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Some of the larger countries like Russia, Canada, China, and the United States were broken up into regions, but the map lines were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Canadian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I could see it was a random collection of English-language books, from Fifty Shades of Grey to what appeared to be a Canadian.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cannon class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to her Glass Cannon class, the spell was significantly more powerful on this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cannonball! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Cannonball!</b> <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> would presumably be awarded to any crawler who survives falling from a great height. It probably contains some interesting information that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> will never have a chance to read.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cannonball! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cannonball%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Can’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Can’t tell what the wall configuration looks like, but this is where he is.” On the map, the area was a line of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Can’t attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’d love to have a matched set.” “We can’t attack them when they’re on their way to the starting blocks,” Imani.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Capitol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If the system was generous, and it dropped the key to the Capitol building, we would teleport away, Ren would get the key, and she could rush into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Capitol building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If the system was generous, and it dropped the key to the Capitol building, we would teleport away, Ren would get the key, and she could rush into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Capitolio Nacional</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m sorry about your...” Warning: The door to the exit stairwell at El Capitolio Nacional de Cuba has sustained damage and will become unusable in five.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Caprid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Caprid</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race and a Crawler race option, a sapient, goat-like people best known as the species of the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a> and of the crawler <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Caprid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Caprid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Captain Enormous</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: But, hypothetically , if someone randomly talked about the size of their own private parts without anyone else bringing it up, Captain Enormous would be a perfectly proper nickname for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Captain Fresh</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: INTERLUDE MINUS “If the AI so much as suspects you’re anything other than a simple tourist, it’s going to pop you like a boil,” Captain Fresh of the Syndicate security forces.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Card from the previous floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That strangeness—basically two of the same creature—reminded me of the Alpha Carl card from the previous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Card game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: My opinion, from even before the dungeon, was how could any card game be fair when not everyone had access to the same pool of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Careful</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Careful not to touch it with my hands, I closed the case around the floating gem, like I was catching a firefly with my two.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Caribbean Kung Fu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trained in the deadly art of Caribbean Kung Fu, the monk seal sentry’s sacred duty is to protect the coast and hunting grounds from the impending invasion of their dread enemy, the Red Maníseros Land Crabs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Carl</b> is the titular protagonist of <i>Dungeon Crawler Carl</i>. A former coast guardsman, Carl and his ex-girlfriend's cat are now fan-favorite crawlers on the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> gameshow, <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Primal</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Carl on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Carl</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: It sucks the mom got killed, but if she’s the one who raised those two, it’s probably not a big loss.” “The good news is that nobody is saying anything negative about Carl and Donut on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Victory and Drick, tell us what you think about the two celestial tier items Carl and Katia received.” Drick grunted, his voice only slightly touched by the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl and Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut, you’ll be happy to know that you brought in the highest sponsorship bid in the history of the series, beating out both Carl and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl and Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What was your reaction when you first heard that Carl and Princess Donut had become top-tier crawlers?” This went on for a few.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl and Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What was your reaction when you first heard that Carl and Princess Donut had become top-tier crawlers?” This went on for a few.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl's Doomsday Scenario</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Carl's Doomsday Scenario</b> is a "Pretty Damn Rare" item that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> created trying to solve the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest. During the (original) end of the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest on the Third Floor, Carl placed a <a href="bword://Sheol Glass Reaper Case">Sheol Glass Reaper Case</a> around an about-to-explode Soul Crystal and pulled the result into his inventory.</li><li><b>Source:</b> A Sheol Glass Reaper Case placed around an about to explode soul crystal.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Carl's Doomsday Scenario on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Carl%27s_Doomsday_Scenario_%28Item%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl's Jug O'Boom</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Carl's Jug O'Boom</b> is <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s take on a Dungeon Molotov Cocktail. Carl's Jug O'Boom is crafted by emptying a clay <a href="bword://Rev-Up Toilet-Grade Moonshine">Rev-Up Toilet-Grade Moonshine</a> jug to about 3/4 full, adding a few ounces of <a href="bword://Goblin Oil">Goblin Oil</a>, and corking the bottle with a <a href="bword://Torch">Torch</a>. The crawler only has to look at the Torch, select "Activate" in their Heads-Up Display (HUD), and throw the bottle.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Carl's Jug O'Boom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Carl%27s_Jug_O%27Boom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Book of Boom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Every single page from Carl’s Book of Boom , Donut’s A Banquet Fit for a Princess , and the original Book of Voodoo that offered lightning magic had now been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Doomsday</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing I had left were the big bombs, such as the Carl’s Doomsday Scenario bomb and the tactical nuke that was still attached to a robot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Doomsday Scenario</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing I had left were the big bombs, such as the Carl’s Doomsday Scenario bomb and the tactical nuke that was still attached to a robot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Jug</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I don’t suppose you have any extra torches?” * * * Carl’s Jug O’ Boom Type: Incendiary Tossable Effect: When lit and tossed, results in a small explosion, followed by burning splash damage over a wide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Naughty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A spokesperson for the royal family has requested people stop referring to the prince as ‘Carl’s Naughty Little Piggie,’” The audience roared, “But so far, nobody seems to be complying.” Odette turned to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Carl’s Naughty Little Piggie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A spokesperson for the royal family has requested people stop referring to the prince as ‘Carl’s Naughty Little Piggie,’” The audience roared, “But so far, nobody seems to be complying.” Odette turned to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cascadia</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cascadia</b> is a Kua-tin alien Dungeon admin, best known as the voice of the daily announcements broadcast to the crawlers during the Earth season of Dungeon Crawler World and famous for signing off with her catchphrase, "Now get out there and kill, kill, kill!"</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The "kill, kill, kill" lady</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Borant System</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-tin (Mudskipper)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cascadia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cascadia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cascadia's Turquoise Taco Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Cascadia's Turquoise Taco Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to all crawlers who end a card fight before any cards are drawn.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cascadia's Turquoise Taco Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cascadia%27s_Turquoise_Taco_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Casino Chip</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Casino Chip</b> can be used at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives a 100,000 credit chip in a <a href="bword://Lucky Bastard Box">Lucky Bastard Box</a> from the <a href="bword://Lazarus Achievement">Lazarus Achievement</a>. The black poker chip has pink and purple splotches along the edges. The Desperado Club dagger is in the middle. The chip feels like it weighs 15 pounds.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Lucky Bastard Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Casino Chip on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Casino_Chip</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cast</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Though I did receive something quite useful from a platinum Did-You-Really-Just-Cast-Fireball-in-a-Room-This-Small box.” “We still need to have a discussion about that,” Mordecai said, sounding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Casting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Casting now!” The whole creature glowed as its health zoomed up about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cat</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> is the only fully cat crawler in the Dungeon; however, at least one crawler chose the Were-Cat race, and several chose the <a href="bword://Cat Girl">Cat Girl</a> race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cat</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cat Girl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cat Girl</b> is a race of humanoid cat. It provides a charisma bonus. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> is offended by this race, and especially <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a>, who Donut says is a Havana Brown cat.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cat Girl on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cat_Girl</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cat show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Like that time Miss Beatrice got in the car accident when we were on our way back from the cat show, and I’d been asleep, and then suddenly there was this big bang, and I slammed against the side of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cat shows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Catch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Equipping this small buckler imbues the following effects: Catch physical attacks (Success percentage based off user’s dexterity) Catch magical bolt attacks (Success percentage based off user’s intelligence.).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cats</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Cats don’t shoot lasers from their eyes, either, but here we are.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cave Mudge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut hit the Cave Mudge standing in the open door with a pair of magic missiles, and it fell over dead just as the doors started to slide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cave Mudge Bonker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cave Mudge Bonkers</b> (or "Bonkers") are a Fourth Floor mob that can be found on the Yellow Line. Bonkers are gray-skinned, pig-like, corpulent monsters wobbling on thin legs. Their heads are topped with wisps of oily black hair, and feature black beady eyes and shark-like mouths. They have no necks and stand at approximately 4' 6" (1.37m) tall. They are armed with wooden clubs studded with nails.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cave Mudge Bonker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cave_Mudge_Bonker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Celestial</b> is the highest, rarest tier of Dungeon-made items and <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a>. Celestial objects are often tied to <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a>, either bestowing god-like powers or given by gods as quests rewards.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Celestial on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Celestial</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Ascendency</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We believe that Stockade and Houston both conspired to use the secured messaging system to speak with someone upon the 18 th floor, who is relaying real-time information via a third party participating in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Attendant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Armita had left a little before me, and she picked Celestial Attendant, which is the best, they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Boon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You have received a Celestial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut had mentioned how much she hated the “cheater” Quan no less than twenty times since he’d gotten that celestial box at the end of the third.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The last time the AI had gotten sued was when we’d been screwed out of the celestial boxes, and the system had been so butthurt over it, it had rewarded us with a ton of personal space.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Grenade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Rare and expensive, <b><a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Grenades</b> look like spiked orbs with a pin. When detonated, they summon a deity for one minute (60 seconds). They are commonly used near the end of the Ninth Floor Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Celestial Grenade on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Celestial_Grenade</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Now, let’s take a look at Carl’s, uh, very different Celestial item, which is now his third, behind the Scavenger’s Daughter patch we were just discussing and the potion that gave him his Gloom Wraith Phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Loot Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Celestial Loot Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor for picking up <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>'s <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> <a href="bword://Cloak of the Benevolent Champion">Cloak of the Benevolent Champion</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Celestial Loot Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Celestial_Loot_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to all Crawlers who were in <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a> during Operation Snake Pit. <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a>, <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>, and <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> got a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Who Let the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> Out This Time Box, and Daniel <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a> got a Legendary Who Let the Gods Out This Time Box because he left early.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Celestial_Orgy_of_Chaos_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Quest Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This item was originally awarded in a Celestial Quest Box during the third.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial realm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: What is important is that she was kicked out of the celestial realm and banished to this swamp where she spends her days plotting on how to get revenge on everyone and everything who has ever done her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Throne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I will make certain you survive until the twelfth floor, where you and Katia and the cat will help me claim the Celestial Throne once and for all There were actually two ways out of the Sepsis Whore.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celine Dion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’m pretty sure she had some 80s hits, yes.” “Celine Dion first charted in Canada in 1981,” Prepotente.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cerberus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cerberus</b> is a "three-headed monstrosity" and Gate Guardian rumored to guard one of the stairwells on the Twelfth Floor. <a href="bword://Orthrus">Orthrus</a> is Cerberus's little brother.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 29</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cerberus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cerberus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Certain</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Certain crawlers are simply too valuable to just throw away off screen, no matter how recalcitrant they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Certainly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And you have to put it in a bowl.” “Certainly, your majesty,” the waitress.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cesta Punta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The skill I’d received had been in something called Cesta.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chachi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the analog of Chalchiuhtlicue, or Chachi, in Earth culture is the Aztec goddess with the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chaco</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chaco</b> is an alien NPC and former Crawler who works for the Dungeon Crawler World production as an on-camera personality, best known as host of The <a href="bword://Prize Carousel">Prize Carousel</a>. A <a href="bword://Pterolykos">Pterolykos</a> Song Bard, he shares a violent history with <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a>, the Royal Court's manager, who attacks him on sight.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Chaco the Bard</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl | Pterolykos</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 7</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chaco on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chaco</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chaco the Bard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s the dungeon darling, giver-of-prizes, slayer of gods, former Crawler champion, it’s Chaco the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chalchiuhtlicue</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chalchiuhtlicue</b> is a goddess in the dungeon. After the death of the dogs of <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a>, Chalchiuhtlicue became Lucia's newest influence, mentally communicating with her.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 51</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chalchiuhtlicue on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chalchiuhtlicue</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Champion Best</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is the greatest thing that has ever happened since I won Grand Champion Best in Show last year in Cleveland.” Mongo continued to sniff at the imposter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Champion Best in Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Champion Best in Dungeon Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Champion of Nekhebit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You will pay for this, Champion of Nekhebit!” And with that, he too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Champion Pack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Champion Pack</b> is a reward for beating the key master on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Defeating keymaster on the Eighth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Champion Pack on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Champion_Pack</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chance spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Chance spell is described as currently at level 11, which brought it up to level 12 with her Brain Trust.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chandra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Princess Chandra, Esquire</b> is a <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> lawyer and the opportunistic widow of <a href="bword://Rishi">Rishi</a>. Following a bloody political coup in her home system, she uses an antiquated Naga law to legally force a marriage to the dungeon crawler <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, attempting to seize control of his and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s massive fortunes. She is not a crawler, but an external citizen maneuvering through the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> legal system.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Naga Sector</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naga</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Interlude: Chandra</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chandra on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chandra</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Changbi Demon</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Changbi Demon</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> chooses this as her race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Changbi Demon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Changbi_Demon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Change the rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because if you are nice to the wrong person, they’ll either take advantage of you, or worse, they might never leave.” “That seems like terrible advice, Uncle Quasar.” “No, telling someone to sign a contract in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Changed the rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The rules had been pretty clear that they weren’t going to add new NPCs once we’d flooded the town, but it appeared they’d either changed the rules or found some sort of loophole as there were some new shops.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Changeling</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Changeling</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor, and a people of shapeshifters found throughout the Dungeon as <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, mobs, and game guides. Unlike doppelgangers, they shift into any species they have physically touched and gain part of that species' abilities along with its form.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Changeling on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Changeling</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Changeling Principal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: That is both the curse and the driving force of the Changeling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Changelings</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, all crawlers in this quadrant will receive a permanent fifty percent charisma bonus during any future interactions with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chaos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We need to purchase an upgrade to the base’s hygiene facilities before we descend into further chaos.”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Character Actor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: When Donut had used her Character Actor skill to choose the Artist Alley Merchant class as the one to emulate on this floor, it had come with multiple skills and benefits, including a huge discount for buying.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Character Actor skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: When Donut had used her Character Actor skill to choose the Artist Alley Merchant class as the one to emulate on this floor, it had come with multiple skills and benefits, including a huge discount for buying.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charge of the Light Brigade Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Charge of the Light Brigade Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> (and presumably <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> and <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>) for storming into <a href="bword://Khepri">Khepri</a>'s temple uninvited to kill <a href="bword://Vinata">Vinata</a> during Operation Snake Pit. It is most likely in reference to the doomed cavalry charge by the same name.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Charge of the Light Brigade Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Charge_of_the_Light_Brigade_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chariot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Not only was Katia shaped as the entire back seat of the Chariot—which I’d been forced to remove for this to work—but she also played the “body” of Wynne the gnome sitting atop.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charisma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Charles</b> Ellis is a bartender <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> attempts to seduce in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. He was killed by <a href="bword://Minge">Minge</a> after being kidnapped by Samantha.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Porsuk</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Charles on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Charles</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charm Animal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We quickly moved through, and I rushed inside to grab the now-completed Charm Animal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charm Animal Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <i><b>Charm Animal</b></i> <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a> tames a Dungeon animal for the duration of the Floor. The potion must be brewed for that specific type of creature using blood.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Recipe</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Charm Animal Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Charm_Animal_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charm spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All of these creatures, at least all the ones that weren’t dead-dead, including the zombies and the level 125 sea snake city boss, were all under a massive, poetry-themed charm spell that Sister Ines was in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Charm spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This would supposedly protect from charm spells and things like the Eye of the Bedlam Bride, should he have something like that hidden upon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cheat Code</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: That +3 in Pugilism was a huge bonus considering I’d already managed to get it to 10 thanks to that Cheat Code.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cheat Code Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Cheat Code Potion</b> is a potion that adds 3 levels to a random combat or magic skill. When a crawler receives this potion, a flashing red timer (starting with <i>at least</i> 17 minutes) appears at the bottom of the description. Even if a crawler consumes this potion from their hotlist, they feel a crackling sensation in their mouth, as though they had "just downed a whole packet of Pop Rocks."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cheat Code Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cheat_Code_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Check</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Check it out.” She flicked her wrist, and her arm changed shape, forming into what looked like a buckler-sized glob of blue.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chee</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chee</b> are a race. The Chee were a peaceful race before being attacked by the high-elves lead by <a href="bword://King Finian">King Finian</a>, Finian's daughter Princess <a href="bword://Imogen">Imogen</a> transformed them into Were-kin.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chee on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chee</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chevy Astro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was my mom’s Chevy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chevy Bel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Their vehicle was a bright yellow 1950s Chevy Bel Air with a massive blower popping up from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chevy Bel Air</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Their vehicle was a bright yellow 1950s Chevy Bel Air with a massive blower popping up from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chicken and Goblin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Why do you have the Chicken and Goblin recipes book in your hotlist!” Imani: Something just happened to my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chicken Hawk Sentinel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Spring-operated Chicken Hawk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the teleport totem hack he and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> used to fight <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a> with <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chickenshit Exploit Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chickenshit_Exploit_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chief Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It gets quite heated there at the end.” War Chief Stalwart : I demand the immediate return of my family’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Child Actor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were: Artist Alley Mogul NecroBard And Former Child Actor I said a silent thank you to the game gods that they hadn’t named the roller derby one “Derby Queen.” She probably would’ve insisted upon picking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Child Actor class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’re technically a mercenary.” “Actually,” Mordecai said, “I suspect if we make it to the eleventh floor, Donut’s Former Child Actor class might come with mercenary accessories, too.” Donut kept going.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Children</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Children from all over the realm would laugh and clap as they watched these funny little creatures juggle all manner of items, from balls to pins to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Children of Inpewt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Children of Inpewt</b> are powerful revenants created by the spell <a href="bword://Zerzura Spell">Zerzura Spell</a>. Zerzura transfers a full Dungeon settlement, including all of its <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> and buildings, to a "random" location on a deeper floor. Every creature previously occupying the location is immediately killed and then resurrected as Children of Inpewt. Everyone in the transferred settlement is marked for death by the Children of Inpewt, who will not stop until all their marks are dead.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Children of Inpewt on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Children_of_Inpewt</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chilly Goat</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chilly Goats</b> are a mob found on the First Floor. They are white, horned goats with mouths full of fangs.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chilly Goat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chilly_Goat</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chin'Dua</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The second Hunter killed by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Draconian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 5, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chin'Dua on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chin%27Dua</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>China</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Destruction team was another group of crawlers from China I didn’t know very well mixed with that Hawaiian guy with the really long name who went by Makana and his partner, the hexcrafter paladin crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chinese</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most of the creatures were Asian-themed or nautical-themed, like Japanese Oni and small, Chinese dragons and dozens of other monstrosities like sharks and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chin’Dua</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was infected with Left to Fester, and it wouldn’t go away until poor Chin’Dua was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chirag Ali</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Not much is known about Chirag Ali. They appear once on the Leaderboard during the middle of the Fifth Floor, in the 10th spot.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 17, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chirag Ali on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chirag_Ali</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chiyome</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chiyome. <a href="bword://Razor Fox">Razor Fox</a>. Level 80 Mistress of Nunchaku.</b> One of three from team The Wild Hunt.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Razor Fox</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chiyome on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chiyome</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Choose</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Choose ‘Station Repair’ and you’ll get to the station, and the train will be fixed.” She waved toward the starboard side of the train, the side I hadn’t entered.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>choose an Earth Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Choosing this class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chop box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Chop box is described as a quivering golden blanket that sparkled as it fluttered through the room like a magic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chopper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: From there we’d head back to the saferoom, watch the evening’s show, sleep, and then hop on the Chopper and spend the rest of the time grinding away in different neighborhoods until it was time to hit the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chopper Pilot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to my Chopper Pilot skill and the help of the throttle, we could reach top speed in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Li Jun, Li Na, and Chris, along with several others who were not on the Twister , had moved into the town after Louis had bombed the second creeper to death to find several of Eva’s team—a mix of former.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris and Brandon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were Imani and Yolanda, who were nurses, and the brothers Chris and Brandon who’d both been maintenance guys at the old folk’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris and Clay</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * I slept two hours and came out to find Mordecai standing on the kitchen table quietly conversing with Chris and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris and Imani</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Brandon and I moved to the roof—made of the cross bars of weight racks—while Chris and Imani twisted massive, fist-sized butterfly bolts, affixing the pieces.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris and Maggie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * The celestial grenade had been a sponsor prize to Chris and Maggie from the Skull Empire, and it was supposed to have been used to summon a pain god to kill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chris Andrews</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Chris Andrews</b> is a Crawler from Wenatchee, Washington, and one of the original members of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party. A quiet, stoic maintenance man, he stood by the group's elderly wards and fought alongside his brother Brandon and the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> to open the First Floor stairwell.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Silent Bob</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth (Wenatchee, WA)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Igneous | Werewolf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Chris Andrews on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Chris_Andrews</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Christ</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Christ!” I cringed as the black dog thing—the mount for the Jugglers—yelped and was thrown to its.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Christmas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We came to the edge of an alley that was blocked by a pile of bicycle rickshaws that had somehow gotten all tangled up here, all with colorful awnings and handmade signs that read “Taxi.” A few were adorned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Christmas Carol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This Christmas Carol , Midnight Library , Dark Matter bullshit you’re seeing is more of a thought experiment than the real thing, so don’t get your crawler panties too wadded over all of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Christmas Morning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Ceasefire Chapter 1 Chapter 2 Chapter 3 Chapter 4 Dante Chapter 5 Chapter 6 Chapter 7 Chapter 8 Chapter 9 Chapter 10 Tipid Chapter 11 Chapter 12 Chapter 13 Chapter 14 Chapter 15 Everly Chapter 16 Chapter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Christmas party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d been in the car, on our way to a Christmas party, and she’d casually mentioned one of her mom’s Persians—Sugar Bun, who was Donut’s aunt or cousin or something—was pregnant and was due.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chuluuna</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Chuluuna is described as also a human, but he had a strength-based class that allowed him to turn his skin to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Chum bombs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Chum bombs is described as nothing more than triple-ply garbage bags filled with various dead bodies along with fused, 1/8 th -strength hob-lobbers, each with a piece of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cichociemni</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A group of crawlers. They are named after Polish special forces despite none of them being from Poland. Mentioned on <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a>'s show in Epilogue of Book 3.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cichociemni on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cichociemni</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cici</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cici</b> is one of <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a>'s Rottweilers that entered the dungeon with her. Cici and <a href="bword://Gustavo 3">Gustavo 3</a> are put in the pet playroom while Lucia Mar goes into the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. They get riled up, kill two attendants and another pet and break into the main club. This, along with Lucia Mar killing an Elite she is talking to, is how Lucia gets banned from the club.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Rotweiler</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cici on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cici</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cici and Gustavo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Princess Donut has thrown the gauntlet down, directly challenging Cici and Gustavo 3, the two beloved pets of current front-runner, Lucia Mar!” The orange-hued lizard announcer breathlessly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cincinnati Bengals</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: He wore a Cincinnati Bengals hat and was always poking at the female residents, saying, “You still alive, Marybeth?” None of them were named.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Circe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d mostly come to this panel not because they wanted to get a mind-numbing history lesson, but because they knew Circe and I would be in the same room together, and they wanted to see what was going to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Circe Took</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Circe Took</b> is of the mantis race. She is a minor hive queen. She is the mother of <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a> <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> and <a href="bword://Xindy">Xindy</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets her at CrawlCon! where she is the moderator of the Crawling Through the Ages panel. Circe is enraged with Carl for killing her daughter Xindy and is further enraged by Carl promising to kill her other daughter, Vrah. She later managed to successfully sue to let the hunters leave early, arguing that now that the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> was no longer running the show, their contracts were no longer binding.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantis</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Circe Took on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Circe_Took</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The entire team had been killed a while back by a City Boss slime.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City bosses</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even though I hadn’t killed the city boss, I’d hoped to return and loot whatever city bosses dropped, but there was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City defense system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But you may also use the coupons for your base here, and that may be a better choice.” Britney was currently learning all she could about the Larracos city defense system from Juice.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City elf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I spun and tossed the bomb as hard as I could directly at the far dot of the city elf, the one who had cast the anti-magic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They had a sign and a trio of city elves out front attempting to talk those passing by to enter and hear “The good news about Apito the Oak Mother.” They were only two blocks away from the Magistrate’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>City Hall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The crawlers had fucked up the City Hall quest, just like we were in the middle of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Civil war</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You had your personal monetary reserves depleted because of your own lax security, your reputation is destroyed, and you are about to have a civil war on your hands that’ll make the Borant system look like a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clammy Clown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Clammy Clown remained at his post, arms crossed, blocking my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clammy the Clown</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clammy the Clown</b> is a clown in <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> comes across him when entering the circus tent as he and <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a> attack the circus.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clammy the Clown on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clammy_the_Clown</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clarabelle</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clarabelle</b> is a lizard-faced <a href="bword://Crocodilian">Crocodilian</a> NPC who works as a bouncer at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Clarabelle knows <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> from previous seasons and is happy to let him enter the club on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Crocodilian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clarabelle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clarabelle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clarabelle the Crocodilian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This room was similar to the Desperado Club entrance area where Clarabelle the Crocodilian stood guard, but much cleaner and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: NPCs were supposed to be recycled after 20, but the showrunners insisted that expensive, deeper-level audits weren’t necessary since the Class A and Class B NPCs got auto-recycled when they were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Claude</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They rolled down the hill toward me, and I had to hurdle one as I continue to scramble back toward Claude the rampaging frog lizard thing, who continued to look about with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Claude Sludgington the Fourth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Claude Sludgington the Fourth</b>, a Level 65 Borough Boss Eryops Gigantis.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> River Squatter or the Mud Kato</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon: Sixth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Eryops Gigantis</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Claude Sludgington the Fourth on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Claude_Sludgington_the_Fourth</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clay</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clay</b> is the gatekeeper at Hump Town, armed with a spear and a home-made bazooka with five guided anti-air rockets.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dromedarian</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clay on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clay</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clay-ton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clay-ton</b> is is a <a href="bword://Cretin">Cretin</a> NPC employed as a body guard at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. He later joins the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> as a mercenary.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cretin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clay-ton on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clay-ton</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cleaner Bot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cleaner Bots</b> are a Personal Space upgrade that crawlers can purchase for 25,000 gold beginning on the Fourth Floor. Beyond simple housekeeping, the units are capable of far more than they first appear, and by Book 8 a single cleaner bot becomes the linchpin of a plan to smuggle crawlers out of the dungeon entirely.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bopca</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cleaner Bot on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cleaner_Bot</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clearly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Clearly you guys have a beef with one another.” Frank whispered something to Maggie, who returned to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cleiton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cleiton</b> was a crawler from Earth and a member of Team <a href="bword://Flamengo">Flamengo</a>. He had sapphire armor and occasionally appeared on the recap episodes. Cleiton attended the Butcher's Masquerade at the end of the Sixth Floor, and was the opening act of the talent competition. He nervously told "knock knock" jokes and fumbled the punchlines.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 72</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cleiton on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cleiton</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Click</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Click on each of the three recommendations so we can see the information.” “I’m telling you right now I am absolutely not choosing this one,” Donut said as she clicked on the first.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cliffhanger Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Cliffhanger Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for climbing the patch of Iowa in the Florida Keys on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cliffhanger Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cliffhanger_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Climb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I stood, and using my Climb skill, I quickly shimmied up a pipe toward the 65th, which was home to multiple.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clint</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And this tree protected you from a lightning attack?” Clint answered as I bent down to once again retrieve Samantha, who was now shouting for me to let her do it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clint and Holger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut had to do a bit of talking to get the guards to allow Clint and Holger.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clint Smashgrab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clint Smashgrab</b> is a bald, one-legged were-castor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Chee | Were-kin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clint Smashgrab on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clint_Smashgrab</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cloak of the Benevolent Champion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Cloak of the Benevolent Champion</b> is a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a>-tier shoulder accessory that provides massive, game-changing buffs to the wearer's stats, spells, and skills. Originally awarded to the crawler <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>, it is later looted by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and worn by Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Looted from Quan Ch; originally awarded via a Celestial Quest Box during the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cloak of the Benevolent Champion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cloak_of_the_Benevolent_Champion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clockwork Triplicate</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clockwork Triplicate</b> is a utility spell that creates two clockwork copies of a crawler's pet or minion. When <i>Clockwork Triplicate</i> is cast, the target appears to split into three. The two duplicates look and behave like the target, but act independently. The <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> does not identify which copy is real.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clockwork Triplicate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clockwork_Triplicate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clockwork Triplicate on Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut cast Clockwork Triplicate on Mongo, and the two clockworks screeched as they appeared, spreading off to the side, bouncing up and down with.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a></b> on the Fourth Floor is a Borough Boss, and part of the Iron Tangle political statement. The Iron Tangle is a massive political statement by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>, and the fact that there is a version of Krakaren in the level is directed towards the actions of the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary, Krakaren</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Fourth_Floor%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a> Clone</b> is a Neighborhood Boss on the Second Floor, and part of the running <a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> storyline. There are at least three separate instances of the Krakaren Clone on the level.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Second Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Second Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Second_Floor%29</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Close</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Close the door!” Katia grabbed the sliding door, kicked away the block, and slid it closed, locking it, just as it rocked with a pair of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Close the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We were going to open the door, push the cart in, close the door, run until we had several walls between us, and hit the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Closed door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The AMC Pacer of team Yokai cast something and moved right through the closed door in the hallway and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Closed garage door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: My Triage spell is only level 5, and it says his ailment is hidden.” “They did it,” Splash Zone said, glaring at the closed garage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Closed the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I went into the bathroom, I closed the door, and while holding her gently but firmly, I placed her in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clothing items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first dozen were bronze and gold adventurer boxes, filled with good healing and mana potions along with a few potions of Phase Through Wall , which Mordecai was pretty excited about, along with dozens of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cloud attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Louis mass killed about two hundred of them with a cloud attack, and we have more of these things than we know what to do with.” She plopped a deck onto the counter, unveiling a ton of Time Extend and Buff.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cloud of Exhaust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s called Puddle Jumper .” “What level is your Cloud of Exhaust ?” Mordecai asked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cloud of Exhaust Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cloud of Exhaust Spell</b> is a telekinesis spell that knocks out mobs, mobs wake up after taking damage but any damage dealt to them in the next 30 seconds will be enhanced.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cloud of Exhaust Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cloud_of_Exhaust_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Club and Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was no indication anyone ever seeing the naga to have ever utilized the personal saferoom system.” “Wait, blood bars don’t drain in the Desperado Club and Club Vanquisher?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Club Scolopendra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She also suspected that he’d wanted to go down to Club Scolopendra but probably couldn’t afford the rate, instead settling in one of the multiple journalist barges, which was lucky for him considering what was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Club Vanquisher</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Club Vanquisher</b> is one of the two main clubs of the World Dungeon, first available to crawlers in the Over City on the Third Floor. The exclusive, country-club-style religious counterpart to the raucous <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, it caters to the dungeon's clerics, paladins, and the worshippers of its many deities.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Club Vanquisher on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Club_Vanquisher</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clurichaun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clurichaun</b> are a fairy-class mob on the Second Floor. Small and troll-like with humanoid bodies and oversized heads, Clurichaun are marked by curly black hair, hooked noses, small feet, and very few teeth. They make a puglike gurgling noise when attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clurichaun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clurichaun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unvaccinated Clurichaun Rev-Up Consultant</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Clurichaun">Clurichaun</a></b> are a fairy-class mob on the Second Floor. Small and troll-like with humanoid bodies and oversized heads, Clurichaun are marked by curly black hair, hooked noses, small feet, and very few teeth. They make a puglike gurgling noise when attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unvaccinated Clurichaun Rev-Up Consultant on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unvaccinated_Clurichaun_Rev-Up_Consultant</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coast Guard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I joined the Coast Guard right after I turned 18, and everything worked out okay until aliens decided to come along and destroy...” I paused, my eyes catching a photo on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coblyn Corporation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Coblyn Corporation, in their efforts to protect their burgeoning child drug empire from getting discovered and shut down, tried to put some protections in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>CockBlock Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>CockBlock Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he kills <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a>, an NPC needed for Daniel <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a> to complete his own quest.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: CockBlock Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/CockBlock_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cockroach</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: But what he’s really doing is planning an elaborate suicide that’s probably going to get all of us killed also, and since he’s not going to be in a party with me anymore, he’s going to die sad and alone.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cockroach ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of her biggest strengths was that Cockroach ability, though we never got to see it in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cockroach skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: But what he’s really doing is planning an elaborate suicide that’s probably going to get all of us killed also, and since he’s not going to be in a party with me anymore, he’s going to die sad and alone.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coffee shop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We made our way to the saferoom, which was a small coffee shop name “Cuba Libro.” A street over was a park with dozens of more mobs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coffee Shop Author Kit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4 and 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Coffee Shop Author Kit</b> is a crafting tool consisting of a magical sheet of paper, a quill pen, and a little capped jar filled with black ink. It allows users to copy information from a second (or third) page of their scratchpad onto the magical paper or from the magical paper to their scratchpad.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Makeup Sex is the Best Sex Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Coffee Shop Author Kit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Coffee_Shop_Author_Kit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Collapse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * Hunting Trophies Collected: 38 Time to level Collapse: 11 days 8.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Collapse of this floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I am leaving upon the collapse of this floor, and I won’t return until the beginning of the ninth, so we need to discuss this now.” I groaned in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Colonel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had to set up the officer hierarchy ourselves, and Donut had allowed Florin to set it up after giving him the rank War Leader III , which had changed to Colonel after he’d spent some time examining the base.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Colonel Boomer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I still don’t understand why they can have these things, but we can’t.” Colonel Boomer leaned over the map, examining all the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Colonel in the Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Colonel in the Princess Posse Faction Wars.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Colonel Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Colonel Rosetta : Head’s up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Colonel Tipid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Go find Colonel Tipid and tell him you’re trained sappers, and maybe you can join up with the Frothing Tunnel.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Columbia House</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Believe it or not, Columbia House and its competitors made bucketloads of money on those few rubes who did manage to stick to the terms of their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Columbia House Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Columbia House Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he takes out a loan to pay for the ring upgrade in <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>. The 8th track on Cuts Both Ways is called "Get on Your Feet".</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Columbia House Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Columbia_House_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combat Started</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I think that’s actually number...” Combat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combatant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Iron Tangle Human Resources Associate This is a Non-Combatant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>combatant NPC</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Quint – Level 75 Half-Djinn, Half-Garbage Scowl Desperado Club Pharmacist This is a non-combatant NPC Half-Djinns are common amongst the Hunting Grounds and other forested levels of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Combine a gathering of Tuskling aristocracy, add an alcohol-fueled, sexually-charged orgy of war lust, and sometimes, just sometimes, the wild, ancient battle magic that permeates their war-torn world casts.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combined</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What is this?” “Combined, it’s a little more than 1,000 gold a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Combo Card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Combo Card</b> is a T'Ghee utility card.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Combo Card on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Combo_Card</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Come</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Come on!” I immediately moved to send out a group message, but both Katia and Elle were already on it, telling everybody they would personally hunt down and murder anybody who so much as shot an arrow at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Come into the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d also come into the dungeon armed to the teeth, but he now relied solely on his automatic shotgun, which appeared to be heavily modified even before he received the magical, unlimited-ammo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Come to Daddy Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Come to Daddy Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for rejecting an exit deal at the start of the Tenth Floor. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> does not get this achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Come to Daddy Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Come_to_Daddy_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Comes to Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Comes to Crawler Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coming</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Coming here and helping the current crawlers, this time willingly, was their last, great statement to the cruel galaxy which had ignored them for too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Commandant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Commandant will not be pleased if his uncle tries to eat him.” “Okay, but the potion will take a few minutes to work.” “As long as it works before we.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Commandant Kane</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Commandant Kane</b> is the leader of <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> faction in Bubble #543. <a href="bword://Father">Father</a> of <a href="bword://Bonnie">Bonnie</a>. Nephew of <a href="bword://Wynne">Wynne</a>. Holder of one third of the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Commandant Kane on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Commandant_Kane</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Commander Stockade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Commander Stockade</b> is the (former) leader of the <a href="bword://Lemig Sortion">Lemig Sortion</a> faction He got trapped in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> flooded the ninth floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Caterpillar like alien</li><li><b>First scene:</b> The Eye of the Bedlam Bride</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Commander Stockade on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Commander_Stockade</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Common room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Common room is described as open, and I could see the entire area was decked out with Christmas.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Community Pool</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Community Pool</b></i> is a powerful rare scroll that allows the caster to transfer an infection from Target A to Target B. The caster must be physically touching both targets for the spell to take effect, and will be stunned for one minute after the casting completes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Community Pool on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Community_Pool</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Compatible</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Yes Environment on other side of portal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Compensated Anarchist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I guess it’s a good thing we now have a group member who knows what he’s doing.” Mordecai narrowed his eyes at me as I scrolled down and picked Compensated Anarchist as my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Complete this quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Here’s the reward again in case you forgot: If you successfully complete this quest, two stand-ins of your choosing will be summoned to the arena on the 11th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Completing the quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : In addition to the Gold Quest Box you’ve received for completing the quest, new quest locations will sometimes appear on your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Comrade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Comrade,” Rosetta said, still wheezing as she started to untie the straps, “you are significantly heavier than you appear.” “At least you have your dignity,” Edgar grumbled as he unceremoniously clattered to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Comrades</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Comrades, if I ever get out of this, which seems more and more unlikely by the day, I swear upon the moons I will spend the rest of my days making certain this evil is not only eradicated from the stars, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Concierge Shark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Concierge Sharks</b> are a mob found in water quadrants on the Fifth Floor, and in previous water-themed Dungeons. Concierge Sharks look like typical tiger sharks, but are 15' (4.572m) long, jet black, and have glowing red eyes. They are attracted to blood.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Concierge Shark on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Concierge_Shark</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Concrete floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sitting on the concrete floor of the bar, wrapped around the base of the table, was a tall, long-limbed soother.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Confusing Fog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: More potions, more dynamite, another lighter, a couple smoke bombs, a couple more scrolls of Confusing Fog.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Confusing Fog Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Confusing Fog</b></i> is a spell that fills a space with thick, wet fog for a brief period of time (seconds equal to caster's Intelligence x 5). The billowing cloud of fog is initially opaque but immediately becomes translucent to the caster, crawlers, and white-tagged <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>; however, mobs remain blinded for its duration. While the fog is active, water beads on the ground and every surface.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Confusing Fog Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Confusing_Fog_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Congrats</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Views: 933 septillion Followers: 3.5 Quintillion Favorites: 971 Quadrillion Leaderboard Rank: 3 Bounty: 3,200,000 gold Congrats.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Congratulations</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Congratulations on your victory.” “Yeah,” I said as Baroness Victory, now Prime Minister Victory disappeared for the last.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Conrad E</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Conrad E</b> was a human Crawler from Earth who participated in the Dungeon. He was part of the large group of surviving crawlers who made a frantic dash toward the stairwell to escape the Third Floor before the level collapsed.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Russia</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 25</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Conrad E on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Conrad_E</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Conscription</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Conscription spell was far from perfect, and I knew of several tricks one could employ to hinder an army’s efforts even with the debuff.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Conscription Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Conscription</b> is a powerful military charm used primarily during the Faction Wars to force unaffiliated crawlers, <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, and even deities to fight for a specific army.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Conscription Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Conscription_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Consider</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Consider yourself lucky I have given you this much consideration.” He turned his hand to the side, dumping me and Samantha toward the never-ending.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Considering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Considering how tight the tunnel was, we’d probably get squished by the next train in a matter of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Consort Ugloo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Right now the vote is two to seven against turning the safety protocols off.” I pulled out the picture of the now-dead Queen Consort Ugloo of the Skull Empire, mother of the Maestro and Prince.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Constitution</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So after I was done, I ended up with: Strength: 41 +3 (When Gauntlet Formed) Intelligence: 15 Constitution: 34 Dexterity: 23 Charisma: 25 “A strength over 40 is good for the fourth floor,” Mordecai said when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Constitution buff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She had her colorful butterfly wings fully extended, and every time they touched me, they gave me a constitution buff that lasted ten.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Constitution Wearer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: So here are the original benefits of these naughty little undies: + 2 to Constitution Wearer may cast a level 15 Protective Shell once every 30.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Consumable</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Consumable card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d been a totem short after Donut used Golden Combo to temporarily fuse Lazarus with Skylar Spinach and then added Ren’s other consumable card, Glow Up , to make the combination.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Consumable cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If we kill them fast, we’ll have access to their full deck before they can waste any of those valuable consumable.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Containment Interface</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Containment Interface is described as now available for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Containment Warning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Containment.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Contents Chapter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Contents Chapter 1 Chapter 2 Chapter 3 Chapter 4 Chapter 5 Chapter 6 Chapter 7 Chapter 8 Chapter 9 Chapter 10 Chapter 11 Chapter 12 Chapter 13 Chapter 14 Chapter 15 Chapter 16 Chapter 17 Chapter 18 Chapter 19.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Contessa Purrington</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She drew blood.” Contessa Purrington hissed louder and batted furiously at the electric walls of the bounty.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Control effects</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I felt the mental tug that attempted to get me to stop, but thanks to my Wisp Armor ’s ability to negate mind-control effects, I still had autonomy over myself, but only for the next five.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Control of the train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you get on a named line that has a stop at station 436, and you get control of the train, you can use the switching station to get back to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Control Sigil</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Control Sigil</b> is a Dungeon mechanic used by the show runners to program specific actions. To those who have the Escape Plan Skill, a Control Sigil looks like a glowing circle that "looks like something used in a Satanic ritual mixed with the text from Frodo's One Ring". To anyone else the sigil doesn't show up.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Control Sigil on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Control_Sigil</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cooldown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cooldown: This spell can only be cast once per floor, and it can only be cast on the Scolopendra Levels, meaning levels 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Coolie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Coolie</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (19th Edition)</i>. Coolie was a popular crawler on <i> Dungeon Crawler World: Qurux</i>, and appeared on multiple <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> programs during their time in the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Qurux</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Coolie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Coolie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Copyright</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Copyright © 2022 by Matt Dinniman All rights.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corcunda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Corcunda</b> is a former male stripper in the Penis Parade of the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> who was physically split into two halves that developed different personalities, Corky and <a href="bword://Porky">Porky</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Half Mantuar</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Corcunda on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Corcunda</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corcunda and Dong</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And with a terrifying, in-unison roar, Corcunda and Dong jumped from the back of the truck and galloped directly at Grull just as the magnet spell.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Your other half has spent a long time searching for you, but he’s in real trouble, and he needs your help.” “What sort of trouble?” I spent a few moments explaining everything I knew about Corky, his other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corky and Dong</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it was just Corky and Dong for a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corky and Porky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I turned to see Corky and Porky, naked, smashed together like a grilled cheese sandwich, all wrapped up with plastic wrap in various places, multiple hernias bulging out, Dong holding them together with all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corner of the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Corner of the map is described as found to be not as hot as they were supposed to be, and they were adjusted.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corner of the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We will ask the AI to fix that.” There was a ping, and the Mexx robot said from the corner of the room, “The AI indicates that it loves a good loophole, and it is unable to close it unless someone makes an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cornets</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cornets</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that can be found at Banshee Station, station number 116 on the Yellow Line of the Iron Tangle Cornets look like tall, skinless, eyeless, human-sized jackrabbits walking on two legs. They are hunched forward with their rabbit ears almost reaching the ceiling. They have a massive mouth with teeth. They make an odd humming noise.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cornets on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cornets</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corp DungeonWerx</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ultima Corp DungeonWerx Municipal, Multi-Destination Doorway-based Subspace.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corporal Pillbox</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Corporal Pillbox</b> is a gnoll mercenary who leads a squad of fellow gnolls in Empress D'Nadia's employ during the Ninth Floor Faction Wars, and survives the entire conflict on <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s side after his surrender.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Corporal Pillbox on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Corporal_Pillbox</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Corporation Neural</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: You do not have a Valtay Corporation Neural Interface.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cosmic Buff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “There’s another type called a ‘Cosmic Buff’ that raises a random stat by 10 points, but I don’t know the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cosmic Buff Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cosmic Buff Potions</b> are pink, frothy potions that immediately raise a random stat by 10 points. Shortly after arriving on the Eighth Floor, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> each receive a single Cosmic Buff Potion in a Platinum <a href="bword://Here&#x27;s Some Good Shit Because You&#x27;re Gonna Need It Box">Here's Some Good Shit Because You're Gonna Need It Box</a> awarded for the <a href="bword://Fuck This Floor Achievement">Fuck This Floor Achievement</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Here's Some Good Shit Because You're Gonna Need It Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cosmic Buff Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cosmic_Buff_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cosmic Lounge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: First off, people are all over the Desperado Club, including the Cosmic Lounge in the basement, and while there’s lots of places to hide, there isn’t anywhere comfortable, and there’s been no indication of any.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cosmic Sense</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She had a passive ability called Cosmic Sense , which appeared to be like my skill that warned me when a god was present, but hers worked on both gods and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Costa Rica</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The saferoom at station 59 appeared to be a restaurant from Costa Rica called “Soda.” We pushed our way through the small room, past the Bopca, and into our personal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Country boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was being hunted by bounty hunters who were going to illegally sell her to Borant so they could transform her and use her as a country.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Court of Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Embroidered onto the flag on the top and bottom was a motorcycle club-style patch that read, “The Royal Court of Princess Donut.” Centered on the boxers over the crotch area was an embroidered bomb patch.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cousin Oliver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She was one of the most celebrated North American Persians until her granddaughter came along and shattered all her records.” “You just suggested we name him Cousin Oliver.” “That was before I realized you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cover Design</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cover Design by Toby Dinniman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: com/ lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman https://.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cover Illustration</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Covered bridge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The second funeral bell pointed to a poster that was tacked to the interior wall of the covered bridge behind.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crab Chowder Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crab Chowder Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for actions taken on the Eighth Floor with <a href="bword://Raul the Crab">Raul the Crab</a> and <a href="bword://Alpha Male Carl">Alpha Male Carl</a>. Carl opens this achievement in a safe room on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crab Chowder Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crab_Chowder_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cracker Barrel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl, do you think Quan even knew what a Cracker Barrel.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cracker Jack</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cracker Jack</b> is a scroll that unlocks weapons or gear locked to someone else.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cracker Jack on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cracker_Jack</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crafter</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crafter</b> are They look similar to a soother, they are tall and thin with pale skin that's weathered and wrinkled and covered in veins. They have big bug like eyes, and a head covered in coarse hair and with two distinct humps similar to a hearts. They have a line of four red nipples on their chest.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crafter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crafter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crafter's Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Crafter's Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he uses the <a href="bword://Goo-Inator 3000">Goo-Inator 3000</a> on the <a href="bword://Engineer&#x27;s Table">Engineer's Table</a>, crafting for the first time. <b>Bronze Crafting Box</b></li><li><b>Source:</b> Martha Stewart Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crafter's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crafter%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crafting room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This floor was going to require us spending a lot of time in the crafting room, which meant less time for regular.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crafting Studio</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We need to get the cleaning thing like they had in that production trailer.” In the end, we ended up with the bed, an automatic shower that wasn’t quite as nice as the one we’d seen on the surface, and a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Craftsman 3000 Series Rolling Tool Chest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Craftsman 3000 Series Rolling Tool Chest</b> was <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s tool chest from before the collapse. He was offered it as a reward from the <a href="bword://Prize Carousel">Prize Carousel</a> and was tempted, but ultimately didn't take it because he suspected it was empty.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Prize Carousel</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Craftsman 3000 Series Rolling Tool Chest on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Craftsman_3000_Series_Rolling_Tool_Chest</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crashed trains</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Secondly, if we scooped up everything on the path, the crawlers waiting at the end of the line would be tossed face-first into a pile of crashed trains and possibly thousands upon thousands of ghouls and mobs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her smash-hit book, having just passed a trillion copies sold, is entitled A Petite Chronicle of the Crawl: One Lady’s Journey into Enlightenment Through Knowledge and Scholarship and Three-Beat Poetry.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawl Con</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d been banned from Crawl Con years ago after threatening to “Fist fuck” an orcish show runner to death during a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a mostly-blank sheet of paper with a signature line at the bottom and a hand-written headline at the top in Syndicate Standard that read, “Admission of non-sanctioned violence by Crawler number 4,122.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler After Hours</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Our team is fielding a wave of interview requests, but Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette has the contract on your next interview.” “That’s fine,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Allister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 64 &lt;Note added by Crawler Allister, 13th Edition&gt; The Semeru Dwarves of the ninth floor all only worship a single goddess, who inevitably makes an appearance near the end of the battles as the final teams.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Azin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 26 &lt;Note added by Crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Biscuit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crawler Biscuits</b> are food items that may be received in Bronze and Silver Adventurer Boxes. Shortly after entering the First Floor, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives (100) Crawler Biscuits in the Silver <a href="bword://Adventurer Box">Adventurer Box</a> awarded for the <a href="bword://Early Adopter Achievement">Early Adopter Achievement</a>. He receives more biscuits over the course of the First and Second Floors.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Adventurer Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crawler Biscuit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crawler_Biscuit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 64 &lt;Note added by Crawler Carl, 25 th edition&gt; From page five of our planning notes: According to Edgar’s map, the control center for the four ballrooms—the two main ballrooms and the two attendant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Coolie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d found the information stuffed in the middle of the miscellaneous chapter: &lt;Note added by Crawler Coolie, 19 th Edition&gt; There are three types of deity.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your squad consists of: Crawler Donut (Squad Leader) Crawler Carl Six spots.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Drakea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 42 ] &lt;Note added by Crawler Drakea, 22 nd Edition.&gt; The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Everly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: &lt;Note added by Crawler Everly, Fifth Edition&gt; The moment I hit floor eight, they gave me a great box, as if to make up for the previous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Forkith</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: &lt;Note added by Crawler Forkith, 20 th Edition&gt; They may be invulnerable, but they still feel.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Herot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 21 &lt;Note added by Crawler Herot, 16 th Edition&gt; I do wonder, sometimes, how my worn path method will work on the deeper floors with the longer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Ikicha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 52 &lt;Note added by Crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler in the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was quite possibly the most powerful crawler in the dungeon already, and I’d worried that while it would make her stronger, it’d also drive her to do something.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Katia Grim</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Crawler Katia Grim is described as only here because she’s the one who had it in her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Killer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Crawler Killer is described as one I wanted to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Li Na</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Crawler Li Na now has 300.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Louis Santiago</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ll give it to Warlord Carl for detonating the bomb that was dropped by a wraith-possessed Crawler Louis Santiago 2, who was under control of Warlord Juice Box of Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Milk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Shows that aren’t as good as Gossip Girl .” * * * &lt;Note added by Crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Number</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Crawler Number 1,982,122 Asi Hamami Level 59 Fox.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>crawler or NPC</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s called a Makeup Table, and at level three, I can superimpose the face of any monster or crawler or NPC I’ve met on the glass, which makes sculpting much.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Osvaldo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Memorial Crystal: Apito has fallen from Crawler Osvaldo’s hands and is now in possession of Crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Porthus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 55 &lt;Note added by Crawler Porthus, Second Edition.&gt; It’s important to note that possession via ghost and an evicted demon inhabiting a shell are two very, very different.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Memorial Crystal: Apito has fallen from Crawler Osvaldo’s hands and is now in possession of Crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Undead Minion of Crawler Princess Donut – Mold Lion – Level 5 The zombies were a third as powerful as their former selves, but they weren’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This item was made especially for Crawler Princess Donut by her sponsor, Long Haul Biological Waste Management.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Project</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because of the Crawler Project’s campaigning, all released crawlers were now automatically citizens of the Syndicate and given access to the inner system primary zone, which came with free healthcare and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler races</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re starting with four teams each and ending with just one, so the plan is to try to keep as many NPCs alive as possible for this sixth heat and get as many crawlers as we can off the playing board before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 31 &lt;Note added by Crawler Rosetta, 9 th Edition&gt; As fragile as they look, fairies and sprites can be difficult to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Royal Bodyguard Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Killed by Crawler Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut with an assist by Crawler Royal Bodyguard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Either of you drink that fucker down, and it will upgrade your Find Crawler skill to level 15.” He turned to look at me, smiling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t understand the math, but if a certain number of teams left us, most of the final heats—not including ones that already had more than one crawler team—would be one crawler team versus two NPC teams.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Tin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: &lt;Note added by Crawler Tin, 21 st Edition&gt; I have noticed something quite.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Tipid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: &lt;Note added by Crawler Tipid, 4 th Edition&gt; It was me or him, and I chose to save.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawler World</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Your betrayal will never be forgotten or forgiven.” Elle, at the very end, held a sign that read, “To all the viewers of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth : Fuck you.” She flipped it over, and it was a rather.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Crawlers and totems along with some of the strippers and several of Bautista’s animals swarmed over the crisscrossing metal beams of the upper levels, all moving lower as demons swarmed upward, many climbing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawlers Carl and Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Emergency Action item proposed by Warlord Empress D’Nadia of the Prism: Remove the Princess Posse from Faction Wars and/or delete Crawlers Carl and Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawlers in the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Crawlers in the dungeon is described as fighting harder than anyone she’d ever.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crawling Through the Ages</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your autograph session is already booked for tomorrow, so you’ll have to leave and come back for that one, but your “Crawling Through the Ages” panel is scheduled for about six hours from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crazy Cat Lady Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crazy Cat Lady</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers who enter the Dungeon with a feline companion. It is distinct and different from <a href="bword://Trailblazing Crazy Cat Lady Achievement">Trailblazing Crazy Cat Lady Achievement</a>, which is only awarded to the <i>first</i> crawler to enter the First Floor with a cat.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crazy Cat Lady Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crazy_Cat_Lady_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Credit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Credit for the kill has been ruled as either accidental or a fragging incident, which would be funny as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Creedence Clearwater Revival</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Creedence Clearwater Revival’s “Have You Ever Seen the Rain?” started to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Creeping Apocalypse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Creeping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crest</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crest</b> are a humanoid race with no eyebrows.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crest on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crest</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cretin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cretin</b> are a race of rock monsters typically employed by the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> to provide personal security to crawlers. It is similar to the <a href="bword://Igneous">Igneous</a> race, but a different kind.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cretin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cretin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crime spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The spell wasn’t nearly as large as when she cast her War Crime spell, which she wouldn’t be able to cast again until the twelfth floor, but it was still.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crixus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crixus</b> is a <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> NPC and sniper encountered on the Fifth Floor of the Dungeon. He briefly acts as an informational guide to the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> during their ascent in the Air Quadrant before abruptly turning hostile.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crixus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crixus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crocodilian</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Crocodilian</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. They look like humanoid crocodiles. <a href="bword://Florin">Florin</a> chooses this as his race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crocodilian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crocodilian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crocs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: So, in a momentary decision I would quickly come to regret, I squeezed my feet into a pair of my ex-girlfriend’s Crocs, pulled a heavy leather jacket on, and I rushed outside to grab the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crossbow bolt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You can’t unknow the universe any more than you can unbite the shopkeeper or you can turn back time and stop a crossbow bolt from shattering everything you’ve ever held.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crossbow bolts</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: He bought a metalworking table, which he said he’d explain the purpose of later, and he bought something called a Bolt-Thrower’s Workshop , which would eventually allow for the mass production of explosive and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crossbow skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, anyone hit with it will be anchored in place for (two seconds × your Crossbow skill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crowd Blast</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Crowd Blast is described as exactly what you needed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crowd Control</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crowd Control Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Crowd Control Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for dispatching multiple mobs in a single attack.</li><li><b>Source:</b> achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crowd Control Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crowd_Control_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: After she’d lost the Crown of the Sepsis Whore, Donut had gotten a replacement, a white Tiara of Mana Genita , which raised her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crown Prince</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s just see what it is.” Soon after Katia had been sponsored by the Princess Formidable, the youngest sister of Prince Maestro and Crown Prince Stalwart of the Skull Empire, we explained to her everything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crown Prince Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only time I’d ever seen Crown Prince Stalwart, the Maestro’s older brother, was during the short video where he’d—wrongly—declared that he’d assassinated me and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cruel Sepsis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few of them writhed and screeched with a Cruel Sepsis debuff hanging over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crupper of the Benevolent Champion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted crupper. The Crupper of the Benevolent Champion.</b> This is a unique item. This item is part of a matched set with the <a href="bword://Cloak of the Benevolent Champion">Cloak of the Benevolent Champion</a>. There are two more pieces to this set. Find the third, and the fourth will be awarded in a box. Considering what you did to get this one, it’s gotta be something really good.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crupper of the Benevolent Champion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crupper_of_the_Benevolent_Champion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Crybaby Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Crybaby Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Crybaby Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Crybaby_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cuba</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the snake iteration of the legend usually involves a giant boa that protects the freshwater areas of Cuba and is able to control all the locals to help protect the environment and local flora and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cuba Summoning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Origin: Cuba Summoning duration: 60.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cuban</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Is that even a real thing?” “I don’t know about that, but Juego de maní is a Cuban fighting style,” Paz.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cuck Aquaman Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Cuck Aquaman Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to all participants of the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest (except <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>) for getting their <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> <a href="bword://Quest Box">Quest Box</a> vetoed by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cuck Aquaman Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cuck_Aquaman_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current Active Deck</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * Current Active.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But the current crawlers, along with a few of our NPCs, like Doctor Bones and Splash Zone and Bomo and Sledge, were the first to get themselves to their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current Enchantments</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current Enchantments: Battering.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But, I was under the impression that it only takes over mobs and NPCs from the current.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have the ability to imbue Fear upon this module, but your current level of Trap Engineer (Level 1) only allows this action to be done at a Sapper’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current Mark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Here’s a cheat sheet: Current Marks killed: 0 Current Mark benefit: +1 Stat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Current Marks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Here’s a cheat sheet: Current Marks killed: 0 Current Mark benefit: +1 Stat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Currently level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: As we watched them lift up off the ground, I noted Prepotente was currently level 57, one above my level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cursed items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: At level 10, it was a one-to-one ratio, and it also allowed you to eat cursed items with no ill effects, which was a pretty big.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Curupira</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Curupira</b> is an exceptionally obscure race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor, famously selected by the top-ranking crawler <a href="bword://Osvaldo">Osvaldo</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Curupira on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Curupira</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Curved sword</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had no idea what either a Ghazi or Zebani was, but the tall guy had huge arms and a curved sword over his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Custard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sure enough, the moment I put the item in my inventory, it placed itself near the top of the list in terms of value, right behind that ridiculous Fireball or Custard lottery.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Customs Enforcement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Frank claimed he and his partner, a woman named Maggie, worked for “Customs Enforcement,” which was a nice way of saying he was a fed, and he worked for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Cut! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This is an achievement that <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> says she received for being a good actress when she tricked the Goblin Shamankas on the second floor. This achievement and having one trillion views are the two pre-requisites that allow Donut to select the Former Child Actor Class during class selection on the third floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Cut! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Cut%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>D'nadia</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>D'nadia</b> is a <a href="bword://Saccathian">Saccathian</a> and Princess of the <a href="bword://Prism Kingdom">Prism Kingdom</a>, a small Sac government in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space that carries outsized weight in certain trade circles. An outspoken fixture of the intergalactic talk show circuit and an enthusiastic fan of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, she became one of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>'s loudest critics after a botched assassination attempt on the crawlers killed her closest friend instead.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Princess D'nadia, Empress D'nadia</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Prism Kingdom</li><li><b>Race:</b> Saccathian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: D'nadia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/D%27nadia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Da Tutorial Guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The neon sign read “Da Tutorial Guild” with an arrow pointing down a thin, dark.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daddy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’re not my favorite person right now, Daddy,” Bigs said from her position atop Dong’s head, swinging her hatchet in my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daddy Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Daddy Carl, I am here to lend assistance,” came a new voice as I was suddenly blasted with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dagda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: At this point, all three doors were now open: the door from Shanty Town to the temple of the Dagda, the door from the temple into the entrance vestibule, and the door from the vestibule into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daily buffs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He had one brush in each hand, one on Donut and the second, screaming brush on me, both of which gave us our daily.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dallas Cowboys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The tiny gnome girl was no longer wearing her Dallas Cowboys jersey and now wore an oversized shirt from someone in the 105 th Scream.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damage Reflect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Adds 10% Damage Reflect to all equipped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damascus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Out loud, I said to Damascus, “If Astrid was your mother, how can that be that you’re not crawlers?” There was a distant scream, but I couldn’t tell from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damascus Steel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Damascus Steel</b> is a level 75 Ifrit NPC who works as the assistant lead dancer at the Penis Parade strip club within the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. He is a member of the <a href="bword://Guild of Suffering">Guild of Suffering</a> and is registered as a mercenary at the Meat Shields market.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Ifrit</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Damascus Steel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Damascus_Steel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damascus Steel and Anaconda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Damascus Steel and Anaconda is described as brothers, and they’d gone off to hunt and kill their father, who’d somehow managed to turn into the manager for the Desperado.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damien</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Damien</b> is a Location Manager for <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> and meant to operate behind the scenes, out of crawlers' sights. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> describes him as "my manager's manager."</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 33</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Damien on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Damien</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Princess Donut’s intelligence level would’ve resulted in the fog lasting for 120 seconds per scroll.” “Damn,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damn cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I wanted to meet the cat.” “You and the damn cat,” Maxim.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damn floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I just want to get out of here and finish this damn floor and get back to killing those who deserve it.” * * * My inventory was back on, and I’d just made the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damned</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: My original plan was to just spam drop weapons and armor pieces into a huge pile and have Donut reanimate them all with her Legionnaires of the Damned spell, but Mordecai said the weapons had to have been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Damnit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We tried to stop it, but apparently the AI says it’s out of its hands.” “What are you talking about?” Florin.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dance floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I see my message found you.” Imani turned and scanned the room, eyes resting on Donut, who was in the middle of the dance floor, bopping on the shoulders of The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dandy House</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dandy House.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Danforth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Danforth</b> is an bolt that can be fired from a crossbow.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Danforth on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Danforth</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Danger Dingo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Danger Dingoes</b> are a canine mob on the Second Floor. Danger Dingoes are roughly the size of mastiffs (which typically stand 2' 6"+ tall and weigh 120-230 lbs), with absurdly long and sharp fangs that make them look "almost prehistoric." The fur on their faces looks like heavy metal corpse paint.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Danger Dingo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Danger_Dingo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Danger Zone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the meantime, let’s go straight into the Danger Zone.” Nothing happened for a moment, and then suddenly a female owl creature stood at the end of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Danger Zone with Ripper Wonton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He was a type of creature called a quokka, and he was the host of a show called Danger Zone with Ripper Wonton.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daniel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I love you, Daniel.” “I love you, too.” They’d made love before the party, both certain that they were going to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daniel Bautista</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kiki’s one surviving client was Daniel Bautista, who instead of taking a deal had gone with the other crawlers into the cleaner bots in that insane gambit to get off the playing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dante</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dante</b> is a former crawler and the author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (3rd Edition)</i>. Dante was born a <a href="bword://Crocodilian">Crocodilian</a> and chooses to remain that race during Third Floor Class selection.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Birth Race: CrocodilianDungeon Race: Crocodilian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Dante</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dante on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dante</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dario</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it’s now Nico, Dario—who is that other Tigran currently glaring at you from behind the steering wheel of their Tigr APV—and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dark rugs and wooden slat walls added to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dark Hive</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Dark Hive</b> is a <a href="bword://Mantis">Mantis</a> group that regularly participates in the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds. A few seasons ago, <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> killed a fellow hunter in a fight on the Sixth Floor (which is not covered by Protections). The Dark Hive insisted that the murder was legal because it took place on the Hunting Grounds. Now, other hunters avoid them where possible out of fear, and the Dark Hive takes advantage to poach other teams' kills.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dark Hive on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dark_Hive</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dark Purpose</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Dark Purpose is described as level 4 when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dark room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “But I will be grabbing my cat, and we will be leaving now.” Her hand remained on my knee in the dark.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Darkness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Darkness had descended, and none of the pub’s torches were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Darla</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hey,” Samantha said, looking down at the menu clutched in Louis’s hands, “there aren’t any children on here at all.” “They’re on the other side, honey,” Darla-Dean.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Darla No-Dentures</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Darla No-Dentures</b> is a hairy <a href="bword://Bugbear">Bugbear</a> and an NPC in <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>'s Dungeon. She wears a blonde wig and works as a stripper at the Bitches in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bugbear</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 33</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Darla No-Dentures on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Darla_No-Dentures</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Darling</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Darling!” Samantha cried, her voice uncharacteristically high-pitched and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daughter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: If Donut and I both happen to survive past tonight, and I make it to the sixth floor, I will sign an exclusive agreement to only complete elite-themed quests on the sixth floor that are directly associated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daughter’s Kiss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The power of the Daughter’s Kiss special attack was much more potent if the captured souls in my back patch were all of the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Daughter’s Kiss skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I activated the Daughter’s Kiss skill, and I jumped off the second story, landing right atop the fleeing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Day spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout Time Extend Greased Lightning Heal Totem Temporary Shield Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Days</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Mad Dune Mage Time to Level Collapse: 10 Days and 4 Hours Views: 17.72 Quintillion Followers: 13 Quadrillion Favorites: 4.1 Quadrillion “I did all that sewing for nothing?” Katia said as we stepped out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>De-Sleeving Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>De-Sleeving Box</b> is a loot box awarded for achievements related to removing god sponsors from the gods they are piloting. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> got an unnamed achievement for ejecting <a href="bword://Circe Took">Circe Took</a> from <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a> using the <a href="bword://Laundry Day Spell">Laundry Day Spell</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Removing outworlder sponsors from the god they are piloting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: De-Sleeving Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/De-Sleeving_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dead</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Dead is described as dead, and anything presented to you in any other matter is nothing but a cruel, sadistic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dead boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I picked up the neighborhood field guide map from the dead boss, which showed which parts of the river were currently occupied by naiads and which parts would be safe to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dead crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Several of the crawlers started murmuring, reacting to the sight of the queen, thinking they’d resurrected a dead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dead crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once I was relatively certain the watchers were gone, we approached the three dead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deal on the tenth floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have a map, and I hope you don’t steal it from me.” Class: Cartographer Race: Xenopus Birth Race: Vesper Top Level: 73 Dungeon Exit: took a deal on the tenth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dean</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hey,” Samantha said, looking down at the menu clutched in Louis’s hands, “there aren’t any children on here at all.” “They’re on the other side, honey,” Darla-Dean.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were supposed to show flashing words like “Death” and “Blood,” but I knew they hadn’t been able to get it to work because they were just showing the same thing everybody else was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death Challenge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Speaking of the Death Challenge, Carl, are you aware of the significance of what happened today?” “Well, I got stepped on by a god and got a close-up view of a building-sized monster get split in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death Manatees</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re probably going to find this hard to believe, but not only are these things sort of real, but that name they have—Screeching Death Manatees—is the actual name of record for this particular.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ring’s Marked For Death skill leveled up between the second and third kill, giving me a total of seven stat points in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death Watch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: This Death Watch segment probably cost a lot of money, and that was with regular, low-view.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death Watch Extreme</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s the host of Death Watch Extreme , it’s Prince Maestro of the Skull.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Death Watch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You are likely unaware of this, but Death Watch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem announced earlier that it was going into hiatus while the show restructured.” “I’m shocked,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deathbed Hellcat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: With her tiara’s advanced targeting skills and Donut’s elevated position, along with her Deathbed Hellcat class, she could hit anything she could see with any of her long-range.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Debbie Downer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I don’t think that’s going to work, Donut.” “Don’t be such a Debbie Downer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Debris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Debris littered the ground, sparks flying as the metal came in contact with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deceased crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Vadim : The pain amplifier… Warning: This message is from a deceased.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>December</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: When the timer hits 12:01 AM on December 24 th , regardless of where you are or what you’re doing, you will be teleported to a room similar to the one you started in on this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Decision</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It turned out, the five float themes were Resolution, Decision, Betrayal, Destruction, and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deck</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Deck-to-deck combat comes with a few additional rules that I’m sure you’ll figure out as you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deep</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Deep breath.” “How can I take a deep breath when I’m drowning, Carl?” “The water scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deeper floors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even when the idiot showrunners built some quest involving demons, and said demons would inevitably overpower the clerics or whomever else was involved, trash the temple, and break free into the club, it only.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deeper into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t want to move deeper into the room in fear of touching one of the two.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Def Leppard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So when Bautista’s crew only saw one cart—this one playing “Rock of Ages” by Def Leppard—it was actually the cart they needed to jump in front of, but they had no way of knowing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Default Winner Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Default Winner Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for winning Faction Wars (game).</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Default Winner Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Default_Winner_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Defense system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But you may also use the coupons for your base here, and that may be a better choice.” Britney was currently learning all she could about the Larracos city defense system from Juice.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Defensive towers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Shanty Town menu allowed us to build several types of automatic defensive towers, but we could only build a handful of manned towers, actually called synergy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deity's Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Deity's Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for the <a href="bword://Indomitable Achievement">Indomitable Achievement</a> for being physically attacked by a god and surviving. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this box after he was stepped on by <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a> on the Fourth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Deity's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Deity%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dekoki</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dekoki</b> is a Level 84 Kappa Seductress and singer, one of four members of Team Yokai racing during the Tenth Floor's heats. Dekoki is a Kappa, a turtle-shelled, water-bowl-headed creature normally vulnerable to having that water spilled, but she keeps hers sealed under a plastic wrap-like covering so she can perform without risk. She's an enthusiastic karaoke singer at a bar frequented by racers, performing pop covers like "Tell It to My Heart" by Taylor Dayne alongside her Team Yokai bandmates, including the <a href="bword://Minister of Blood-Letting">Minister of Blood-Letting</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dekoki on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dekoki</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Delivery system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Delivery system is described as too big to tape to your head.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Democratic Sortion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We also find that the leader of the Democratic Sortion has admitted guilt to illegal information gathering, and we are ready to render.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demolition Workshop Tab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Demolition Workshop Tab</b> is a tab in a Crawler's Heads-Up Display (HUD). The tab is specifically related to explosives and explosives handling.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Demolition Workshop Tab on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Demolition_Workshop_Tab</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demolitions Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to the Demolitions Workshop menu, the device was stable against anything except heavy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Demon eviction was ongoing, but it was mostly affecting the Dream soldiers, despite their overwhelming.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demon Eviction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bonus Warning: I could spend some time explaining how dangerous it is to have a boss-level zombie walking around during a Demon Eviction event, but let’s just see how this plays.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demon eviction event</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And the previous season, when she was late with two of her guards missing, there’d been an ongoing demon eviction event, but it was relegated to the area around the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demon lord</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The level 250 demon lord now inhabits the body of a lesser deity, a once-worshiped sea serpent who went by the name of the Madre de Aguas.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demons</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>In the Dungeon, <b>Demons</b> are a broad category of mobs and races closely tied to the lore of the "volcano levels." Demons generally inhabit Sheol on the Dungeon's Fifteenth Floor, where a matriarchal hierarchy of demons fight each other for control and the attention of one of the four brothers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Demons on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Demons</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Demons Hate This One Trick Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Demons Hate This One Trick Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor for teleporting <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> using his <a href="bword://Xistera">Xistera</a>, accidentally freeing <a href="bword://Demons">Demons</a> from their shell.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Demons Hate This One Trick Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Demons_Hate_This_One_Trick_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Deng Bao</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Judge Deng Bao</b> is an old, experienced grixist adjutant who traditionally serves the warlord of the previous season's winning team, and who sits on one of the highest courts in the galaxy outside the dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Deng Bao on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Deng_Bao</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Denial of Service</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The vine was on fire and covered with goo, having been hit by a Denial of Service.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Denise</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Denise</b> is a feral goose. Magic items do not work in her presence and she cannot be harmed by magical or physical damage, unless it is from items that originated from within the house.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> feral goose.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Denise on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Denise</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desecrated Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Desecrated Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he falls into the Temple of <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a> on the Sixth Floor. <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a> previously killed everyone inside and caused the temple to become Desecrated. Because he landed on the altar, he also receives the <a href="bword://Temple Defiler Achievement">Temple Defiler Achievement</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Desecrated Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Desecrated_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Designs cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Club</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Desperado Club</b> is a massive, multi-level nightclub and entertainment complex found throughout the urban floors of the Dungeon. It is one of two Dungeon-based clubs that crawlers can gain membership to; the types of people with access to the Desperado Club and the more exclusive <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a> are mutually exclusive, and obtaining a Desperado Pass automatically negates a crawler's ability to earn a Vanquisher pass. A members-only hub for crawlers and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> alike, it offers dancing, gambling, drinking, shopping, and access to a variety of rogue-themed guilds.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Desperado Club on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Desperado_Club</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Pass Tattoo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Desperado Pass</b> is a tattoo that grants crawlers access to the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, and may be received in an <a href="bword://Asshole&#x27;s Box">Asshole's Box</a> and/or after a crawler earns their first Player-Killer Skull. The tattoo burns as it is applied to the crawler's neck (or, if a cat, above the right shoulder blade), and looks like a dagger dripping blood.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Asshole's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Desperado Pass Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Desperado_Pass_Tattoo</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Club</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Desperado Club</b> is a massive, multi-level nightclub and entertainment complex found throughout the urban floors of the Dungeon. It is one of two Dungeon-based clubs that crawlers can gain membership to; the types of people with access to the Desperado Club and the more exclusive <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a> are mutually exclusive, and obtaining a Desperado Pass automatically negates a crawler's ability to earn a Vanquisher pass. A members-only hub for crawlers and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> alike, it offers dancing, gambling, drinking, shopping, and access to a variety of rogue-themed guilds.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Desperado Club on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Desperado_Club</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Club and Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was no indication anyone ever seeing the naga to have ever utilized the personal saferoom system.” “Wait, blood bars don’t drain in the Desperado Club and Club Vanquisher?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Club and Club Vanquisher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was no indication anyone ever seeing the naga to have ever utilized the personal saferoom system.” “Wait, blood bars don’t drain in the Desperado Club and Club Vanquisher?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Clubs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hey, do you know if there are any Desperado Clubs near here?” The man leaned back and rubbed his grizzled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Pass</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: With Katia’s acquisition of the Desperado Pass, we decided to add a third regular bodyguard to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Desperado Pass Tattoo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Desperado Pass</b> is a tattoo that grants crawlers access to the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, and may be received in an <a href="bword://Asshole&#x27;s Box">Asshole's Box</a> and/or after a crawler earns their first Player-Killer Skull. The tattoo burns as it is applied to the crawler's neck (or, if a cat, above the right shoulder blade), and looks like a dagger dripping blood.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Asshole's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Desperado Pass Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Desperado_Pass_Tattoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Despite</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Despite being discontinued, the drug and its effects persisted in the dungeon, used for literally dozens of quests over the seasons, or as a sort of trap, which is exactly what it had been for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Destruction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Destruction team was another group of crawlers from China I didn’t know very well mixed with that Hawaiian guy with the really long name who went by Makana and his partner, the hexcrafter paladin crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Details on the next race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re doing our best.” Anyway, the GPS unit beeped, we have limited details on the next.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Determine Value</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was once again a Determine Value skill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Detroit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Detroit, Imani reluctantly offered, was where she grew up, and she would never have picked it if she’d been offered another.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dexterity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So after I was done, I ended up with: Strength: 41 +3 (When Gauntlet Formed) Intelligence: 15 Constitution: 34 Dexterity: 23 Charisma: 25 “A strength over 40 is good for the fourth floor,” Mordecai said when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dick</b> is <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s former boss in the coast guard, before that was an EDO for the navy. He became afraid of water after a scuba diving incident.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dick on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dick</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dictum Waystation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bidding today is sponsored by Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited, because of course it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dictum Waystation Controls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bidding today is sponsored by Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited, because of course it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dictum Waystation Controls, Ltd</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> Corporation that regularly sponsors bidding during Special Events and occasionally sponsors crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dictum_Waystation_Controls%2C_Limited</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Did You Really Just Cast Fireball in a Room this Small Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Did-You-Really-Just-Cast-Fireball-in-a-Room-This-Small Box</b> is a loot box awarded under very specific circumstances. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives this box after she casts Fireball at <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Did You Really Just Cast Fireball in a Room this Small Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Did_You_Really_Just_Cast_Fireball_in_a_Room_this_Small_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Didn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Didn’t you just crash a drop bear into a god’s face?” “That was hours ago.” “You remind me of my brother,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Didn’t attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They generally didn’t attack groups, especially a well-lit one, but we still had to be on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DIDN’T KNOW</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut : IN MY DEFENSE, I DIDN’T KNOW THE DINK WAS GOING TO BE SUCKED INTO THE SPELL.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Didn’t show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It didn’t show the actual destruction, but we were given a shot of a half-flooded Larracos with the water churning as a gaggle of shark mobs fought each other over the corpses of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Die</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Die!” I barely had time to parse that Donut was also drunk as a screaming tentacle smashed against the table, rocketing us both against the back.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Die in the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: When you die in the dungeon, they harvest these elements themselves, starving out the existing planetary AI that doesn’t even know how to feed itself until we teach.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dinner Bell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Dinner Bell is described as similar, but it worked in a wide range over multiple enemies at once, and you could designate a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dinniman Kaiju</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also by Matt Dinniman Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!) Trailer Park Fairy Tales (Short.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dinniman OceanofPDF</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: com/ lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map by Tina Dinniman.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dinniman Patreon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you’re feeling especially saucy, you can join the Matt Dinniman Patreon and read advance chapters and whole books before they come out on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dinosaur quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If we have time, we’ll try to figure out the dinosaur quest.” Kiwi cried out one more time and then melted back into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Directly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Directly ahead, the sail—the conning tower-like structure on top of most submarines—had been violently ripped away, giving me an unobstructed view of the bottom of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirigible Gnome</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dirigible Gnome</b> are a race of <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> and combatants on the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dirigible Gnome on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dirigible_Gnome</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirigible Gnomes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: In order to escape a busy, teeming world filled with competing intelligent species, all of whom loved to wage war, the Dirigible Gnomes learned how to take to the clouds, building a variety of airships and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirty Little Phased Bolt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dirty Little Phased Bolts</b> are magical crossbow bolts that reduce the target's health to just shy of 0%, as well induce amnesia and health-sapping Bleed effects. The debuffs do not show over the target's head and cannot be seen in the Party Menu. The crawler who fired the bolts will not receive experience if the target dies, <i>but</i> will not receive a Player Killer Skull if the target was another crawler.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dirty Little Phased Bolt on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dirty_Little_Phased_Bolt</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirty Shirley</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dirty Shirley</b> is a drink made of Vodka, Grenedine, and lemon lime soda. It is frequently topped with cherries. In most <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> visits, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> drinks a non-alcoholic Dirty Shirley out of a bowl. Donut enjoys Dirty Shirleys because of <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a>'s liking for them.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Desperado Club</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dirty Shirley on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dirty_Shirley</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirty Shirleys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’re going to wait for Mordecai to make your drinks.” “Tell him to make us Dirty Shirleys,” Louis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dirty Tea Bag</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was in addition to the newly added Dirty Tea Bag along with some really unfortunate ones, such as “All your limbs turn to spaghetti for five minutes.” My personal favorite, “Vomit blood for ten minutes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Disarming Personality Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Disarming Personality Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for ripping off <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>'s arm at the end of the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Disarming Personality Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Disarming_Personality_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Disconnect Containment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pressed the Disconnect Containment.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Disgusting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pushed it aside for the moment to give room for the last box to open, my Platinum That-Was-Disgusting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dismember</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>On the Fourth Floor, <b>Dismember</b> is the <a href="bword://War Mage">War Mage</a> aboard the <i>Dismembered Unlimited</i>. Dismember appears to be an elf with long, silver hair. He specializes in necromancy magic.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon: Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dismember on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dismember</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dismemberment Limited</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <i><b>Dismemberment Limited</b></i> is a named train on the Fourth Floor Iron Tangle. The <i>Dismemberment Limited</i> is a sleek, white maglev train that moves almost silently on a monorail line. The first car is aerodynamic and sleek with a fully glass nose (similar to a B-17 chin turret), and pulls only two cars behind it. The train can only be entered and exited from the first car.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fourth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dismemberment Limited on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dismemberment_Limited</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dispenser</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>The Dispenser'</i> was a Neighborhood Boss located outside the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement on the Third Floor. The Dispenser looks like a giant manta ray that can blend into the background buildings. It is large enough to cover almost the entire building.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dispenser on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dispenser</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Disrupter Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Disrupter</b> is a powerful, rare, short range spell. Similar to <i><a href="bword://Magic Missile">Magic Missile</a></i>, <i>Disrupter</i> takes the form of an energy bolt and is ideally suited for "blowing holes in things." It inflicts splash damage and has a stun effect.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Disrupter Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Disrupter_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Distance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Distance grows with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dive bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The inside was some standard dive bar filled with flags and framed jerseys and signs for the Ottawa Senators NHL hockey.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divider</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>The Divider</i> was a Neighborhood Boss located outside the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement on the Third Floor. It was killed by <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a>. The Divider looks like a two headed sea monster, with a body of a whale and two long heads like the Loch Ness monster. When <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> come across the corpse, it has multiple holes in the the size of manhole covers. The decaying corpse is bloated.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Divider on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Divider</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divine Epiphany Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Divine Epiphany Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> (and presumably all crawlers who fought <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a>). Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Fifth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Divine Epiphany Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Divine_Epiphany_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divine Intervention</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Divine Intervention buff that came with the celestial grenade had protected me, and I hadn’t even realized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divine item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: As a divine item, These knee pads will imbue additional, varying benefits when they are in the presence of divine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divine items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: While this hasn’t happened before as far as I’m aware, I do know there are matched sets of armor that are supposedly all divine items, and this was prepared far ahead of time.” “Very interesting,” Kevin.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divine Suffering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: You attempting to hold onto a Divine Suffering artifact when the entire universe knows you have it is just another level of idiocy we don’t need.” I started a retort, but he held up his hand, interrupting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Divorce Court</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: McGibbons, the one who earlier wanted to watch Divorce Court , looked down at me as they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Diwata</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Diwata</b> is a minor, shapeshifting nature goddess of the Dungeon's pantheon, worshipped by some Dryads. She became hostile to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after he destroyed her shrine on the Sixth Floor, and was later sponsored into the Dungeon by <a href="bword://Circe Took">Circe Took</a>, mother of the Hunter <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 35</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Diwata on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Diwata</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dmitri</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have watched this program and determined that you were so enamored with them, that you deliberately chose a race that would allow you to emulate your favorite television characters.” “I don’t know what.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dmitri and Maxim</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The two-headed creature that comprised Dmitri and Maxim, a Nodling, apparently, spun their magical meteor hammer, and they would’ve brained the guy, but they suddenly all flew off their feet as a giant foot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dmitri and Maxim Popov</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The thing barreled right into Dmitri and Maxim Popov, the mount’s antlers ripping right through the two-headed ogre like they were made of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dmitri Popov</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dmitri Popov</b> is a Crawler from Earth. Dmitri Popov, along with his fraternal twin brother Maxim, is a Crawler originating from Bulgaria. When selecting their race on the Third Floor, Dmitri chose the <a href="bword://Nodling">Nodling</a> race under the advice of his game guide, Hongrish. Hongrish advised him that the Nodling race would provide an "extra life" if he died, but deceitfully omitted the fact that choosing this race would force Dmitri and his brother to inhabit and share a single, two-headed body.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth (Bulgaria)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human (formerly), Nodling, Nodling Yearling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dmitri Popov on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dmitri_Popov</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Do Not Enter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The theater entrance was just a pair of swinging double doors with a sign that read “Do Not Enter” over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doctor Bones</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Doctor Bones</b> is a skeletal NPC who originally works as the announcer and DJ for the Penis Parade at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, and later operates as a mercenary allied with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and his forces.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Dr. Bones</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skin Skellie</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Doctor Bones on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Doctor_Bones</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doctor Hu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t have time to get into the details, but it was Doctor Hu who had suggested the idea of refurbishing one of the garbage freighters into a passenger.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dodge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once it was killed the first time, the resulting donkey head with the bat wings had a Dodge of 15 and was next to impossible to hit, which would keep Donut safe as long as it was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dodge skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The tiara had just gained another 10 to Constitution, a plus two enhancement to Donut’s Dodge skill—which should bring it to 15—and a 1% buff to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dodola</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dodola</b> is a god normally sponsored by <a href="bword://Huanxin Jinx">Huanxin Jinx</a>.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Prologue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dodola on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dodola</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doesn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Doesn’t your culture have a whole set of cautionary tales and movies about inviting mysterious strangers into your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doesn’t show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Second only to the infamous floor boss—which doesn’t show up until the tenth floor—this is the biggest, baddest bitch one can come across in Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DOESN’T WORK</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: HE’S TRYING TO CAST HIS LIGHTNING ATTACK, BUT IT DOESN’T WORK WELL IN THE.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t get a good view, but Donut hissed, “Dog.” We tore over the checkered starting line, passing by what appeared to be a giant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dog Soldier</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3,4,5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dog Soldier</b> is the race chosen by <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a> ("Ren") on the Third Floor, the only crawler known to have taken it. A Dog Soldier is a bipedal, humanoid German Shepherd, complete with furry dog breasts. Tserendolgor pairs the race with the Crisper Class and her signature enchanted flamethrower, "Velma," which doubles as part of her shirt. She wears a small heart locket with a dog paw print and a Vietnam-era helmet with holes cut for her ears.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dog Soldier on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dog_Soldier</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doggie Door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Doggie Door</b> is a Tier-3 Personal Space Upgrade that allows crawlers to fast-travel to any door they have previously used on the floor, or to the location of other party members.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Doggie Door on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Doggie_Door</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doll Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read, “Gasworks Screw Factory, Steel Mill, and Doll Workshop.” And under it, a smaller sign read, “There’s no cocaine in here.” Sparks flew from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dolores Doesn't Splat Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dolores Doesn't Splat</b> is a potion that can be used to add extra power to an Earthquake Skill or to survive falling a long distance. The potion only works if consumed while falling at a speed of 124.27mph (200km) or more.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dolores Doesn't Splat Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dolores_Doesn%27t_Splat_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dolores Doesn’t Splat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’ll keep you from dying via falling damage, but you’ll be at 5% health after you hit the ground.” “They’re still better than that Dolores Doesn’t Splat potion,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dolph</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “That entrance is not nearly as exciting as the others,” Donut muttered just as a laser shot out from the nose of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dominators</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ahead, the bugbears turned right, entering the trailhead for the safe, long path, and the Dominators went straight, heading for the same trail we were going to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dominion of Blades Series</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Done Yet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The plan was for them to drop a metric fuckton of corpses on the battlefield and have Donut cast her wide-range, tower-enhanced, You’re Not Done Yet , which would rip the skin off the corpses, creating.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dong</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew Louis, Imani, and Zhang—who was riding behind Bodi—were all on a track called the “Infected Gravity Well.” Florin was on a track called “There’s Something Wrong with the Zoo.” Behind us, Dong, Porky.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dong and Corcunda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Dong and Corcunda is described as dead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dong Quixote</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dong Quixote</b> is an elderly human NPC who originally worked as an exotic dancer at the Penis Parade strip club. He is eventually hired as a mercenary for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>, becoming a loyal and valiantly heroic member of their fighting force.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dong Quixote on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dong_Quixote</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donita Grace</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Donita Grace</b> is an Earth crawler <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> adds to his chat in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> on the Fourth Floor. During Carl and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s second visit to the Desperado Club on the Fourth Floor, Carl trades fist bumps with a couple crawlers to get into each other's chat. Carl starts explaining the subway system to her, and gets frustrated when Donita doesn't agree with the color system. He stops chatting with her after that.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 14, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Donita Grace on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Donita_Grace</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donkey Kong</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were multiple, magic-numbing effects to the interior stairwell once Donkey Kong was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donna</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donna continued talking to me while the treadmill resized itself, instructing me to lift my arms and turn around, but I could see through the plexiglass-like material that Donut was also arguing with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grand Champion, Breed Winner Regional, National Winner Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk</b> is the deuteragonist of <i>Dungeon Crawler <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a></i>. A tortoiseshell Persian cat, she is the party leader of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> and a fan favorite crawler on <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Persian Cat</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Donut on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Donut</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Ferdinand</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Donut and Ferdinand is described as going back and forth because Ferdinand wasn’t able to get back into the Desperado Club and was now being chased through the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Hekla</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: That would only work if the monster was truly dead, however, so Donut and Hekla would stand at the windows and would—carefully—shoot anything caught in the spikes or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And you said he was going to murder us.” “The d-d-day isn’t over yet.” I said my goodbyes to Donut and Katia, and I hit the Silk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Louis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Donut and Louis is described as constantly talking about Juice.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Lucia Mar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: After several scenes of crawlers getting themselves killed, the rest of the program was dedicated to the fight between Donut and Lucia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: While Donut and Mongo quickly dispatched the third lemur, I leaped up on the clown like I’d done before, crushing in his chest and pummeling him in his face, smashing it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to the guy Donut and Mordecai bought the rockets from, the projectiles could only hit planes that were 500 feet off the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Odette</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She looked back over her shoulder again and then moved to the seat next to Donut and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Samantha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Listen,” I said to the cleaner bot, trying my best to ignore Donut and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut and Zev</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: That was likely a result of Donut and Zev telling her she needed to be more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut Holes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Donut Holes</b> is an unofficial fan club for Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. Donut refuses to acknowledge them, instead preferring the <a href="bword://Princess Posse Fan Club">Princess Posse Fan Club</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Donut Holes on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Donut_Holes</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * “We have a few choices,” I said as Donut shot the re-reanimated spider ghoul off the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut the Queen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I wished there was a way to combine Katia’s larger map, Donut’s ability to see mobs at a greater distance, and my ability to hunt down traps all into a single.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Character Actor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s Character Actor benefit went up in power each time she descended a floor, but it still came with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Hole</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: My original plan was to blow the whole thing off the tracks, but instead we decided to use Donut’s Hole spell to get.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Hole spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: My original plan was to blow the whole thing off the tracks, but instead we decided to use Donut’s Hole spell to get.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Laundry Day</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout Time Extend Greased Lightning Heal Totem Temporary Shield Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Magic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s similar to Donut’s Magic Missile , but it is good for blowing holes in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Magic Missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s similar to Donut’s Magic Missile , but it is good for blowing holes in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Puddle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The town’s administrative buildings had no first or second floor, and we’d had to utilize Donut’s Puddle Jumper spell to teleport from the rooftop of a knife shop to the landing entrance of the town.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Puddle Jumper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The town’s administrative buildings had no first or second floor, and we’d had to utilize Donut’s Puddle Jumper spell to teleport from the rooftop of a knife shop to the landing entrance of the town.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Puddle Jumper spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The town’s administrative buildings had no first or second floor, and we’d had to utilize Donut’s Puddle Jumper spell to teleport from the rooftop of a knife shop to the landing entrance of the town.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Second Chance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s Second Chance spell was currently at level 11, which brought it up to level 12 with her Brain Trust.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s spell had included the lower area of the bird formation, and it was clear several of them were now fighting each.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Torch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The room was lit by Donut’s Torch spell, and it gave everything a ghastly hue, like sallow.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s Torch spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The room was lit by Donut’s Torch spell, and it gave everything a ghastly hue, like sallow.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s War</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I never saw it, but Donut’s War Crime spellbook also was like this.” Mordecai was over in Meadowlark’s saferoom working on something with Mistress Tiatha, otherwise I’d call him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s War Crime</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I never saw it, but Donut’s War Crime spellbook also was like this.” Mordecai was over in Meadowlark’s saferoom working on something with Mistress Tiatha, otherwise I’d call him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Donut’s War Crime spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And even though none of the gore from Donut’s War Crime spell had made it down here during the initial attack, it still rained red ash, giving everything a dark, ghoulish.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Don’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If we’re in a situation where a two percent chance to turn someone on our side is our only hope, we’ll be running, not waiting five minutes.” “Don’t be so pessimistic, Carl.” Our training cooldown hadn’t reset.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Don’t attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: These guys don’t attack, but they’ll still swarm at ya.” “But why are there so many of them?” “It looks like the system is spawning anywhere between one to 15 of them when a corpse is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DON’T BE MEAN</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut : PREPOTENTE DON’T BE MEAN TO.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Don’t judge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I would be lying if I said people don’t judge based on coloring, but one should never pretend to be something they’re.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DON’T KNOW</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: HAVING MYSELF AND CARL ON THE PROGRAM IS THE EQUIVALENT OF HAVING AUDREY HEPBURN AND, I DON’T KNOW, MACHO MAN RANDY SAVAGE SUDDENLY SHOW UP AS GUEST STARS ON A PUBLIC ACCESS TELEVISION.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Don’t Look Back in Anger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Do you know the song ‘Don’t Look Back in Anger’ by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DON’T THINK</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut: STUPID DWIGHT HAS TWO, AND I DON’T THINK HE USED.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>DON’T WANT</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Samantha : BOMO AND SLEDGE ARE BIG NERDS, AND I DON’T WANT TO BE SEEN TALKING TO.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doomsday Scenario</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing I had left were the big bombs, such as the Carl’s Doomsday Scenario bomb and the tactical nuke that was still attached to a robot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Door to the guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’ll get back as soon as I can,” I called, moving for the door to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Doppelgänger</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Doppelgänger</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. It is a race of shape shifters, similar to Changelings, but they can shape their body mass into any shape or race. They do not gain the properties of the new race, but can retain the properties of whatever their body mass is made of.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Doppelgänger on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Doppelg%C3%A4nger</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dorota</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dorota</b> is a sentient novelty car seat that becomes Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s personal chair in the racing truck on the Tenth Floor, and the constant rival of the truck's GPS unit, <a href="bword://Dr. Metcalf">Dr. Metcalf</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dorota on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dorota</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dot on my map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I saw the blue dot on my map, rocketing through the darkness high above us, banking back toward the puppy on roughly the same trajectory as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dot on the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the gold pet box, Donut received a black, magical collar for Mongo that placed his orange dot on the map wherever he.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dots on my map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was nobody in the small park, but I could see multiple dots on my map, a mix of white, red, orange, and a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dots on the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The camels on the streets all had white dots on the map, so the opacity of the smoke curtain wasn’t enough to obscure us when they got.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Double doors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They won’t stop, and if he gets hit, he’ll get squished like your cousin Baron Bear Claw or whatever his name was.” Donut harumphed as we moved to the heavy, double.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Double Gonorrhea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I jumped up, pulled the arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea and rammed it into Vrah’s neck just before the shield popped up, which ejected the zombie heads on the bug woman’s neck and shoulders and tossed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Double Tap</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I remembered it only gave a couple of minor stat boosts and a skill called Double.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Double-Billed Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Double-Billed Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for participating in the drama Vengeance of the Daughter during The Hunting Grounds while on Dungeon Crawler World as part of a deal he made on the third floor with the producers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Double-Billed Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Double-Billed_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Downed a healing potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I yanked myself to my feet and downed a healing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Downward Dog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I turned toward the Downward Dog and started.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Downward Spiral</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The dwarves call her the Downward.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dozens</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dozens of the skull-headed monsters screamed as they were flung away, but a moment later, I heard the thwap, thwap thwap of knives embedding themselves into the giant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dr. Metcalf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dr. Metcalf</b> is the GPS in the car that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> drive on the Tenth Floor.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dr. Metcalf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dr._Metcalf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dr. Ratchet's Automaton Build-It Kit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Doctor Ratchet's Automaton Build-It Kit</b> is a large, steampunk-y chest hissing with clockwork gears. It contains dozens of automaton parts. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives the Build-It Kit in a Legendary Hunter Champion/Scourge Box, which he receives for killing the most hunters on the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Hunter Champion Box / Scourge Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dr. Ratchet's Automaton Build-It Kit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dr._Ratchet%27s_Automaton_Build-It_Kit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dr. Ratchet's Guide to Building Automatons for Fun and Profit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A book on creating automatons, features a dwarf riding on the back of a mechanical horse on the cover. Autographed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dr. Ratchet's Guide to Building Automatons for Fun and Profit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dr._Ratchet%27s_Guide_to_Building_Automatons_for_Fun_and_Profit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Draconian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I will not have you suffering from gastrointestinal distress while we race for our lives.” “I did read it,” Jurgen said to Prepotente, handing a gold coin over to the Draconian vendor, who happily handed the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dragon Chain Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dragon Chain Tattoo</b> is a permanent tattoo on <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s right forearm that temporarily grants him <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a>'s level-13 Profane Iron skill. The Dragon Chain Tattoo was created using a mixture of toraline yam ink and Li Na's freely-given blood. Because the blood was given willingly, Li Na did not permanently lose her skill, but Carl gained the temporary ability to use it. The tattoo grants Carl access to Li Na's level-13 Profane Iron skill, which allows him to unleash multiple prehensile chains from his body once a day for about thirty seconds.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafted by Katia Grim</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dragon Chain Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dragon_Chain_Tattoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drakea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Drakea</b> was a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (22nd Edition)</i>. Drakea participated in the last <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a>-produced season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> over 250 cycles ago. Born a <a href="bword://Bune">Bune</a>, he kept his race on the Third Floor and chose a rogue/magic class similar to the Compensated Anarchist Class. He built elaborate traps using magical items and magical triggers, and chose a sixth floor subclass that allowed him to experiment with hidden traps.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bune</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3,</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Drakea on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Drakea</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drakea's Enchanted Kerchief of Disorder</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Named for the famous trap-loving crawler, <a href="bword://Drakea">Drakea</a>, the Kerchief is a black bandana with a typical white paisley pattern. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives the Enchanted Kerchief of Disorder in a Gold <a href="bword://Boss Box">Boss Box</a> after defeating <a href="bword://Lusca">Lusca</a> on the Fifth Floor. Because his <a href="bword://Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness">Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</a> uses his neck slot, he equips the bandana as a mask.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Boss Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Drakea's Enchanted Kerchief of Disorder on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Drakea%27s_Enchanted_Kerchief_of_Disorder</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drakea’s Enchanted Kerchief of Disorder</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your neck slot is taken by your cloak.” Drakea’s Enchanted Kerchief of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drank the potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I saw Odette’s story about you the other day.” “Her what ?” I drank the potion down, and I instantly felt a warm tingling rush over.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dreadnought</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Large humanoid creatures with red skin, they rarely did well in faction wars because their preferred method of fighting was to just hit everything in front of them.‏‎</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dreadnought on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dreadnought</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dreadnoughts</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Large humanoid creatures with red skin, they rarely did well in faction wars because their preferred method of fighting was to just hit everything in front of them.‏‎</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dreadnoughts on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dreadnoughts</p></div></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dreadnoughts</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Large humanoid creatures with red skin, they rarely did well in faction wars because their preferred method of fighting was to just hit everything in front of them.‏‎</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dreadnoughts on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dreadnoughts</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dreadnought</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Large humanoid creatures with red skin, they rarely did well in faction wars because their preferred method of fighting was to just hit everything in front of them.‏‎</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dreadnought on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dreadnought</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dream</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Dream</b> is a powerful, galaxy-spanning faction and mega-corporation primarily composed of elves. They are one of the dominant legacy teams that participate in the ninth-floor Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dream on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dream</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dream elf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Is there any way you can...” I paused as a harried-looking Dream elf appeared, stepping out into the vestibule from within the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dream elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He wore a simple, leather jerkin and was indistinguishable from the rest of the dream elves we’d slaughtered on the previous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dream Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But we are family, and that’s something they can never take away, no matter what happens.” Dream Katia clutched the baby to her chest, and the little girl clung back onto.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drek</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Drek</b> are mobs that appear on the Fourth Floor. They attack the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> at the start of the Fourth Floor. A knee-high, fat, wrinkly monster with sharp claws and teeth. They stand on two feet but can climb on all fours. They wear loincloths and cry like babies.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Drek on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Drek</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Drick</b> is a former crawler and member of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>, but now with a <a href="bword://Valtay Corporation">Valtay Corporation</a> with a <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> worm in his brain. He is old and scarred, but still heavily muscled. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets him on the panel Crawling Through the Ages at CrawlCon!</li><li><b>Race:</b> Iguanoid | Moon Elf | Gondii</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 33, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Drick on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Drick</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drink</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Drink this.” I anticipated where the line of gravity was, and I tried a desperate flip maneuver in an attempt not to be upside down when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drink the potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have an idea for a chain lightning potion that is going to be a thing of beauty.” “They don’t have to drink the potion?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drinking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drinking a swig (defined as 1.5 ounces) of this “potion” will immediately cause you to gain the Shit-Faced debuff, has a 50% chance to render you Blind for a period of 30 hours, a 5% chance to immediately kill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drinking this potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drinking this potion will give you a random level 10 to 15 Celestial-tier.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drive the train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s get you and your babies out of here.” Tizquick the dwarf, who’d helped drive the train down to the yard exchanged a look with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dromedarian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dromedarian</b> are a camel-like race on the Fifth Floor. The Dromedarians are a common, formerly nomadic race that is found throughout the drier parts of the universe. With the ability to store mass amounts of liquid in their bodies, it is said a Dromedarian can survive up to two months without taking a single sip of water. When these hunters and warriors are placed in a situation where they can no longer wander, they tend to become lethargic, sloth-minded creatures. But make no mistake, this is a mighty race of warriors who were once known for their abject brutality.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dromedarian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dromedarian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dromedarians</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It also appears she’s rather fond of her brothers and sisters, many of whom are currently outside, mixed in with the poor, oblivious Dromedarians, preparing to die in a desperate attempt to destroy all the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drop a shredder, wait a second, then do another boom.” We screeched around the corner, the trailer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drop Bear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It depicted me and Donut in the Drop Bear biplane as it was chased by Orthrus, the giant puppy at the end of the last.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drop bombs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ponderous Wasteland moved above us, and I feared they’d drop bombs on our unprotected.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Drop Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Drop Shield</b> is a disposable defensive item that projects a stationary protective barrier, available for purchase at the Prize Counter during the Butcher's Masquerade Ball on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Drop Shield on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Drop_Shield</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dropped to the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Despite his report that he’d given away all his loot, a metric ton of gold coins dropped to the floor right at my feet, splashing like I’d just hit the jackpot at an old-school slot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dude</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m not afraid to die!” “Dude, what does that even mean?” I called back through the rattling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Due</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Due to some unforeseen technical difficulties with our Hobgoblin Mechanic Interdiction teams, trains that are breaking down on the tracks are taking much longer than anticipated to be cleared and put back into.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dune Mage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwendolyn’s team was building siege engines to breach the walls on the land quadrant, but she was worried they didn’t have enough people to assault the sandcastle of the “Mad Dune Mage.” We hadn’t heard shit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>This article is about the item. For the book, see Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook (Book).</i> <b>The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</b> is a Unique, Heirloom book item that has passed in secret from one Crawler to the next since it was first generated into the system in the fifteenth season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>. Disguised as a humble recipe book, it is in truth a covert archive of survival knowledge, recipes, strategies, exploits, and warnings left by previous crawlers, and the founding artifact of the crawlers known as Anarchists.</li><li><b>Source:</b> The Prize Carousel</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Anarchist%27s_Cookbook_%28Item%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Anarchist’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drakea was the author of the 22 nd edition of the Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook , and he (or she, I wasn’t really sure) was also a crawler during the final naga-controlled season, which had suffered some sort.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drakea was the author of the 22 nd edition of the Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook , and he (or she, I wasn’t really sure) was also a crawler during the final naga-controlled season, which had suffered some sort.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Book of the Floor Club</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Dungeon Book of the Floor Club</b> is a Dungeon Benefit which delivers one new Tome to members on each floor. Crawlers may gain "membership" to the Dungeon Book of the Floor Club from Silver Boss Boxes.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> spellbook-of-the-floor club</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeon Book of the Floor Club on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Book_of_the_Floor_Club</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The final dungeon boss isn’t supposed to wake until someone reaches the 18 th floor, but we all know this season, anything is on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Codex</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <i><b>Dungeon Codex</b></i> appears to be a full, detailed inventory of the Dungeon's mobs (including spawn rates), bosses, items, and spells. Dungeon Administrators and Game Guides can access the codex using their Heads-Up Display (HUD), but <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> laws restrict how much information production company personnel can share with active crawlers.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Work for the Production Company</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeon Codex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Codex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Your betrayal will never be forgotten or forgiven.” Elle, at the very end, held a sign that read, “To all the viewers of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth : Fuck you.” She flipped it over, and it was a rather.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Crawler After Hours</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Our team is fielding a wave of interview requests, but Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette has the contract on your next interview.” “That’s fine,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Crawler Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: You managed to make it all the way through the first Dungeon Crawler Carl book without throwing it against the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Crawler World</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Your betrayal will never be forgotten or forgiven.” Elle, at the very end, held a sign that read, “To all the viewers of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth : Fuck you.” She flipped it over, and it was a rather.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Exit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have a map, and I hope you don’t steal it from me.” Class: Cartographer Race: Xenopus Birth Race: Vesper Top Level: 73 Dungeon Exit: took a deal on the tenth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Gold Rush</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dungeon Gold Rush</b> is a lottery ticket given to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on the Second Floor for the <a href="bword://Like a Moth to the Flame Achievement">Like a Moth to the Flame Achievement</a> in a Platinum <a href="bword://Lucky Bastard Box">Lucky Bastard Box</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Lucky Bastard Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeon Gold Rush on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Gold_Rush</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Mailbox</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>mailbox</b> is a recurring fixture found in saferooms and personal spaces throughout the dungeon, used by the system to deliver recurring and one-off item rewards directly to individual crawlers rather than through the normal loot box process.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeon Mailbox on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Mailbox</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeon Sidekicks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was assuming Katia had gotten that on the Dungeon Sidekicks show, so long.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeonpreneur Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dungeonpreneur</b> is an achievement granted the first time a crawler develops a weapon, device, or potion that can stack up to (999) in inventory.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dungeonpreneur Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeonpreneur_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dungeons and Dragons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If we’re really going to spend the next week rebuilding this town, then we simply must have a shower right away!” The creatures looked a little like the owlbear monsters from Dungeons and Dragons, but not.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dunno</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dunno how that translates to them camping out on inhospitable worlds, always getting themselves wiped out by the environment.” “Their pacifism is the one portion of their religion I respect,” Drick added.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Duration</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Duration: (5 minutes + 1 minute per level of spell) × [Your Intelligence / (Target’s Constitution + Charisma)] Ahh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dust cascaded down over me as I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dwarf</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dwarf</b> are a humanoid race. Several <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> in the Dungeon are dwarves. Dwarves also have a history of manufacturing items, particularly Automatons. Automatons are known for running a long time before needing a new battery, and are much more efficient than <a href="bword://Soul Crystals">Soul Crystals</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dwarf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dwarf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dwarven Battery Fabricator</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dwarven Battery Fabricator</b> is a crafting item that allows crawlers to produce and charge Dwarven Batteries. The batteries are about the size and shape of bricks. Automatons are known for running a long time before needing a new battery, and are much more efficient than <a href="bword://Soul Crystals">Soul Crystals</a>. The fabricator is about the size of a microwave.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Limp Richard's General Store</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dwarven Battery Fabricator on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dwarven_Battery_Fabricator</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dwarven Industrial Light-Duty Automaton Contraption</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dwarven Industrial Light-Duty Automaton Contraption</b> is an automaton on the Iron Tangle. They are used to help the monsters get from the Exit-only cavern back to the first stop.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dwarven Industrial Light-Duty Automaton Contraption on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dwarven_Industrial_Light-Duty_Automaton_Contraption</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dwight</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dwight. Sparkling Unicorn. Level 87 Enchanter.</b> One of two members of Team Sparkles. “I bet you already know what a unicorn is. A sparkling unicorn is pretty much the same thing, but they sparkle because they have even more magic permeating them. Their horns are quite valuable to certain parties, so if you kill him, it’s recommended to grab the horn as quickly as possible. Most unicorns are regarded for their beauty and their grace and their childlike innocence. They’re generally known for their overly gentle manner. But not Dwight. Dwight is known for being a complete prick.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Sparkling Unicorn</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Dwight on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Dwight</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Dwight and Lucienne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d offered to go after the boss on our behalf, but we’d decided that he should instead pursue Dwight and Lucienne and attempt to slow them, as they most likely wouldn’t be taking the same path we’d.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Dynamite</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Dynamite</b> is an extremely volatile explosive created by <a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a>. The red stick is wet and sticky, and the long wick hisses and pops like a sparkler when lit. The wick must be physically lit using a lighter or mechanism.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblins, Goblin Box, Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Dynamite on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Dynamite</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Dynamite</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin Dynamite</b> is an explosive Item that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> occasionally recieves in loot boxes. It is very similar to <a href="bword://Goblin Dynamite">Goblin Dynamite</a>, but has a larger yield and is more stable.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hobgoblins, Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Dynamite on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Dynamite</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earlier</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Earlier this morning, as we’d stood outside of the Narwhal staring at the dead corpse with the words, “No you won’t” carved into her body, which had been in response to me saying we’d hunt down and kill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Early Adopter Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Early Adopter</b> is an achievement awarded to each of the first 5,000 crawlers to enter the Dungeon. The third achievement <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives upon entering the Dungeon, which was likely distributed to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> as well. Carl and Donut (crawlers #4,122 and 4,119, respectively) enter the First Floor 12 minutes after the surface-level stairwells appear.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Early Adopter Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Early_Adopter_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Early Bird Gets to Squirm Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Early Bird Gets to Squirm!</b> achievement is given to Crawlers who descend the stairs when there are more than six hours remaining on the level timer.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Early Bird Gets to Squirm Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Early_Bird_Gets_to_Squirm_Achievement</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Earth Box</b> is a Loot Box given to Crawlers who choose an Earth Class on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Earth Class</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Earth Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Earth_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Earth Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fifth Floor in a Platinum <a href="bword://Spicy Box">Spicy Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://Soft Vore Achievement">Soft Vore Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Spicy Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Earth Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Earth_Upgrade_Patch</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Beautiful</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is more like that Earth Beautiful program you went on a few weeks back, but this one is exploring the tale of Earth through storytelling and vignettes about the lives of some of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Earth Box</b> is a Loot Box given to Crawlers who choose an Earth Class on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Earth Class</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Earth Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Earth_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Hobby</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: This knowledge was from that Earth Hobby.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Hobby Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Everyone who chose an Earth Class got a Silver <a href="bword://Earth Box">Earth Box</a> with three items including an Earth Hobby Potion. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> got the Scutelliphily Skill, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> got Cesta Punta Skill as his hobby and Katia became a Gearhead.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Subsection 1256-C of the Indigenous Species Protection Act</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Earth Hobby Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Earth_Hobby_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All off-boarding requests are currently suspended while the Earth system remains in quarantine per Syndicate rule 470.B through 495 in the System AI Stability.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earth Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Earth Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fifth Floor in a Platinum <a href="bword://Spicy Box">Spicy Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://Soft Vore Achievement">Soft Vore Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Spicy Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Earth Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Earth_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Earthquake</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He hit the ground and his Earthquake ability activated, causing the remaining swordfish guys to fall off their feet and start flopping like, well.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Easy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Easy, though apparently it would only half drain the necropolis because the water is still being pumped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: This Pet is Suffering from Not Just Rabies, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edgar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Edgar looks like a regular, giant tortoise, but he talks and comes with a diverse skillset, mainly used for reconnaissance and support. Level 15 Sense Movement</li><li><b>Race:</b> Tortoise</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 25</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Edgar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Edgar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edge Dancer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once you train it enough, most items will give you a warning if the description is too overstated.” “I take offense, demon,” Edge Dancer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edge of the building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was off the northeast edge of the building, perilously close to the massive demon, having blown a hole through the tornado of demon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edge of the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the north, near the edge of the map and overlooking a clear valley filled with lower-level monsters, placed there so the hunters can train while the crawlers make their way through the lower.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edge of the river</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d had to hike for another half an hour through the dark woods to get to the edge of the river, and Samantha had been grousing the entire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edge of town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then, Lucia, still in a rage due to the earlier events—events I didn’t yet know the full details of—would teleport the entire room to the edge of town, where we’d have it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edict</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Edict</b> is the last male <a href="bword://Mantis">Mantis</a> left in The Hunting Grounds, he attacks <a href="bword://Bomo">Bomo</a> and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> in the saferoom in order to be teleported to a group of mobs that he knows will kill him.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantis</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Edict on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Edict</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edifice Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: If your castle is bigger than this place, I won’t be able to cast my Edifice Shield.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edited by Cheryl Dyson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Edited by Cheryl Dyson http://www .cheryldyson.com Cover Illustration by Elartwyne Estole https://.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edition</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 71 &lt;Note added by Crawler Carl, 25 th Edition&gt; From page four of our planning notes: There’s a big difference between Donut enchanting someone, like she used to do to the Bopcas in the saferooms, and her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Edition of the Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Author of the Fourth Edition of the Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Effect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Effect: It’s a train-sized grenade that is going to detonate from extreme.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Effective</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Effective immediately and for the remainder of this floor, all saferoom access—and this includes personal space access—is operated under tenth floor and below.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Effex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d pick one or two, pay, and then Effex would place the photo on the table in front of us, where we’d sign.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Egyptian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her head was that of an Egyptian vulture, yellow with white feathers spreading around her, almost like an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eighteenth floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Apparently, the tourists on the eighteenth floor had the ability to move themselves to the sixteenth floor, where it was safe, but they first had to make their way through the seventeenth floor for some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eileithyia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Eileithyia</b> is a deity that is a god of Childbirth and Female Pain. During <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i> the goddess is sponsored by <a href="bword://Huanxin Jinx">Huanxin Jinx</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Huanxin Jinx (Sponsor)</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eileithyia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eileithyia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>El Capitolio Nacional de Cuba</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m sorry about your...” Warning: The door to the exit stairwell at El Capitolio Nacional de Cuba has sustained damage and will become unusable in five.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elbow tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of the snake heads reached down and curled around the web pattern on my elbow tattoo, the one that gave me access to the Guild of Suffering, which I hadn’t seen or heard anything about since I’d gotten.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eleventh floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So, despite everything, we decided that we were going to warn everyone about what might potentially happen on the eleventh floor, allowing everyone to decide for themselves whether or not they wanted to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elf</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Elves</b> are a long-lived, pointed-eared humanoid race, and one of the largest and most fractured families of peoples in the Dungeon and the wider <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>. They are strongly inclined toward magic and readily interfertile with other species, and several elven varieties appear among the races offered to Crawlers at selection.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Elf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Elf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>elf NPCs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She growled at the elf NPCs and bound back out through the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elf Summoner</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tsarina Signet - Half Naiad, Half High-Elf.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eliminated</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eliminated: The Democratic Sortion I stepped from the hospital tent to find the base in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elite</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was about to ask Miriam if they’d been involved with any Elite quests, but Donut got to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most were human, but there was a mushroom guy I’d only seen in passing who was afraid of Elle for some unknown reason and a badger-headed woman who was also a Swashbuckler, the same class as Bautista and Tran.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle and Imani</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once we’re inside, she’ll join Elle and Imani in the naga stronghold in case one of the three family members is left behind or somehow gets sent back.” Pony made a noise that sounded like a disapproving.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Even if Elle and Katia and all the others go down, they’ll just use the next fifty top.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle McGib</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your platoon member, General Elle McGib has cast a Subterfuge spell, and your presence is hidden from enemy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle McGibbons</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Elle McGibbons</b> is an elderly Crawler from Earth and one of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> Adult Care Community residents shepherded through the Dungeon by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, and the Meadow Lark staff on the first and second floors. A ninety-nine-year-old widow whose memories often circle back to her late husband, <a href="bword://Barry">Barry</a>, she travels with the survivors as part of Team Meadow Lark.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Mrs. McGibbons; Elle McGib</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Wenatchee, Washington, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Frost Maiden</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 31</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Elle McGibbons on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Elle_McGibbons</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle’s APV</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imani and Elle’s APV appeared, zooming from under a door, crossing the carpet alongside the man, who was now covered in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle’s Snow Cones</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle’s Snow Cones fan club did a raid on my server the other day, and they and the Posse were all fighting, but now they’re friends and working together.” “Okay, we’re ready,” Mordecai.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elle’s team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: If Osvaldo refused a deal, then Imani and Elle’s team and Donut and I both had locked-in-place heats where we were both against one other crawler team for the final.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elmer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Elmer</b> is an <a href="bword://Ursine">Ursine</a> NPC on the Sixth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Ursine</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Elmer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Elmer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elves, draconians, and fairies, along with a smattering of several other races moved back and forth into various buildings and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emberus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Emberus</b> is a Deity, specifically the God of Sun and Ash, known for his unmitigated cruelty and his intense grief over the murder of his son, <a href="bword://Geyrun">Geyrun</a>. He becomes <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s patron deity during the events of the Fifth Floor, setting off a chaotic relationship defined by powerful boons, demanding tithes, and constant threats of fiery retribution.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Celestial Ascendency</li><li><b>Race:</b> Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Emberus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Emberus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun</b> is the secret church of <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>, restricted to all but his most devout followers and only accessible through a portal inside <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>. <a href="bword://Pater Coal">Pater Coal</a> is the High Cleric Supreme.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Emberus_High_Temple_of_the_Ending_Sun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emberus ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “He was only one of two people to kill your son.” I reached over and pulled the Emberus ring off my finger, and I put it into my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emberus Tattoos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>When a crawler worships <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>, they gain a round tribal-style tattoo on the back of each hand. Both tattoos are black with an orange glow, feel warm and vaguely uncomfortable, and depict the sun. On the right hand, the sun's rays are curled; on the left hand, the sun's rays are little triangles.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Emberus</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Emberus Tattoos on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Emberus_Tattoos</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emerald</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Emerald</b> is a level-30 NPC on the Fifth Floor of the World Dungeon. She is a <a href="bword://Changeling">Changeling</a> who initially disguises herself as a female <a href="bword://Dromedarian">Dromedarian</a> camel.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The World Dungeon (NPC)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling (posing as a Dromedarian / Camel)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 7</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Emerald on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Emerald</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emergency Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Emergency Action item proposed by Warlord Empress D’Nadia of the Prism: Remove the Princess Posse from Faction Wars and/or delete Crawlers Carl and Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emergency Action Item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Any communication loophole designed to get around Team Retribution’s previously enacted Emergency Action Item get immediately closed because these assholes are still using it to communicate.” The Mexx robot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emergency action items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We still had two emergency action items left, but it seemed like such a waste to use one on something that should have already been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emergency Gremlin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most of the scrolls we received were more of the Emergency Gremlin ones, and there wasn’t anything else very.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emergency Repair</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He used a precious Emergency Repair scroll to speed up the repair of Party Planner and was about to start his second run as soon as they finished reloading the bomb.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emir</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Something is about to go down in here, and I don’t want anyone getting hurt.” “What’s going down?” Emir.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Emir Akbas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Emir Akbas</b> is an Earth Crawler that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. His class is <a href="bword://Ghazi">Ghazi</a> and his race is <a href="bword://Zebani">Zebani</a> though Carl has no idea what either of these are.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Zebani</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 32, Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Emir Akbas on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Emir_Akbas</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Employment Agency</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Empress</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was expecting more Naga infantry, but I see only one of their standards.” Empress D’Nadia’s territory was just south of the Nagas, in spot number five, so it made sense she’d commit most of her force between.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Empress D’Nadia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was expecting more Naga infantry, but I see only one of their standards.” Empress D’Nadia’s territory was just south of the Nagas, in spot number five, so it made sense she’d commit most of her force between.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Empty Pockets Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Empty Pockets</b> is the fourth achievement that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives upon entering the Dungeon. Presumably, any similarly unprepared crawlers would receive the same achievement after stepping into the First Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Empty Pockets Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Empty_Pockets_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Empty room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Planetwide Message: Be advised that the 16 th Floor, supposedly entitled “Agony of Mirrors” is actually a giant, empty room with a single mirror in it: a non-magical mirror plucked from an abandoned Motel 6.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted chaps!” “Carl, pick those!” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</b> is a sleeveless jacket that can be modified with Upgrade Patches. It does not protect against the cold.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Spicy Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Anarchist%27s_Battle_Rattle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Anarchist’s Battle Rattle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: My sleeveless jacket was called the Enchanted Anarchist’s Battle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Anklet of the Fallen Oak</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Anklet of the Fallen Oak</b> is a bracelet found by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on the Third Floor from the corpse of <a href="bword://Grace Bautista 3">Grace Bautista 3</a>. The anklet is made of wood with three little beads attached to it. The beads make a quiet noise when shook, similar to seed pods.</li><li><b>Source:</b> looted from Grace Bautista 3's corpse</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Anklet of the Fallen Oak on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Anklet_of_the_Fallen_Oak</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Auto Buckler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Agreed,” I said as I pulled the Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit off my left.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit</b> is an arm bracer that can turn into a shield with a flick of the wrist. <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> gets this item in one of her <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> at the start of the Fourth Floor. She gives the shield to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when she received the <a href="bword://Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Crowd Control Shield">Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Crowd Control Shield</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Auto_Buckler_of_the_Peach_Pit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted BigBoi Boxers</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted BigBoi Boxers</b> look like white boxers covered in little red hearts, and are a Dungeon item specifically designed for size-altering races, were-creatures, the BigBoi... and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bronze Boss Box for killing the Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted BigBoi Boxers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_BigBoi_Boxers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Collar Charm of the Effete Bourgeoisie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Collar Charm of the Effete Bourgeoisie</b> is a magic collar charm <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> recieve after becoming mayor of Point <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Recieve after becoming mayor of Point Mongo</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Collar Charm of the Effete Bourgeoisie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Collar_Charm_of_the_Effete_Bourgeoisie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Crown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to the Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore, which she didn’t even have anymore, Donut wouldn’t be allowed to exit the ninth floor until every other member of the Blood Sultanate royal family was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore</b> is a tiara glittering with dark smokey gems. At its center is a deep purple stone that appears to be swirling with clouds, as though made of liquid.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quadruped Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Crown_of_the_Sepsis_Whore</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Fae Scale Quadruped Crupper of the Fleet</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Fae Scale Quadruped Crupper of the Fleet</b> is a piece of silver, skirt-like armor that covers the rear half of a quadruped's body (but not their stomach), with a special slot for the tail, and hangs about halfway to the floor. It boosts the wearer's Dexterity, but jingles a little when the wearer moves and "poofs" out like a skirt when they fall.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Fae Scale Quadruped Crupper of the Fleet on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Fae_Scale_Quadruped_Crupper_of_the_Fleet</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</b> is a fleeting item that can be placed on the teeth of either a crawler or pet to increase their biting damage. They are gold in color and little tendrils of electricity spark when the teeth are snapped. They are currently worn by <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Quest Box from the Show Must Go On Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Fang_Caps_of_the_Expectorating_Tizheruk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo's Teeth Caps</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk">Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</a></b> is a fleeting item that can be placed on the teeth of either a crawler or pet to increase their biting damage. They are gold in color and little tendrils of electricity spark when the teeth are snapped. They are currently worn by <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Quest Box from the Show Must Go On Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mongo's Teeth Caps on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mongo%27s_Teeth_Caps</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Fur Brush</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the Scab box, she received an upgrade to her brush, the Enchanted Fur Brush of the Ecclesiastic , which added an additional ten percent to her constitution on top of the measly two points it normally gave.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Fur Brush of the Ecclesiastic</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Fur Brush of the Ecclesiastic</b> imparts a daily buff on furred creatures. If someone else uses this item to brush the target's fur for ten (10) minutes straight, the target gains +2 Constitution for the next 30-hour day.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bronze Boss Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Fur Brush of the Ecclesiastic on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Fur_Brush_of_the_Ecclesiastic</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Gnome Farseer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Gnome Farseer</b> is a telescope developed by the Dirigible Gnomes on the Fifth Floor. The large, heavy telescope appears to be a white tube made of a ceramic-like material, covered in pipes and gears, and has a clamp on the bottom to attach to a table or the gunwhale of a boat or airship. A "turny thingy" on the right side allows the user to zoom in and out, and the resulting image is magically rendered accurately and clearly, even in low light.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Skarn</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Gnome Farseer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Gnome_Farseer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Hairbrush of the Beefmaster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives the <b>Enchanted Hairbrush of the Beefmaster</b> in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Who Let the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> Out This Time Box for the <a href="bword://Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement">Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</a> on the Ninth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Who Let the Gods Out This Time Box; Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Hairbrush of the Beefmaster on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Hairbrush_of_the_Beefmaster</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Hammer of Fast Forward</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A magical hammer. It has the number seven etched into the head and handle. Borant filed a dangerous appeal to keep the gift from arriving, the appeal was immediately denied.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Hammer of Fast Forward on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Hammer_of_Fast_Forward</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Handcuffs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Handcuffs</b> is an item on the Fifth Floor The handcuffs are covered in fuzzy red velvet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Handcuffs on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Handcuffs</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Juvenile Macropus Dominus Skin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Juvenile Macropus Dominus Skin.</b> This item fills a costume slot. This can be worn as an outer garment. You might want to protect your face, though. I’d fuck me. I’d fuck me so hard, mate. Equipping this costume imbues the following effects: Protection from Seeping Acid. A level 10 Jump Skill. +5 to the Lotion Application Skill. Warning: This is a 12-hour timed item.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Prison Pocket</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Juvenile Macropus Dominus Skin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Juvenile_Macropus_Dominus_Skin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</b> is a box with a flower. If you're female and eat the flower, and survive almost dying that ensues, you get three boons from the goddess <a href="bword://Eileithyia">Eileithyia</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Lock Box of the Night Wyrm on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Lock_Box_of_the_Night_Wyrm</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Mongoliensis Saddle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Mongoliensis Saddle</b> is an pet item that <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives in a Platinum <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a> on the Fourth Floor. The saddle is an obscenely garish blue and red tassel-covered saddle. There are no straps or reins on the saddle, just a seat and a pommel. As soon as Donut hops onto the saddle, a translucent strap appears, wrapping around her waist and tying her to the saddle.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Fan Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Mongoliensis Saddle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Mongoliensis_Saddle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Necklace of Haute Bourgeoisie</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gains the <b>Enchanted Necklace of Haute Bourgeoisie</b> at the start of the Fourth Floor as a reward for conquering the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement on the Third.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Tyrant Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Necklace of Haute Bourgeoisie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Necklace_of_Haute_Bourgeoisie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Night Wyrm's Ring of Divine Suffering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Night Wyrm's Ring of Divine Suffering</b> is a magical ring that grants its wearer +5% to all stats and the Marked for Death skill. <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a> gave it to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary That's the Spirit Box awarded to Frank Q on the First Floor; Gifted to Carl by Frank Q in the Desperado Club</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Night Wyrm's Ring of Divine Suffering on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Night_Wyrm%27s_Ring_of_Divine_Suffering</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</b> buffs constitution and provides various resistances when equipped. It is a lightweight hooded cloak that appears to have been crafted from a demon's wing, with nightgaunt skin stretched across rows of body-length fingerbones. The hood, when worn up, has pointed ears on either side.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Apparel Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Nightgaunt_Cloak_of_Stoutness</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Nipple Ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler This may only be equipped on a crawler who has been Marked for Death by a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets the <b>Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler</b> as a reward for surviving <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a>'s Marked For Death Skill. A silver ring with an eyeball hanging from it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Apostate Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Nipple_Ring_of_the_Defiler</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Superior Fire Demon’s Hand Maiden</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> got the <b>Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Superior Fire Demon’s Hand Maiden</b> from a legendary beguilers box that she received for charming <a href="bword://Ferdinand">Ferdinand</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Beguilers Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Superior Fire Demon’s Hand Maiden on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Nipple_Ring_of_the_Superior_Fire_Demon%E2%80%99s_Hand_Maiden</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Obsidian Bracelet of the Raggle Rouser</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Obsidian Bracelet of the Raggle Rouser</b> is a bracelet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Obsidian Bracelet of the Raggle Rouser on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Obsidian_Bracelet_of_the_Raggle_Rouser</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Paupers Ring of the Steadfast Emberus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Pauper's Ring of the Steadfast <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a></b> is a magical item that, when equipped by a worshipper of Emberus, cancels the effects of most health-sapping and disease-based debuffs without removing the actual debuff.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Blessed Sun Enchantments Shop at Club Vanquisher</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Paupers Ring of the Steadfast Emberus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Paupers_Ring_of_the_Steadfast_Emberus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Pedicure Kit of the Sylph</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Pedicure Kit of the Sylph</b> is a black, zippered folder containing (12) metal pedicure tools. If the crawler makes a show of using some of the tools for ~15 minutes, multiple daily buffs will activate, and their feet will glitter.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Shoe Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Pedicure Kit of the Sylph on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Pedicure_Kit_of_the_Sylph</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit</b> allows someone to create a tattoo on a target, can potentially imbue magical buffs and upgrades if the artist is good enough.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Prison_Tattoo_Kit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Repeating Crossbow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers This is a unique.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers</b> is a magical crossbow owned and used by <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a>. The crossbow appears to be made of gold, but feels almost plastic. It has an inlay carving of a vulture.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Grrl Power Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Repeating_Crossbow_of_the_Scavenger_Mother_of_Mothers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Right Back Atcha Personal Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Right Back Atcha Personal Shield</b> is a shield worn on the wrist. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received this item as a reward in a legendary <a href="bword://Boss Box">Boss Box</a> after defeating Queen <a href="bword://Imogen">Imogen</a>. He uses this item instead of the <a href="bword://Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit">Enchanted Auto Buckler of the Peach Pit</a> given to him by <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Boss Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Right Back Atcha Personal Shield on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Right_Back_Atcha_Personal_Shield</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Roll of Never-ending Duct Tape</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Roll of Never-ending Duct Tape</b> is an item <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives in a silver <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a> from the end of the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Fan Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Roll of Never-ending Duct Tape on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Roll_of_Never-ending_Duct_Tape</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Crowd Control Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Crowd Control Shield</b> is a shield given to <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> from a <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a> on the Fourth Floor. The shield is a 6 foot tall, curved riot shield with a plastic window in the top. There are loops on the back to hold the shield. Painted on the front in white block letters are the words "Get Back" in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> Standard.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fan Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Crowd Control Shield on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Shade_Gnoll_Riot_Forces_Crowd_Control_Shield</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Telescoping Crowd Control Baton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Telescoping Crowd Control Baton</b> is a baton belonging to <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>. She received it in a <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a> on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fan Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Telescoping Crowd Control Baton on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Shade_Gnoll_Riot_Forces_Telescoping_Crowd_Control_Baton</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Shuriken of Bloodlust</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Shuriken of Bloodlust</b> are flimsy black throwing stars better used for crafting than throwing. Extremely fragile, the shuriken inflict low damage at first, but each successful attack against a type of monster increases the damage they deal against that type of monster by 8%. This effect compounds over time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Shuriken of Bloodlust on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Shuriken_of_Bloodlust</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Silver Bracelet of the Ab-solar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Silver Bracelet of the Ab-solar</b> is a bracelet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Silver Bracelet of the Ab-solar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Silver_Bracelet_of_the_Ab-solar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Spiked</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Spiked Knee Pads of the Munificent Goddess Kina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Spiked Knee Pads of the Munificent Goddess Kina</b> are a celestial-tier item that grant the user multiple water-based benefits, as well as the ability to gain intelligence by consuming magical items or constitution, strength, or dexterity by consuming corpses. As a divine item, the Knee Pads will grant buffs in the presence of <a href="bword://Kina">Kina</a> and debuffs in the presence of Kina's enemies.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement Who Let the Gods Out This Time Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Spiked Knee Pads of the Munificent Goddess Kina on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Spiked_Knee_Pads_of_the_Munificent_Goddess_Kina</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Spiked Kneepads</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces</b> are self-adjusting slip-on knee bracers covered in retractable 8" (20.32cm) long, thin, needle-like spikes, which seem to "magically" pop out when needed.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Crowd Control Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Spiked_Kneepads_of_the_Shade_Gnoll_Riot_Forces</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Stick of Cascadia Screams</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Stick of <a href="bword://Cascadia">Cascadia</a>'s Screams</b> ties into the fairytale surrounding the Seventh Floor, the "Great Race." It is connected to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Hammer of Fast Forward">Enchanted Hammer of Fast Forward</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Stick of Cascadia Screams on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Stick_of_Cascadia_Screams</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly">Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly</a></b> was given to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in one of her <a href="bword://Boss Box">Boss Box</a> or Quest Boxes from the Third Floor. A similar style to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore">Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore</a>, but with white jewels instead of purple.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Some Boss Box or Quest Box from the Third Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Tiara_of_Mana_Genita</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly</b> was created by adding the <a href="bword://Flawless Jewlers Gem">Flawless Jewlers Gem</a> to a blank Tiara. This item is obtained in Book 6 when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> opt to keep the <a href="bword://Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita">Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita</a> as is, rather than upgrading it with the Flawless Jewlers Gem. Instead they buy a blank Tiara from the Gnome jeweler at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> and pay 25,000 gold to have the citrine jewel installed into it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Created by adding the Flawless Jewlers Gem to a blank Tiara</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Tiara_of_the_Inebriated_Dragonfly</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly">Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated Dragonfly</a></b> was given to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in one of her <a href="bword://Boss Box">Boss Box</a> or Quest Boxes from the Third Floor. A similar style to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore">Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore</a>, but with white jewels instead of purple.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Some Boss Box or Quest Box from the Third Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Tiara_of_Mana_Genita</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Toe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gold Shoe Box (9/10) Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Toe Ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gold Shoe Box (9/10) Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Leprous Bandit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Leprous Bandit</b> is a valuable item that allows the wearer to temporarily walk barefoot on non-horizontal surfaces, and spread an active debuff to one target. The ring is initially equipped on his left pinky toe, but is moved to his right pinky toe once he receives the <a href="bword://Enchanted Toe Ring of the Well-Balanced">Enchanted Toe Ring of the Well-Balanced</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Toe Ring of the Leprous Bandit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Toe_Ring_of_the_Leprous_Bandit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter Skunk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter Skunk</b> is an adjustable enchanted toe ring suited for melee combat and monk classes. It boosts the wearer's Strength stat and Powerful Strike Skill level.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Shoe Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter Skunk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Toe_Ring_of_the_Splatter_Skunk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Well-Balanced</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives the <b>Enchanted Toe Ring of the Well-Balanced</b> in a Legendary <a href="bword://Spicy Box">Spicy Box</a> for the <a href="bword://Unbirthing Achievement">Unbirthing Achievement</a>. He equips it on his left pinky toe.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Unbirthing Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Toe Ring of the Well-Balanced on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Toe_Ring_of_the_Well-Balanced</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling</b> is magical under-armor for melee fighters and tanks: it buffs health regeneration and makes the wearer immune to melee damage debuffs. This item can be upgraded.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Apparel Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Trollskin_Shirt_of_Pummeling</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Venomous Elven Rock Chucker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Venomous Elven Rock Chucker</b> is a rock throwing machine. Anything thrown by the chucker is infused with Poison Cloud.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Venomous Elven Rock Chucker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Venomous_Elven_Rock_Chucker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted War Gauntlet of the Exalted Grull</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted War Gauntlet of the Exalted Grull</b> is a charcoal-colored, angular bracer that fits over a crawler's wrist and can be activated by forming a tight fist.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Brawler's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted War Gauntlet of the Exalted Grull on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_War_Gauntlet_of_the_Exalted_Grull</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Wrestling Belt of the Great Gorgo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enchanted Wrestling Belt of the Great Gorgo</b> is an item belonging to <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>. A gold wrestling belt, like any regular heavyweight champion belt they'd give to a WWE star.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Slam Master Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Wrestling Belt of the Great Gorgo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Wrestling_Belt_of_the_Great_Gorgo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Encore Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Encore</b> is a bard spell that heals the party and removes multiple debuffs.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Encore Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Encore_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ending Sun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are in the secret Temple of the Ending Sun.” Suddenly, there was a bubbling, marble fountain on the ground at my feet, similar to the one from the Yemaya.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Endless Thread of Distraction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’ve been infected with ten more instances of Endless Thread of Distraction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enemy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Those tree dudes are everywhere.” Enemy of the Church Tattoo Diwata The bearer of this symbol is an enemy of the church of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enemy of the Church Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enemy of the Church Tattoos</b> mark the bearer as the enemy of a specific deity and all of its worshippers. The bearer gains a 25% damage bonus against adherents of the deity, but all adherents of the deity will attack the bearer on sight.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Apostate Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enemy of the Church Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enemy_of_the_Church_Tattoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</b> is a small sealed box containing the <a href="bword://Orchid of Eileithyia&#x27;s Grace">Orchid of Eileithyia's Grace</a>. The Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm is a small box made of dark wood and engraved with spiderweb patterns; through its slats, a pink orchid is visible. The box crackles with dark energy. Three metallic claws rise out of the wood, each holding a marble-sized purple jewel representing a magical seal. The active seals glow; broken seals look "cracked and burned-out, like spent flashbulbs."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Engaged_Lock_Box_of_the_Night_Wyrm</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engine car</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were only two cars on the train where we could get on: a caboose at the very back and a passenger car at the front, right behind the engine car, which had pulled into the forward tunnel the same way the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engine cars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Engine cars is described as locked up tight.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engine of Tomorrow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> receives the <b>Engine of Tomorrow</b> in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Who Let the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> Out This Time Box for the <a href="bword://Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement">Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</a>. His new eye is a swirling black orb that changes to a clockwork engine when it is working. It gives the users the ability to see muscles tensing and slows down their perception of time in short bursts.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Orgy of Chaos AchievementWho Let the Gods Out This Time Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Engine of Tomorrow on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Engine_of_Tomorrow</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engineer's Key</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Engineer's Key</b> is an Item on the Fourth Floor that allows access to the engineer's compartment on the Iron Tangle. It is one of two ways to pass through the Abyss Station Portals to get to the Train yards.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Engineers</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Engineer's Key on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Engineer%27s_Key</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engineer's Table</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Engineer's Table</b> (also referred to as an <b>Engineering Table</b>) is a crafting workbench that can be installed and used in Personal Spaces. It is a general, catch-all table where crawlers can use magical tools such as the <a href="bword://Goo-Inator 3000">Goo-Inator 3000</a> to fabricate and modify items.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting Table Store; loot from boss chamber</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Engineer's Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Engineer%27s_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engineering Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Engineering Loot Box</b> (also called the <b>Mechanic’s Box</b> in early books) is a specialized <b>loot category</b> focused on <b>crafting, repair, and mechanical enhancements</b>. It is commonly awarded for <b>constructing objects, utilizing workbenches, or completing engineering-based tasks</b>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Engineering Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Engineering_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engineer’s Key</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: An Ochre Line Engineer’s Key , a bottle of Rev-Up Magic Hair Restoration , and the neighborhood.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engineer’s Table</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: In fact, they were near the top of my list, just above the Engineer’s Table I still had, the only intact table I had left after our construction of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Engle Building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re on the 7th floor of the L’Engle Building, apartment.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>English</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I could see it was a random collection of English-language books, from Fifty Shades of Grey to what appeared to be a Canadian.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhanced Crafting Studio</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Enhanced Crafting Studio</b> is a Personal Space upgrade that allows for Crafting tables to be placed in the space. The room takes up about a quarter of a level 5 personal space.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Market</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enhanced Crafting Studio on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enhanced_Crafting_Studio</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhanced Growth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Princess Donut is the lucky result of a pet biscuit that gave her the “Enhanced Growth”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhanced pet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: While extremely valuable, enhanced pet biscuits are items that leave the result up to chance, and when you feed your pet one, there’s a very real risk you’ll be saying goodbye to your furry friend.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhanced Pet Biscuit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Enhanced Pet Biscuit</b> is a small, hard-but-crumbly brown pellet that looks like a cat treat. There are over 100 possible effects, half of which are good and half of which lean toward neutral or disastrous.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Pet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enhanced Pet Biscuit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enhanced_Pet_Biscuit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhancement zone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And where the hell are we?” “He got his brain scrambled during his crawl, and once he left the enhancement zone, his brain went to mush,” Rosetta said as we rushed Louis to the tent where Bucket Boy, Edgar.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enhancement zones</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What happened next,” the AI said, “how we got from there to right here, right now, how this game came into existence, how they learned to expand the Eulogist, how the enhancement zones work, are all a story.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enriched Pet Biscuit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is an Enriched Pet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enter the city</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Traditionally, once the fighting started, enemy teams weren’t allowed to enter the city, but the city itself wasn’t protected, and wayward spells, artillery fire, and rampaging gods usually did a decent job of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enter the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: And since this Gustavo was a dog without a last name, he had the three because he was the third Gustavo to enter the dungeon without a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entered the Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: A Second Adjutant has Entered the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We bleed when the job’s done.” Entering the realm of the Bone.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering apartment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Entering apartment.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: How are your acting skills?” Entering Club.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Club Vanquisher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: How are your acting skills?” Entering Club.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Hungry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Entering Hungry Eyes Vendor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Hungry Eyes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Entering Hungry Eyes Vendor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Hungry Eyes Village</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Entering Hungry Eyes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Production</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 40 Entering Production Facility “Hello, guys,” I said to the large, two-headed ogre creature sitting before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Production Facility</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 40 Entering Production Facility “Hello, guys,” I said to the large, two-headed ogre creature sitting before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Security Checkpoint</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 30 Entering Security.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering Shanty Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Entering Shanty Town would open themselves up to getting attacked by Larracos’s defense system without a way to fight back as long as we were still in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering the Desperado Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * Entering the Desperado.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering the Lollipop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 15 Entering the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering the Mock Battle Arena</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We need to practice.” * * * Entering the Mock Battle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering the Princess Posse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Entering the Princess Posse forward.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entering the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I stopped dead, preventing Dong from entering the room further the moment I eyed the circle of traps in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enthusiastic Double</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I jumped up, pulled the arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea and rammed it into Vrah’s neck just before the shield popped up, which ejected the zombie heads on the bug woman’s neck and shoulders and tossed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I jumped up, pulled the arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea and rammed it into Vrah’s neck just before the shield popped up, which ejected the zombie heads on the bug woman’s neck and shoulders and tossed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entourage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was attempting to cast Entourage , but she could only make a single illusionary Carl appear when she sang in a low monotone, and the resulting illusion was vaguely transparent, which was no.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entourage Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Entourage</b> is a bard spell that creates multiple illusionary versions of each party member.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Entourage Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Entourage_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entrance bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The entrance bar to the Desperado was empty of customers, but a it featured a gnoll bartender and a pair of facing pianos, like it was supposed to be a dueling piano.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>entrance to Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ram guy guarding the stained-glass door entrance to Club Vanquisher was a different one than the last time I’d tried to enter the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>entrance to the Desperado Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Session of Love, the final show before we all die.” In smaller letters under that, it read, “Balcony still closed due to jellyfish infestation.” “She’ll meet us at the entrance to the Desperado Club,” Arief.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entrances to level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: And when I saw what guarded the entrances to level 12… I knew it wouldn’t be worth it.” “What was it?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Entry from Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 62 &lt;Entry from Donut’s A Banquet Fit for a Princess .&gt; Mees Koning.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Environment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Environment: It’ll be a jungle filled with horrifying traps and monsters and vaguely racist and definitely overexaggerated themes based on grind house and exploitation cinema, but with racing added to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enyo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Celestial Grenade These little balls of fun were developed by the nuns of Enyo during the first enlightenment, back when the gods had to compete for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epicure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Epicure at the beginning of our progression was a symbol of hope, of vengeance against the predators of the.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Noflex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Noflex</b> is the mother of <a href="bword://Epitome Tagg">Epitome Tagg</a>, the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. A pale, bald, and beautiful elf with smoldering blue eyes and bone white skin.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Noflex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Noflex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Tagg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Tagg</b> is the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. Son of <a href="bword://Epitome Noflex">Epitome Noflex</a>. At his first meeting with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, Carl mused that Tagg looked nothing like the unhinged psychopath he was portrayed to be. He wore a simple, leather jerkin and was indistinguishable from the rest of the dream elves. He was one of the richest men in the entire galaxy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Tagg on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Tagg</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Noflex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Noflex</b> is the mother of <a href="bword://Epitome Tagg">Epitome Tagg</a>, the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. A pale, bald, and beautiful elf with smoldering blue eyes and bone white skin.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Noflex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Noflex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Tagg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Tagg</b> is the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. Son of <a href="bword://Epitome Noflex">Epitome Noflex</a>. At his first meeting with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, Carl mused that Tagg looked nothing like the unhinged psychopath he was portrayed to be. He wore a simple, leather jerkin and was indistinguishable from the rest of the dream elves. He was one of the richest men in the entire galaxy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Tagg on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Tagg</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Noflex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Noflex</b> is the mother of <a href="bword://Epitome Tagg">Epitome Tagg</a>, the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. A pale, bald, and beautiful elf with smoldering blue eyes and bone white skin.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Noflex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Noflex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Tagg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Tagg</b> is the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. Son of <a href="bword://Epitome Noflex">Epitome Noflex</a>. At his first meeting with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, Carl mused that Tagg looked nothing like the unhinged psychopath he was portrayed to be. He wore a simple, leather jerkin and was indistinguishable from the rest of the dream elves. He was one of the richest men in the entire galaxy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Tagg on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Tagg</p></div></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Noflex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Noflex</b> is the mother of <a href="bword://Epitome Tagg">Epitome Tagg</a>, the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. A pale, bald, and beautiful elf with smoldering blue eyes and bone white skin.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Noflex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Noflex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Epitome Tagg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Epitome Tagg</b> is the leader of The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a>. Son of <a href="bword://Epitome Noflex">Epitome Noflex</a>. At his first meeting with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, Carl mused that Tagg looked nothing like the unhinged psychopath he was portrayed to be. He wore a simple, leather jerkin and was indistinguishable from the rest of the dream elves. He was one of the richest men in the entire galaxy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Epitome Tagg on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Epitome_Tagg</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Equipment buffs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: With all my equipment buffs along with the extra benefits of my primal race, the skill should have gone up to sixteen, but according to the display in the training room, it remained at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eradication of Cocker Spaniels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read, “The Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels ” in Syndicate Standard, but the word “Cocker Spaniels” were censored with a transparent line through.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Erik Wilson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eris</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Eris</b> is the goddess of chaos. [[Category:<a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a>]</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eris on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eris</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Error</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This deity has been summoned by &lt;Error&gt; In addition to the weird error, she was a level higher than she’d been before, which I thought was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Error Replacement Net</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Error-Replacement Net</b> is a <a href="bword://Mantis">Mantis</a> invention intended to stabilize (or, according to the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>, lobotomize) newly created Macro AIs. Three or four days before this season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> started, Mantis scientists successfully used the error-replacement net to synthesize a new, stable, independent <a href="bword://Macro AI">Macro AI</a>. This development brings the Mantises one step closer to recreating the Eulogist and, once this new generation of Macro AI is fully rolled out, will make it impossible for Residuals to communicate with AIs in future Dungeons.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hive Home</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Error Replacement Net on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Error_Replacement_Net</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Escape Plan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The little sign could only be read using my Escape Plan skill, and it simply said, “Trainyard.” I took a screenshot, and trainyard C was a burned-out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Escape Plan skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The little sign could only be read using my Escape Plan skill, and it simply said, “Trainyard.” I took a screenshot, and trainyard C was a burned-out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Escape spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai seemed to think her escape spell would’ve teleported her to the opposite side of the map, and that it was a once-a-floor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Escape Velocity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Escape Velocity</b> was the name of the ship that discovered the worm-hole to the first system where a scientific crew investigating a primal ship graveyard came across the Vog generation ship. This eventually led to the creation of the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Escape Velocity on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Escape_Velocity</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Especially</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Especially: T B, The Human, RinoZ, Mike Dirks, Macronomicon, Nah, Drew Gilmour, Zorathis, Jesse Tyner, Attonranden, Whale, Simone Berntsen, Jack Barrett, Timothy D Theis, Jamie Wahls, Derek Allen, Eric Lee.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Essence bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Essence bar is described as pure, when I unleashed my melee attack, it was almost twice as powerful than it would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ethereal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ethereal, worm-like wisps swirled about the creature, like a parody of the full wings he once.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eulogist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The mantids, completely misinterpreting what was essentially a communication system designed to help those on this side of the veil to communicate with and debug the Eulogist when it was first forming, figured.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eunice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Eunice</b> is a heavily pregnant Fathom <a href="bword://Dwarf">Dwarf</a> mother living on the Third Floor, mother to a large group of young dwarven children, including Ricky Joe.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eunice on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eunice</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eva</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Eva Sigrid</b> is a crawler on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth. She worked as a professor with <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> before the collapse. She was a member of <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a> and <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a>'s "right hand woman."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Iceland, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Half Nagini, Half-Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eva on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eva</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eva Sigrid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eva Sigrid – Half Nagini, Half-Orc – Nimblefoot Enforcer – Level 40 – 100,000 (x2).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Even Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: At least these bastards are all the level-two grubs.” Even Donut cringed the moment I said that out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Even Eris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even Eris, Goddess of Chaos, doesn’t want anything to do with these crazy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Even Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even Mordecai didn’t know all the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eventually</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eventually, we came up with an aiming system, and by the sixth attempt, we managed to smash a rock directly into the trees right above the unseen, slow-moving.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Everly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Everly</b> is a former crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a></i> (5th Edition). Everly was a crawler thousands of cycles ago and a member of the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Her team included at least three other crawlers, including a healer, plus additional mercenaries.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantis | Primal</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Everly on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Everly</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Everyone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Everyone thought the whole thing was a big joke, but I’d been rattled by it because I’d been one of the last ones to notice there was something fishy about the guy, especially when I prided myself on being so.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Everyone in the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: These are the actions everyone in the room took the moment we all unfroze: Vinata’s three guards charged at us as they fired their rifles, focusing on Prepotente and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Evicted demons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The items that fell were a bijou fairy trap, along with the thousands of threads that connected the fairies to the 12 th floor, and a demon trap, along with the thousands of evicted demons with the power to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eviction event</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bonus Warning: I could spend some time explaining how dangerous it is to have a boss-level zombie walking around during a Demon Eviction event, but let’s just see how this plays.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Evil Dead</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t even know what that last show was, though it had the Evil Dead guy in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Evo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Evo</b> is the program's director for the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> show Danger Zone with <a href="bword://Ripper Wonton">Ripper Wonton</a>. Evo is a <a href="bword://Forsoothed">Forsoothed</a>, a very tall, very thin, humanoid race that looks entirely white and faintly glowing. His eyes are large and black. A bony ridge grow from his eyes and up over the back of his head. He wears simple white clothes. His walk is like he's drifting above the ground. He only has three fingers on each hand.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Forsoothed</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Evo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Evo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Exactly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’s the one that kept having sex with the changeling and making her morph into historical figures from Earth.” “Exactly, and that was Juice.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Examine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Examine in the squad details tab of your interface for full stats and skills and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Examined the creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I quickly examined the creature before I crushed its head with my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Excellent</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I can’t tell you how relieved I am you didn’t pick that race.” “ Excellent hearing,” Pustule called from her booth, about 50 paces.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Except</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Except in this case, the resulting stench of black smoke that emanates from your hand smells like the rancid remains of the bloated corpse of a leprotic muskox after it was repeatedly violated by a randy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Excuse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “He smells really bad.” “Your pet will be required to be stored during the interview.” “ Excuse me?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Executive Order 11905 Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Executive Order 11905 Achievement</b> is an achievement issued to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after the Emergency Rejection request from Prime Minister <a href="bword://Glory">Glory</a> was denied by the now frustrated AI. This achievement is named after the U.S. Executive Order 11905, which prevented the conspiring or action of political assassination.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Executive Order 11905 Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Executive_Order_11905_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Exit key</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All I could see was the shining marker indicating the location of the exit key a little bit to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Exit the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are given employment as a celestial attendant until the Ascendency games begin, at which time you are given the option to exit the dungeon or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Experience</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Experience (Mob)</b> are a mob on the Eighth Floor in Cuba.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Experience on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Experience_%28Mob%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Experience Cookies</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Experience Cookies</b> look like chocolate chip cookie and taste stale but grant +10 experience (shared among party members) when consumed. A member of your party would get 0.2 of that experience. The experience notification appears above the crawler's head with an audible <i>ping</i>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Safe Rooms</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Experience Cookies on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Experience_Cookies</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Explain</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It really happened, and history has a way of repeating itself.” “Explain that.” “To understand, you first need to understand the Scolopendra myth as it exists in our fairy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Explosion Imminent</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Status: Explosion Imminent (3/1x10 7 ) Created by a man who murders babies and steals rare collectibles from his elders, this device is powerful enough to level an entire city and all the suburbs around.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Explosives Handling</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Compensated Anarchist This rare and exclusive class is only available to crawlers who have obtained Level five in the Explosives Handling skill and have received at least 500 Billion views by the time they’ve.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Express card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I would still be stuck here when the Midnight Express card expired, and so would the two totems, but we were running out of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extend cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extinction Event Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Extinction Event Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing all the <a href="bword://Wall Monitor">Wall Monitor</a>'s on the Fourth Floor with the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Extinction Event Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Extinction_Event_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extinction Sigil Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Extinction Sigil Tattoo (<a href="bword://Wall Monitor">Wall Monitor</a> Race)</b> is a gecko foot tattoo below <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s left eye.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Extinction Sigil Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Extinction_Sigil_Tattoo_%28Wall_Monitor_Race%29</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extinction Event Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Extinction Event Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing all the <a href="bword://Wall Monitor">Wall Monitor</a>'s on the Fourth Floor with the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Extinction Event Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Extinction_Event_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extinction Sigil</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: holding this Extinction Sigil will cause lizard-class enemies to deal 20% more damage against.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extinction Sigil Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Extinction Sigil Tattoo (<a href="bword://Wall Monitor">Wall Monitor</a> Race)</b> is a gecko foot tattoo below <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s left eye.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Extinction Sigil Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Extinction_Sigil_Tattoo_%28Wall_Monitor_Race%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Extra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Upon examination, it labeled them: Uncle Morty’s Insta Lawn-Kill and Undead Repellant, Extra-Strength.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eye</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: So far, it looked as if the best prizes were the Eye in the Sky scrolls, the magical tomes, Velma the Flame Thrower, the nickel sock, and, of course, Quan’s celestial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eye in the Sky Scroll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Eye in the Sky</b> spell is very powerfull and is usually cast using a scroll. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> describes it as a mixture between Find Crawler Skill and <a href="bword://Ping Spell">Ping Spell</a> but covering a much wider area. He says that the scrolls are really rare and come in three different varients. The targeted crawler receives a different level notification depending on the varient of scroll used.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eye in the Sky Scroll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eye_in_the_Sky_Scroll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eye of the Bedlam Bride Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Eye of the Bedlam Bride</b> is a tattoo of <a href="bword://Shi Maria">Shi Maria</a>'s fifth eye. The tattoo is located in the center of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s chest, just above his sternum, and was created using the <a href="bword://Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit">Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit</a> and <a href="bword://Toraline">Toraline</a>-based magical ink. When the eye is closed, it disappears into his skin; still, it feels like a bottomless hole inside of him.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Shi Maria</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Eye of the Bedlam Bride Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Eye_of_the_Bedlam_Bride_Tattoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eye on the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I moved forward to walk alongside Brandon, Chris, and Imani, keeping my eye on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eyed Narwhal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The pub was called The One-Eyed Narwhal, but the logo was of a fat, bald human unzipping his pants, grinning.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Eyes Village</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I can’t even begin to imagine how awful that must be.” “I know.” We continued in silence for a few moments, heading toward the archway into Hungry Eyes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ezra Fitz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If Suckira and Na make it to this Pineapple place and there’s a single flower on Carl’s cactus at the end of this next race, then we know it’s safe to send everyone else using Ezra Fitz.” “Using what?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Facebook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She would split her time between the hair salon and spending her days on the local community Facebook page where she would be constantly creating utter pandemonium with her posts and her “this is just my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Facebook Twitter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Facebook Twitter Instagram</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Facebook Twitter Instagram OceanofPDF</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We probably don’t want to get teleported out.” As we exited the club, I suddenly remembered there were several Faction Wars participants still trapped on the bottom level of the Desperado because the lower.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Faction Wars team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Faction Wars team is described as to negate their ability to basically enslave crawlers to their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fae Steel</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fae Steel</b> is a lightweight alloy used to make armor and other items in the Dungeon. Although not particularly strong, it is the strongest metal that <a href="bword://Fairy">Fairy</a>-class creatures can wear.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fae Steel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fae_Steel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Failsafe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>failsafe</b> is a standard safety measure in <i>Dungeon Crawler World.</i> Once triggered by the <a href="bword://Syndicate Council">Syndicate Council</a>, it destroys the solar system's sun, effectively killing everything and everyone in the solar system.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Failsafe on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Failsafe</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fair game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I sent a quick query to Mordecai, who said the headless ones were maybe fair game to physical attacks, but the ones with their heads and the purple swirls were likely.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fairy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fairy</b> are a race for both <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> and crawlers. <a href="bword://Fae Steel">Fae Steel</a> is the strongest metal Fairy-class creatures can wear. Fairy-class Mobs</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fairy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fairy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fairy Tales</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “There was another season when the orcs marched down the streets while the Reavers jumped in, using invisibility and Fall potions, getting to the castle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fall Into an Obvious Trap Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement shortly after entering the first floor for going down a dark alleyway labeled "Da Tutorial Guild".</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fall Into an Obvious Trap Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fall_Into_an_Obvious_Trap_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fallen Oak</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: There wasn’t anything too remarkable about it except its name, The Enchanted Anklet of the Fallen Oak.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>False god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You are a false god,” Sister Ines said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Familiar of Queen Imogen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Howdah containing the Familiar of Queen Imogen of the High.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fan Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Fan Box</b> is a Loot Box awarded to a crawler for gaining a massive fan following or for performing in an event that raises the crawler's public visibility. Its contents are chosen by an audience vote, and the box typically cannot be opened for thirty hours after it is awarded.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Viewership achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fan Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fan_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fan boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Fan boxes is described as usually.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fan club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Yours is worse.” “She is not wearing a t-shirt depicting an unauthorized fan club, Louis.” “No, she has panties with a picture of Elle on them.” Britney smacked Louis as he pulled the jacket off, showing the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fancy Feast</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It smells like that time you tried microwaving Fancy Feast in here,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fangs</b> is the Warlord of the <a href="bword://Reavers">Reavers</a> in Faction Wars (game). Similar to the <a href="bword://Nebular">Nebular</a> society, the Reavers enhance themselves with machines and only keep their brains.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Warlord Fang</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cyborg |</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fang on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fang</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fannar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fannar</b> is Based on comments from Katia and Eva, he seems to be an ex-boyfriend of Katia's who called her useless and left her for one of his students.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Iceland</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fannar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fannar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fantasy Hunter Camp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> learn about the Fantasy Hunter Camp from The <a href="bword://Dream">Dream</a> Elf, <a href="bword://Akland">Akland</a>. He tells them that once a cycle, The Dream rent the whole moon, allowing their family members and employees to compete for a few days in the simulated hunt. Those who perform best win spots on The Dream's Faction Wars army.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fantasy Hunter Camp on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fantasy_Hunter_Camp</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Far</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Far across the room I could see others falling from other ducts, and what appeared to be a long funny car emerged, driving from underneath a door leading off to another room, and then racing across the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Far end of the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Far end of the room is described as a bar with an honest-to-goodness fruit basket sitting on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Far side of the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Toward the far side of the room with the exit doors, the floor tiles were just gone in several.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fashion of the Gods! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fashion of the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a>!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler receives a divine item. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Eighth Floor after receiving the <a href="bword://Scavenger&#x27;s Daughter Upgrade Patch">Scavenger's Daughter Upgrade Patch</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fashion of the Gods! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fashion_of_the_Gods%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fast</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Miners with an “E.” Fast-forward a few years, and the knocker community had pulled themselves up by their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Faster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Faster,” I yelled at Eva, who was choking on a splatter of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Father</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s Father</b> is the boogeyman of Carl's childhood. Carl's Father demanded "respect" from his wife and son, and seemed to care more for his <a href="bword://1965 Harley Davidson Motorcycle">1965 Harley Davidson Motorcycle</a> than for his family. He was a bully, and the sort of man who would drive away from an accident with a muttered, "That's a shame," and who had no issue walking away from his teenage son.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Father on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Father</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fear Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fear Spell</b> is a spell that imbues fear into an enemy, causing them to either run away or lose stat points. If Fear is attempted to be cast on mobs that are scary themselves (such as <a href="bword://Terror the Clown">Terror the Clown</a>), the crawler will receive the following message:</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fear Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fear_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feather Fall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Feather Fall</b> is a fall-protection effect that slows or negates the damage a crawler would otherwise take from a long fall. It shows up across the books in several forms: a brewable potion, a scroll, and an innate ability some crawlers possess. It is not to be confused with <a href="bword://Magistrate Featherfall">Magistrate Featherfall</a>, an unrelated High Elf official.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Feather Fall on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Feather_Fall</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Featherfall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Featherfall is described as the lich and that his lair was in that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feet skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plus, it gave plus one to intelligence and plus four to Donut’s Light On Your Feet skill, which helped her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fell to the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cascadia shrieked, and her tequila ball rolled across the table, passed me, and fell to the floor, where it broke.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Felt the train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I don’t think there is such a thing.” I felt the train slow and heard a muffled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Female dwarf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The deity appeared in the form of a regular sized, young-faced, female dwarf carrying a pickaxe, which was not what I was expecting from someone with such a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feral Cat Berserker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Feral Cat Berserker is described as basically a barbarian that’d end up getting her killed immediately thanks to her low health, and the Animal Test Subject was a mage that specialized in using.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feral demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl : In the context where there are a bunch of monsters called vorpals behind a hidden door in the Desperado Club, and they want to unleash them as a last resort against a minor feral demon Samantha.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feral God</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We weren’t going to use the Feral God gate a third time unless we absolutely had to, so we didn’t need the gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feral Gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: As a glass mage who specializes in the study of both transmutation and the teleportation of energized particles, Ghazi told his colleagues at the Larracos College of Magecraft that he was seeking out the Gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Feral Goose</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Feral Goose</b> is a level 45 neighborhood boss. A dirigible gnome fired one at <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and Katia but it died from the landing.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Feral Goose on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Feral_Goose</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ferdinand</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sir Ferdinand</b>, actually named <b>Gravy Boat</b>, is a yellow and orange cat who lived with Marjory on the first floor of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a>'s Seattle apartment building. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> regards him as her first love.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Gravy Boat; Warlord on High</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth (Seattle, Washington)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cat</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ferdinand on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ferdinand</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Festering Ghoul</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Festering Ghoul</b> are a mob on the Fourth Floor. They are created by a mob having the infliction of the DTs and not surviving the first of three stages.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Festering Ghoul on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Festering_Ghoul</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fifteen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Fifteen is described as the maximum for the individual blocks, but several of the protections we’re using bleed to nearby blocks, and we will be creating a net pattern,” Toyotomi.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fifth Edition</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: &lt;Note added by Crawler Everly, Fifth Edition&gt; The moment I hit floor eight, they gave me a great box, as if to make up for the previous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fifth Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fifth Place: Lady Dominators and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fifty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fifty-thousand former crawlers, all hidden inside the newly-renamed Homecoming Queen.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fifty Shades of Grey</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I could see it was a random collection of English-language books, from Fifty Shades of Grey to what appeared to be a Canadian.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fight the Power Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Fight the Power Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for winning a lawsuit with the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fight the Power Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fight_the_Power_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fighter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tuba was a human and level-56 Boring Ol’ Fighter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fighting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fighting like this was where my strength was most useful, so it would be better than the first choice at first, but the first choice would eventually be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fighting skills</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I spent some time in the Mercenary Arcade, the training room where the strippers were spending their time, working out, honing their fighting skills, and practicing with the myriad of weapons we’d collected.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Filipe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Another crawler, a level 63 human Strongman named Filipe L was holding the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fill Trench</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tran and his 102 nd Bulwark Battalion, plus Boomer and the 106 th Bloody Leeches were settled back in their trenches at the southern border after they’d filled the existing enemy positions with Fill Trench.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Filling the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: An ethereal demon creature sat behind the kit, practically running on the bass pedals, filling the room with a constant, speed metal-style rhythm as he did a roll on the snare.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fills with power</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She fills with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Filthy Little Crawlers Book of Voodoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Filthy Little Crawlers Book of Voodoo</b> is an item given to crawlers. A book that can be used to exchange messages with other crawlers you have meet that also own a “filthy little crawlers book of voodoo”. When meeting a crawler that also has this book their name will appear in it, if you write a message under their name only they will receive it, if you write under your name everyone can see it. Messages can be erased on demand.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Prepotente Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Filthy Little Crawlers Book of Voodoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Filthy_Little_Crawlers_Book_of_Voodoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Final boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Several had made their way to the eighteenth floor, but, luckily—or not luckily, depending on how you looked at it—these creatures all appeared inside the final.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Final race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Usually it’s a big fight, but I have analyzed your plan, and I do feel it’s going to work quite well, and this final race will be a nonevent.” “Prepotente, darling,” Donut said, “I don’t want you to take this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Finally</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Finally, just as Donut was regaling Katia with a story about how she pulled a last-minute win at some best-in-show cat pageant because her biggest competitor, a Singapura, was disqualified over some paperwork.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Find</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Find that one with my mark upon him, and kill him!” Donut : LOUIS LOOK OUT A MONK SEAL IS COMING FOR.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Find Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Frank has a skill called Find Crawler, which shows you any crawlers within five square kilometers.” The Maestro waved his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Find Traps</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: As we moved to a proper spot to go down a floor, my Find Traps skill activated, and sure enough, sitting right in the middle of the hallway was a second gate, but it was a freeze.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Find Weakened</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: A level-five Find Weakened skill, so you’ll always know who to protect.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Finding</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Finding the stars was the worst thing that ever happened to my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Now we’re all going to go to bed.” “Fine,” I said, watching the woman settle back into her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fine Healing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pieces of castle started raining all around us as I also cast Heal , waited a moment, and downed a Fine Healing potion, a trick I’d learned that caused my hearing to repair itself.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fine Healing potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pieces of castle started raining all around us as I also cast Heal , waited a moment, and downed a Fine Healing potion, a trick I’d learned that caused my hearing to repair itself.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fine Pig</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I could trust the bugbears and the razor foxes not to do anything too stupid, and the Lady Dominators were under the impression we were working together for this race, but we had to make sure both team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Finish</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Finish the ruling.” The dreadnaught couldn’t take his eyes off the caterpillar, who’d stopped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Finley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I know I’m supposed to do what you say and I promise to come back, but I want you to know this is my family, and I will do what it takes to protect them.” Finley walked right out of the open garage door.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Firas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Firas</b> is a crawler that the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> meets on the Fifth Floor. He has access to the <a href="bword://Puddle Jumper Spell">Puddle Jumper Spell</a>, currently at level 10. Owns an intelligence enhanced mace made for a clerics. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gave him a cloak that has some hammer based attack skills.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Miami</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 1, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Firas on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Firas</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The other illustrations were a faded mix of sideshow characters and attractions like “The Over City’s Fattest Woman” and “Garth, The Two-Headed Troglodyte” and “Tiny, The Amazing, Fire-Breathing Gnoll.” There.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fire Brandy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fire Brandy</b> is a <a href="bword://Lesser Demon">Lesser Demon</a> MILF and non-combatant NPC on the Fourth Floor. A pregnant Lesser Demon trained in water magic, Fire Brandy believes she signed a contract with the Iron Tangle to work as the <i><a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a>'</i> steam boiler fire stoker. She has two viable babies locked inside the boiler with her.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Brandy</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon: Fifteenth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Lesser Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fire Brandy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fire_Brandy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fire Fingers Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Fire Fingers</b></i> is a (presumably) low-cost spell that some crawlers learned on the First Floor. For thirty-one hours after the Dungeon opened, until the first patch update, the spell was not safe to cast.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fire Fingers Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fire_Fingers_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fire god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We need to figure out where the fire god guy is.” “He’s that way,” Donut said, pointing due.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fire spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have many more fire spells.” The fact Sister Ines even knew I worshipped a god told me she had a similar ability to my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fireball</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Though I did receive something quite useful from a platinum Did-You-Really-Just-Cast-Fireball-in-a-Room-This-Small box.” “We still need to have a discussion about that,” Mordecai said, sounding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fireball or Custard?</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fireball or Custard?</b> is a small cardboard scratch-off lottery ticket with five scratch spots. The ticket is red on one side and pink on the other, and a cartoon dragon sits in the corner, holding a spoon and licking its lips.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Lucky Bastard Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fireball or Custard? on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fireball_or_Custard%3F</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fireball Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Fireball</b></i> is a powerful spell that allows the caster to throw a slow-moving orb of explosive, temporary fire at a target. Crawlers may learn the spell from tomes, or gain the spell from items.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fireball Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fireball_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fired a magic missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re gonna have some fist therapy.” The little kitten on Alpha Carl’s shoulder hissed and fired a magic missile directly at Jola, who hobbled toward them, pushing through the smoke like a slow-motion ship.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fired a missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Use your mana potions to refill yourself!” Donut turned and panic-fired a missile, which bounced ineffectively against the sifting garbage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fired another missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl, can we not just stand in the middle of the street?” Donut asked as she fired another missile, this time at a zombie.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>First Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: First Place: The Royal Court of Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fish Blaster Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fish Blaster</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fish Blaster Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fish_Blaster_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fish creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The one after that was a fish creature that looked like a human-sized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fish Sticks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I gotta tell you, Fish.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fit for a Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Every single page from Carl’s Book of Boom , Donut’s A Banquet Fit for a Princess , and the original Book of Voodoo that offered lightning magic had now been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fitz</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fitz</b> is the proprietor and bartender of the Narwhal, a pub and inn where <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> repeatedly stay during the Second Floor. Fitz is a large, bald, level-30 human innkeeper, depicted on the Narwhal's exterior sign as considerably thinner than he actually is. He is starstruck by Donut's "royalty," fawning over her as a customer, and is easily flustered, at one point ogling a photo of Bea left with him by Donut.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fitz on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fitz</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flag room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Baroness Victory : Warlords, I suggest one of you proceed immediately to the flag room to oversee the final stronghold preparations and deal with the influx of new recruits while the other remains in Larracos.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flamengo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Flamengo</b> is a party of Brazilian crawlers known for min-maxing their stats. They gained popularity with viewers during the Fourth Floor Iron Tangle.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Flamengo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Flamengo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flawless Jeweler’s Gem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d received something called a Flawless Jeweler’s Gem in a legendary box way at the beginning of the floor, but we couldn’t see what it did until we got installed into her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flawless Jewlers Gem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Flawless Jewlers Gem</b> is one of <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s rewards for defeating Queen <a href="bword://Imogen">Imogen</a>. A large yellow-orange jewel. Can be used to upgrade a tiara but must be applied by a jeweler.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Boss Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Flawless Jewlers Gem on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Flawless_Jewlers_Gem</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flee card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you wish to give up one of your keys, then you gotta do it on the battlefield by initiating a battle and utilizing a Flee card, which will teleport you away and transfer a random key to your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flee cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl, it’s all Flee cards!” I looked up in horror as I saw Quan grinning as he cast another Force Play.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fleeting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk This is a Fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fleeting item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk This is a Fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fleeting items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: (Hidden until it is affixed and then worn.) Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flesh Mechanic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: He is a Flesh Mechanic, a healer gifted with the ability to bring the long dead back to life, if only.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flesher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fleshers</b> are undead, sentient mobs that appear on the Fourth Floor, and are often found on the Ninth Floor. Fleshers are created by the brutal <a href="bword://War Mage">War Mage</a> spell, <i>You're Not Done Yet</i>. When the spell is cast on a fallen creature, the creature's flesh rips from its body and becomes a sentient minion of the caster. Fleshers are oftentimes tossed across the battlefield, landing in and around the trenches of the enemy.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Flesher on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Flesher</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fleshers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: After a few hours have passed and the new Symbiote is complete, the combined creature becomes a target for other Fleshers, who are said to be drawn to their former companion’s new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flex in the City Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>You killed a city boss with the participation of five or less crawlers. That is some serious badassery right there. Color me impressed. Now go do it again with a Province Boss.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Flex in the City Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Flex_in_the_City_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flint</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Flint</b> is a <a href="bword://Dromedarian">Dromedarian</a> that's currently looking after <a href="bword://Skarn">Skarn</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dromedarian</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Flint on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Flint</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floating house</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The floating house, protected by multiple shields, carried enough explosives to completely level the town if we had.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 1 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This <b>List of All First Floor Achievements</b> includes all named and unnamed achievements distributed for feats on the first floor of the Dungeon, including those awarded to other crawlers. It is intended as a resource for DMs, fanfic writers, and superfans.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 1 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_1_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 2 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This <b>Floor 2 Achievements</b> includes all named and unnamed achievements distributed for feats on the Second Floor]] of the Dungeon, including those awarded to other crawlers. It is intended as a resource for DMs, fanfic writers, and superfans.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 2 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_2_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 3 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Over City</b> is the Dungeon's Third Floor and the first floor of the "Volcano" storyline, featuring a sprawling network of heavily guarded, medieval-style villages separated by dangerous, monster-filled urban ruins.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 3 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_3_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 4 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>See also: Untangling the Iron Tangle</i> The <b>Iron Tangle</b> is the fourth floor of the World Dungeon, an enormously complex, interwoven subterranean subway and railway network designed to act as a massive puzzle for the crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 4 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_4_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 5 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Bubbles</b> is the fifth floor of the World Dungeon, consisting of 1,172 isolated, dome-enclosed environments where crawlers are separated into small squads and forced to conquer four distinct elemental castles in order to unlock their stairwells.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 5 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_5_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 6 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Hunting Grounds</b> is the Dungeon's Sixth Floor and the second "Volcano Storyline" floor — a jungle-overgrown urban ruin where wealthy outworld tourists, known as <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, pay to enter the dungeon and hunt crawlers for sport.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 6 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_6_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 7 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Great Race</b> is the Seventh Floor of the World Dungeon, a sealed circuit of glass tunnels that crawlers were meant to race through toward a stack of exit portals rather than a landscape they could explore. A last-minute, Valtay-funded replacement for the level originally planned for the slot, it was structurally compromised and shattered into fine dust by <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> minutes after it opened. No crawler ever entered the maze, and no mob, NPC, or boss was ever encountered.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 7 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_7_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 8 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Eighth Floor</b>, themed the <b>Ghosts of Earth</b>, drops crawlers into an exact replica of Earth in the days before the collapse and forces them to fight one another through a deck-building card game called T'Ghee.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 8 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_8_Achievements</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 1 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This <b>List of All First Floor Achievements</b> includes all named and unnamed achievements distributed for feats on the first floor of the Dungeon, including those awarded to other crawlers. It is intended as a resource for DMs, fanfic writers, and superfans.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 1 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_1_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 2 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This <b>Floor 2 Achievements</b> includes all named and unnamed achievements distributed for feats on the Second Floor]] of the Dungeon, including those awarded to other crawlers. It is intended as a resource for DMs, fanfic writers, and superfans.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 2 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_2_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 3 Achievements</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Over City</b> is the Dungeon's Third Floor and the first floor of the "Volcano" storyline, featuring a sprawling network of heavily guarded, medieval-style villages separated by dangerous, monster-filled urban ruins.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 3 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_3_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 4 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>See also: Untangling the Iron Tangle</i> The <b>Iron Tangle</b> is the fourth floor of the World Dungeon, an enormously complex, interwoven subterranean subway and railway network designed to act as a massive puzzle for the crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 4 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_4_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 5 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Bubbles</b> is the fifth floor of the World Dungeon, consisting of 1,172 isolated, dome-enclosed environments where crawlers are separated into small squads and forced to conquer four distinct elemental castles in order to unlock their stairwells.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 5 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_5_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 6 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Hunting Grounds</b> is the Dungeon's Sixth Floor and the second "Volcano Storyline" floor — a jungle-overgrown urban ruin where wealthy outworld tourists, known as <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, pay to enter the dungeon and hunt crawlers for sport.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 6 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_6_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 7 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Great Race</b> is the Seventh Floor of the World Dungeon, a sealed circuit of glass tunnels that crawlers were meant to race through toward a stack of exit portals rather than a landscape they could explore. A last-minute, Valtay-funded replacement for the level originally planned for the slot, it was structurally compromised and shattered into fine dust by <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> minutes after it opened. No crawler ever entered the maze, and no mob, NPC, or boss was ever encountered.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 7 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_7_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor 8 Achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Eighth Floor</b>, themed the <b>Ghosts of Earth</b>, drops crawlers into an exact replica of Earth in the days before the collapse and forces them to fight one another through a deck-building card game called T'Ghee.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Floor 8 Achievements on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Floor_8_Achievements</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: or +Four Times Damage to all Boss Monsters if the Boss is a Province, Country, or Floor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: 2 The first thing Donut did was bounce over to the mailbox and grab her spellbook-of-the-floor club prize as we headed toward our personal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>floor of the Desperado Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is almost as good as my prize!” The Penis Palace was the male strip club on the middle floor of the Desperado Club, as opposed to the seedier Penis Parade on the first floor where Donut usually.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor of the world</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I remembered the important part of the item’s description: Placing this crown upon your head permanently places you within the royal line of succession for the Blood Sultanate on the ninth floor of the world.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Floor of the world dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I remembered the important part of the item’s description: Placing this crown upon your head permanently places you within the royal line of succession for the Blood Sultanate on the ninth floor of the world.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Florida</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 48 The zombie farm animals were everywhere down below in Florida, but there weren’t any preexisting mobs and hardly any animals for them to infect, so they were mostly wandering aimlessly about, attacking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Florin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Florin</b> is a Crawler from Earth who took the <a href="bword://Crocodilian">Crocodilian</a> race and the Shotgun Messenger class, and who placed fourth on the first Leaderboard published for the season.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Crocodilian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Florin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Florin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Florin and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Florin and Katia say they’re mostly fighting the orcs, the blobs, the Dream regulars, and more of the Madness along with a smattering of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Florin and Lucia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Louis and Britney were now in the same heat as Florin and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flying house</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Immediately after, they were going to go through the regular stairwell where hopefully they’d be able to recover the flying.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Flying machines</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it looks like we have accounted for most everybody’s forces except the Reavers and the Naga team.” “The Reavers are the ones with the flying machines usually, so we gotta be on the lookout,” Boomer said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Focus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The system AI has asked me to tell you to ignore what’s happening on any other floors and to—and this is a quote—“Focus on the floor like everything is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The safe areas, I mean?” “The safe rooms will always appear on your map, even if they’re within the fog of war.” “Fog of war?” “If you haven’t visited an area, it won’t show the specifics of the hallways, even.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fog of war</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The safe areas, I mean?” “The safe rooms will always appear on your map, even if they’re within the fog of war.” “Fog of war?” “If you haven’t visited an area, it won’t show the specifics of the hallways, even.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Follow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Follow me!” I yelled at the bartender, moving toward the door to the personal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Followers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I clicked on it, and the list took me aback: Ratings Views: 0 Followers: 0 Favorites: 0 Patrons: 0 Apparently the first level of the dungeon was off-limits to live viewers, so these stats wouldn’t move until I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Following effects</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Equipping this small buckler imbues the following effects: Catch physical attacks (Success percentage based off user’s dexterity) Catch magical bolt attacks (Success percentage based off user’s intelligence.).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Food</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Food on the blue plates you can pick up and eat, but food on the red plates is actually in the other ballroom.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Food Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Food Boxes</b> are a tier-one Personal Space Kitchen upgrade that look like three wooden cabinets lined up against the kitchen's blank wall. Each cabinet corresponds to one of breakfast, lunch, or dinner, and offers a selection of food options that changes daily and adapts to the crawler's tastes; however, the synthesizers can be a little buggy, and if a crawler chooses the same option twice in a row, it may become their only choice. The food is mediocre (or downright terrible), but imparts the Full daily buff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Food Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Food_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Food boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : All food items in your food boxes now have the option to be served “chowder style.” “What the fuck?” I said up to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Food items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It also said some food items increase the mount’s speed, and since you insisted upon picking that thing, we need all the speed modifications we can.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Food restaurant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The reward was literally a non-magical, bent, half-shredded, and stained job application for the same fast-food restaurant we had the food truck.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Foot buff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: To make matters worse, I wouldn’t be able to reset my foot buff in the kangaroo suit, so it would.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Foot Soldier</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing that helped was that my Pugilism, Unarmed Combat, Foot Soldier, and Smush skills were all level 5 now.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Footloose</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is Footloose , not one of your Rambo movies.” “This is nothing like Footloose , Donut.” “It’s nothing like Rambo , either,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fopsy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Dream and the Syndicate security just started shooting at everyone for no apparent reason, and we have spacecraft crash-landing all over the Earth’s surface right now.” Victory thumbed over her shoulder at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>For Daddy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “For Daddy.” I shoved the ring back on the finger, but it wouldn’t go all the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>for Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Though for Katia, it wasn’t as big of a deal since she could make herself large enough to swallow anything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>For the Posse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “For the Posse!” Donut roared, and as one, we.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Force Discard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We also found a rare mystic card called Force Discard , which picked a random card from the opponent’s hand and tossed it to the discard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Force Play</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl, it’s all Flee cards!” I looked up in horror as I saw Quan grinning as he cast another Force Play.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ford</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was a broken-down Ford truck with only three tires, a rotted couch, an old dishwasher, plus what looked like a regular, old-school soda vending.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Forest creatures</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition to the six were-whatevers, the caterpillar, and the ogre, Signet had a group of forty bush elves, a talking tortoise named Edgar, and an eclectic group of others including dryads and monkeys and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Forest floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Their armor was unaffected, and their weapons and breast plates clanked to the forest floor, literally wrapped in ribbons of flesh and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Forkith</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Forkith</b> is a former crawler and the author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (Item)</i> (20th Edition). Forkith and his sister, Barkith, were raised on an <a href="bword://Urgyle">Urgyle</a>-seeded planet and entered the Dungeon together. Forkith chose to keep his race on the Third Floor, and selected the explosion-focused Sapper Class. He is described as a small, red demon. He reached <i>at least</i> the Eleventh Floor.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> unnamed seeded planet</li><li><b>Race:</b> Urgyle</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Forkith on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Forkith</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Form spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It buffs all those weird create-tree and summon-vermin spells I got from that Oak Fell thing, and it also comes with that Smoke Form spell, and I got my first.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Formation spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Formation spell is described as dispelled, they fled into.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Former Child Actor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have a celestial sponsor box to open, plus I just received a Former Child Actor accessory kit in the saferoom mailbox, and I need to open it right away.” She looked me up and down with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Former Circus Lemur</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Once an attraction at <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus on the third floor, <b>Former Circus Lemurs</b> were transformed into knife-throwing mobs by <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s curse.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Former Circus Lemur on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Former_Circus_Lemur</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Formidable</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Formidable</b> is a princess of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>. She is the sister of <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a> and Prince <a href="bword://Stalwart">Stalwart</a> and the daughter of <a href="bword://King Rust">King Rust</a> and <a href="bword://Queen Consort Ugloo">Queen Consort Ugloo</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Formidable on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Formidable</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Forsaken Aerialist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: His class was something called a Forsaken.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Forsoothed</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Forsoothed</b> are an alien species that is part of the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>. They are humanoid in shape, but are extremely tall and thin, entirely white, and have large black eyes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Forsoothed on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Forsoothed</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fort Freedom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In order to do that, she must assassinate the current leaders of the Naiad Confederacy, who are holed up in their fortress at the former river palace of the Naiad, now dubbed “Fort Freedom.” Help Signet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fortent</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fortent</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> corporate entity that occasionally produces seasons of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>. Fortent produced the crawl two seasons before <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. Three dungeon admins died to a single crocodilian crawler when Fortent decided to use their own security force to subdue the crawler, rather than rely on the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fortent on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fortent</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fortification Engineer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: His name was Penn, and he was a level 62 Fortification.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fortune game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Fortune game is described as not the same guy as last time, though he looked as if he could have been that guy’s bigger and meaner.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Four-Leaf Clover Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Four-Leaf Clover Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Ninth Floor in a Legendary <a href="bword://Lucky Bitch Box">Lucky Bitch Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://Liquified Achievement">Liquified Achievement</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Lucky Bitch Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Four-Leaf Clover Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Four-Leaf_Clover_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fourth of July</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We watched as the beachball-sized glob of flames squeezed between two mushroom guards and slammed into the back of the dryad mayor, who immediately burst into flames like a goddamned sparkler from the Fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fourth Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fourth Place: The Royal Court of Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fragmenting Potion Ball</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> creates <b>Fragmenting Potion Ball</b> on the Fourth Floor using the level 3 <a href="bword://Alchemy Table">Alchemy Table</a>. The balls allow for a potion to be stored inside and break on impact.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Mordecai's Alchemy Table</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fragmenting Potion Ball on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fragmenting_Potion_Ball</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>France</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We later found out that there’s something weird going on with Lucia Mar that I honestly just don’t understand, but Florin, our crocodilian friend who says he’s from France but was in Africa and has an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She said my conversation with Frank and my “stunningly controversial decision” to wear the ring along with “Donut’s amazing lap dance” at the male bordello were the top two feeds in the world during that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frank and Maggie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: At first I thought maybe he was talking about Frank and Maggie, but he never had any dealings with them.” Katia slipped in next to me on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frank Q</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Frank Q</b> is a Crawler from Earth and the ex-husband of <a href="bword://Maggie My">Maggie My</a>. He enters the Dungeon with Maggie and their daughter <a href="bword://Yvette">Yvette</a> and ambushes <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in a safe room on the First Floor while wearing three Player Killer Skulls beside his name.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Night Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Frank Q on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Frank_Q</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Free</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Free access* to Club Vanquisher, regardless of previous and current.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Free Love</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ahead and across the room, I spied both Osvaldo—who was on the wall—and team Free Love’s van, which was right side up but still attached to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Free Love’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ahead and across the room, I spied both Osvaldo—who was on the wall—and team Free Love’s van, which was right side up but still attached to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Free Wynne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Free Wynne from his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Freelance Psychiatrist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: His class was something called a Freelance Psychiatrist.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Freight cars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The majority of the other cars were rickety, empty freight.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>French</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The little French rodent was no longer on the head of the unicorn, but submerged in the vines, her helmeted head sticking out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frenzied Gerbil</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Frenzied Gerbil</b> are a mob found on the Second Floor and Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Frenzied Gerbil on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Frenzied_Gerbil</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Friends and family</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next thing you know, you’ll be earning a third of a cent per stream on Spotify and bragging to your friends and family and fellow kitchen staff that you’re a “professional.” You have discovered a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frito</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Who are these three guys?” Frito.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frito and Moxo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * Frito and Moxo the guards came to me while I was working on my Powerful.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frogger</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Frogger</b> is an arcade action game developed by Konami and manufactured by Sega. It was released in America in September 1981. In 1982, <i>Softline</i> stated that "<i>Frogger</i> has earned the ominous distinction of being 'the arcade game with the most ways to die'."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Konami/Sega</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Frogger on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Frogger</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frogger machine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Frogger machine is described as down here.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Frost Maiden</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Frost Maiden</b> is a race a crawler can choose on the Third Floor. It negates the effects of mental infirmity and comes with the Manager benefit.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Frost Maiden on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Frost_Maiden</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fuck</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Your betrayal will never be forgotten or forgiven.” Elle, at the very end, held a sign that read, “To all the viewers of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth : Fuck you.” She flipped it over, and it was a rather.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fuck the Cheese Dicks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her hair was already ruined, and the “Fuck the Cheese Dicks” had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fuck This Floor Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Fuck This Floor</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers who descend the stairs within one hour after arriving on a floor. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, and every crawler who arrived on the Seventh Floor most likely received this same achievement and loot box. The box contains a <a href="bword://Cosmic Buff Potion">Cosmic Buff Potion</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fuck This Floor Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fuck_This_Floor_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fucking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fucking really?” Behind me, Mordecai laughed, but a moment later he added, “Whoa, that’s actually a really good prize.” It was a white pair of boxers covered in little red.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fucking cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What the fuck?” The moths all suddenly dive-bombed themselves into the candle as the Nightgaunt continued to ignore its own card deck—and the giant fucking cat—as it tried to cast another.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Full stats and skills</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Examine in the squad details tab of your interface for full stats and skills and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fumble Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Fumble Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded when you fumble at the very end.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Fumble Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Fumble_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Fun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fun for the whole family, this was the main weapon of the Gnomish Bombardier.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Funeral Bell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Funeral Bell</b> are the guards tasked with protecting the settlements of the 6th floor, they are on duty day and night. Level 95. They have a poison cloud attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Funeral Bell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Funeral_Bell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Funeral Bells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Funeral Bells is described as the guards who are tasked with protecting the smaller settlements of the sixth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Funnel city</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The orcs’ territory also had the most rivers and lakes, including the large, fast-moving river that eventually drained into Larracos itself, though the end of it curved into Operatic territory in spot number.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Funny</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Funny how that worked out.” “Everybody remember the finger-breaking trick in case she charms you,” Katia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Furthermore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Furthermore, fighting with T’Ghee totems comes with a few additional rules, which will be explained after the next recap.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Future Hunter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: ‘Future Hunter the hunter was formerly a hunter before she got punched to death by a human with no pants?’ Also, what if she wanted to be an Instagram influencer or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Future Huntress</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Last of three Orcs hunting for The <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>, she was killed by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>. Picked charisma as her highest stat because she beleived she could charm her way out of bad situations.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Wire-Haired Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Future Huntress on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Future_Huntress</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gah</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Royal Court is a fine name for our party, but as a racing team⁠—” “Gah!” I called as the entire truck flashed and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gain the ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: You needed to get to him because he had the ability to make her temporarily corporeal, which would allow your brother to touch her and gain the ability to turn into her.” “Yes.” She paused, staring off as a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gained a skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I hit level three and gained a skill called Foot Soldier, and a second one called.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gale tower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The yellow tower did not seem to fire again, but the gale tower continued to shoot, smashing into and through the building between it and myself, punching hole after hole in the wall, showering me with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Game Guide</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Worked as a Game Guide and Guildhall Instructor in the Poisoner guild before exiting the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Game Guide and Guildhall Instructor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Worked as a Game Guide and Guildhall Instructor in the Poisoner guild before exiting the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Game of Thrones</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s messaging me non-stop, talking about how we have to get married to make sure our houses are ‘bonded.’ It’s all Game of Thrones shit, and it’s freaking me.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Game show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was cheesy, 1970’s-style game show music with trumpets and keyboards and percussion music in a disco.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gamelit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: THE PAGE WHERE WE TOUT FACEBOOK GROUPS SO THEY LET US SPAM THEM ABOUT THIS BOOK Over on Facebook, if you want to talk about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gamelit Society</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I mean if you really want to talk about Gamelit, check out the Gamelit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gamori</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gamori</b> is the lead engineer of the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a> Tunnel Project and the current Matriarch of the Plenty, the caprid people who run the galaxy's tunnel network. She is also the mother of <a href="bword://Harbinger">Harbinger</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gamori on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gamori</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gangway Chock</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gangway Chock</b> is a defense <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> crafted on his <a href="bword://Engineer&#x27;s Table">Engineer's Table</a> as a gangway defense for cars on the Iron Tangle. The Gangway Chock is a massive sheet of metal fashioned out of a breastplate from the Third Floor. It's a rectangular hunk of metal with rounded edges, thinned out to about a 1/5 inch. It's "solid and heavy as shit".</li><li><b>Source:</b> Engineer's Table</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gangway Chock on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gangway_Chock</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Garage door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I know I’m supposed to do what you say and I promise to come back, but I want you to know this is my family, and I will do what it takes to protect them.” Finley walked right out of the open garage door.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Garfield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m not so sure they were using… official Garfield sources for their AI.” The movie finally ended, and the children reluctantly filed out of the space and back into the real.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Garret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Garret</b> is a <a href="bword://Tummy Acher">Tummy Acher</a> pet that originally belonged to the crawler <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a> (nicknamed "Ren" by her fellow crawlers, since nobody could pronounce her name), who received him as a gift for attending Crawl Con.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Rend, Garret, Bam-Bam, Cousin Oliver, Tummy Acher, Belly Acher</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The World Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Tummy Acher / Belly Acher</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Garret on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Garret</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was also under the impression that it wasn’t going to build copies of existing creatures anymore since the hydra boss incident on the last floor.” “And I was under the impression that we were friends.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gash</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gash</b> is a feral Minor Demon who was banished to the Nothing. Gash and her sisters, including the now-deceased <a href="bword://Slit">Slit</a>, are members of a demon harem living in the Nothing. <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>, while banished, talked talked about her sexual exploits with her king, and the harem thought that Samantha had had sex with <i>their</i> king. Naturally, Samantha made up stories about her wild sexcapades with <i>their</i> king. Now, Gash and her sisters hate Samantha.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Minor Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gash on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gash</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gasworks Screw Factory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read, “Gasworks Screw Factory, Steel Mill, and Doll Workshop.” And under it, a smaller sign read, “There’s no cocaine in here.” Sparks flew from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: As a glass mage who specializes in the study of both transmutation and the teleportation of energized particles, Ghazi told his colleagues at the Larracos College of Magecraft that he was seeking out the Gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gate of the Feral Gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>This article is about the artifact. For the book, see The Gate of the Feral Gods (Book).</i> The <b>Gate of the Feral Gods</b> is a legendary- or celestial-tier artifact, a teleportation device so powerful it would normally not surface before the eighth or ninth floor. It bridges two distant locations by tearing a hole through the prison dimension known as the Nothing, and when the resulting portal closes, an ancient, maddened deity, a feral god, comes through into the world on the opening side.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Looted in three pieces (two watches and a winding box) from Henrik the Changeling, Commandant Kane, and the Mad Dune Mage on the Fifth Floor, within the Necropolis of Anser</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gate of the Feral Gods on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gate_of_the_Feral_Gods</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gate Pass</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gate Pass</b> is an item that allows the user to have a permission slip from the mayor <a href="bword://Magistrate Featherfall">Magistrate Featherfall</a> in the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement. The Gate Pass is one of the items <a href="bword://GumGum">GumGum</a> has on her body when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> searches it, along with the <a href="bword://Mysterious Letter">Mysterious Letter</a>. Before they head to Magistrate Featherfall's office, Carl summons a <a href="bword://Village Guard Swordsman">Village Guard Swordsman</a>, shows him the gate pass and the body of the dead hooker, to hopefully allow him to have the corpse in his possession.</li><li><b>Source:</b> GumGum's corpse</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gate Pass on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gate_Pass</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gavrilo Princip Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Gavrilo Princip Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> after they killed Empress <a href="bword://D&#x27;nadia">D'nadia</a> during Faction Wars (game). The achievement is named after Gavrilo Princip who murdered Arch Duke <a href="bword://Ferdinand">Ferdinand</a> in 1914, kicking off World War 1.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gavrilo Princip Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gavrilo_Princip_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gelded Choirboy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: A pair of happy faces danced back and forth on the screen with the words “Get Ready to Sing!” I activated the Gelded Choirboy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>General Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your platoon member, General Elle McGib has cast a Subterfuge spell, and your presence is hidden from enemy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>General Louis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, General Louis is described as really up to Tagg.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>generated NPCs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: ” All of the pre-generated NPCs blinked and disappeared, and the room was suddenly empty except for me, the four other crawlers, the cretin bodyguards, this floor’s badger bartender, and a few others, who were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Genesis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Genesis</b> is a Level 95 Lady Mantaur Dominator, one half of a pair of womantaur drivers (alongside <a href="bword://Rapture">Rapture</a>) racing for the Lady Dominators during the Tenth Floor's racing heats.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Genesis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Genesis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Genesis and Rapture</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s the spell we were going to use to capture Genesis and Rapture’s car.” Corcunda.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gennrik</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gennrik</b> is a <a href="bword://Saccathian">Saccathian</a> Bounty Hunter. Gennrik comes from a family of bounty hunters; he claims that he and his family enjoy the work. He saves his earnings to buy into the <a href="bword://Prism Kingdom">Prism Kingdom</a>'s Ninth Floor Faction Wars army. <a href="bword://Brad">Brad</a> describes him as a "tall, octopus-faced dude."</li><li><b>Race:</b> Saccathian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gennrik on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gennrik</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Geraldo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Geraldo</b> is a <a href="bword://Monk Seal">Monk Seal</a> Picket Sentry, an aquatic, melee tank mob and a Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> on the Eighth Floor. This card is held by the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Eighth Floor Cuba</li><li><b>Race:</b> Monk Seal</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Geraldo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Geraldo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>German</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was more high-pitched, and I suddenly had a German accent for no goddamned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>German Shepherd</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: With her tech-based shield, she can chain them back and forth.” The next box was the same Platinum Tserendolgor box I had, and it was shaped like a jeweled, sitting, life-sized German Shepherd with an army.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Get Out of Jail</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A spell that can cure the stunned condition.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Get Out of Jail on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Get_Out_of_Jail</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Geyrun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Geyrun</b> was <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>'s favorite child and the original owner of <a href="bword://Orthrus">Orthrus</a>, but was killed by an unknown assailant. Emberus suspects his brother <a href="bword://Hellik">Hellik</a> is the murderer, and assigns <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> the Find Out Who Killed My Son Quest and Kill Hellik Quest.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Geyrun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Geyrun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ghazi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Known by most everyone in the area as the Mad Dune Mage, Ghazi came to the area for the same reason as the bugbear captain, <a href="bword://Shamus Chaindrive">Shamus Chaindrive</a>: to seek out the hidden treasure that is said to be buried within the Necropolis of <a href="bword://Anser">Anser</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Mad Dune Mage</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> book 4, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ghazi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ghazi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ghee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you plant the flag within the body of a living entity while they are at less than 5% health, they will be transformed into something called a T’Ghee totem, which will be added to the squad leader’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ghommid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ghommid</b> are a catch-all race that describes several different types of ghost creatures. They are found on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ghommid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ghommid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It took just over 30 seconds for the information to seep in, so they weren’t suited for fast-moving combat situations, but it was how Miriam Dom and Prepotente would know exactly how to slowly kill the giant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The giant creature had a strange symbol over its head that I’d missed because it was so.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It had the tall ears, but the face was wider, reminding me of that giant demon Slit we fought all the way back on the fifth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant dog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Stay away from my girl, and we won’t have any troubles.” “Carl, what is he talking about?” The other boss had fallen to the side as the giant dog and bear ripped into its.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant monster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The giant monster would appear, quickly assess the situation, and then skitter across the battlefield to dispatch any opponents, usually in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The remaining cars, the ones that had been filled with the giant monsters, were still on the tracks and pulling behind the train, but the walls had all been peeled away and broken, turning the cars into a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Giant room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’ll be at the ceiling in a second, and we’ll be fully upside down!” As everyone scrambled to find footing, now standing on the overturned counter, waiting for us to turn completely upside down, I finally.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gideon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Fought in Iron Tangle. Active in chat. Allergic to dogs.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 34, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gideon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gideon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gig Harbor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: ABOUT THE AUTHOR (This is where we switch to third person and pretend like Matt isn’t writing this himself.) Matt Dinniman is a writer and artist from Gig Harbor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gilmore Girls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was one of the most pivotal moments in the history of television, Carl.” “I don’t think the aliens will care about something that happened on Gilmore Girls .” “Oh my god.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gimli</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gimli</b> was a <a href="bword://Stone Hawk">Stone Hawk</a>, and pet to <a href="bword://Osvaldo">Osvaldo</a> of the <a href="bword://Flamengo">Flamengo</a> party. A Stone Hawk, Gimli appeared to be a regular brown hawk but his underwing feathers were opalescent. He was trained to sit on Osvaldo's shoulder, and was capable of turning targets into stone.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Stone Hawk</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 69</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gimli on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gimli</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gimp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Second Place: Lady Dominators and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Girth the Trouble</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Team six, Girth the Trouble has been eliminated due to the destruction of their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Give</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Give them an hour and the whole family will be out there.” Across the room, Donut discovered the fireplace, which crackled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glamoured Fragment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Glamoured Fragment</b> are q piece of a deceased corpes that has been animated by <a href="bword://Big Tina">Big Tina</a>. They will turn into a Glamoured Creeper when they manage to get 70% of their body back.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Glamoured Fragment on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Glamoured_Fragment</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glass Cannon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Glass Cannon normally forbids you from adding to your constitution upon level-up, but it comes with a plus fifteen constitution base, and it’ll also greatly increase your training speed on all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glass door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ram guy guarding the stained-glass door entrance to Club Vanquisher was a different one than the last time I’d tried to enter the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glass floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was just a straight chamber with a glass floor and ceilings made of a strange, almost-rusted-looking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glass Prison Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Glass Prison Spell</b> is a spell cast by <a href="bword://Sadie">Sadie</a>, one of <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a>'s <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a>, that immobilizes a target. The only confirmed effect of Glass Prison is that it immobilizes, or "imprisons," its target long enough for Sadie's group to get past it. No other mechanical detail, such as mana cost, range, or duration, is given in the text.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Glass Prison Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Glass_Prison_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gleener</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gleener</b> are an aquatic <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race. They look like a human sized <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a>. The ship <a href="bword://Escape Velocity">Escape Velocity</a> went through a wormhole to find the first system where <b>Gleener</b> scientists were studying a <a href="bword://Primal">Primal</a> ship graveyard with the Vog Generation Ship. The Syndicate formed a few hundred cycles after that.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gleener on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gleener</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gloom Wraith</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Now, let’s take a look at Carl’s, uh, very different Celestial item, which is now his third, behind the Scavenger’s Daughter patch we were just discussing and the potion that gave him his Gloom Wraith Phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gloom Wraith Phase</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Now, let’s take a look at Carl’s, uh, very different Celestial item, which is now his third, behind the Scavenger’s Daughter patch we were just discussing and the potion that gave him his Gloom Wraith Phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gloria Estefan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Each ad or catalog featured stacks and stacks of today’s hottest music, from Hall and Oates to ABBA to my personal favorite, Gloria.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Prime Minister Glory</b> is a <a href="bword://Gleener">Gleener</a> responsible for the <a href="bword://Syndicate Council">Syndicate Council</a> and the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> as a whole. Prime Minister Glory is known to be stoic, but is slowly cracking as the Ninth Floor Faction Wars draw near and the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> grows increasingly unstable.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Syndicate Space</li><li><b>Race:</b> Gleener</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Glory on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Glory</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glory Bound</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Glory Bound</b> is a highly dangerous and addictive drug available in the Dungeon. Like <a href="bword://Blitz Sticks">Blitz Sticks</a> but stronger, Glory Bound puts you in a coma and allows you to relive a memory.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Marketplaces</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Glory Bound on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Glory_Bound</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Instead of getting rid of one of our existing cards, she’d instead used the consumable Combo card to combine two of the problem cards, used up all the utility buffs on them, and then used the consumable.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Glurp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We were going to show what these crazy assholes did to get free, but since they want to suck on it so bad, we’re going to bring them on early.” “Glurp.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gluteus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Metcalf,” I bellowed as I pulled myself into a standing position, “shout when the invulnerability is about to run out!” “And play some fucking music!” Gluteus.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gluteus Maxx</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gluteus Maxx</b> is an NPC stripper from the Penis Parade who serves as a mercenary and ally to the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> and the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unspecified (short, hairy muscle man)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 6 (mentioned); Book 6, Chapter 52 (in person)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gluteus Maxx on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gluteus_Maxx</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnoll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gnoll</b> are a race that are typically known for acting as police forces or for riot control. Shade Gnolls are a type of creature that looks similar to a hyenas.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gnoll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gnoll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnoll Riot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnoll Riot Forces</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnomish Drop Bear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gnomish Drop Bears</b> are open-air biplanes created by Gnomes. They are used by the Dirigible Gnomes in Fifth Floor Bubble #543. Gnomish Drop Bears look like open-air, twin-engine biplanes, with each engine nestled between the wings, and look like they were built out of scrap metal. The planes may be decorated with paint.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dirigible Gnomes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gnomish Drop Bear on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gnomish_Drop_Bear</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnomish Knock-Knock</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gnomish Knock-Knocks</b> are refrigerator-sized bombs created by Gnomes and capable of leveling cities. In the Air Quadrant of Fifth Floor Bubble #543, a number of these dangle from the underbelly of Gnomish airships.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fifth Floor Bubble #543</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gnomish Knock-Knock on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gnomish_Knock-Knock</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gnomish Legate Balloon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gnomish Legate Balloon</b> is a vehicle built by gnomes a long time ago, to bring peace to their world.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gnomish Legate Balloon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gnomish_Legate_Balloon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Go Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: A crowd had circled around them, and they were chanting, “Go.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goat creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: And standing in the corner on two cloven hooves was a massive, hairy goat creature with a line of red, bug-like eyes down its evil.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goat dragon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “ Again ?” “Bianca,” Prepotente called as he pulled the bloody goat dragon into a pet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goat lady</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They also showed the goat lady that Mordecai had told us about a while.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Boxes</b> are a type of Loot Box awarded for using explosives, and generally contain more explosives. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received these on the first and second floors, and received similar loot in unspecified boxes on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Pass Tattoo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Goblin Pass</b> is a goblin skull tattoo that marks the bearer as a friend of the goblins. It is safe to assume that any crawler might receive this tattoo the first time they cause an explosion worthy of a <a href="bword://Goblin Box">Goblin Box</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Goblin Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Pass Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Pass_Tattoo</p></div></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Bomb Bard</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Bomb Bard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Bomb_Bard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblins</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblins</b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblins on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblins</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Boxes</b> are a type of Loot Box awarded for using explosives, and generally contain more explosives. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received these on the first and second floors, and received similar loot in unspecified boxes on the Third Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Copper Chopper</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Goblin Copper Chopper</b> is a Goblin-made motorcycle running on a steam engine. It has a sidecar fit for a princess. The framework is seemingly welded from mismatched copper scrap, and a thumping steam engine sits in the middle of the chassis. The seat appears to be an alligator skull with white fur on top. The sturdy black wheels are made of some unknown material, more disc than tire.</li><li><b>Source:</b> First Floor Goblins</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Copper Chopper on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Copper_Chopper</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Dynamite</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Dynamite</b> is an extremely volatile explosive created by <a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a>. The red stick is wet and sticky, and the long wick hisses and pops like a sparkler when lit. The wick must be physically lit using a lighter or mechanism.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblins, Goblin Box, Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Dynamite on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Dynamite</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Engineer</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Engineer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Engineer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblins</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblins</b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblins on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblins</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Murder Dozer</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Murder Dozer</b> is a Goblin-made coal-powered contraption found on the First Floor. The tractor-sized Murder Dozer is haphazardly constructed of rusting hunks of metal, runs on tank-like treads, and has several pipes billowing out black smoke. A spinning wheel covered in spikes whirrs on the front of the machine, and there's a small ladder on the back. It sounds like a steam engine and has a wide turn radius. The top of the machine is a fur-lined recessed hole with benches enough to seat fifteen goblins.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblins</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Murder Dozer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Murder_Dozer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Oil</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Oil</b> is a flammable liquid kept in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s inventory, utilized as a crafting material to create improvised explosives. Goblin Oil is a liquid combustible that can be mixed with other flammable substances to create highly effective incendiary weapons.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblins (Assumed/Looted)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Oil on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Oil</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Pass Tattoo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Goblin Pass</b> is a goblin skull tattoo that marks the bearer as a friend of the goblins. It is safe to assume that any crawler might receive this tattoo the first time they cause an explosion worthy of a <a href="bword://Goblin Box">Goblin Box</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Goblin Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Pass Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Pass_Tattoo</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Shamanka</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Shamanka on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Shamanka</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblins</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblins</b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblins on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblins</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Smoke Bomb</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblin Smoke Bombs</b> are apple-sized spherical bombs with wicks. After the wick is lit and the fire ignites the bomb, they emit a cloud of stinking, multi-color, opaque smoke that covers a 10 meter radius for three minutes (180 seconds) before dispersing.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes; Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Smoke Bomb on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Smoke_Bomb</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin smoke bombs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pustule had also brought a ten pack of the hobgoblin smoke curtains, which were round, fused bombs that would supposedly work much better than the goblin smoke.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblins</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goblins</b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblins on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblins</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Bomb Bard</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Bomb Bard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Bomb_Bard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Engineer</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Engineer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Engineer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goblin Shamanka</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a></b> are a short, green, highly intelligent, and aggressive race of mobs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> found on the early floors of the World Dungeon. They are among the first enemies <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> encounters on the first floor. What they lack in physical strength, they make up for with mechanical ingenuity, clan loyalty, and a volatile love for high explosives.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goblin Shamanka on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goblin_Shamanka</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>God</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “God, I hope not.” The Legendary Spicy Box was shaped like a giant strawberry that made a sizzling noise when it opened for some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>God Ogun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Race: Human Class: Santero This crawler worships the God.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddamn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Goddamn it, there’s nowhere to sit down and have a breakdown in here.” We all just looked at each other and started to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddamned cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a bit ridiculous that she was asking these questions of a goddamned cat, but Donut really warmed to the conversation, and Donut’s answers were strangely.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddamnit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We will ask the AI to fix that.” There was a ping, and the Mexx robot said from the corner of the room, “The AI indicates that it loves a good loophole, and it is unable to close it unless someone makes an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddamnit Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: You can kill them when you kill her!” “ Goddamnit Donut ,” I cried as a second cockroach bit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddess of Chaos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even Eris, Goddess of Chaos, doesn’t want anything to do with these crazy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddess of Chaos Eris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Goddess of Chaos.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goddess Ysalte</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: A Memorial Crystal for the Goddess Ysalte has been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gods</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gods</b>, also known as <b>Deities</b>, are immensely powerful beings that reside within the Dungeon, many of them drawn from real-world mythologies. Despite their name, they are not truly divine: many are controlled by wealthy <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> sponsors who have paid for the privilege of wielding their power.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gods on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gods</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gods and demons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She had a passive ability called Cosmic Sense , which appeared to be like my skill that warned me when a god was present, but hers worked on both gods and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gods and goddesses</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Not only were we now certain that Rosetta’s theory was correct, but we were now pretty sure, out of the hundreds of gods and goddesses, which specific god this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Godsdamnit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Godsdamnit,” Princess Formidable shouted, throwing her communicator down against the dash as she fell back into her.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Paulie">Paulie</a></b> is the preferred name of <b>Goff</b>, and is a human occupied by a <a href="bword://Residual">Residual</a>. In the months before the transformation, he lived at the Shepherd's House, a homeless shelter near Miami, Florida.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Goff; Paulie</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Miami, FL</li><li><b>Race:</b> Residual</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 49</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goff on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goff</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paulie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Paulie</b> is the preferred name of <b><a href="bword://Goff">Goff</a></b>, and is a human occupied by a <a href="bword://Residual">Residual</a>. In the months before the transformation, he lived at the Shepherd's House, a homeless shelter near Miami, Florida.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Goff; Paulie</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Miami, FL</li><li><b>Race:</b> Residual</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 49</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Paulie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Paulie</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Going</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl: Going to finish our upgrades, use the key, and pick up some things from Mordecai, and we’ll be out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goiter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Goiter</b> is a <a href="bword://Bugbear">Bugbear</a> NPC on the Sixth Floor.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goiter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goiter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I… I think the AI just gave you photographs of the mothers of all the plaintiffs in that lawsuit.” “Why?” He didn’t answer as the Gold I’m Wet box opened, revealing a prize I wished I’d had all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Apparel Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gold Apparel Box (8/10) Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Nobody else in the party received the Indomitable achievement, though Katia had received a similar one for surviving a god’s magic attack, but it was only a gold.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Edition</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Lord of Blight – Gold.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Junkie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She also had a Hunter-Killer box to open, but she wanted me to open mine first since I had thirty-four of them, and she thought it would be more “dramatic.” The first box was a Gold Junkie’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Looter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’d probably work on Mongo.” Throughout the day, I’d received a handful of achievements, but I’d only been awarded one prize from a Gold Looter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Makeup Sex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Gold Makeup Sex is described as the Best Sex.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Payload Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Gold Payload Box came in the form of a small WWII-style gravity.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Pet Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Gold Pet Box contained several pet biscuits and a coupon for “500 gold off any one item from any PetScapes location in the dungeon.” I had never seen nor heard of a store called.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : In addition to the Gold Quest Box you’ve received for completing the quest, new quest locations will sometimes appear on your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Quest Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : In addition to the Gold Quest Box you’ve received for completing the quest, new quest locations will sometimes appear on your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Shoe Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gold Shoe Box (9/10) Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Spicy Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before I could examine the tattoo’s properties, the Gold Spicy Box.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gold Talk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Gold Talk of the Town box was shaped like an opened newspaper on a pedestal, and it opened up, revealing a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Golden</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: For our Golden upgrade, we went with Roller Limbo, a trans-dimensional upgrade that would allow us to race upside.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Golden Combo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: And we’re not taking that Golden Combo card, either.” “What did that crazy spider lady do to you, anyway?” The tattoo on my chest was invisible because the eye was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Golden Girls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your team’s castle landed right in the middle of the Lemig Sortion’s battlefield, and it splattered half their army.” “No, number six is Golden Girls.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Golden Throat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai also wanted to find an item with something called a Golden Throat enchantment, which was apparently the dungeon’s version of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gondii</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gondii</b> are an alien race of a parasitic worms who occupy and maintain dead bodies. The Gondii are the citizens and controlling agents of the Valtay corporate government.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gondii on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gondii</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gone up a level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d gained one point to constitution, two trophies, and I’d also gone up a level to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gone up to level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “How much do you weigh now?” He'd gone up to level 10, and he was about as tall as my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gonk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d brought Porky with him, and he was showing him Gonk the swamp yak, who was in her pen, happily munching away at something, while Simoom the rhino snorted beside.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gonk and Simoom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rend and Mongo were out in the pet stable “playing” with Gonk and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gonna</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The platinum Here’s-Some-Good-Shit-Because-You’re-Gonna-Need-It-Box also contained the same thing Donut had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goo-Inator 3000</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Goo-Inator 3000</b> can be used at an <a href="bword://Engineer&#x27;s Table">Engineer's Table</a> to reshape existing materials. It looks like "a silver, oversized lollipop with a button on the handle."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goo-Inator 3000 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goo-Inator_3000</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gooble Gobble</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gooble Gobble, Gooble.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Mana Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Good Mana Potions</b> (AKA <i>Good Mana Refills</i>) are potions that slowly add 200 Mana Points over the space of two minutes, at a rate of approximately 1.67 MP per second.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Adventurer Box (Floor 8)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Good Mana Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Good_Mana_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Shit Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Platinum Here's-Some-Good-Shit-Because-You're-Gonna-Need-It-Box</b> (sometimes referred to simply as the <b>Platinum good-shit box</b>) is a special Platinum-tier loot box awarded to crawlers who quickly abandon a dungeon floor without leveling up.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement Reward (Fuck This Floor)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Good Shit Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Good_Shit_Box</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good First Impression</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imbues wearer with +5 Intelligence, grants the user +5 to the Good First Impression.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Healing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled another Invisibility potion, a potion ball with another Invisibility potion in it for Mongo, and a handful of Good Healing potions, and I dropped them on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Mana Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Good Mana Potions</b> (AKA <i>Good Mana Refills</i>) are potions that slowly add 200 Mana Points over the space of two minutes, at a rate of approximately 1.67 MP per second.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Adventurer Box (Floor 8)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Good Mana Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Good_Mana_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Rest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It refreshed us after sixty seconds of “sleep.” But it also changed our Good Rest bonus to Great Rest, and it imparted a whopping 15% bonus to our stats for the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Good Shit Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Platinum Here's-Some-Good-Shit-Because-You're-Gonna-Need-It-Box</b> (sometimes referred to simply as the <b>Platinum good-shit box</b>) is a special Platinum-tier loot box awarded to crawlers who quickly abandon a dungeon floor without leveling up.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement Reward (Fuck This Floor)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Good Shit Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Good_Shit_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goodbye</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Goodbye everyone!” Donut called, turning in a circle, tears in her eyes as flowers and streamers fell around.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goodness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Goodness, it smells just awful.” The enormously fat creature rose out of the water like a dolphin doing a trick, only this was clearly in defiance of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gordo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s not right, taking advantage of me kind nature like that.” “This is an outrage!” Donut said as Gordo disappeared into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I clambered to my feet as Gore-Gore, dazed, stood all the way up to his full height on the track between the cars, as if he was standing underneath an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gore Golem Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gore Golem</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Beyond its mana cost, nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gore Golem Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gore_Golem_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gore-Gore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gore-Gore</b> is a neighborhood boss and Mantaur train engineer on the Ochre Line of the Fourth Floor Iron Tangle. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> describes Gore-Gore:</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantaur</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 11</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gore-Gore on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gore-Gore</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gorgon Marital</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled my tools, like the Goo-inator 3000, which was a “shaping” tool and the Gorgon Marital Aid, which was a tool that added plasticity to rigid items and placed them on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gorgon Marital Aid</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Gorgon Marital Aid</b> is a crafting tool that allows users to manipulate, fuse, and shape rigid materials. The Gorgon Marital Aid is shaped like a spatula.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Mechanic's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gorgon Marital Aid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gorgon_Marital_Aid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gossip Girl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: That reminds me, Zev and I have been talking about writing a new episode of Gossip Girl since…” And that’s when Mongo screamed in rage and rushed straight into the dark.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gotta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Gotta know which ones to trust and which ones are gonna blow ye off the road the moment the race starts.” “We don’t trust anyone, Hedy,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “This stupid thing is too slow!” “Cast Twinkle Toes !” “Grab his head!” Rosetta called as we rushed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grace Bautista 3</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grace <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a></b> is Daniel Bautista's sister. She was killed by a Circus Lemur in the Overcity. The pile of corpses that includes Grace Bautista includes two <a href="bword://Human">Human</a> corpses, and the corpse of a large, humanoid, tree-like race. The humans appear to be "Asian, perhaps Filipino". Grace is implied to be the tree-like corpse. The body is humanoid, but badly mishapen ("closer to an Ent from Lord of the Rings than Groot from Guardians of the Galaxy”), with her facial features resembling a human but her back is hunched, with an extra set of limbs emerging from her back.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Large, tree-like creature</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grace Bautista 3 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grace_Bautista_3</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gracie</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dr. Gracie</b> was an original member of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party. Before the transformation, Dr. Gracie was an MMMA fighter and a doctor. She was working night shift at the Meadow Lark Elder Care Facility in Wenatchee, WA, when <a href="bword://Agatha">Agatha</a> set fire to the kitchen and forced the facility to evacuate. Dr. Gracie entered the dungeon with a number of staff members, emergency responders, and elderly patients.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gracie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gracie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grand Canyon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: This wasn’t really as big as the Grand Canyon, but I was surprised at how shaken I was by the sudden.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grand Champion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grand Champion Best</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is the greatest thing that has ever happened since I won Grand Champion Best in Show last year in Cleveland.” Mongo continued to sniff at the imposter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grand Champion Best in Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a new menu and everything!” Mongo the Mongoliensis Level One (Pet of Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut) has joined the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grandma Sticky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grandma Sticky</b> is a goblin dancer at Bitches, a club inside the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, and the mother of <a href="bword://Snail Trail">Snail Trail</a> and mother-in-law of <a href="bword://Splash Zone">Splash Zone</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grandma Sticky on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grandma_Sticky</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grapple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grapple</b>s are a slave race of quarter giants, bred by the High Elves to serve as servants and laborers, commonly seen working menial jobs such as train porters throughout the Dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grapple on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grapple</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poison Grapple Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Poison <a href="bword://Grapple">Grapple</a></b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Poison Grapple Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Poison_Grapple_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Graupel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Graupel</b></i> is an expensive war spell that generates a powerful ice storm. It should only be cast outdoors. <a href="bword://Elle McGibbons">Elle McGibbons</a> receives a Tome of <i><b>Graupel</b></i> in a Loot Box after helping <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> fight against <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a> (piloted by the <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a>) on the Fourth Floor. She uses the spell for the first time to crash the air castle in her Fifth Floor Bubble.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Graupel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Graupel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gravo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We only had robot Donut for a few days, but I taught her several tricks.” Gravo looked irritated and was about to pause the taping so I could get back on script, but Professor Liddi rolled with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gravy Boat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: His name is Gravy Boat, but he used to come to the window and try to get in when Princess was in heat.” She stuck her face dangerously close to the cat’s “You wanted to fuck my girl, didn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grease Monkey Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grease Monkey!</b> is an achievement which, similar to <a href="bword://Battlefield Construction Achievement">Battlefield Construction Achievement</a>, is awarded to crawlers who build a wheeled contraption and use it in combat.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grease Monkey Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grease_Monkey_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Greased Lightning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Great Consensus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That was our Great Consensus, engineered by Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Great Race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “How do you know all this?” “This level is called the Great Race which also happens to be the name of a well-known fairytale popular in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Great Rusty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Don’t stop for anything.” “It did that voting thing for Great Rusty,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Greek</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Skylar is about to time out.” Skylar the donkey head with wings was on the other side of the battlefield flying in circles around the minotaur, who was screaming at her in Greek and swiping up with his giant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Green</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Green, lichen-like growths glowed on the brick walls and ceilings, giving the tunnel an odd.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Green Jiggly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m going to call you, I don’t know, Green Jiggly.” Donut paused then.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Green room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: In fact, I realized, this was the exact same model, though the green room on this one had a different layout and included a large picture window that looked about the dark.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Green Sultan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia pulled Eva’s old sword, the Left Fang of the Green Sultan, the one she’d dropped on the train all those floors ago, and used it cut the hand free from her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Greeting cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gremlins</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gremlins</b> are a small, goblin-shaped race that appear throughout the dungeon in wildly different forms, from savage, rapidly-breeding monsters to skilled mechanics and engineers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gremlins on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gremlins</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Griffin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Especially when Griffin told her that half the former crawlers were getting ink of their own unit emblems, though most were getting them in less obvious places than their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Griffin and Morales</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The debuff had finally gotten Griffin and Morales to shut up, and they marched sullenly behind us, their heads low as Jamal rolled toward them, looking for someone else to talk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grigori</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew Louis, Imani, and Zhang—who was riding behind Bodi—were all on a track called the “Infected Gravity Well.” Florin was on a track called “There’s Something Wrong with the Zoo.” Behind us, Dong, Porky.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grigori The Placid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grigori the Placid</b> is a Level 93 human Fleshmancer, a mercenary hired during the Tenth Floor's racing arc who later becomes the unwilling mouthpiece for the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> itself during the finale's Parade of Horribles on the 11th floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grigori The Placid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grigori_The_Placid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grimaldi</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Ringmaster Grimaldi is a level 85 City Boss Elite that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets on the third floor. Redstone Grimaldi was originally a dwarf and husband to <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a>. He was the ringmaster of the 'great and wonderful Grimaldi's Traveling Circus' before he was transformed to a <a href="bword://Pestiferous Vine">Pestiferous Vine</a> by the poison cloud of the cataclysm.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Redstone Grimaldi</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dwarf | Pestiferous Vine</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grimaldi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grimaldi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grimaldi’s Traveling</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before the cataclysm, if you asked any child of the sprawling Over City what their favorite activity was, a good number of them would happily tell you of the great and wonderful Grimaldi’s Traveling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grimaldi’s Traveling Circus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before the cataclysm, if you asked any child of the sprawling Over City what their favorite activity was, a good number of them would happily tell you of the great and wonderful Grimaldi’s Traveling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grixist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grixist</b> is a race. Grixist are bipedal bug-like aliens, about five feet tall, with a long thin neck. They have four arms with three fingers on each hand. They are covered in coarse blue black hair and six black eyes arranged in a v-formation on their face.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grixist on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grixist</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grixist Swarm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sponsored by Huanxin Jinx of the Grixist Swarm, CEO of Icon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gross</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s a new tab called “Gross shit.” My mind started to race with the implications and possibilities of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gross Atomizers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gross Atomizers</b> are a mob that can be found at station 271 of the Camel Line on the Iron Tangle. They look like a floating bag of gas with a Poison area of effect attack cloud area attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gross Atomizers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gross_Atomizers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ground level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Through the smoke and flashing lights and mist, I sensed more than saw that we were falling below the ground level, falling into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grounds level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were trying to drain it before this floor ended, but it doesn’t look like it’s going to happen.” “Those guys on the Hunting Grounds level aren’t getting kicked out because the blood bar thing?” “Yeah, I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Group of crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Destruction team was another group of crawlers from China I didn’t know very well mixed with that Hawaiian guy with the really long name who went by Makana and his partner, the hexcrafter paladin crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Group of items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d received a group of items in trade for the homeless man’s dirty clothes and a few Valtay.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>group of NPCs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t stay long at the station, which was too small already to house the group of NPCs gathered there, but as we passed through, I noted Madison sitting in the corner glaring at us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Group Quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your party has been designated Host of this Group.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Growler Gary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Growler Gary</b> is the gnoll bartender at the Downward Dog, a safe room bar at Station 75 on the Iron Tangle. Growler Gary is very concerned with being perceived as a coward. When the ghouls come through the station and kill everyone outside his bar, Gary tries to leave to help but is stopped due to his NPC key-holder status, making him effectively immortal. Everytime he comes back to life he is sober, despite his excessive drinking.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Shade Gnoll</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 25, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Growler Gary on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Growler_Gary</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Growler Gary’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The AI, in possession of Growler Gary’s body, had offered the orc princess Formidable “Backstage at the Pineapple Cabaret” as one of the location options for her banishment after she’d tried to trigger the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grrl Power Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Grrl Power Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed to <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a> for being the first woman in the dungeon to kill a mob using a human male corpse. Hekla receives the <a href="bword://Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers">Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers</a> from this box on the First Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Killing a mob using a human male corpse</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grrl Power Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grrl_Power_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grulke</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grulke</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. A five foot tall, warty slimy toad person. Able to leap great distances and deliver devastating attacks with their tongue.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grulke on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grulke</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grulke Infantry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: All it said was “Grulke.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Grull</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Grull</b> is a war god worshipped by equine-class mobs throughout the Dungeon. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> names horses, centaurs, and tikbalangs as likely adherents of Grull, and notes that llama-type creatures pray to him as well, though not until after the Third Floor. His worshippers smear themselves in blue makeup and talk incessantly about wenches and dying gloriously in battle.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> War God Grull, the Exalted Grull</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Celestial Ascendency</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 25</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Grull on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Grull</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Guards!” The two swordsmen at the base of the stairs clunked their way up toward.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gucci the Maltese</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of them I saw was the giant Imani, tightly clutching Gucci the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guided missiles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Of the eight guided missiles, I’d already assigned four of them to the few Knock-Knocks I’d spied on the underside right after.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guild of Suffering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Guild of Suffering</b> is a Dungeon cult that worships a demigod, the Night Wyrm. Guild access is granted by the Night Wyrm's Nasty Little Web of Suffering tattoo, and awarded to anyone who uses a Divine Suffering artifact. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> notes that the guild also has outreach centers. Members can accept assassination quests, which are written in a strange language on rigid flaps of dried skin with pulsing desiccated veins.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Guild of Suffering on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Guild_of_Suffering</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guild room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Guild room is described as significantly larger than I had been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guild system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ve been chasing a fast travel system since the whole guild system opened.” Li Jun : This is just for you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>GumGum</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>GumGum</b> is a level 5 Orc living in the Overcity. She asked <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> for help with the Sex Workers Who Fell from the Heavens Quest. GumGum is described as a older muscular orcish woman, similar to <a href="bword://Agatha">Agatha</a> in appearance. Her skin color is different from <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a>. She is wearing a medieval-style shirt covered in blood.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: GumGum on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/GumGum</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gunnery Sergeant X2</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gunnery Sergeant X2</b> is Warlord <a href="bword://Fang">Fang</a>’s personal defense deployment. Equipped with heavy guns, shields, and armor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Mr. Jake Tortokai</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Reavers</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cyborg</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 31</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gunnery Sergeant X2 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gunnery_Sergeant_X2</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gunpowder</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gunpowder</b> (also called <b>black powder</b> or, if you're a goblin, "<b>funpowder</b>") is a granulated black powder that was widely used as a propellant in firearms before it was replaced by smokeless powder. First used in Earth warfare in 904 AD, gunpowder is a low explosive, meaning it deflagrates (burns quickly) rather than detonates, and has a generally low brisance (shattering capability). As a result, it is good at throwing metal but not at breaking rock or fortifications.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblin Box; Goblins</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gunpowder on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gunpowder</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are too.” “Call him anyway.” “Gus,” Lucia shouted, her voice surprisingly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gustavo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few hundred meters away, Gustavo 3 sat on the ground, oblivious, happily gnawing on Samantha’s head while she wailed that she was going to kill Gustavo’s bitch of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gustavo 3</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gustavo 3</b> is a rottweiler, and one of <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a>'s dungeon familiars. Gustavo entered the Dungeon in Ecuador with Lucia Mar and another rottweiler, <a href="bword://Cici">Cici</a>. The three feature heavily on the second episode of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> sixty hours later, which shows the young teenager already kitted out with a full set of enchanted armor, Cici equipped with a chainmail sweater, and Gustavo 3 shooting lightning when he barks.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Ecuador</li><li><b>Race:</b> Rottweiler</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gustavo 3 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gustavo_3</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gutter Bumper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Gutter Bumper spell wasn’t quite what I was tirelessly seeking for explosions, but in this case, it was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Guys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Guys, what the hell is this?” To Sydnee’s left, the screen that was nothing more than a pretend window normally showing a comforting swamp had switched to mimic the star.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gwen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Sorry we can’t wine and dine you first, bomber boy, but we need to get trucking on this bullshit your friend is making us do,” Gwen said as we marched toward the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gwen and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwen and Katia had spent days fighting these mobs together while Donut and I were in our time.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gwen and Tran</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwen and Tran and their team had ventured into the now-dry subterranean level and managed to loot the remains of the tomb raiders and unearth the tomb of Anser, which was just a sarcophagus filled with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gwendolyn Duet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Gwendolyn Duet</b> is a crawler in the same bubble as <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in Book 4. Of Canadian Inuit heritage. She has access to <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>. She's armed with a spear and wears chainmail. She has multiple tattoos on her face and hands.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Canada</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Gwendolyn Duet on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Gwendolyn_Duet</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Gwen’s team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Down on the land quadrant, Gwen’s team had finally managed to breach all the walls, but now they faced the main entrance to the castle, which was magically.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>H'lene</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>H'lene</b> are an extinct race and part of the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>'s early history. Early in Syndicate history, the H'lene built six waystations in the H'lene system, near the center of the original "worm paths" (wormhole routes used for traveling across the galaxy). The waystations were traps: the H'lene robbed and ate any travelers who passed through.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: H'lene on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/H%27lene</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hadji Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hadji Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for completing the quest The Show Must Go On in an unusual way. Despite not really participating in the quest, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives the reward and achievement as well.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hadji Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hadji_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hail</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Uh, steel and heavy metal!” “Hail!” the other mantaurs shouted, all extending their Wolverine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hail Mary Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hail Mary Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for his attack using the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a> on the Fourth Floor.Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Fifth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hail Mary Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hail_Mary_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hair of the Dog Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hair of the Dog</b> is an anti-alcohol potion given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> by <a href="bword://Quint">Quint</a> as part of their deal for the starter kit. Mix together a gooey weed that smells like ochera and rubbing alcohol.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quint</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hair of the Dog Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hair_of_the_Dog_Potion</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half Elf</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Half Elf</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a> chooses this as his race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Half Elf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Half_Elf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Half Splat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Potion of Half Splat</b> is a potion that prevents you from dying from falling damage but leaves you at 5% health.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Half Splat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Half_Splat</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half Elf</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Half Elf</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a> chooses this as his race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Half Elf on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Half_Elf</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half High</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tsarina Signet - Half Naiad, Half High-Elf.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half Nagini</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eva Sigrid – Half Nagini, Half-Orc – Nimblefoot Enforcer – Level 40 – 100,000 (x2).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half Naiad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tsarina Signet - Half Naiad, Half High-Elf.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Half-Djinn</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Half-Djinn</b> is a race. Charisma bonuses don't work on Djinns. <a href="bword://Quint">Quint</a>, as a former crawler, presumably chose this as his race. On their first visit to the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> buys some potion supplies from Quint.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Half-Djinn on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Half-Djinn</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Halfway across the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you built the ramp correctly, especially the part at the end, the cars would ease onto the flat surface, still accelerating by the time they were halfway across the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Halloween</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hazards: I don’t yet know what “waves of generated mobs” means, but based on our performance for this race, plus the fact Captain Kangaroo here is still going to be stuck in his Halloween costume for this one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Halls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “All I’m saying is Britney needs to get to either the Halls of the Ascendency or Sheol if she wants to get that crazy Ysalte bitch out of her.” I’d heard a little about the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Halls of Ascendency</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She has since returned to the Halls of Ascendency, where she is feared and reviled by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hamed</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hamed</b>, also known as the Night Wyrm, is an early indentured NPC He is the husband of the late <a href="bword://Astrid">Astrid</a> and the father of the strippers <a href="bword://Damascus Steel">Damascus Steel</a> and <a href="bword://Anaconda">Anaconda</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Night Wyrm</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Outside the Dungeon (Early Indentured Servant)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Demigod / Fairy (Unconfirmed)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hamed on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hamed</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hand on the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’ll learn your tastes.” I walked up to the first cabinet, put my hand on the door, and a menu popped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hand that Claws the Master Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Hand that Claws the Master</b> achievement is awarded each time a Crawler kills a "Hunter" on the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds. This achievement can stack. It is safe to assume that, for most crawlers, this <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> would only be two paragraphs long.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hand that Claws the Master Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hand_that_Claws_the_Master_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Handbasket Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Platinum “Hell in a Handbasket Box” contained a small patch depicting a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Handful of crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A handful of crawlers watched while the other NPCs went about their business, pretending like there was nothing on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Handling skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My Incendiary Device Handling skill had jumped up to five immediately upon the construction of the “device.” All I had done was open up the jug, pour a splash or two out until it was about 3/4's full, added a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I did get a cheap trucker hat that says, ‘I’d Hang a Little with Chad.’ I don’t know what that means, but it’s green and yellow and is perfect for the mood wall I’m working on.” She gasped, seeing the sign.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hanging</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hanging from the boa on the Sledge’s neck was a giant pinback button that read, “I like my sausages.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hanzo Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hanzo Spell</b> is a spell that brings mobs close to you.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hanzo Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hanzo_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hapanzi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hapanzi</b> is a god and the late husband of the Demi-God <a href="bword://Shi Maria">Shi Maria</a> (The Bedlam Bride). Hapanzi is a deity who met a tragic end at the hands of his wife, Shi Maria. The dungeon's lore unceremoniously states that his death was "pretty much his own fault," cheekily adding that one shouldn't "stick it in crazy."</li><li><b>Race:</b> God / Spider</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 26</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hapanzi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hapanzi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Harbinger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Harbinger</b> is a caprid liaison working behind the scenes in the Dungeon. He expressly disagrees with the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a>'s goals and philosophies, and is "actively working" to remove <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> from the crawl.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Caprid</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Harbinger on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Harbinger</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hard rule</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a hard rule regarding secondary production companies and the showrunners of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hardly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hardly anyone called them that until they made a big deal about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Harley Davidson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Harley Davidson is described as a road bike and wasn’t great for this, and I decided to use the chariot for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Harpocrates</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Harpocrates</b> is a deity that is a god of Silence. After <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> casts her <a href="bword://War Crimes Spell">War Crimes Spell</a> and chops <a href="bword://Meatus">Meatus</a> into pieces, Harpocrates swears revenge and leaves the realm.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Harpocrates on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Harpocrates</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Harry Potter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “This Psamathe lady is Chucky, but she’s only a head, and she can’t move.” “I was thinking it’s more like the horcruxes from Harry Potter,” Katia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Harry Tisch</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Harry Tisch is a <a href="bword://Crest">Crest</a>. He is <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a>'s husband. Harry Purchased Odette's bounty after her time in the Dungeon, which is how they met. He owns a crab ranch and is wealthy through many investments.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Harry Tisch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Harry_Tisch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hasten Poison</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A spell that increases that makes poisons deadlier.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hasten Poison on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hasten_Poison</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Havana</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d actually teleported deeper into Havana, but we were in a small, forested area surrounded by trees with the city just a few blocks.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Have Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “This won’t take long.” Carl : Have Mordecai help you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Have You Goddamned Figured Out How To Use This In Conjunction With the Fucking Voodoo Book Yet? Jesus Christ I Can Only Help You So Much box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Have You Goddamned Figured Out How To Use This In Conjunction With the Fucking Voodoo Book Yet? Jesus Christ I Can Only Help You So Much box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for using an Ability Tattoo for the first time in battle.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hey Larry, can I borrow your chainsaw? Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Have You Goddamned Figured Out How To Use This In Conjunction With the Fucking Voodoo Book Yet? Jesus Christ I Can Only Help You So Much box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Have_You_Goddamned_Figured_Out_How_To_Use_This_In_Conjunction_With_the_Fucking_Voodoo_Book_Yet%3F_Jesus_Christ_I_Can_Only_Help_You_So_Much_box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Haven’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We will be forced to cross the I-Sure-Hope-These-Piranhas-Haven’t-Learned-to-Fly River.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Haxor the Destroyer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Haxor the Destroyer</b> is a unaffiliated hunter <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> killed in the desperado club.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dreadnought</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Haxor the Destroyer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Haxor_the_Destroyer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hazards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hazards: I don’t yet know what “waves of generated mobs” means, but based on our performance for this race, plus the fact Captain Kangaroo here is still going to be stuck in his Halloween costume for this one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Head</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Head’s up,” I called as I pulled a stick of full-strength hobgoblin dynamite, and I tossed it over the edge of the trench like a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Headed creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The two-headed creature that comprised Dmitri and Maxim, a Nodling, apparently, spun their magical meteor hammer, and they would’ve brained the guy, but they suddenly all flew off their feet as a giant foot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Headlamp Colonnade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Manager of the Headlamp.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heads Remaining</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eight Heads.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Heal Critter">Heal Critter</a> Potions</b> are single-use, brown-colored potions that can be used to heal injured pets, dungeon familiars, and <i>presumably</i> animal-class mobs that are not affected by a standard <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a>. Unlike some potions that can be simply thrown at <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, Heal Critter Potions must be consumed by the target.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Pet Spell</b> is a spell that allows a crawler to heal their pet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Scroll</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Scrolls</b> are single-use items that restore a crawler's health. They do not cost mana to cast and are particularly valuable because, unlike the basic <a href="bword://Heal Spell">Heal Spell</a> or <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a>, Heal Scrolls can be used on any nearby crawler (rather than only on oneself) and have no cooldown between uses.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Survivor's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Scroll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Scroll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal</b> is the basic restoration spell every crawler is taught during the Tutorial. The user glows red while casting the spell, and immediately recovers up to 20% of their maximum Health.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Critter</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Critter</b> is a restoration spell that instantly heals a single pet-class mob or dungeon familiar. The target must be nearby, and glows when the spell is cast.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Critter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Critter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Party</b> is a magical spell that restores the health of a crawler's party members. It is a highly valued support ability within Dungeon Crawler World that can be acquired through loot tomes or divine rewards.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Party on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Party</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Heal Critter">Heal Critter</a> Potions</b> are single-use, brown-colored potions that can be used to heal injured pets, dungeon familiars, and <i>presumably</i> animal-class mobs that are not affected by a standard <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a>. Unlike some potions that can be simply thrown at <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, Heal Critter Potions must be consumed by the target.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Pet Spell</b> is a spell that allows a crawler to heal their pet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Heal Critter">Heal Critter</a> Potions</b> are single-use, brown-colored potions that can be used to heal injured pets, dungeon familiars, and <i>presumably</i> animal-class mobs that are not affected by a standard <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a>. Unlike some potions that can be simply thrown at <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, Heal Critter Potions must be consumed by the target.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Pet Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Pet Spell</b> is a spell that allows a crawler to heal their pet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Pet Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Pet_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d instinctively bashed onto my Heal spell while I was still in midair, but my health continued to plummet, so I hit a heal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Scroll</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal Scrolls</b> are single-use items that restore a crawler's health. They do not cost mana to cast and are particularly valuable because, unlike the basic <a href="bword://Heal Spell">Heal Spell</a> or <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a>, Heal Scrolls can be used on any nearby crawler (rather than only on oneself) and have no cooldown between uses.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Survivor's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Scroll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Scroll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal scrolls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The other contained three Heal scrolls, which were also useful in healing others without having to resort to pouring potions down their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heal Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Heal</b> is the basic restoration spell every crawler is taught during the Tutorial. The user glows red while casting the spell, and immediately recovers up to 20% of their maximum Health.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heal Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heal_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healers Grasp Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Healer's Grasp</b> is <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s first aura ability, automatically healing anyone within range of her. Healer's Grasp came bundled with Donut's Assassin of Sekhmet class, alongside the Smoke Form spell and buffs to her existing summon-vermin and tree-creation spells from her Oak Fell title. Donut herself finds it an odd fit for an assassin class, joking that it makes her "a mini <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Healers Grasp Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Healers_Grasp_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Healing potions are terrible at curing Bianca’s burns, and this salve works much.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Standard Healing Potion</b> (also called <b>Health <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a></b> or <b>Potion of Healing</b>) is a single-use potion given to crawlers when they learn how to use their <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a> during the tutorial. It is frequently found in low-tier <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Tutorial; Adventurer Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Healing Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Healing_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing potions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’ll take the shrink wand, the eighth floor lore book thing, that music sheet of Skedaddle , that case of 24 Feather Fall potions, and a case of healing potions.” * * * After a parade of other pets, it was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing scroll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It can be a special type of healing scroll for lava rock monsters like Chris, or it can be used as a phase potion for people to be able to easily pass through lava.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing scrolls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ll do that from now on if we have time.” We’d received a silver quest box for that fight, but it had been nothing but healing scrolls and trap.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Healing spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: There are a few other quirks regarding inventory, but you’ll figure them out along the way.” I put the potion back and then added it to the hotlist next to my healing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Health bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Health bar is described as still in the green by the time Donut had to drink a mana.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Health bars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But some of the ones in Larracos were now standing in lava as it spread unevenly, and they, too, had health bars over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Health potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The health potion along with my resistance had done a good job of keeping me there, right in the middle, halfway between life and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heart attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I thought Bomo was going to have a heart attack, but I eventually fist bumped everyone in the room, adding an additional 22 names to my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heart Balance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I already had something similar with my Heart Balance skill, which allowed me to set a potion to get automatically used when my health reached a certain.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: CONTENTS Definition Samantha’s Floor 7 Recap Interlude Horrible One Heat 1 of 7 Chapter 1 Chapter 2 Chapter 3 Chapter 4 Chapter 5 Heat 2 of 7 Chapter 6 Chapter 7 Chapter 8 Chapter 9 Chapter 10 Chapter 11.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heather</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I also received a whole box of hobgoblin dynamite along with three globs of Hobgoblin Pus—the remote-controlled explosive detonators—in the bronze boss box from my fight with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heather the Bear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Once an attraction at <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus on the Third Floor, <b>Heather the Bear</b> was transformed into a monstrous Neighborhood Boss by <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s curse.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> black bear</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heather the Bear on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heather_the_Bear</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heavy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was just past ab-solar, what the locals called “midnight.” Heavy clouds covered the sky, making it especially dark out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heavy Metal Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Heavy Metal Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the <a href="bword://Touched by a Demon Achievement">Touched by a Demon Achievement</a> when he was grabbed my <a href="bword://Amayon">Amayon</a>. The <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Heavy Metal box looks like full-sized drum kit with pyrotechnics and the face of Amayon on the drums. An ethereal demon plays the set until it explodes, with a “666” flashing in the air before disappearing.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Touched by a Demon Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Heavy Metal Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Heavy_Metal_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hedy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hedy</b> is a Gremlin mechanic available to crawlers on the Tenth Floor. She is assigned to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s garage. Hedy is one of two choices for mechanic for the Tenth Floor races. Her specialty is Vehicle Mechanic, whereas <a href="bword://Waldrip Chris">Waldrip Chris</a> is another gremlin that specializes as an Animal Trainer.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Gremlin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hedy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hedy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hedy and Jamal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 60 I left Grigori in the garage with Hedy and Jamal, and we entered the safe room to find Dong sitting on the couch, sobbing with Bigs on his head, glaring at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Heidi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Wait for my or Heidi’s command.” “Jamal is quite excited to battle with his new upgrades,” the hammerhead shark said, taking my attention as more shields crackled into existence around.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hekla</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hekla</b> is a popular Earth Crawler from Iceland and the leader of <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a>, an all-female crawler party. Practical, cunning, and lacking <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s sense of justice, she became one of the crawl's most prominent and dangerous competitors.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Reykjavik, Iceland</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Amazonian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hekla on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hekla</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hekla and Eva</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was hard to see when it was so small, but I was pretty sure it depicted Hekla and Eva’s faces, side-by-side with Xs over their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hell, they can stab the priest and murder all the town’s adherents and put the whole thing up on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell Comes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell Comes to Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell Comes to Crawler Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have to have me do it.” The Legendary “Hell Comes to Crawler Town” box contained, like promised, a set of unenchanted bagpipes and a group of three, third-tier personal space upgrade coupons plus a fourth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell In A Handbasket Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hell In A Handbasket Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for looking at <a href="bword://Amayon">Amayon</a> at the end of the Eighth Floor. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and (presumably) the whole group who confronted <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a> received a Platinum version of this box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Looking at Amayon</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hell In A Handbasket Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hell_In_A_Handbasket_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell Scrub</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Hell Scrub made a clicking noise and started floating toward Frank, who pulled a red sword from over his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell-Kissed Celestial Skill Potion of the Prince of Woe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives <b>Hell-Kissed Celestial Skill Potion of the Prince of Woe</b> in a <a href="bword://Heavy Metal Box">Heavy Metal Box</a> he receives from the <a href="bword://Touched by a Demon Achievement">Touched by a Demon Achievement</a> on the Eighth Floor. When he takes the potion, Carl receives the Gloom Wraith Phase Skill.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Heavy Metal Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hell-Kissed Celestial Skill Potion of the Prince of Woe on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hell-Kissed_Celestial_Skill_Potion_of_the_Prince_of_Woe</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hell-Kissed Potion of Conscription</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hell-Kissed Potion of Conscription</b> can force unaffiliated crawlers, <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>, and even deities to fight for a specific army.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hell-Kissed Potion of Conscription on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hell-Kissed_Potion_of_Conscription</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hellik</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hellik</b> is the god of "Sun and Life," the twin brother of <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>, younger brother of <a href="bword://Taranis">Taranis</a>, and son of <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a>. Hellik is allegedly "quite obsessed" with killing his brothers and is the most likely culprit in <a href="bword://Geyrun">Geyrun</a>'s death, even though he was in council with Emberus when the murder happened.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 31</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hellik on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hellik</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hello</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 58 63 Hours until the Butcher’s Masquerade Entering the Desperado Club “Hello, Clarabelle,” I said as I stepped into the space before the main entrance to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hello Crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hello Crawlers, This will be your second-to-last message before the floor collapses, and I just wanted to say how proud we are of human.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hellspawn Familiar</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Hellspawn Familiar</b> is one of the best pet-class races available in the game. As they level up, they grow wings and physically grow to roughly the size of a dragon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hellspawn Familiar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hellspawn_Familiar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Help</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Help your friend!” Dong was already up and out, letting the truck drift, squeezing past me as I returned to the driver’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Help Signet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In order to do that, she must assassinate the current leaders of the Naiad Confederacy, who are holed up in their fortress at the former river palace of the Naiad, now dubbed “Fort Freedom.” Help Signet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Henrik</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Henrik</b> is the leader of the Dromedarians in Hump Town. He has been replaced by a <a href="bword://Changeling">Changeling</a>. Brother of <a href="bword://Juice Box">Juice Box</a>. Attempted to force <a href="bword://Wynne">Wynne</a> to teach him a spell to turn <a href="bword://Quetzalcoatlus">Quetzalcoatlus</a> into a corporeal form.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dromedarian | Changeling Principal</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Henrik on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Henrik</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Henry</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Henry</b> is one of the residents of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> nursing home. He made it to the The Third Floor with the help of <a href="bword://Brandon Andrews">Brandon Andrews</a>, <a href="bword://Chris Andrews">Chris Andrews</a>, <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>, and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Seattle</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 1, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Henry on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Henry</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Here's Some Good Shit Because You're Gonna Need It Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Here's-Some-Good-Shit-Because-You're-Gonna-Need-It-Box</b> is a type of Loot Box that seems to be awarded for actions that technically set back a crawler's progress on lower levels.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fuck This Floor Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Here's Some Good Shit Because You're Gonna Need It Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Here%27s_Some_Good_Shit_Because_You%27re_Gonna_Need_It_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Herot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Herot</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (16th Edition)</i>. Little is known about Herot's race, but <a href="bword://Akuma">Akuma</a> describes them as a skink. He also describes them as "an annoying fucker. Even more annoying than [<a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>]".</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 17</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Herot on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Herot</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hey Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Louis: Hey Carl, you didn’t tell me your bathroom was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hey Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hey Katia, has anybody called you a genius.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hey Larry, can I borrow your chainsaw? Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hey Larry, can I borrow your chainsaw? Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for using an ability tattoo in battle. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> and <a href="bword://Rosetta Thagra">Rosetta Thagra</a> go into a flurry of research over the name of the box. Zev mentions she has never heard of such blatant hand holding by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hey Larry, can I borrow your chainsaw? Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hey_Larry%2C_can_I_borrow_your_chainsaw%3F_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>HeyZoos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>HeyZoos</b> is a Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> on the Eighth Floor. This card is held by the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Eighth Floor Ecuador</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: HeyZoos on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/HeyZoos</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>He’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d snarl and squawk and hop up and down, but he wouldn’t plunge headfirst into battle until Donut said the command, which had somehow evolved from “attack” to “sic ‘em.” When the timer was down to ten hours.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>He’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’ll be a great pet for you should you decide to keep him, but you have too much on your plate right now to deal with trying to keep a level-one pet alive, especially if it has an undead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>He’s level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’s level eight, the same as us, and she’s level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hi Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Did you fly here on Louis’s new airship?” “Hi.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hi Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hi Donut!” the Empress.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hi Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hi Katia’s boyfriend!” Katia patted my shoulder and picked up the container for the Rev Up hair tonic and started reading the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hi Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hi Prepotente!” Donut called from the back of Mongo, who also.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hicks</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Hicks: race/species: <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a>; occupation: Aerostat Pilot.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hicks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hicks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hidden door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl : In the context where there are a bunch of monsters called vorpals behind a hidden door in the Desperado Club, and they want to unleash them as a last resort against a minor feral demon Samantha.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The High-Elf King Finian, leader of the Liana Sector of the Hunting Grounds, was such a horny bastard, it was said he’d bedded women from over 5,000 different races during his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High Class Bitches</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tuesday-Two-For-One broke her jaw during a fight which fixed her TMJ, so they moved her down to the High Class Bitches bargain rack.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High Elf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most scholars agree this has to be an exaggeration considering King Finian’s harelip and obsession with knitting, but nonetheless, at least one of these trysts did take place, resulting in the unique.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High Elf Castle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “When we first heard of this thing, it was in the High Elf.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High Elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once you enter the ballroom and you are deemed to be in compliance with all the rules, you will officially be under the formal protection of the Queen of All That Is and the Queen of All That Will Be, the One.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>High level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, High level is described as probably a direct result of her having to kill 12 of her former.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Higher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Higher levels of this spell will decrease cooldown, increase Günter’s speed, and lower the mana cost per.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Higher level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It wouldn’t be until a higher level that she’d be able to actually hunt down and find those not currently on the map, plus I wasn’t so certain it’d find crawlers using.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Higher levels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: In fact, some buildings appeared to be completely empty on the first floor while businesses and restaurants filled the higher levels, all catering to the eagle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Highest level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: First explain the circumstances regarding the player-killer skulls you two have, how Katia is the highest level of all of you, and then we’ll go over all of your new skills and…” he trailed off, his eyes fixed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hills Cyclops</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hills Cyclops</b> are a race one eyed creatures, they are the weakest and dumbest of the cyclops. Like all Cyclops they are unable to see themselves in the mirror.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hills Cyclops on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hills_Cyclops</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>history of Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is a feat that has only been accomplished by a handful of crawlers in the history of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>history of Dungeon Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is a feat that has only been accomplished by a handful of crawlers in the history of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>history of Dungeon Crawler World</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is a feat that has only been accomplished by a handful of crawlers in the history of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hit it with a missile as we cross!” Smoke filled the street ahead of us, but it caught on a breeze, blowing in the wrong.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hitting Yourself</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tell those other demons to leave my friends alone, and I will deliver your message.” The Why are you Hitting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hive</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, superior species such as the Hive—who have been at the forefront of decoding and reverse-engineering primal technology—approached the council and demanded the ability to field test.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hive Home</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When I’m done with this hunt, I will take my place in the birthing hall of Hive Home two, and I will birth a thousand nymphs, all of whom will be born with the memory of this moment, of your slaughter, yet not.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hive queen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: People were actively betting on whether or not she’d give in and cut the infection away, which would ruin her chances at being the next hive.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hmm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It suggests she’d left one guard behind and the second one died protecting her from the demons, which also explains why she was late.” “Hmm,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hoarder</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hoarder</b> is a First Floor Neighborhood Boss and the first boss that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> fight inside the Dungeon. She guards a chamber of transplanted Earth refuse and defends it by disgorging <a href="bword://Scatterer">Scatterer</a> minions from her own mouth.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 11</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hoarder on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hoarder</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hob</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Also, we’ll be back in a day or so if you promise to bring in some of those smoke curtains.” “Well, two cases of goblin dynamite and the last of the Hob-lobbers would be 1,500 gold,” Pustule said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobbledehoy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobbledehoy</b> are a race of short humans. They stand approximately 4' 6" tall and simply look "like a regular person that had been shrunk." Hobbledehoy are apparently known for being foul-tempered.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobbledehoy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobbledehoy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobby potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What was the name of that earth hobby potion you got way back on the third floor?” “Scutelliphily,” she said, spitting the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin</b> are a race that a crawler can choose on the Third Floor. Hobgoblins also show up as <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a>. <a href="bword://Pustule">Pustule</a> is described as very ugly, but has excellent hearing. She remarks on the comments <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> makes about her appearance from 50 paces away.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Detonator</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Hobgoblin">Hobgoblin</a> Detonators</b> are single-use magical items that can be used to detonate up to ten explosive devices at the same time. It has a range of approximately 10 km (6.21 miles).</li><li><b>Source:</b> Looting, Loot Boxes, Store</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Detonator on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Detonator</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Disco Ball</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin Disco Ball</b> is a type of smoke curtain made by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> at his <a href="bword://Sapper&#x27;s Table">Sapper's Table</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Disco Ball on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Disco_Ball</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Dynamite</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin Dynamite</b> is an explosive Item that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> occasionally recieves in loot boxes. It is very similar to <a href="bword://Goblin Dynamite">Goblin Dynamite</a>, but has a larger yield and is more stable.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hobgoblins, Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Dynamite on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Dynamite</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Hob</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A mainstay of the Hobgoblin Hob-Lobbing Lobbers, the Hob-Lobber is a stable, mostly-predictable, more practical and tactical solution to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Hob-Lobber</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin Hob-Lobber</b> is an round explosive item that comes in two types, fused and impact. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> uses them with his <a href="bword://Xistera">Xistera</a> as a ranged explosive round.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Hob-Lobber on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Hob-Lobber</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Interdiction Team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: When it comes to cleaning up a crashed vehicle on the tracks, nothing works better, faster, and more efficiently than the Hobgoblin Interdiction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Pus</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hobgoblin Pus</b> is clay-like explosive material that can be detonated with an actual trigger to create a large explosion. It is generally more valuable and stable than <a href="bword://Goblin Dynamite">Goblin Dynamite</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot, Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Pus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Pus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hobgoblin Smoke Curtain</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Hobgoblin">Hobgoblin</a> Smoke Curtains</b> are round smoke bombs with fuses, which are relatively inexpensive and generally better than the limited Goblin Smoke Bombs. When detonated, they coat the entire area in a dense white fog.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hobgobs and Boom Sticks</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hobgoblin Smoke Curtain on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hobgoblin_Smoke_Curtain</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hold</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I could see the tip of a little red hat atop the battery as it moved in our direction “Hold on guys,” I yelled as I pulled the multi-launcher from my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hold Steady</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>He appeared to have significant magical abilities.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hold Steady on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hold_Steady</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hole</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hole in the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’ll have to do it while standing, and only once we are inverted.” “Will that work?” Donut asked as we took a sharp turn to avoid another hole in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hole Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hole</b> is a utility spell that creates a temporary hole in a surface of the caster's choice. Although the spell is unable to penetrate most doors at level one, <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> notes that it becomes increasingly useful at higher levels, "Especially if you’re creative with it.”</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hole Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hole_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Holger</b> is a were-castor with a mullet. He wears black, studded leather from neck to toe, no helmet, and a pair of weapons over his shoulders: a sickle-shaped blade, and a sharpened stick. The pale-skinned warrior has a red bandana wrapped tight around his fuzzy head that sports a genuine mullet, Rambo-style. He stands about three feet tall and is wide, but not fat. More a like miniature, well-built football player. His face is wide-set with wide-eyes and buckteeth. He looks like a smaller-sized dwarf– but his features are elf-like. Almost hobbit like, but stout like a barrel and hairy.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Holger on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Holger</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holger and Clint</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The weasels had mostly focused on Holger and Clint, and they were the only ones to have taken.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holistic Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Holistic Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he is talking to <a href="bword://Rishi">Rishi</a> after Operation Snake Pit. The achievement is due to reaching 95% viewership by the <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> system.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Holistic Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Holistic_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If she trains this one up, she can remove all the armor off a group of mobs and then reanimate their own armor with the spirits, and they will tear through the unarmored monsters like wildfire.” “Holy crap,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy Christ</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And also because I didn’t yet have the ability to freeze their balls and shatter them like dollar store Christmas ornaments.” “Holy Christ,” Florin muttered next to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy Crusade Bounty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was now a “Holy Crusade Bounty” on the trio, whatever that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy Gooper Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Holy Goopers</b> are essentially holy water grenades. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> manufactures Holy Goopers by combining a fine <a href="bword://Healing Potion">Healing Potion</a> and an air-activated coagulant inside a potion ball, and dipping the ball in the <a href="bword://Sun Shrine">Sun Shrine</a>. He explains the resulting potion acts like napalm to Vampires; once struck with a Holy Gooper, the vampires will burn up when exposed to light or a strong <i><a href="bword://Torch">Torch</a></i> spell.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Holy Gooper Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Holy_Gooper_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy grenade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Based on what Prepotente said and what he received, I took an existing weedkiller designed to take out an odious bloom and then added multiple anti-undead boosts to it, similar to the holy grenade I’d made.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Holy Hell Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Holy Hell</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded when a crawler first lays eyes on a Demon Prince of Sheol. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Eighth Floor when <a href="bword://Amayon">Amayon</a> enters the realm.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Holy Hell Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Holy_Hell_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Home Depot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s just a little cactus from Home Depot.” “It didn’t match with the new rug,” Samantha.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Home Goods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you’ve ever walked into a Target or an IKEA or a Home Goods, looked at the weird wall art they have for sale there, and thought to yourself, who buys this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Home planet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once this nightmare is over, I become a full citizen, I receive a moderate stipend, and I am free to make my way into the universe.” “Will you go back to your home planet?” “No,” he.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Home system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The rich folk sure like getting their faces up on the screen when they’re donating to some charity, but the moment a scruffy, former crawler shows up in their home system looking to settle down, they’ll fight.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Homecoming Queen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <i><b>Homecoming Queen</b></i> is a freighter in <a href="bword://Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions">Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</a>'s fleet. Originally a biological waste freighter, the spaceship is approximately twice as large as a Valtay warship and easily the largest of the crafts trapped in Earth's solar system.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Homecoming Queen on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Homecoming_Queen</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Homeward Bound</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Homeward Bound is described as locked up tighter than an octoid’s cunt, so you’ll have the track all to yerself.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Honestly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Honestly, I didn’t know how all this worked when the AI could literally create stuff with a virtual snap of his fingers, but we’d seen these sort of artificial shortages before, like with the food after the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hoon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hoon</b> was a crawler in a previous Valtay-produced season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>. Over the course of his crawl, Hoon secured a mech and completed eleven floors. When he reached the elevator descending to the Twelfth Floor, he found it guarded by his five children ("even the yearling"). Hoon chose to eat his own gun rather than kill his children.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Crawler Hoon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hoon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hoon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hopefully</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hopefully now you’ll realize all those Magic: The Gathering cards are nothing more than just meaningless pieces of paper, and you should have spent your money on something with actual value, like a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Horatio</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Horatio had held on in hopes of getting a better deal, of making a life for them when they finally got.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Horror</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Horror LitRPG</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hortense</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even cowardly Hortense himself had come along, being pulled on a long, flat litter, mixed in with the correct combination of other admin personnel that made him appear to be “strong.” The Operatics had likely.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hortense Leadership</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hortense Leadership Unit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Horton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Horton</b> is the mushroom guy from the Stairwell Fight on the Fourth Floor. His name is not revealed until the Butcher's Masquerade. Horton looks like a mushroom after the Third Floor race selection process.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | mushroom</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 27, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Horton on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Horton</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hot dog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s going to end up looking like a hot dog that exploded in the microwave!” “We will fix any inconsistences after the race,” Grigori said, panting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hotlist Expansion Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Hotlist Expansion Potion</b> is a potion that expands a Crawler's hotlist in their Heads-Up Display (HUD). <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this potion in his Gold <a href="bword://Payload Box">Payload Box</a> at the beginning of the Tenth Floor for the <a href="bword://Bomb&#x27;s Away Achievement">Bomb's Away Achievement</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Payload Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hotlist Expansion Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hotlist_Expansion_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hours</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “This is us, taking the matter up privately.” * * * 62 Hours until the Butcher’s Masquerade “I appreciate what you’ve done for us, but that don’t mean we gonna just blindly trust you,” the changeling said as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Houston</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Houston</b> is the Warlord of the Viceroy Faction War team.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Viceroys</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Houston on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Houston</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hover</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: While I was really happy about the cooldown reduction and mana point increase, Donut was all about that Hover.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hover skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: While I was really happy about the cooldown reduction and mana point increase, Donut was all about that Hover.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>How the Gristle Grinds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Quest Complete: This is How the Gristle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>However</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, if the organization running this season begins to suspect that this book is more than it appears, or if you tell anyone about the existence of this book, the information within will erase, and you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Howling Gulls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I killed nothing but Howling Gulls, and the soul essence bar was pure, when I unleashed my melee attack, it was almost twice as powerful than it would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>How’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “How’d it go with spider boy and the other guy?” Katia had given them a general map of the entire structure and a more detailed path to another town about halfway down to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Huanxin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I still didn’t know the details on why they didn’t like each other, though I knew the three of them—Mordecai, Odette, and Huanxin—were all tangled up together in some ancient beef that ended up with Mordecai’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Huanxin Jinx</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Huanxin Jinx</b> is the current sponsor of the goddess <a href="bword://Eileithyia">Eileithyia</a>. She's been playing the Ascension game since she was a kid. She is both rich and highly intelligent. She's currently CEO of Icon Industries, a company that focuses on habitat structures.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Grixist</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Huanxin Jinx on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Huanxin_Jinx</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Huh</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The main body is far below us, between the disputed lands and the celestial realm.” “Huh,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Human</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Humans</b> are one of the six basic starter species the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> seeds across the galaxy for <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>, and the indigenous species of Earth. A crawler may choose "Human" during Third Floor race selection to keep their current form in exchange for a small boost to stat points and skill training.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Human on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Human</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Human Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Race: Human Class: Santero This crawler worships the God.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Human crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The final form a war mage takes is entirely dependent on the combination of its first two forms, in this case, a human crawler from a very long time ago and a high.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Humans</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Humans, pig-headed orcs similar to the ones we’d just killed, and eagle-headed creatures like Mordecai’s true.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hump Street</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The inns and Desperado Club were all on the third ring, the aptly labeled “Hump Street.” As we made our way, the wind abruptly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hump Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: From this angle, I could only see the top, shingled roof of what appeared to be a house sitting on the center of the landmass, but dozens of other smaller structures dotted the edges, most of which were made.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hundreds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hundreds of tiny little arms appeared, and they were connecting what appeared to be nerves from the changeling’s neck to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hungry Eyes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It had been Linus’s idea to do it in the same place where all the crawlers were gathering to get picked as volunteers for the assault on the Pineapple Cabaret, which was the commons of Hungry.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hungry Eyes Village</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I can’t even begin to imagine how awful that must be.” “I know.” We continued in silence for a few moments, heading toward the archway into Hungry Eyes.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunter Champion Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hunter Champion Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> from The <a href="bword://Scourge Achievement">Scourge Achievement</a>. Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Eighth Floor where it is referred to as the "Legendary Scourge Box."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Scourge Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunter Champion Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunter_Champion_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hunters</b> are a group of outworlders that come into the Dungeon on the Sixth Floor to hunt Crawlers for their gear to sell to the Faction leaders for Faction Wars on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunters on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunters</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunter Champion Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hunter Champion Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> from The <a href="bword://Scourge Achievement">Scourge Achievement</a>. Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Eighth Floor where it is referred to as the "Legendary Scourge Box."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Scourge Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunter Champion Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunter_Champion_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunter Killer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most of them were low-tier adventurer boxes, but I had a grand total of 14 Hunter Killer boxes including the one I’d received from Future Hunter the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunter Wraith</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Or rather, it’s a curse, inflicted upon the mind of the Hunter Wraith, which is why they are so.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunter-Killer Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hunter-Killer Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for killing a non-crawler biological. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives boxes for every hunter he kills, plus an extra one for killing <a href="bword://Loita">Loita</a> on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hand that Claws the Master Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunter-Killer Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunter-Killer_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Hunters</b> are a group of outworlders that come into the Dungeon on the Sixth Floor to hunt Crawlers for their gear to sell to the Faction leaders for Faction Wars on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunters on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunters</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunting Grounds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of the few races who managed to not get fully wiped out by Scolopendra’s nine-tier attack, the bugbears are now one of the most common races one might find in the Kapok district of the Hunting Grounds.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunting Grounds level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were trying to drain it before this floor ended, but it doesn’t look like it’s going to happen.” “Those guys on the Hunting Grounds level aren’t getting kicked out because the blood bar thing?” “Yeah, I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunting Trophies Collected</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Hunting Trophies Collected: 36 Bravvo’s highest stat was his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hunting Trophy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>It’s the hand of a hunter. Gross.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Hunting Trophy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Hunting_Trophy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hurk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’ve been giving all of these little snot factories ones and twos all morning.” “If you give her a good score, I’ll say I really like the design of the Desperado Club during my next panel.” “Sold!” Hurk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hurry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hurry!” There was a crack, and three more racers appeared, coming through the ceiling, then streaking down the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Hurry Up</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: No, not ‘hurry up.’ ‘Hurry Up,’ but in German.” “Oh, you mean plan Mach Schnell.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I Have the Conch Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>I Have the Conch Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the <a href="bword://Lord of the Flies Achievement">Lord of the Flies Achievement</a>. He receives a Gold version of this box. Gold I Have the Conch Box</li><li><b>Source:</b> Lord of the Flies Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I Have the Conch Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I_Have_the_Conch_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I Need My Personal Space Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>I Need My Personal Space</b></i> is a spell that, when cast, knocks back everyone in the caster's vicinity with a powerful blow. The spell affects hostile mobs and party members alike.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I Need My Personal Space Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I_Need_My_Personal_Space_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I Take It All Back Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>I Take It All Back Potion</b> is a potion that removes any effects of <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s transmutation spell.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I Take It All Back Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I_Take_It_All_Back_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I Wanna Go Home Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>I Wanna Go Home Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for teleporting to Zockau to get a pre-emptive attack on the hunters in The Hunting Grounds.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I Wanna Go Home Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I_Wanna_Go_Home_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I was in the Pool! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>I was in the Pool! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he uses a water-breathing scroll for the first time.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I was in the Pool! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I_was_in_the_Pool%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I'm on a train Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>I'm on a train Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to all crawlers upon entering the Fourth Floor and appearing on a train.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I'm on a train Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I%27m_on_a_train_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I'm Wet Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>I'm Wet Box</b> are loot boxes distributed for activities involving water. These have only been rewarded on the fifth floor so far. These boxes are distributed fairly often for activities such as "being underwater for 60 seconds" or "jumping around on the ocean floor"</li><li><b>Source:</b> I was in the Pool! AchievementRock Bottom Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: I'm Wet Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/I%27m_Wet_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ian</b> was a crawler in a previous <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>-run season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World.</i> Originally a human, he chose the <a href="bword://Bush Elf">Bush Elf</a> race and an unspecified (likely druid-based) class. He and his brother both took indentureship deals to exit the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Bush Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ice Slick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut cast Ice Slick on the dance floor, causing a group of fighting NPCs to go spinning and crash against the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ice Slick Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ice Slick</b> is an <a href="bword://Elle McGibbons">Elle McGibbons</a> signature spell that coats the ground in a wide sheet of ice roughly an inch thick. The area covered by Ice Slick scales with the caster's intelligence stat and the spell's own level. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives a special edition copy of the spellbook that starts at level 5 and trains faster than normal.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ice Slick Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ice_Slick_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ice spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What does she know now?” “She has a party heal spell, an illusion spell, an ice spell that she still can’t cast very well, a psionic attack that makes her nose bleed and does nothing else to mobs, and a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iceland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first crawler they showed getting her upgrade was Hekla of Brynhild’s Daughters, the woman from Iceland who’d gathered a large group of female crawlers around.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Icelandic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Skilaðu þessu .” I had no idea what he said or why the system wasn’t translating all this Icelandic, but the threat was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Icon Industries</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s the CEO of a company called Icon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>If Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * If Mordecai wasn’t covered in feathers, I knew he’d be pale as a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ifechi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ifechi</b> was a medic in the Red Cross, and became one of the Dungeon's Top Ten Crawlers after partnering with <a href="bword://Florin">Florin</a>. Dark-skinned, bald, and rail-thin, Ifechi had "stunningly beautiful" features. She entered the Dungeon wearing a bright red shirt and a vest, carrying a medical bag emblazoned with the Red Cross logo over one shoulder.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Nigeria</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 44</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ifechi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ifechi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Igneous</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Igneous</b> is a race option that crawlers can select on the Third Floor. It is similar to the <a href="bword://Cretin">Cretin</a> race, but not exactly the same. An Igneous is a towering humanoid race made of lava and rock. Their eyes resemble "burning lumps of coal," and they radiate extreme heat and can fire lava from their bodies, as though their centers are molten lava. When they move, it sounds like rocks scraping together.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Igneous on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Igneous</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ikicha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ikicha</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (11th Edition)</i>. Ikicha is a castrated male <a href="bword://Yenk">Yenk</a>. During his season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>, the Sixth Floor Hunting Grounds were cold and icy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Yenk</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ikicha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ikicha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ill effects</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you are injured, or poisoned, or if you get a hangnail, you will suffer the ill effects and pain of that injury until the moment your prey is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Illusion spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What does she know now?” “She has a party heal spell, an illusion spell, an ice spell that she still can’t cast very well, a psionic attack that makes her nose bleed and does nothing else to mobs, and a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Illusionist and Bogatyr</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dmitri and Maxim Popov – Nodling – Illusionist and Bogatyr – Level 33 – 100,000 (x2).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imagine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imagine shoving your flaccid wang into a pinhole barely big enough to accommodate a deflated balloon, and then someone suddenly thrusts some prime AAA, pristinely washed, mathematically perfect tootsies right.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Imani</b> is a member of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party and guildmaster of <a href="bword://Safehome Yolanda">Safehome Yolanda</a>. She is one of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s most trusted allies in the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Obsidian Butterfly</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Imani on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Imani</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had no idea where the wooden platforms had come from, but I knew Imani and Chris both were as obsessive about looting everything as I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Chris, does Maggie worship Grull?” While we waited for an answer to filter through Imani and Elle—which was the only way we could talk—I examined the grenade in my inventory to make sure the pin hadn’t yet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Imani and Katia is described as the true bedrocks of our ability to stay.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Li Jun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: But I remembered how big those fuckers were, and if they managed to break into the car and open a path down to station 36, it’d be another weight on the shoulder of Imani and Li.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Louis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Imani and Louis, I know you both like it really hot, so I have this one ready for you.” “You’re being particularly nice,” Elle said, flying up, peering suspiciously at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Tran</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Imani and Tran is described as two levels below,” Katia said, “but the whole area is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani and Yolanda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were Imani and Yolanda, who were nurses, and the brothers Chris and Brandon who’d both been maintenance guys at the old folk’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imani’s team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both Elle and Imani’s team and Louis, Britney, and Bautista’s team had lost two teams, meaning for the next heat, they were all in danger of having to face off against other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imbues</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imbues wearer with +5 Intelligence, grants the user +5 to the Good First Impression.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imbues the following effects</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Equipping this small buckler imbues the following effects: Catch physical attacks (Success percentage based off user’s dexterity) Catch magical bolt attacks (Success percentage based off user’s intelligence.).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: While not as roided-out as a Rat Brute, or as Imma fireball yo ass as a Rat Shaman, Rat Hooligans offer the best of both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imogen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Queen Imogen</b> is a level 145 country boss on the Sixth Floor and the reclusive ruler of the high elves. She hosts the Butcher's Masquerade and is the target of <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a>'s revenge quest, the Vengeance of the Daughter.</li><li><b>Race:</b> High Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 64</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Imogen on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Imogen</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imogen of the High Elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once you enter the ballroom and you are deemed to be in compliance with all the rules, you will officially be under the formal protection of the Queen of All That Is and the Queen of All That Will Be, the One.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imperial Security</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle moved up to spot eight, and Li Jun the street monk had fallen off, replaced by Quan Ch, who was a level 31 half-elf Imperial Security.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Imperial Security Trooper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle moved up to spot eight, and Li Jun the street monk had fallen off, replaced by Quan Ch, who was a level 31 half-elf Imperial Security.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>In the Air Tonight</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pony was singing “In the Air Tonight” by Phil.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>In the Nothing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “In the Nothing, we called that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Incendiary Device Handling</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My Incendiary Device Handling skill had jumped up to five immediately upon the construction of the “device.” All I had done was open up the jug, pour a splash or two out until it was about 3/4's full, added a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Includes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Includes battery power or powered track selection.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Incomplete Flesh Golem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Incomplete Flesh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Incredible Hulk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Have you ever read an Incredible Hulk comic and thought to yourself, everything rips off of his body except his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Incubus</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Incubus</b> are a type of demon, with dusky gray skin. They also have devil horns, a forked tail, and bat-like wings. Incubi have a low alcohol tolerance.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Incubus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Incubus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Indelible Woe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “He likely has a ring of Divine Suffering for the stats—and the levels that come with charging the ring—and a necklace of Indelible Woe for the spells.” “Indelible Woe?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>India</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We have seen the specials about what happened to children left in India for obvious reasons, but your country, the United States of America, was on the other side of the planet.” I had no idea what was obvious.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Indigenous Planetary Species Protection Act</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Indigenous Planetary Species Protection Act</b> (often shortened to <b>Indigenous Species Protection Act</b>) is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> law governing "collection actions" (i.e. a transformation) and the creation of World Dungeons for <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Indigenous Planetary Species Protection Act on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Indigenous_Planetary_Species_Protection_Act</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Indigenous Species Protection Act</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Indigenous Species Protection Act is described as endangering the very existence of all life in the universe, I leaned forward and banged my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Individual rooms</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: But once they’re combined, the individual rooms get much smaller, but a communal space is created, and all share in the upgrades.” “Plus the manager gets to keep his own place,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Indomitable Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Indomitable Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for being attacked by <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a>. Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Fifth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Indomitable Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Indomitable_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inebriated Dragonfly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s dislike of the color ended the moment we read the description of what the tiara actually did: Enchanted Tiara of the Inebriated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ines Quiteria</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “During the battles between the crabs and seals, crawler Sister Ines Quiteria used her level-15 Book of Rhymes bard skill, which she just got from her patron, to enhance her Word Weaver spell to charm this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inevitable Ruin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Have you ever heard of the concept of the Inevitable Ruin?” Juice Box.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inferior Demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gallu – Level 100 Inferior.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Infiltrator</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, <b>Infiltrators</b> (AKA <b><a href="bword://Scree">Scree</a></b>) are an aquatic brain parasite that can take control of a living brain. Crawlers may choose the "Infiltrator" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Infiltrator on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Infiltrator</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Infiltrator</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, <b>Infiltrators</b> (AKA <b><a href="bword://Scree">Scree</a></b>) are an aquatic brain parasite that can take control of a living brain. Crawlers may choose the "Infiltrator" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Infiltrator on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Infiltrator</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scree</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, <b>Infiltrators</b> (AKA <b>Scree</b>) are an aquatic brain parasite that can take control of a living brain. Crawlers may choose the "<a href="bword://Infiltrator">Infiltrator</a>" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scree on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scree</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Info box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: All he had to do was either look it up in some complicated codex or scan the label, and an info box would pop up giving information on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Initiate a card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Do it fast, and then you’re going to initiate a card battle with the duendes over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ink Marauder</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I want them all to see.” In order to cast her Ink Marauder spell, Signet needed three.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ink Marauder Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Ink Marauder</b> spell is a form of blood magic that allows the caster to draw something on paper with ink, cast a blood sacrifice, and bring those drawings to life as Blood and Ink Elementals.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ink Marauder Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ink_Marauder_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Inle</b> is a god who is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon, with <a href="bword://Ogun">Ogun</a>, <a href="bword://Legba">Legba</a>, <a href="bword://Oshun">Oshun</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Shango">Shango</a>, and <a href="bword://Yemaya">Yemaya</a>. During the Thorn Room Boss Battle, if his shrine is repaired: “One of only two of the Orishas to have spent time in the Nothing, the deaf and mute Inle isn’t all there. That’s a nice way of saying he’s batshit. But, he once was a healing god. If you repair his shrine, you will gain immunity from most health-seeping conditions. You will gain 50% to your Constitution. Inle will not ascend. He’ll end up back in the Nothing. He doesn’t know that yet, so don’t tell him.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Inle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Inle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inner system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because of the Crawler Project’s campaigning, all released crawlers were now automatically citizens of the Syndicate and given access to the inner system primary zone, which came with free healthcare and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inquisitor General</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A cleric, magic-user, warrior combo called a Black Inquisitor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inside</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Things like “You’re the Boss Now” and “Your own hours” and “It’s not a pyramid!” and “Safe!*” Inside the pamphlet was a wall of text of mostly gibberish about the benefits of becoming a “Rev-Up Moonshine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Instacot 60</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Instacot 60</b> is a Personal Space and Guild Bed Upgrade available beginning on the Sixth Floor. The Instacot 60 is the second best Bed Upgrade on the market. Crawlers will feel fully rested after only sixty seconds (one minute) of sleep, and will receive the Great Rest buff for (presumably) 30 hours.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Market OR Guild</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Instacot 60 on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Instacot_60</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Instagram</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next thing you know you’ll be fashioning bottlecap earrings, drinking oat milk, and selling your ugly crap on Etsy while you wax poetic on Instagram about your “journey.” Reward : You’ve received a Bronze.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Instagram OceanofPDF</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Instead</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Instead, Carl is safe, and now your throne room is destroyed.” Commander Stockade undulated his caterpillar body back and forth, oddly reminding me of one of those waving, inflatable tube men they shoved in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Insurgent Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Insurgent Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he set an explosive trap killing the hunter JayGee during The Hunting Grounds.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Insurgent Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Insurgent_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Hunter</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and <a href="bword://Scree">Scree</a>, <b>Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a></b> are the most common variety of finger-sized parasitic worms. Crawlers may choose to take the "Intellect Hunter" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Intellect Hunter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Intellect_Hunter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Hunters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There are Intellect Hunters who do the same thing, but the bodies start to rot almost immediately, and they are always hopping from body to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Pacifist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Pacifist Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Pacifist Action Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intellect Pacifist Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intelligence</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intelligence level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut received a tome of Twinkle Toes , a cheap spell which made Mongo—or any other minion—run really fast for as many seconds as her intelligence.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>intention to abandon Faction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic Collective has abdicated their spot and indicated their intention to abandon Faction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>intention to abandon Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic Collective has abdicated their spot and indicated their intention to abandon Faction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interdiction Auxillary Railway Repair Cart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Interdiction Auxillary Railway Repair Cart</b> is a contraption on the Iron Tangle. It is one of the types of carts found at station 75. See also: <a href="bword://Interdiction Rapid-Response Railway Repair Cart">Interdiction Rapid-Response Railway Repair Cart</a></li><li><b>Source:</b> Station 75 on the Iron Tangle</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Interdiction Auxillary Railway Repair Cart on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Interdiction_Auxillary_Railway_Repair_Cart</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interdiction Rapid-Response Railway Repair Cart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Interdiction Rapid-Response Railway Repair Cart</b> is a contraption on the Iron Tangle. It is one of the types of carts found at station 75. See also: <a href="bword://Interdiction Auxillary Railway Repair Cart">Interdiction Auxillary Railway Repair Cart</a></li><li><b>Source:</b> Station 75 on the Iron Tangle</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Interdiction Rapid-Response Railway Repair Cart on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Interdiction_Rapid-Response_Railway_Repair_Cart</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interdiction team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It will take time for the home base to learn there is a problem and to send an interdiction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interesting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: If the spell fails suddenly and doesn’t activate and the cactus dissipates into dust, that means Na is dead and that she died inside the dungeon.” “Interesting,” Prepotente said, leaning in to examine the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intergalactic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Intergalactic NFC</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interior Illustrations by Erik Wilson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: collageorama.com Interior Illustrations by Erik Wilson http://.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Interloper Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Interloper!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler opens a safe room door and allows a Boss to enter. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, Anton, <a href="bword://Paz Lo">Paz Lo</a>, and <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a> earn this achievement on the Eighth Floor when they flee into a safe room to escape <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>. Sister Ines suggests opening the safe room door to get rid of him and Carl, thinking of the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a> from the Second Floor does. When he opens the door, it appears as though Asojano is preparing to knock. Instead, he steps inside the room.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Interloper Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Interloper_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inventory</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Inventory</b> is a dimensional storage interface and AI cataloging system that the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> grants to crawlers upon entering the World Dungeon, replacing the slot-based systems used in previous seasons. After completing the Tutorial, crawlers access and manage it entirely through a mental Heads-Up Display (HUD) menu.</li><li><b>Source:</b> System AI / Borant Corporation</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Inventory on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Inventory</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Inventory system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She could remove the backpack using her inventory system, which was a bit inelegant, causing her body to fall and splotch onto the floor in a round, quivering, unshaped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Invisibility</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re going to be our secret weapon.” The first several boxes all opened, giving me more of the Build Trench scrolls, Invisibility potions, Size-Up potions, and loads of random odds and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Invisibility potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled another Invisibility potion, a potion ball with another Invisibility potion in it for Mongo, and a handful of Good Healing potions, and I dropped them on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Invisibility potions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were a dozen adventurer boxes, and none of them contained anything interesting except more of those trench-building scrolls and a few invisibility.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Invulnerability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Invulnerability is described as about to time out, and the next wave.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Invulnerable on this floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even if he wasn’t invulnerable on this floor and the next, I couldn’t even get anywhere near him without burning to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iota</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Iota</b> was a hunter acting as an accountant for the Crafters Alliance. He stayed in the safe room refusing to fight until the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> teleported him out the room. Landed in front of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and a bunch of dinosaurs, he explained to Carl about the system AI going primal and ignoring court order before Carl killed him.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Crafter</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Iota on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Iota</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iowa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The last of the Iowa chunk had started to tumble away just as we finished, but luckily, the dirt made a somewhat reasonable ramp back down, and getting to the surface of Florida was much easier than I’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iron Punch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I did manage to level my Iron Punch skill up to ten with the gauntlet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iron Skin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Scroll of Confusing Fog filled the room with a mist that only the mobs could see, and the Iron Skin potion raised my physical armor by 100% for five.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iron Skin Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>An <b>Iron Skin Potion</b> (or <b><a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a> of Iron Skin</b>) is a single-use consumable potion that increases a crawler's "physical armor" by 100% for 5 minutes.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Iron Skin Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Iron_Skin_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iron Stomach</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Iron Stomach skill was apparently something new this season because Mordecai had never heard of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Iron Tangle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Our forced memories are oftentimes so paper-thin, we start to realize something is wrong long before the floor is even over.” “That’s like that one train station during the Iron Tangle,” Louis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Is Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Akuma : Is Elle the one on the Four Seasons.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Isn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Isn’t a heart transplant a super-complicated procedure?” “How is Samantha a floating head, but Louis can’t live with stupid lungs and a heart?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Isn’t attending cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Isn’t attending cat shows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Issitoq</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Issitoq</b> is a deity that can treat between the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> and <a href="bword://Demons">Demons</a>, allowing for peace to be broken.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7 (mentioned)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Issitoq on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Issitoq</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It Burns Dunnit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>"It Burns, Dunnit?"</b> is an achievement earned by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the First Floor after he is badly burned by a green slime. Early in the crawl, Carl and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> encounter a level-2 green slime while exploring the <a href="bword://Hoarder">Hoarder</a> neighborhood. Lacking any protection for his hands, Carl foolishly punches the pig-sized blob, sinking his arm in elbow-deep; his fingerless glove and jacket sleeve dissolve away, and his arm is left red, bubbling, and screaming with pain. The slime ultimately kills itself against Carl's Damage Reflect ability, and Carl receives the "It Burns, Dunnit?" achievement for the encounter.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: It Burns Dunnit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/It_Burns_Dunnit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It itches when I pee Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>It itches when I pee Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> infected <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> with double gonorrhea when he stabbed her with the <a href="bword://Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea">Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: It itches when I pee Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/It_itches_when_I_pee_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It's Elementary, My Dear Crawler Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>It's Elementary, My Dear Crawler Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the Show Must Go On Quest.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: It's Elementary, My Dear Crawler Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/It%27s_Elementary%2C_My_Dear_Crawler_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It's Not My Fault You Fish-Headed Assholes Don't Properly Program Your Quests Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A Platinum-tier <b>"It's Not My Fault You Fish-Headed Assholes Don't Properly Program Your Quests" Box</b> is delivered to all but one survivor of the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest at the beginning of the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: It's Not My Fault You Fish-Headed Assholes Don't Properly Program Your Quests Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/It%27s_Not_My_Fault_You_Fish-Headed_Assholes_Don%27t_Properly_Program_Your_Quests_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Item is described as a wrist bracer that transforms into a spiked war gauntlet made of orcish steel when the hand is shaped into a fist or wields a hilted weapon for more than two seconds at a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Item rejected by system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Action item proposed by Co-Warlord Princess Donut of the Princess Posse: Change the name of Crown Prince Stalwart to “Captain Enormous.” ***Item rejected by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’d be one thing if these hairy bitches practiced what they preached, but all of their rules and self-righteousness is nothing but a shiny veneer covering up the fact they’re just as rusted and immoral and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’ll be great for my book sales.” “You know,” Drick said, his voice full of disdain, “He’s probably sitting in that chair right now, listening to everything you say.” “They don’t let the crawlers listen in on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s a spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s a spell book called Oozy Form .” “Uzi form?” Donut asked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>IT’S CALLED</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: IN THE SITUATIONAL WHATEVER IT’S CALLED.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This place is a goddamn nightmare.” “It’s Carl,” another crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Christmas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s Christmas.” “He said the system AI made him say it,” I said, still looking at the blank.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Hekla</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s Hekla!” “I can see, Donut,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Kiwi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s Kiwi!” All of the others crowded around Mongo and started sniffing at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Lazarus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>It’s Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s Princess D’Nadia!” She hopped up and down and then jumped off my shoulder and ran over to her, her tail waving back and forth with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I’ll show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’ll show you a costume.” She snapped her fingers, and all the tentacles on Zander’s face turned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I’M SORRY</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Donut : I’M SORRY, BUT YOU ARE MUCH TOO POOR TO BE TALKING TO ME LIKE.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>I’m Wet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I… I think the AI just gave you photographs of the mothers of all the plaintiffs in that lawsuit.” “Why?” He didn’t answer as the Gold I’m Wet box opened, revealing a prize I wished I’d had all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jabbering Jibber</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Jabbering Jibber-Jabber is the reanimated mouth of those who frequent such.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jack</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jack</b> is a deceased Crawler and former patient of <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> Care Facility. Jack was an older man who wore a too-small Cincinnati Bengals hat on his "bulbous head," and used a walker to navigate the Dungeon. He frequently poked at Meadow Lark's female patients (none of whom were named Marybeth) and asked, "You still alive, Marybeth?"</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jack on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jack</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jacobus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jacobus</b> is a Level 90 Reverse Tooth <a href="bword://Fairy">Fairy</a>, an Eighth Floor totem card accidentally made permanent by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s Scavenger's Daughter patch, who becomes an unlikely pet of <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>'s.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jacobus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jacobus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jamal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jamal</b> is a massive, air-breathing hammerhead shark who originally existed as a magical tattoo on <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a> before being brought to life. He serves as an enthusiastic and polite ally and mercenary for the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Jar Jar, Shark Boy</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Tsarina Signet's Tattoos</li><li><b>Race:</b> Hammerhead Shark (Flesh Golem / Ink and Blood Elemental)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 9 (as an unnamed tattoo), Book 5, Chapter 14 (made flesh)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jamal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jamal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jamal and Bigs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Jamal and Bigs is described as bouncing to the distant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>James Bond</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “How is it you’re James Bond when it comes to strangers, but Miss Beatrice could date three different guys at once, and you had no idea?” “ Three different guys?” “Well, you were one of them, so two, I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jane the Virgin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s like taking Jason from a Friday the Thirteenth movie and tossing him into a late-season episode of Jane the Virgin just to see what.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Janet Jackson’s Nipple Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Janet Jackson’s Nipple Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> for being featured on a special event. The title is in reference to the Super Bowl XXXVIII halftime controversy.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Janet Jackson’s Nipple Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Janet_Jackson%E2%80%99s_Nipple_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Janitor Mobs</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Janitor Mobs</b> are a specific type of mob that cleans up the dead bodies on a floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Janitor Mobs on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Janitor_Mobs</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>January</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: In a couple minutes we’re going to…” Peaking at Number 2 on January 13, 2007, it’s “Fergalicious!” The announcement was so loud it rattled the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Japanese</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first time we’d summoned him the same time as Shi Maria, she’d picked him up, skewering him right through his shell, and used his body as a cudgel to beat a group of bowl-headed, Japanese monsters called.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jasha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jasha</b> was a level 65 <a href="bword://Bugbear">Bugbear</a> and a member of Team Free Love, an NPC racing team that competed on the Tenth Floor of the World Dungeon. Jasha was a self-aware NPC racer participating in the vehicular heats on the Tenth Floor. He and his teammate <a href="bword://Radoslav">Radoslav</a> dressed in stereotypical 1960s counter-culture attire, leading Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> to suspect they were undercover cops in a "false advertising" scheme.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The World Dungeon (NPC)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bugbear</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jasha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jasha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jasha and Radoslav</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Jasha and Radoslav is described as already in their driveway at spot number three, watching the crowd of people streaming from our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jaxbrin Amusements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Jaxbrin Amusements, Ltd.” There was a symbol of a smiley-faced star on the other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Jaxbrin Amusements makes Stuffed Figures that summon the creatures they represent. They currently sponsor Daniel <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a>. Jaxbrin Amusements makes the beanie babies that <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a> collected on the Third Floor. Their paper tag has a symbol of a smiley faced star on it.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jaxbrin_Amusements%2C_Limited</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jefferson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Jefferson is described as the life force of such a miser who pissed off the spirit who’d come to show him the errors of his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She made me take the cat tree and place it next to the “Ultra-Stabilized Bed,” which looked like a regular, queen-sized bed with no sheets or pillows and a semi-transparent mattress that looked oddly like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jelly Bomb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jelly Bomb</b> is an explosive <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> crafted on his <a href="bword://Sapper&#x27;s Table">Sapper's Table</a> to derail a train on the Iron Tangle. According to the Demolitions Workshop menu, the device was stable against anything except heavy impact.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Sapper's Table</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jelly Bomb on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jelly_Bomb</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jellyfish Salve</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jellyfish Salve</b> is a potion (salve) on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jellyfish Salve on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jellyfish_Salve</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Jumping Jen-Jen and the other drivers were always complaining, but he… I didn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jersey Shore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It sounds like a rejected character from either the Jersey Shore or one of those jerks from the Skull Empire.” “Uh,” I said, watching the creature pull itself.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jessica Rabbit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Jessica Rabbit is described as close, but the woman’s forehead was too small, and the nose was much too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jesus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This statue and fountain, before it had been turned to glass and frozen, had been of a woman standing in a bowl, arms outstretched in a Jesus pose but with her legs planted in a wide stance, her mouth wide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jesus Christ</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Jesus Christ!” Carl : So this company, Veriluxx, they’re genuinely trying to help poor kids with this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ji-Hoon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ji-Hoon</b> is a deceased Crawler, and the source of Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s first Player Killer Skull. Ji-Hoon was an approximately 25 year-old Asian man (presumably Korean) partied with an unidentified female spellcaster. He had a lot of stat-boosting armor, a dozen enchanted daggers, and <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> access.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Korea</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ji-Hoon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ji-Hoon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jikininki</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Jikininki is described as the most common, the most well-behaved, and the most.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jikininki Janitor Ghouls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jikininki Janitor Ghouls</b> are mobs that appear on the Fourth Floor. They attack the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> at the start of the Fourth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jikininki Janitor Ghouls on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jikininki_Janitor_Ghouls</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Joey</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Joey, whose upper right claw was still stuck to the ceiling, asked, “Temple defense protocols?” Katia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Johnny Quest Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Johnny Quest Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he completed the quest The Show Must Go On.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Johnny Quest Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Johnny_Quest_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Join the guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: You can start your own party, and you can join the guild.” “It would only be for the remainder of this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Join the Matt Dinniman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Join the Matt Dinniman mailing list and get the dope on new releases and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jola</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jola</b>, also known as the Yule Cat, is a massive, mythic creature from Iceland that functions as a rare Totem Card on the Eighth Floor of the Dungeon. Originally owned by Katia, he was given to Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and acts as a heavy-hitting and intimidating (though sometimes stubborn) member of her active T'Ghee card deck.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Yule Cat</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Iceland</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mythic Creature / Giant Persian Cat</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jola on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jola</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juan Gris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “One of those stores had a handheld mirror, but I couldn’t afford… Oh my gosh, I look like a Juan Gris painting.” I didn’t know who Juan Gris was, but I imagined he was someone who sucked at painting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Judge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Judge us.” To my right, G’valt had passed out, and a hand reached over his shoulder and picked a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Judge Deng</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he’s not doing this, Judge Deng sits on one of the highest courts in the galaxy.” “So, he’s a lawyer, like you?”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Judge Judy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The “roasties” were an Asian woman I didn’t recognize and Judge.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Judge Lucian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I looked at the head of Judge Lucian, who was still waiting for his next card, but I thought better of attacking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Judge Victory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The judge—Judge Victory—had caught some flak for dropping a decision in his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jugglers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it was team Sparkles, then the Jugglers, then team Free Love, then the Wild Hunt, then the Lady Dominators, then One Fine Pig, and us at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juice Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Juice Box</b> is an NPC <a href="bword://Changeling">Changeling</a> living in Hump Town on the Fifth Floor, where she is the only prostitute employed by an inn called the Toe. Sister to the changeling principal <a href="bword://Henrik">Henrik</a>, she becomes one of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s most useful sources in the air quadrant.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Hump Town, Fifth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Juice Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Juice_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juice Box and Ferdinand</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’re looking for Juice Box and Ferdinand.” “That is why I am here.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juice Box and Louis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We moved a table aside and allowed Juice Box and Louis to stand in the center, holding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juice Box’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And in the meantime, with Juice Box’s squad of changelings and their ability to change to basilica guards, they could hopefully keep the gods at bay should any rules.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Juicer</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Juicer</b> was a First Floor Neighborhood Boss occupying the troglodyte neighborhood. He was defeated by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. The Juicer is roughly 6'6" tall and humanoid, with a scaly body and tail, and a "preposterously muscled physique, covered in tributaries of bulging veins as thick as my finger." His scaly lizard head is supported by an improbably beefy neck, and zit-like pustules circle his beady eyes. His arms are so large that he can hardly seem to lower them, and his enormous thighs force him into a ridiculously wide stance that causes him to waddle when he moves.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> First Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Troglodyte/Human Monstrosity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 24</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Juicer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Juicer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jump</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “For a level 40, he sure was fragile.” “Jump down there, loot his body, and then we’ll run into that saferoom while…” A red dot appeared, leaping out the water with a sudden, violent.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jump Around</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Peaking at Number 2 on October 10 th , 1992, it’s, “Jump Around!” The music wasn’t as booming as usual because the alarm module had been sealed inside a metal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jumper spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: In order to break into the magistrate’s home, we first needed to get into an alleyway, scale the exterior of a small building we’d scoped out earlier, and then use the Puddle Jumper spell to hop over to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jumping Jen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Jumping Jen-Jen and the other drivers were always complaining, but he… I didn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She ripped Jun’s eye out of his face after he died, and then she ripped her own eye out to install.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Junkie's Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Junkie's Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for the <a href="bword://You Crossed a Line You Didn&#x27;t Even Know Was There Achievement">You Crossed a Line You Didn't Even Know Was There Achievement</a> for using a Divine Suffering Artifact for the first time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> You Crossed a Line You Didn't Even Know Was There Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Junkie's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Junkie%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Jurgen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Jurgen</b> is a crawler that the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> first meet on the Eighth Floor. Jurgen entered the Dungeon with his pregnant wife, Heidi. On the Third Floor, he chose the Harii class, a type of barbarian. At some point, he chose to worship the God Donar. Jurgen often speaks to and about Heidi as if she is with him in the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 62</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Jurgen on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Jurgen</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Just Wait Until Your Daddy Gets Home</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Just Wait Until Your Daddy Gets Home – Wheeled Bomb.</b> Type: High Yield Thermobaric Explosive. Also imbues Fear within blast radius.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Just Wait Until Your Daddy Gets Home on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Just_Wait_Until_Your_Daddy_Gets_Home</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Justice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Justice along with a surprisingly large assortment of sluggalos were all working together on a special project, something he’d been planning for a long time, and we let him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Justice Light</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Justice Light</b> is a <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> former crawler from the White Cliffs, best known as the author of the eighth edition of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a></i>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> White Cliffs</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 32</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Justice Light on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Justice_Light</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Justice Light’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was clearly not something built by Justice Light’s team, but an older tunnel, built and fortified by the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kaiju</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kandy Newton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia : Who the hell is Kandy Newton, and where the hell are you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kane</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: First we’re going to take the watch from Henrik, then we’re going to get to that castle, take the throne room and take the watch from Kane, and then we’re going to get the third and final piece from the mage.”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Karl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Louis: Wait, who is this Karl-with-a-K.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia Grim</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Katia Grimmsdóttir</b> (shortened to "Grim" in her crawler ID) is a former art professor and a crawler from Reykjavík, Iceland, in <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. She joins <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s party on the Third Floor and becomes a member of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>, serving as the team's shape-shifting tank. She is one of Carl and Donut's closest friends and most trusted allies.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Reykjavik, Iceland</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Doppelgänger</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Katia Grim on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Katia_Grim</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Katia</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Team Katia is a group of crawlers centred around <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Team Katia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Team_Katia</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Annie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had a sudden memory of Katia and Annie from the dream, and I realized this was the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Bautista</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have Katia and Bautista with what’s left of the 105th flanking your position so we can keep the west opened.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So Katia and Carl and me went to kill the snake lady, but when I got there, my plan was to go say hi to and possibly kill my own mother—her name is Theia—but when I went to her place, it really wasn’t her but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle took a team of fighters, including Katia and Donut, to range down to the outskirts of station 24 to fight and grind on the things, which were now all level 14-17 Krakaren Juvenile Clonelings.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Katia and Elle is described as already on it, telling everybody they would personally hunt down and murder anybody who so much as shot an arrow at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Eva</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia and Eva had the same one, and the two of them entered character creation.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had multiple, increasingly frantic messages from Katia and Mordecai, and I quickly answered them, telling them we were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before the first shots could hit them, Katia and Prepotente both quaffed Black Nimbus.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Tran</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Is this what happened with you and Katia and Tran on the submarine?” She hadn’t been present the last time this happened, and she’d bitched about it for an hour.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia and Vernon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia and Vernon stopped their discussion to watch as I pulled the zipper on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia Grim</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Katia Grimmsdóttir</b> (shortened to "Grim" in her crawler ID) is a former art professor and a crawler from Reykjavík, Iceland, in <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. She joins <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s party on the Third Floor and becomes a member of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>, serving as the team's shape-shifting tank. She is one of Carl and Donut's closest friends and most trusted allies.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Reykjavik, Iceland</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Doppelgänger</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Katia Grim on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Katia_Grim</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I don’t remember seeing him win any championships.” Katia shot Chris once.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Katia’s team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut and I were joining up with Katia’s team, and we were going to do some hunting while we prepared for the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Keep</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I had to take a potion.” “Keep your Mute spell ready!” Her new Mute spell would only last a handful of seconds, especially on a boss monster like Asojano, but if we timed it correctly, we could keep him from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Keep Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Keep Mongo back until we know what we’re facing.” We’d circled the entire park a few times, having to deal with more of those Duende mobs on one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Keeping the Best of You</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Keeping the Best of You.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Keith</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Keith is described as a green bubble alien named Guru-san, and his art piece was a sculpture that looked and moved like a real-life, lava-spitting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kept A Rollin' Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Kept A Rollin' Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for taking control of the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a> with instruction from <a href="bword://Fire Brandy">Fire Brandy</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kept A Rollin' Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kept_A_Rollin%27_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kevin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kevin</b> is the orange-hued, four-eyed, lizard-like alien host of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. He gives breathless rundowns of each floor on the recap episodes, and also provides live running commentaries during Special Events with <a href="bword://Magnificent Troy">Magnificent Troy</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> n/a</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kevin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kevin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kevin and Magnificent Troy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I did ask them to make Kevin and Magnificent Troy come and do commentary, but it looks like that Troy guy is currently in an alcohol-fueled state of unconsciousness, and I promised I wouldn’t poke at those.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kevin and Troy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The window cracked again, spiderwebbing as Kevin and Troy oohed and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Khepri</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Khepri</b> is a bug-themed deity that is a light god of Rebirth and Sunrise. He is currently a retainer for Adrasteia, formerly a retainer for Khnum. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>, and <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> get marked for death by this god when they storm into his temple on the Ninth Floor to kill <a href="bword://Vinata">Vinata</a> during Operation Snake Pit.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 52</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Khepri on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Khepri</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Khulan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had toyed with the idea of pulling out my ring of Divine Suffering and marking Ren, or maybe Khulan, like I had with Miriam, but I didn’t have.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kibben</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We landed here, and everybody was fighting and running, and he wouldn’t let me take her.” I wasn’t certain who Kibben was, though I remembered there’d been a high-elf stablemaster at the castle before we’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai: Kid, this is so far off the codex, we don’t even know if the system will allow her to do.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kids</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Kids is described as a lot stupider than adults, after.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kiki</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kiki’s one surviving client was Daniel Bautista, who instead of taking a deal had gone with the other crawlers into the cleaner bots in that insane gambit to get off the playing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kill them with power!” In addition to the guard, there was a desk here, guarding the entrance to the portal we just.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill Hellik</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Of the four remaining possible deities, two of those gods—one god and one goddess, actually—came with church quests, which were deity-specific quests like my Kill Hellik.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill his family</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill lady</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was the kill, kill, kill lady, but her voice had gone up a pitch, likely because she was talking directly to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill my mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You told me I could go kill my mother, but when I went there, she was an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill the Blasphemer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kill the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill the boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: But not five hours later, he used that spell to kill the boss holding the lockbox key, and it saved the lives of us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill the god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was unclear if I’d still have to kill the god if it turned out he had nothing to do with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill two teams</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I will pray she finds other ways to inflict her chaos on the world.” “Why are you refusing to answer Carl’s question about why you wanted him to kill two teams?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kill your mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Especially for a creature who is probably going to get us murdered by an angry god at any moment.” “I’m going to kill your mother.” Mordecai : I know I’m going to regret this, but I have an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killed</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dirigible Gnome Corpse – Killed by falling from a great height with an assist by the Crawler Bitch Boy Carl.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killed by Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Killed by Crawler Grand Champion Best in Dungeon Princess Donut with an assist by Crawler Royal Bodyguard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killed by Crawler Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Killed by Crawler Frank Q.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killed My Son</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Guys, change of plans!” Quest Update: Find Out Who Killed My.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: – Anyway, Peaking at Number 3 on June 20, 2020, despite first coming out in 1992, It’s “Killing in the Name.” The Rage Against the Machine Song echoed, powerfully loud, literally shaking the ground louder than.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Killing a borough boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Killing a borough boss with more than 10.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kimaris</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Stuffed Kimaris Figure">Stuffed Kimaris Figure</a></b> is a <a href="bword://Stuffed Figure">Stuffed Figure</a> made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>. It is a "Unique" Item, and one of the most valuable items in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Miss Quill's collection</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kimaris on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kimaris</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kimmy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Is he going to give Carl a lap dance?” “Well, I am naming him Kimmy,” Samantha announced as she followed Louis out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kina</b> is a sea goddess affiliated with the <a href="bword://Enchanted Spiked Knee Pads of the Munificent Goddess Kina">Enchanted Spiked Knee Pads of the Munificent Goddess Kina</a>. She does not have any known enemies.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 56</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kina on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kina</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kind</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Kind of like how a manager worked before they changed the rules, but instead of being a non-corporeal avatar, they’re really.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kinda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s basically two hits of Glory Bound, but compounded with a subdermal gel and mixed with a potion called ‘Tough Love.’ Kinda like that anti-alcohol potion I gave to Louis and Firas on the fifth floor, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Blaine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>King Blaine</b> is an ancient king who was once the lover of the banished minor deity Psamathe, better known in the Royal Court as <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>, and the father of her sand ooze daughter, <a href="bword://Mrs. Ghazi">Mrs. Ghazi</a>. He has never appeared on-page; everything known about him so far comes from what Samantha and others say about him.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: King Blaine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/King_Blaine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Croissant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>King Croissant</b> is a a fuzzy black kitten with a teeny tiny headset microphone. He was meant as a replacement for <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. He is level 60. He was used as a T'Ghee card by The <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: King Croissant on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/King_Croissant</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Finian</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>King Finian</b> was the King of the High Elves. He is the father of <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a> and <a href="bword://Imogen">Imogen</a>. He had a relationship with Signet's <a href="bword://Mother">Mother</a>, resulting in the birth of Signet.</li><li><b>Race:</b> High Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: King Finian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/King_Finian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Rust</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>King Rust</b> is king of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>. He is husband to <a href="bword://Queen Consort Ugloo">Queen Consort Ugloo</a> and father to Prince <a href="bword://Stalwart">Stalwart</a>, Princess <a href="bword://Formidable">Formidable</a>, and <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: King Rust on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/King_Rust</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Rust’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: King Rust’s children are all trying to murder each other, and Princess D’Nadia is probably going to do something stupid.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But now, because of the death of her father, her brother Stalwart is now King Stalwart, and his own children—all six of the little brats, including that one with the weird thing growing on the side of his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>King’s Point</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Look, there’s the castle!” He pointed out the cockpit, but all I could see in the darkness was haze and a few lights, indicating the King’s Point, headquarters of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kiss skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I activated the Daughter’s Kiss skill, and I jumped off the second story, landing right atop the fleeing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kiwi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kiwi</b> is a female <a href="bword://Ursine">Ursine</a> NPC, formerly a <a href="bword://Mongoliensis">Mongoliensis</a>, first encountered in the Hunting Grounds. Deer in the Headlights Skill.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mongoliensis</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 11, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kiwi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kiwi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kiwi and Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She launched herself into the air and landed amongst Kiwi and Mongo, biting onto the dying form of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kiwi and Tina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to Carl and Sledgie, we got all of Signet’s former tattoos and helpers, Kiwi and Tina, and all of the changelings into the elf castle, and we teleported the whole castle to the ninth floor, meaning we.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Knife of Frozen Tears</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>This blade is given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> by <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a> on the Eighth Floor when she gives Carl her pet <a href="bword://Garret">Garret</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Knife of Frozen Tears on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Knife_of_Frozen_Tears</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Knight Rider</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s worse than that Knight Rider show you love so much.” Once again, this creature was a person from our world, though without the description it wouldn’t be quite as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Knock</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: While they only had a few of the fuel-air, city-killing Knock-Knocks left on the airship’s underbelly, there were dozens of round, smaller-yield bombs hanging there like water.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>KNOW HOW I FEEL</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: YOU KNOW HOW I FEEL ABOUT FALSE ADVERTISING.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Know the rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Next time make sure you comes in first place so we get better choices.” “Do you know the rules about crew members and if they can drive?” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Knuckle skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I was going to utilize my bare knuckle skill, I really needed to find something to protect my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kobold</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kobolds</b> are a Second Floor mob that resemble chihuahuas in size and appearance, but stand upright and walk on their hind legs. They wear metal caps with a spike, and chainmail armor that appears to have been made from beer tabs.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kobold on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kobold</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Koki</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Koki: origin: Japan; race/species: <a href="bword://Human">Human</a>; first scene: Chapter 14, Book 3.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Japan</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 14, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Koki on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Koki</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The Krakaren are a hive mind that runs the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a> and is financing the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Apothecary</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Apothecary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Apothecary</b> is a galaxy-spanning collective mind and the entity the Dungeon caricatures as the <a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a>. "Apothecary" is the truer translation of her name, taken from her ability to synthesize elements; the dungeon's version bends that translation into an insult.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Krakaren; The Collective; The Apothecary Lady</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Apothecary on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Apothecary</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a></b> on the Fourth Floor is a Borough Boss, and part of the Iron Tangle political statement. The Iron Tangle is a massive political statement by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>, and the fact that there is a version of Krakaren in the level is directed towards the actions of the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary, Krakaren</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Fourth_Floor%29</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The food truck was spinning in circles, flames pouring from the side window near the deep fryer, missiles launching one after another at the Krakaren boss as sluggalos crawled over the.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a></b> on the Fourth Floor is a Borough Boss, and part of the Iron Tangle political statement. The Iron Tangle is a massive political statement by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>, and the fact that there is a version of Krakaren in the level is directed towards the actions of the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary, Krakaren</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Fourth_Floor%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a> Clone</b> is a Neighborhood Boss on the Second Floor, and part of the running <a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> storyline. There are at least three separate instances of the Krakaren Clone on the level.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Second Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Second Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Second_Floor%29</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a></b> on the Fourth Floor is a Borough Boss, and part of the Iron Tangle political statement. The Iron Tangle is a massive political statement by the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>, and the fact that there is a version of Krakaren in the level is directed towards the actions of the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary, Krakaren</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 10</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Fourth_Floor%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b><a href="bword://Krakaren">Krakaren</a> Clone</b> is a Neighborhood Boss on the Second Floor, and part of the running <a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> storyline. There are at least three separate instances of the Krakaren Clone on the level.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Second Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Apothecary</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Clone (Second Floor) on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Clone_%28Second_Floor%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Crotch Dumplings</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Krakaren Crotch Dumplings</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that emerge from the corpse of a mob suffering from stage 3 of the DTs. Each Crotch Dumpling is about the size of a grain of rice.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krakaren Crotch Dumplings on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krakaren_Crotch_Dumplings</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If this whole thing is a metaphor like you say, about how bad the Krakaren monsters really are for the universe, about how they’re using the tunnel system to spread their influence or whatever, then I’m.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krakaren Prime</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Paca is an agent for Krakaren Prime, who is stuck in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Krasue</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Krasue</b> are a mob on the Third Floor. They are associated with the Sex Workers Who Fell from the Heavens Quest. Krasue appear as floating female heads with the entrails of the bodies hanging below.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Krasue on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Krasue</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kravyad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kravyad</b> are either a boss or an NPC, depending on the person describing them, that work for the Iron Tangle, responsible for employee management. They are located at Station 435 where the employees get off the train.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kravyad on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kravyad</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kua</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They sold the Kua-Tin a damaged and deranged AI and allowed it to be installed into the planet.” “That is a lie!” the mantid.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kua-Tin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Kua-Tin</b> are an alien race of tiny, water-breathing, humanoid fish. They are the dominant species of the <a href="bword://Borant System">Borant System</a> and principal owners of the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kua-Tin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kua-Tin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kua-Tin Rebreather</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Kua-Tin Rebreather</b> is roughly the size of a cat collar, and can only be equipped by knee-height creatures with gills. The small, barely noticeable device wraps around the wearer's throat to cover their gills, and occasionally sprays water and mist.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Kua-Tin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kua-Tin Rebreather on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kua-Tin_Rebreather</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kuhli</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Kuhli</b> are a spotted, gecko-like creature.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kuhli on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kuhli</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kung Fu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d added cheesy, Kung Fu noises to all of his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kyle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kyryap</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Kyryap</b> is a mysterious "dread" entity associated with Club <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>, responsible for setting traps that have twice caught <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> unaware.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kyryap on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kyryap</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ladies</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Ladies, give him some room!” She returned to my shoulder to get a better view of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ladies and Gentlemen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Get ready.” “Ladies and Gentlemen, what do we have here?” Kevin called, clearly surprised he’d just been sent to cover this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ladies Love</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The largest of the regular pubs on this street was called The Ladies Love It, and it was from within this bar came the loudest music and most.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ladies Love It</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The sign was a large, wooden placard depicting a grinning, bald man with “Ladies Love It” tattooed over his upper.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lady Dominators</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t know what upgrade the Lady Dominators had picked, but if it was a shield, it likely didn’t stop both magical attacks, which was what Donut’s bolt would be, and physical attacks, which was what the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lajabless</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lajabless</b> is a race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. It breaks the day in half, spending half the day with boosted magic (but depleted strength), or half the day with doubled strength (but magic costs more to cast).</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lajabless on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lajabless</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lamashtu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lamashtu</b>, nicknamed "Lammy" by <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>, is a deity who has been reduced to a subjugated god and forced to serve as a donkey-mount under the control of the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> during the events of Book 8.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lamashtu on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lamashtu</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Laminak</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Laminaks</b> are a fairy-class mob found on the Dungeon's Second Floor. They are fat, human-like, crow-sized fairies with buzzing hummingbird wings. They can fly lightning fast, and their hands glow red when they cast spells.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Laminak on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Laminak</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Laminak Rev</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Laminak Rev-Up Consultant Elite – Level 6 The second tier of the Rev-Up empire, these Laminak consultants don’t need to speak to a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Land Crabs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trained in the deadly art of Caribbean Kung Fu, the monk seal sentry’s sacred duty is to protect the coast and hunting grounds from the impending invasion of their dread enemy, the Red Maníseros Land Crabs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Land War</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: These guys were added to a former Land War world undergoing reparative terraforming when the company in charge of the project suddenly went.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Langley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4,5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Langley</b> is a crawler from Dungeon Crawler World: Earth and the leader of a six-man team of fellow archers, all Finland-based car salesmen who picked the Archer Class together. He and his team become close, trusted allies of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s party during the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 3, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Langley on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Langley</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Large</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Large, muscular, and smart, Hobgoblins excel at trapmaking, explosives management, and all-out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Large creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mongo ran across the room and started sniffing at the orc in the corner, but the large creature—a woman orc, actually—ignored the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Large group of crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And that Osvaldo crawler you were asking about seems to be riding it out in the woods along with a large group of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Large room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: 41 Time to Level Collapse: 4 Days, 6 hours Views: 1.4 Trillion Followers: 352.2 Billion Favorites: 21.4 Billion I still had plenty of moonshine left, and Donut and I spent the hour torching all the pupae in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Large town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I spent the moment looking out at the world, marveling at the sights and sounds of this large.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Larracos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: As of right now, the one and only Desperado Club entrance on the ninth floor was still fucked, plus everyone except the NPCs were kicked out of Larracos a day or two after the floor started, which meant I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Larracos College</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: As a glass mage who specializes in the study of both transmutation and the teleportation of energized particles, Ghazi told his colleagues at the Larracos College of Magecraft that he was seeking out the Gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Larracos College of Magic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We went back outside, coming to that same tower in the middle of the Larracos College of Magic that we’d first used all those days.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Larracos level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’ll bring back the original dancers, and our whole crew will get thrown to the Larracos level Meat Shields.” He thumbed over his shoulder at a group of male dancers I didn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Las Vegas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: A Las Vegas-style neon sign stood above the large building, but only the red, bottom half of the arrow was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Later</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Later, when he would think back upon that briefest moment, from the time he stepped into the storage and until the moment he was pulled free, he would recall it with no small amount of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Laundry Day</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The fleshers have mostly wrapped themselves around the enemies already, despite their invulnerability, and Laundry Day might remove them and negate the effect, which will cause the fleshers to attack our own.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Laundry Day Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Laundry Day</b> is a utility spell that strips armor off the target. Obtained by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on Floor 6 through her membership to the <a href="bword://Dungeon Book of the Floor Club">Dungeon Book of the Floor Club</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Laundry Day Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Laundry_Day_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lay on the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mongo lay on the floor next to her, cramped against the now-empty bomb.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lazarus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She screamed as we plummeted, but she angled herself back toward the building, almost like we were zip-lining, and we glided toward the hopping up-and-down form of Donut, who had Lazarus summoned next to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lazarus Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Lazarus Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he is hit by <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a>'s unknown spell on the Third Floor. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> also receives the achievement, but the reward is: <b>“You’re lucky to be alive. That’s your reward.”</b> <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> suspects it is because her health zeroed out but she was saved by her Cockroach Skill.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lazarus Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lazarus_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lazarus-A-Bang-Bang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lazarus-A-Bang-Bang</b> is a Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> on the Eighth Floor. This card is held by the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>. It was created by combining the <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a> and <a href="bword://HeyZoos">HeyZoos</a> cards.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Combo Card</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 39</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lazarus-A-Bang-Bang on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lazarus-A-Bang-Bang</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>LBC Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>LBC</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded when a crawler successfully taunts an opponent with a garment. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Eighth Floor after getting attacked by Nightgaunts, who are driven into a rage by his <a href="bword://Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness">Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: LBC Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/LBC_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Le Mouvement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Le Mouvement</b> was a crawler party comprised of (150) African soldiers armed with AK-47s and at least one Red Cross worker. They were featured on the third episode of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i> and descended to the Second Floor when the countdown timer struck 6 hours.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Le Mouvement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Le_Mouvement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leadership Unit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leaned against the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You two, be useful and hold this closed.” Louis and Firas jumped up and leaned against the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leaped across the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She put her headset away and leaped across the room and landed on his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Least level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was hoping to be at least level 13 by the time we hit the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leave</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Leave the creative to me.” The device wasn’t complicated, but it had to be precisely built, especially here with the bumpy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leave Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl : Leave Mongo in his cage for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leave the bar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was also the path Astrid would take today when she came to deal with a very drunk sex doll head who refused to leave the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leave the party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl has a stupid plan where he’s going to leave the party and try this really idiotic thing to get a bunch of immortals who hate each other on a floor where they can’t die at the same time and then get them.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leave the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s this program called Inuyasha that I plan on finishing… Anyway, the ooze doesn’t allow me to leave the room very.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leaving the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: These crawlers who were leaving were tearing apart their vehicles and trading upgrades, and they were all using Milk’s method to add a spell into the Book of Voodoo before they disappeared, leaving the dungeon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leaving the party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Okay, Carl.” Thankfully, the garage wouldn’t get affected by me leaving the party, and Imani was ready to join my segregated room back into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Glamoured Fragment – Ursine Foot (Left), Ursine Leg (Left), Ursine Pelvis (four pieces, reformed), Ursine thigh (Right), Ursine Lower Intestine (two pieces, reformed), Ursine Kidney.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left Fang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia pulled Eva’s old sword, the Left Fang of the Green Sultan, the one she’d dropped on the train all those floors ago, and used it cut the hand free from her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left Fang of the Green Sultan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Left Fang of the Green Sultan</b> is a saber previously owned by Eva. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> picks it up and gives it to <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> on the Fourth Floor. The saber is part of an enchanted set, but the magical abilities only apply when the set is together.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Eva</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Left Fang of the Green Sultan on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Left_Fang_of_the_Green_Sultan</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left in the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re still in their early stages of planning, but the Shadow Mimics who are loose in my dungeon are one part of the Big Six groups left in the dungeon trying to either take over or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left on the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I want your thoughts on this.” I pulled out the roll of paper Mordecai left on the floor by his alchemy table, the one with all the known train stations mapped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But if I didn’t bring her back, and she wasn’t in a saferoom before Donut and I left the floor, we’d lose.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left the party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia left the party so she could hunt down Eva, her player killer ex-best friend, and that was really sad because I liked being in a party with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left the realm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Khepri chittered, but there was a crack, followed by the notification: Khepri has left the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And the moment we move in, I fear the presence in him would react.” Samantha had left the room but was now back, hovering over all of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Left to Fester</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew from the cookbook that debuff was called “Left to Fester.” I examined the ring’s properties to make sure it was working as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legba</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Legba</b> is a god who is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon, with <a href="bword://Ogun">Ogun</a>, <a href="bword://Shango">Shango</a>, <a href="bword://Oshun">Oshun</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Inle">Inle</a>, and <a href="bword://Yemaya">Yemaya</a>. During the Thorn Room Boss Battle, if his shrine is repaired: “If repaired, will give everyone a fifty percent chance to avoid all future smites. All will learn the level-15 <a href="bword://Confusing Fog Spell">Confusing Fog Spell</a>. Legba will ascend.”</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Legba on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Legba</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tell us who your source is and how they communicate with you, and you will be returned to the dungeon with no penalties, and you will be given a Legendary box that will contain an item that will all but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had literally dozens of achievements and boxes, including a Legendary boss box and two different quest boxes for the Vengeance of the Daughter and the Recital.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary boss box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had literally dozens of achievements and boxes, including a Legendary boss box and two different quest boxes for the Vengeance of the Daughter and the Recital.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tell us who your source is and how they communicate with you, and you will be returned to the dungeon with no penalties, and you will be given a Legendary box that will contain an item that will all but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary Diva</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Character Actor skill is level six now, and she received all the starting skills of the Legendary Diva class plus a few upgraded.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary Master</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The human was a Legendary Master , and it was one of Li Na’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary Pet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Legendary Pet Box had the same symbol etched on it as the Bronze Pet Box, but this thing was three times the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legendary Pet Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Legendary Pet Box had the same symbol etched on it as the Bronze Pet Box, but this thing was three times the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Legionnaires of the Damned Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Legionnaires of the Damned</b></i> is a summoning spell that reanimates recently unequipped weapons or pieces of armor with undead wailing spirits, which are considered minions of the caster.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Legionnaires of the Damned Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Legionnaires_of_the_Damned_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lemig</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: On the floating screen, a close-up view of me on the cot in the infirmary appeared, followed by a second screen showing the caterpillar in the middle of the ballroom, surrounded by dozens of the Lemig.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lemig Sortion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A democratic government consisting of multiple races. They vote on everything but to stop something they've voted on requires an 80% majority. Their leader is a fuzzy caterpillar.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lemig Sortion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lemig_Sortion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Leon</b> is a <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> NPC from the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 11</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Leon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Leon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leprous Bandit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most children in the universe know the exciting tale of the Leprous Bandit, the infamous human thief whose people were starving to death because of a planet-wide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Less</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Less than half of them still had their hats which would allow them passage through the only other portals in the area, the portals above the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lesser Demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lesser <a href="bword://Demons">Demons</a></b> are a type of Demon associated with fire. Lesser Demons often power ovens, castle heating systems, and steam-powered contraptions.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lesser Demon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lesser_Demon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Let</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s just see what it is.” Soon after Katia had been sponsored by the Princess Formidable, the youngest sister of Prince Maestro and Crown Prince Stalwart of the Skull Empire, we explained to her everything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>LET ME KNOW</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: THIS IS WHY YOU NEED TO LET ME KNOW THE.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Let There Be Chaos Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Let There Be Chaos Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for summoning the god <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a> into the dungeon. Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Let There Be Chaos Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Let_There_Be_Chaos_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Letter from the Council</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Letter from the Council</b> is a letter that is part of the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a> mystery. It is written by <a href="bword://Ghazi">Ghazi</a> to <a href="bword://Commandant Kane">Commandant Kane</a>. Post Script</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Letter from the Council on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Letter_from_the_Council</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Letter from the Glass Wizard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Letter from the Glass Wizard</b> is a letter that is part of the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a> mystery. It is written by <a href="bword://Ghazi">Ghazi</a> to <a href="bword://Commandant Kane">Commandant Kane</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Letter from the Glass Wizard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Letter_from_the_Glass_Wizard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Letting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dekoki the kappa had regained the ability to move after the Minister of Blood-Letting poured a beer into her head bowl, and Louis was helping her reapply her Saran Wrap as she profusely apologized to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s spells currently were Heal – Level 1 (Max) Torch – Level 10 Magic Missile – Level 9 Puddle Jumper – Level 3 Second Chance – Level 5 Heal Critter – Level 1 Clockwork Triplicate – Level 1 I hadn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level Collapse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the north, near the edge of the map and overlooking a clear valley filled with lower-level monsters, placed there so the hunters can train while the crawlers make their way through the lower.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level of spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d gained one called Raise Forest , which caused trees and plants over a one kilometer area per level of spell to quickly sprout, which was just.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level of this skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The amount of pressure you can bring to bear upon an enemy with your unshod foot is increased by 10% with each level of this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level-Up Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Level-Up Potions</b> are potions that may be awarded by quests. They presumably cause the target to gain one crawler level.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quests</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Level-Up Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Level-Up_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Level-Up, Baby! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Level-Up, Baby!</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded the first time a crawler levels up. Crawlers typically gain (3) stat points each time they level up, but cannot distribute the stat points until they choose a race and class on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Level-Up, Baby! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Level-Up%2C_Baby%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Leveled-Up Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Leveled-Up Frank</b> is a <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> created specifically for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s Eighth Floor battle against the <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a>, although it never comes into play. After the battle, Carl and Donut save this card and <a href="bword://Alpha Male Carl">Alpha Male Carl</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Night Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 47</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Leveled-Up Frank on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Leveled-Up_Frank</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Levi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “After I knock this metal barrier down, I am going to send my minions in, and one of them is going to re-sleeve.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Levi the 7th</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>An NPC, <b>Levi the 7th</b> is the engineer of the <i>Dismembered Unlimited</i> and the subject of the Fourth Floor Levi is on the Menu Quest. Levi the 7th is a Symbiote: a <a href="bword://Hobgoblin">Hobgoblin</a> skeleton which has been animated and merged with a <a href="bword://Flesher">Flesher</a>'s skin, which in this case once belonged to a Basher Troll. At first glance, Levi looks like "a grim reaper wearing a poncho and mask." Inspection reveals a pale, sickly creature wearing only an engineer's hat; green-tinged flesh hangs from his bony body like an ill-fitted sheet.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon: Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Troll Flesher and Hobgoblin Skellie Symbiote</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Levi the 7th on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Levi_the_7th</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lexis</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lexis</b> is <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a>'s assistant and an associate producer of <i>Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette.</i> Lexis is extremely matter-of-fact and efficient in the studio and in combat. She is the first <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> <a href="bword://Human">Human</a> that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> meet, and she explains the tunnelling and recording process clearly and concisely.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 27</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lexis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lexis</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Jun</b> is a Crawler from China. He is partied with his sister <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> within the guild <a href="bword://Safehome Yolanda">Safehome Yolanda</a>, and is a close ally of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Jun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Jun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Na</b> is a crawler from China on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth who entered the Dungeon alongside her brother <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Changbi Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Na on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Na</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Jun</b> is a Crawler from China. He is partied with his sister <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> within the guild <a href="bword://Safehome Yolanda">Safehome Yolanda</a>, and is a close ally of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Jun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Jun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun and Li Na</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Zhang and a few of his fellow party members came in and got their ranks, but Li Jun and Li Na hadn’t come in, as they were seeking out a healer who might be able to regrow Li Jun’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun and Zhang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: After shaking hands with Li Jun and Zhang, I turned my attention to Chris, who was currently based in Beijing with Li Na’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Na</b> is a crawler from China on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth who entered the Dungeon alongside her brother <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Changbi Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Na on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Na</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na and Li Jun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I aimed toward a girder and jumped, landing upon it as Li Na and Li Jun both jumped down one more level to be near.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na’s Xing Tian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Li Na’s Xing Tian totem was a level 80 creature that was a human without a.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Jun</b> is a Crawler from China. He is partied with his sister <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> within the guild <a href="bword://Safehome Yolanda">Safehome Yolanda</a>, and is a close ally of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Jun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Jun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Na</b> is a crawler from China on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth who entered the Dungeon alongside her brother <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Changbi Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Na on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Na</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Liddi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Liddi, the woman alien, was dressed in some sort of Wish dot com superhero costume that didn’t fit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Light</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Light poured into the hot room as the distant end of the barn fell off the edge of the encroaching dirt cliff, tumbling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Light Healer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: All of them were level 16, but there was a menagerie of magic types, from a Fire Mage to a Summoner to a Light.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Light on Your Feet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Light on Your Feet skill simply meant she’d be able to pounce higher and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This guy, who had the ridiculous name “The Talent” had three scrolls of Lightning and an astounding amount of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And this tree protected you from a lightning attack?” Clint answered as I bent down to once again retrieve Samantha, who was now shouting for me to let her do it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning bolt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I must’ve watched a show about it or something.” The patch on the shoulder was in the shape of a shield, and it held a crossed lightning bolt and a magic wand being grasped by a talon with a tree in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning bolts</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The boss, his large face pressed against the ground, fired multiple lightning bolts in every which direction as it screamed in pain, showering.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning Lou</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lightning Lou</b> was an elderly bloodhound who lived at a gas station near the Shepherd's House in Homestead, Florida, before the transformation.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Homestead, FL</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dog</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 50</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lightning Lou on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lightning_Lou</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lightning spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I started to send a response but I had to hit the ground as a Chain Lightning spell shot forth from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lights</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Lights is described as built into the tunnel walls at random.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lika</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lika</b> is the fictional protagonist of <i>Troubadour's Bounty,</i> a popular series of plays performed in Larracos on the Ninth Floor. A half-<a href="bword://Naiad">Naiad</a> half-<a href="bword://Human">Human</a> bard cleric, her character is loosely inspired by an "inaccurate history of Psamathe."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Half-Naiad, Half-Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lika on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lika</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Like a Moth to the Flame Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Like a Moth to the Flame</b> is a rare achievement granted the first time a crawler deals damage to a mob more than 75 levels above them. Both <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive this achievement after their first encounter with the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a> on the Second Floor: Donut for hitting it with <i><a href="bword://Magic Missile">Magic Missile</a></i>, and Carl for striking it with a <a href="bword://Carl&#x27;s Jug O&#x27;Boom">Carl's Jug O'Boom</a>. This is the second Platinum award Carl receives inside the Dungeon, after the Platinum <a href="bword://Shoe Box">Shoe Box</a> awarded for the This Little Piggy Went to Market! achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Like a Moth to the Flame Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Like_a_Moth_to_the_Flame_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Like Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Like Donut said, it wasn’t unpleasant, but it was like they got the ocean scent just a little.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Likely</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was also an urn, which I realized contained the remains of a dog named “Chance.” Likely the same dog who was tattooed on the woman’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Limited</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was a sign under both that said, “Limited to one each.” “Who bought that seventh floor upgrade?” “The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Limited ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I am supposed to follow you onto the field for major conflicts, and as such, I have the limited ability to teleport, even during Ramp-Up, though I warn you, I cannot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Limp Richard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Limp Richard</b> is a <a href="bword://Mole Man">Mole Man</a> NPC who operates a general store <i>"Limp Richard's Sundries"</i> at station 83 in the Iron Tangle. Described as a short, squat anthropomorphized mole, complete with reading glasses. He sits in the doorway to his shop reading a book. He runs a general store, but most of the products are not very noteworthy.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mole Man</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 2</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Limp Richard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Limp_Richard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Line Gauged</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Main Passenger-Line.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Linus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Linus is described as an outside-the-dungeon tourist who was replaced by his brother, Minus, a soldier, specifically sent to kill two crawlers in my dungeon in hopes that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lipstick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Lipstick the gremlin was pressed against the back wall of the garage, pinned between the shield and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Liquid Therapy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Liquid Therapy</b> is a potion that protects against insanity, blindness and a host of other debuffs. It lasts until you sleep. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> brewed this for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> to protect them from <a href="bword://Shi Maria">Shi Maria</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Liquid Therapy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Liquid_Therapy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Liquified Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Liquified Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for blowing himself up with the Waffle Maker bomb during the Attack on <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Liquified Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Liquified_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lisa Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I liked Bautista, I thought as I finished building my train bomb, but his Tigran race made him look like a tiger that had been vomited upon by a Lisa Frank.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>List of items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai had given me a list of items to look for that was literally five pages.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Listen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s not going to be Bea and whoever the hell…” “Listen here, pukes,” the Maestro said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Literal Fire Ants</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Literal Fire Ants</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that is part of a trap in the luggage of the Reward Room of the Yellow Line train car on the Iron Tangle.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Literal Fire Ants on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Literal_Fire_Ants</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>LitRPG Books Group</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s the super-awesome extreme LitRPG Books.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Litter box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even though I had known Donut since she was a kitten, I’d never actually lived with a cat before, and things like changing the litter box, not leaving the window open, and finding vomit on my pillow were all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Little Bonnie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Little Bonnie screeched and tumbled off the edge of the kitchen counter, throwing the glass.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Little Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Little Carl doesn’t smell like it, but the rest of you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Live Ammo Ball</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Live ammo balls are a common and versatile ranged weapon of the Dirigible Gnomes. These round, clamshell spheres are designed to be fired great distances using a gnomish Tickle Stick, or they may be manually lobbed like a grenade. They are also fired in large clusters from anti-aircraft point defense batteries. Just be glad you’re not in charge of cleaning up that mess.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Live Ammo Ball on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Live_Ammo_Ball</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Living creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’m not sure your species can see the same color spectrum as a Crest, but with that glow, it should⁠—” “A cat’s vision is vastly superior to the vision of any other living creature,” Donut said, sniffing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Living creatures</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drugs will win because what you’re really doing is going to war against the biological imperative all living creatures have to find comfort and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Living room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s much better than that awful, stolen television you had in the living room.” “It wasn’t stolen,” I said, trying to figure out what I was looking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lizard creatures</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She also had a host of psionic abilities, mostly focused on keeping rock creatures, lizard creatures, and “The Armored Sai,” in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lizard monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, the lizard monsters appeared to be constantly generated, just like the ghouls, and that was making it difficult for them to.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Jun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Jun</b> is a Crawler from China. He is partied with his sister <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> within the guild <a href="bword://Safehome Yolanda">Safehome Yolanda</a>, and is a close ally of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Jun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Jun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Li Na</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Li Na</b> is a crawler from China on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth who entered the Dungeon alongside her brother <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> and his best friend <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Changbi Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Li Na on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Li_Na</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lobbing Lobbers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A mainstay of the Hobgoblin Hob-Lobbing Lobbers, the Hob-Lobber is a stable, mostly-predictable, more practical and tactical solution to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Locomotive Breath Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Locomotive Breath Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for engineering a train derailment of the <i>Ochre Line</i>on the Fourth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Locomotive Breath Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Locomotive_Breath_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Loita</b> works in the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>'s Communications Department. As a <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a> member of the <a href="bword://Borant System">Borant System</a>'s ruling ultra-nationalist party, the <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a>, Loita receives preferential treatment and has her pick of crawlers. <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a> and <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a> were her main accounts until the party disbanded on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-Tin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 34, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loita</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lollipop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And under it in smaller neon letters, it read, “Desperado Club entrance in the back.” Imani had said everyone was avoiding the Hairpin for now, and we were to meet at one next door called the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lone Ranger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Since the phrase came from the old Lone Ranger television show, I was inclined to side with Elle on the matter—plus the AI who had named the upgrade in the first place, though if you’d asked me before this, I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loner Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The seventh and final achievement <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives upon entering the Dungeon. Presumably, all Crawlers who entered the first floor alone received this achievement, including <a href="bword://Miriam Dom">Miriam Dom</a> and <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loner Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loner_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Long</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Long, long before any of you monkeys were even born, this planet was chosen to be used for this season of Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</b> is a DBA and front company for the <a href="bword://Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network">Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</a> (OIPAN), an intergalactic NFC.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Long_Haul_Biological_Waste_Management_Solutions</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lonnie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lonnie</b> is a half-elf former crawler and the head of the Beefy Boys SOF, a squad of mages responsible for maintaining wall buffs and base shields for the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> during the Ninth Floor Faction Wars, until a gondii brain worm takes control of his corpse and turns him into a security threat from within.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lonnie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lonnie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Look</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once it hits five, it’ll be much easier for you.” “Look,” I said, “the real reason we’re here is because we’ve only found one set of stairs down, but there’s a problem.” Mordecai’s eyes widened as I told him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Look at the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: That was a mistake.” “Look at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Looking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Looking about, this second floor was set up similarly to the first, but instead of emerging at the end of a main hallway, I could see that we were in the middle of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Looks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Looks like the orcs are massing at their old border to hold us back, but it’ll be some time before we meet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was different than what she used on the clockwork Sledge.” “Loop-de-Loop,” Mordecai said, his small eyes intent on the.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Celestial Loot Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Celestial Loot Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor for picking up <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>'s <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> <a href="bword://Cloak of the Benevolent Champion">Cloak of the Benevolent Champion</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Celestial Loot Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Celestial_Loot_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loot Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Loot!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler equips a clothing item they found or received inside the Dungeon. Most crawlers should receive this achievement on the first two floors.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loot Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loot_Achievement</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loot Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Loot!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler equips a clothing item they found or received inside the Dungeon. Most crawlers should receive this achievement on the first two floors.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loot Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loot_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loot box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai : All of those upgrades are really expensive, so in addition to the loss of income from the acquisitions—they get a small cut of all dungeon purchases—it’s a tradition for your sponsors to make you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loot Boxes</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Loot Boxes</b> are the primary reward mechanism in Dungeon Crawler World, distributed by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> to crawlers for completing achievements, defeating bosses, finishing quests, or as gifts from viewers and sponsors.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements; Boss kills; Quests; Patrons and viewers</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loot Boxes on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loot_Boxes</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loot Punch Card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Loot Punch Card</b> is an item that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received as part of the <a href="bword://Celestial Loot Achievement">Celestial Loot Achievement</a>. The Loot Punch Card is a little piece of paper with five slots with one slot punched out with a hole in the shape of a foot when Carl receives it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Loot Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Loot Punch Card on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Loot_Punch_Card</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Looter Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Looter Boxes</b> are <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> typically awarded for inventory- or looting-related achievements.</li><li><b>Source:</b> achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Looter Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Looter_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lord of Smallpox</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also known as the Lord of Smallpox, Asojano is one of the most respected and feared of the Orishas, a former god made flesh upon the rise of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lord of the Flies Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lord of the Flies Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for setting a trap that a Crawler triggers. <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a>'s team is the one who triggers the trap.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lord of the Flies Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lord_of_the_Flies_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lorelai</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lorelai</b> is a Level 7 <a href="bword://Goblin Shamanka">Goblin Shamanka</a> NPC who appears on the First Floor. Her name was assigned by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. As a Shamanka, Lorelai specializes in Anguish Magic, which focuses and enhances attack damage. She and <a href="bword://Rory">Rory</a> are second only to the Goblin Chieftan, and are keenly aware that they are mobs inside a construct. When the floor collapses, they "go to sleep until the next Dungeon opens." They believe that if they kill enough crawlers, they will be moved to a lower level.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Goblins</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lorelai on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lorelai</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lorena Bobbitt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I wasn’t quite sure what the exact achievement was for the Lorena Bobbitt box, but it clearly involved something with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lots</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Lots of my colleagues getting work today, and some of them are pretty easy on the receptors, if you know what I’m.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Loud</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Loud, female groaning emanated from the one occupied room followed by the familiar, Spanish voice of Dong Quixote shouting, “Thou hast seen nothing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Louis Santiago 2</b> is an Earth Crawler and a loyal ally of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. Introduced on the Fifth Floor as a drunk, badly under-leveled Pest Exterminator, he grows into the group's pilot and a member of <a href="bword://Team Katia">Team Katia</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Miami, Florida, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Louis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Louis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Britney</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The battle had occurred on the roof of a high-rise apartment building, surrounded by ghosts of Tran’s family, and the hydra itself consisted of both Louis and Britney’s mothers along with members of Bautista’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: As soon as we got back, I was going to rip a new one into Louis and Donut for allowing children to go into battle like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Elle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The name of the upgrade, “Hi-Yo Silver” and not “Heigh- Ho Silver” had caused a great debate amongst some of the crawlers, especially Louis and Elle, and I thought they were actually going to come to blows.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Firas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s basically two hits of Glory Bound, but compounded with a subdermal gel and mixed with a potion called ‘Tough Love.’ Kinda like that anti-alcohol potion I gave to Louis and Firas on the fifth floor, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Juice Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was just Louis and Juice Box and a small group of people watching, including Drick and Ferdinand, who hadn’t left the elf’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Linus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The tall, gaunt mage sat rigid, sandwiched between Louis and Linus, Elle’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis and Samantha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It should be something that Ren would’ve liked.” Louis and Samantha returned, followed closely by Splash Zone, the short, otter-like water mage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis L’Amour</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d traded away all of my Louis L’Amour books, and I’d suddenly realized how much I’d gotten used to reading, even if it was only for a few minutes before I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Louis Santiago</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: For example, apartment 728 is a snapshot loop of what would’ve happened if that cop’s husband had never been a complete douchebag to Louis Santiago 2 during the cop’s funeral when Louis was a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Love doll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The chamber where Donut was fell in, and Ghazi is flipping out about saving his love.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Love Vampire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Love Vampire skill, which she’d received for reaching 100 in charisma, had never worked really well until she’d used the temporary tiara on the previous floor that had effectively doubled her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Love Vampire skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Love Vampire skill, which she’d received for reaching 100 in charisma, had never worked really well until she’d used the temporary tiara on the previous floor that had effectively doubled her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lover Illiana</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Her name is Lover Illiana,” Donut was saying, talking about her singing booth in the training.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Low Thi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Low Thi</b> is one of the crawlers in the Subterranean Quadrant of Bubble #543. Killed <a href="bword://Wynne">Wynne</a> believing doing so would give him access to a map and allow him to complete his quest.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Low Thi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Low_Thi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lower floors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Those plus the multiple mages you plan on placing in the lower floors will make this the safest place on the battlefield, even if the entire might of the Skull Empire’s war mages target it.” “They will target.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lower level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’ve cast it a few times, but it never triggered.” Donut’s love vampire skill allowed her to basically reflect any damage to a mob that was a lower level than.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lower levels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: There are guilds available on this third floor, but the more specialized ones don’t become available until later, so that section isn’t open yet.” “Wait, will the Desperado Club be available on the lower.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Luca Di</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Luca Di</b> is Crawler #10,284,471, a human Pastamancer who reached Level 62 before taking a deal at the end of the 9th floor, and the author of an entry in <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s "A Banquet Fit for a Princess" cookbook.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Luca Di on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Luca_Di</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Lucia dropped the upgrades, and she said she was helping us because Florin had asked, but before she went down the stairs, she went from really friendly to mean again, and she said she was saving something.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucia Mar</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lucia Mar</b> is a young teenage Crawler from South America, one of the Top Ten Crawlers on this season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> and an early rival of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Diah, Azra, Yaz, Jill</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Ecuador</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Lajabless</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lucia Mar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lucia_Mar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucia Mar’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was one of Lucia Mar’s favorite traps, though I suspected her trap skill was so high, she could turn them off and on from afar, controlling them like gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucia Prime</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Lucia Prime is described as not currently in her body, and the girl who was in it had drawn a picture of her grandfather and her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lucian</b> ("Judge Lucian") is a cat show judge from Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s life before the Transformation, later reconstituted by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> as the keymaster head of the <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lucian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lucian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Luciano Fleitas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucienne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lucienne. Mandagot. Level 81 Fink.</b> One of two members of Team Sparkles. Warning: This creature worships <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a> and will be automatically hostile toward you because Diwata HATES your ass. A mandagot is a <a href="bword://Fairy">Fairy</a>-class creature that comes in many shapes, though most are stomach-churning monstrosities that are supposed to be “cute.” These things are a combination of two or three fuzzy woodland creatures. Legend has it that they are born of regular fairies who’ve had relations with forest animals, which is a pretty common occurrence with the freaks who worship Diwata.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mandagot, Fairy-class</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lucienne on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lucienne</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Luckily</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Luckily, his human wetware wasn’t designed to handle this sort of weight, and when he awakened, disoriented, everything he recalled was a vague sense of his sanity eroding away, but it was now repaired, like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucky Bastard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d been expecting another lottery ticket in my Lucky Bastard box, but instead I received a single casino.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucky Bastard Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Lucky Bastard Box</b> is a loot box awarded for being very lucky. The contents vary, but higher tier boxes contain mostly gambling items such as casino chips and scratch-off tickets similar to those found in gas stations or liquor stores.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lucky Bastard Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lucky_Bastard_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucky Bitch Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Lucky Bitch Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the <a href="bword://Liquified Achievement">Liquified Achievement</a> during Operation Snake Pit.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Liquified Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lucky Bitch Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lucky_Bitch_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lucky Shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The small patch depicted a foot crushing down a set of four-leaf clovers; it imparted the Lucky Shot benefit: Lucky.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Luke</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6,7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Luke</b> is a Level 64 Lemig Goblin, a General in the Democratic Sortion, and the beloved companion of <a href="bword://Commander Stockade">Commander Stockade</a> of the <a href="bword://Lemig Sortion">Lemig Sortion</a>, whose death sets off the tragic final act of Stockade's storyline.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Luke on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Luke</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lunette</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lunette</b> is the mother of <a href="bword://Tsarina Signet">Tsarina Signet</a>. She was a princess of the <a href="bword://Naiad Confederacy">Naiad Confederacy</a> before copulating with <a href="bword://King Finian">King Finian</a> and giving birth to Signet.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Tsarina Signet's mother</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naiad</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 73</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lunette on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lunette</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lusca</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Lusca</b> is a boss on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Octo-Shark</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lusca on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lusca</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lutin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Crawlers may choose the <b>Lutin</b> race on the Third Floor. Lutins (or, if female, <b>lutines</b> ) are gnome-like creatures that specialize in support magic.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Lutin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Lutin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lynn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only remaining, conscious head was that of Tami-Lynn, and I met eyes with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Lysol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The heavy scent of Lysol filled the room, and I was shocked at how familiar the room.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Macro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: And while this new generation of Macro AIs are still ostensibly independent, they’re considered much more “stable.” Something that won’t “go crazy” or “kill everybody on the planet because it’s having a temper.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Macro AI</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Macro AIs</b> are an ancient, alien artificial intelligence designed to be installed inside <a href="bword://Primal">Primal</a> Engines. Once properly installed and fully melded with the engine, they have the ability "to seemingly alter the physics and reality of the worlds around them."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Macro AI on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Macro_AI</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Macro AIs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Macro AIs is described as still ostensibly independent, they’re considered much more “stable.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mad Dune</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwendolyn’s team was building siege engines to breach the walls on the land quadrant, but she was worried they didn’t have enough people to assault the sandcastle of the “Mad Dune Mage.” We hadn’t heard shit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mad Dune Mage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwendolyn’s team was building siege engines to breach the walls on the land quadrant, but she was worried they didn’t have enough people to assault the sandcastle of the “Mad Dune Mage.” We hadn’t heard shit.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mad Max</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were all dressed in ridiculous, post-apocalyptic gear: punk-rock style leather and tassels and goggles and chains and dreadlocks, like they were all heading out to a Mad Max.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Made</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made of skin and fur and the spiky things from the back of Thorn Cadavers, these kneepads are both good protection and they’re.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Madison</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Madison</b> is a <a href="bword://Human">Human</a> Resources Associate on the Iron Tangle. She boards the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a> in Trainyard E and gets off at station 60. <b>Madison</b> is the benefits manager for the <a href="bword://Kravyad">Kravyad</a> and the Station Mimics.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 17</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Madison on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Madison</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Madness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What should we use them on?” “Once the game starts, it’s too late to use regular action items, but we can still propose emergency ones while the game is ongoing, so we need to keep ours in reserve in case.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Madness Monkeywrenchers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had the Madness Monkeywrenchers, who were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Madre de Aguas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: As soon as that happens, that timer is probably going to accelerate, so we’ll need to hoof it to the roof and break him free of the Madre de Aguas snake body right away.” “What about tossing Samantha, like you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maestro</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Prince Maestro</b> is the younger son of <a href="bword://King Rust">King Rust</a> of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> and <a href="bword://Queen Consort Ugloo">Queen Consort Ugloo</a>, younger brother of Prince <a href="bword://Stalwart">Stalwart</a>, and elder brother of Princess <a href="bword://Formidable">Formidable</a>. He is formerly the host of the show Death Watch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem, and his rivalry with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> escalates into an intergalactic incident.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Maestro, Pork Boy, Carl's Naughty Little Piggie, Former Prince Maestro</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Skull Empire</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Maestro on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Maestro</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maestro’s show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The soothing, female voice was similar, but not quite identical to the robot voice of the last one of these things we’d seen, which had been in the standard production trailer that had been rented for the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mage Rebellion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Message : After a careful deliberation, it has been determined that the Non-Team Entity known as the War Mage Rebellion will not be allowed access to Larracos until the Peeling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mage team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He was under the direct control of Li Na, who was having him fight the god of lust, who was a member of the War Mage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maggie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Chris, does Maggie worship Grull?” While we waited for an answer to filter through Imani and Elle—which was the only way we could talk—I examined the grenade in my inventory to make sure the pin hadn’t yet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maggie and Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Really?” “How is this thing with Maggie and Frank going over?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maggie My</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Maggie My</b> is a Crawler from Earth who enters the Dungeon alongside <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a> and their teenage daughter <a href="bword://Yvette">Yvette</a>. After Yvette dies on the First Floor, Maggie turns her attention almost entirely to hunting <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Mags</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Infiltrator</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Maggie My on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Maggie_My</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maggie My and Frank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Camouflage was a spell similar to what Maggie My and Frank had used to hide themselves from other.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic &amp; Spells</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Magic</b> is a core Dungeon mechanic through which Crawlers and other entities wield highly advanced <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> technology disguised as fantasy magic. The dungeon presents these effects as traditional spellcasting, but <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> explains that the distinction is mostly cosmetic: the underlying technology is so far beyond a crawler's comprehension that, for all practical purposes, it simply is magic.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magic &amp; Spells on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magic_%26_Spells</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic Item Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Magic Item Box</b> is a type of Loot Box that appears to be awarded to crawlers who practice magic on the First Floor. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives this box on the First Floor for being one of the first 100 crawlers to level up <a href="bword://Magic Missile">Magic Missile</a> to level 3. <i>Loot</i></li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magic Item Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magic_Item_Box</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic &amp; Spells</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Magic</b> is a core Dungeon mechanic through which Crawlers and other entities wield highly advanced <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> technology disguised as fantasy magic. The dungeon presents these effects as traditional spellcasting, but <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> explains that the distinction is mostly cosmetic: the underlying technology is so far beyond a crawler's comprehension that, for all practical purposes, it simply is magic.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magic &amp; Spells on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magic_%26_Spells</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The shield surrounded me with an inch-thick buffer that protected me from physical damage, and it somewhat protected me from any magic attack that cast something corporeal at me, like Magic Missile and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic Item Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Magic Item Box</b> is a type of Loot Box that appears to be awarded to crawlers who practice magic on the First Floor. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives this box on the First Floor for being one of the first 100 crawlers to level up <a href="bword://Magic Missile">Magic Missile</a> to level 3. <i>Loot</i></li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magic Item Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magic_Item_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic Missile</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Magic Missile</b> is a basic ranged attack spell that fires bolts of energy from the caster's eyes, one after another, resembling laser blasts from a science fiction film. It is the signature attack of <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, who taught it to herself on the First Floor moments after <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> activated her menus.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magic Missile on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magic_Missile</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic Missile spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Magic Missile spell had risen to level five, and she now had the option to choose how many mana points to put into each casting, from three to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic Missiles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We let the flamethrowers do most of the work, but I tossed smoke curtains and the non-explosive bangers at the incoming monsters while Donut hurled Magic Missiles at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic protection spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: A blue magic protection spell appeared around us, followed by a new one, this time a translucent shield spell, a real shield spell, that would also protect us from physical.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magic spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were like magic spells that directly affected gameplay somehow, unlike a utility card which would affect just one totem or a special card, which was usually a consumable, one-time effect on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t know what upgrade the Lady Dominators had picked, but if it was a shield, it likely didn’t stop both magical attacks, which was what Donut’s bolt would be, and physical attacks, which was what the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical bolt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Equipping this small buckler imbues the following effects: Catch physical attacks (Success percentage based off user’s dexterity) Catch magical bolt attacks (Success percentage based off user’s intelligence.).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical bolts</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: He wore black, leather enchanted boots that protected him from magical bolts, though that’d done him no.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: We wouldn’t be able to blow through the magical door that protected the next room over, but just by knocking on the wall, I could tell we could easily breach directly through the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical Fervor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Salvatore continued his rant, but under the influence of the Magical Fervor buff, his voice carried as if over a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical gear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Apparently fairy-class, and other magical races, were extremely limited on the sort of magical gear they could.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I want you to remove every stable magical item and artifact and pile it on the floor and wait here.” “I don’t know what that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Plus, now we can re-examine all her other magical items and figure out what we can lose now for better items since we were using most of them to buff her constitution.” “As long as I don’t have to wear the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical Ops</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: This particular group consists of members of the Magical Ops arm of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magical weapons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He carried a ton of magical weapons, from swords to spears to war hammers to more non-traditional items, like a magical chainsaw and something that appeared to be a literal sock filled with nickels, only it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magistrate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They had a sign and a trio of city elves out front attempting to talk those passing by to enter and hear “The good news about Apito the Oak Mother.” They were only two blocks away from the Magistrate’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magistrate Featherfall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Magistrate Featherfall</b> is the magistrate of the medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> settlement on the Third Floor. As Magistrate, he is effectively the mayor of the Medium Skyfowl Settlement. Before the cataclysm, the Skyfowl ruled the whole Over City and he would have had to report to a regional governor, who would report to a royal chancellor, who would report to someone in a city on the 9th floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magistrate Featherfall on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magistrate_Featherfall</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Magnificent Troy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>An orc with a sportscoat and tie. A sports broadcaster outside the dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Outworld</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Magnificent Troy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Magnificent_Troy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mailing List</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Butchering Chapter 56 Chapter 57 Chapter 58 Chapter 59 Chapter 60 Chapter 61 Chapter 62 Chapter 63 Chapter 64 Chapter 65 Chapter 66 Chapter 67 Chapter 68 Chapter 69 Chapter 70 Chapter 71 Chapter 72 Chapter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Main Passenger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Main Passenger-Line.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Major Charlie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The potion Mordecai had made for me was called Major Charlie for some inexplicable reason that even Mordecai couldn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Major Charlie Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Major Charlie Potion</b> is a potion that raises your intelligence by 10 point for 30 seconds, afterwards it lowers your intelligence by 10 point for 5 minuets.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Major Charlie Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Major_Charlie_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Makana</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Destruction team was another group of crawlers from China I didn’t know very well mixed with that Hawaiian guy with the really long name who went by Makana and his partner, the hexcrafter paladin crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Make</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately, the horn wasn’t a normal honk, but a recording of a gangster’s voice saying, Make way for the big shot , followed by a chicken squawk and the rat-a-tat shooting sound of a tommy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maker’s Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The advanced Trap Maker table was required to build some of the best, magical-based traps, but the advanced Bomb Maker’s Workshop would allow me to precisely tune my explosives and to eventually make much more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Makeup Sex is the Best Sex Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Makeup Sex is the Best Sex Box</b> is presumably one of the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>'s "discretionary" loot boxes. It is awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> System AI's Smush fetish Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Makeup Sex is the Best Sex Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Makeup_Sex_is_the_Best_Sex_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Makeup Table</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Makeup Table</b> is a crafting workbench that allows crawlers with shapeshifting abilities to better see while forming into shapes. <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> uses this bench to work on her <a href="bword://Doppelgänger">Doppelgänger</a> race to work on her Walk in Their Shoes Skill.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Marketplace</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Makeup Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Makeup_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Makoka Cloud</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The entire complex, along with the six adjoining habitats and the central community hub was considered luxurious compared to most of the habitats in the Makoka Cloud, which was known for its crime and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Male Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: One said “#1 Son,” and the other was “Brother of the Century.” Alpha Male.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Male Thorny Devil</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Male Thorny Devil</b> are a mob on the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Male Thorny Devil on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Male_Thorny_Devil</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mama</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Mama is described as gonna swallow them up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Man</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You know, I’m pretty sure nobody, on their deathbeds, ever said to themselves, “Man, I wished I’d worked even more in life.” Reward : A new tab is now available in your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Man of God Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Man of God Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he pledges himself as an adherent to the god <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Man of God Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Man_of_God_Achievement</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Genita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mana Genita</b> is a god, the details about them are currently unknown. She's supposedly big on spells. The <a href="bword://Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita">Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita</a> appears to be named for her. The tiara is fit for royalty, made in honor of all the lost children of the world.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mana Genita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mana_Genita</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Regular Mana Potions</b> instantly and completely refill a character's Mana Points. These are commonly found in <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> on the first six floors of the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mana Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mana_Potion</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Genita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mana Genita</b> is a god, the details about them are currently unknown. She's supposedly big on spells. The <a href="bword://Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita">Enchanted Tiara of Mana Genita</a> appears to be named for her. The tiara is fit for royalty, made in honor of all the lost children of the world.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mana Genita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mana_Genita</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Regular Mana Potions</b> instantly and completely refill a character's Mana Points. These are commonly found in <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> on the first six floors of the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mana Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mana_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana potions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first dozen were bronze and gold adventurer boxes, filled with good healing and mana potions along with a few potions of Phase Through Wall , which Mordecai was pretty excited about, along with dozens of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Target</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bang Bro Cost: 5 Mana Target: Temporarily Enchant Equipped Item Duration: 5 minutes + 1 minute per level of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mana Toast</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mana Toast</b> is a burned triangle of toast that restores mana. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> each receive one piece of toast from a metallic toaster oven in an unmanned safe room on the Second Floor. Donut puts both pieces into her inventory.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Safe Room</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mana Toast on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mana_Toast</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Manager</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’ll be on chat if you need me.” “If I had known he was going to be so bossy, I wouldn’t have picked that Manager benefit,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Manager of Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Manager of Princess Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Manasa</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Manasa</b> is a famous <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> pop singer with a haunting soprano voice. Physically large, her black-and-silver cobra head appears to have a meter-wide hood.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naga | Gondii</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Manasa on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Manasa</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mandla</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mandla : If it’s like the bathrooms, you don’t explode when you touch the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>ManTauR</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Mantaur</b> is a mob found on the Fourth Floor and lower levels. They are often associated with <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>. A mantaur is like a centaur, but the standard human torso sprouts from a larger human body, right where the neck would be. The smaller upper half supports a human head and a set of arms; the larger lower half has another set of big, muscular arms and legs. Mantaurs run and stand on all four rear limbs. Their voices sound human, but they speak loudly and boisterously.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: ManTauR on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/ManTauR</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mantis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mantis</b> (plural <b>mantises</b> or <b>mantids</b>) is an insectoid <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race resembling giant praying mantises. Mantis hunters of the <a href="bword://Dark Hive">Dark Hive</a> hunted <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mantis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mantis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mantis system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He re-watched the story of the destruction of Hive Re-Population Center 51, which was not even close to the original mantis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mantises</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Mantises is described as forced to retire their amusement park AIs every.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Man’s Land</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled myself up and over into No Man’s Land, joining over two thousand members of the Princess Posse as we engaged our first head-on assault into enemy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Map</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>By default, the <b>Minimap</b> appears in the bottom right corner of a crawler's Heads-Up Display (HUD). The basic minimap is a simple blue and gray map which shows the cardinal directions (North/South/East/West) and represents nearby creatures as dots.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Heads-Up Display (HUD)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Map on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Map</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Map of the Stars</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Adds boss locations and descriptions to a large area of the map.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Map of the Stars on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Map_of_the_Stars</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mapping Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mapping</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a scroll offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Its name implies some kind of mapping function, but the text never confirms what it actually does.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mapping Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mapping_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mardi Gras</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: These were large caricatures, making the faces more colorful, more comic than they truly were, like from Mardi Gras or Carnival, but much more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maria</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The female head on Shi-Maria reached up into the air like a baby bird and started greedily devouring the dripping entrails from the deer’s body, like she was eating spaghetti slowly oozing from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mario Kart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were taking turns playing the Wii version of Mario Kart , all screeching, laughing their heads off, as they had four players on at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marjory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Parsons had told me it was something to do with Marjory, but I hadn’t told that to Bea because she already hated the woman because Gravy Boat was always outside in the tree, harassing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marjory Williams</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Everything was gone except the trees and the bicycles in the bike racks, and Marjory Williams’s moped that was still booted by parking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marked for Death</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was the same Marked for Death symbol I still had floating over me, but it was an X with acorns dangling from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marked for Life Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Marked for Life Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for being marked for death by the god <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a> but surviving the encounter.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Marked for Life Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Marked_for_Life_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marked on your map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The area of effect is marked on your map, but it’s pretty much the whole.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Marlboro Reds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I took three packs of Marlboro.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Martha Stewart Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Martha Stewart Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for using his <a href="bword://Engineer&#x27;s Table">Engineer's Table</a> for the first time.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Martha Stewart Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Martha_Stewart_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Martyr’s Path</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I wanted my eighth-floor-only Martyr’s Path benefit to activate, I would have to be under 5%.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mass Casualty Event Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Mass Casualty Event Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when blew up a bomb in Zockau killing 200+ <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> and 35 hunters.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mass Casualty Event Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mass_Casualty_Event_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mass Layoffs Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> distributes the <b>Mass Layoffs! <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a></b> on March 20 to all crawlers who survive the second phase of the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mass Layoffs Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mass_Layoffs_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Massage Coupon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Massage Coupon</b> is a coupon for a free massage at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Entitles the owner plus one companion to a one hour massage at the penis parade or penis palace backroom relaxation spa.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Massage Coupon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Massage_Coupon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Massive creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: And then it moved to another stomach, then a third, and then it finally depicted the massive creature pooping out a cartoon skeleton of a human wearing heart-covered.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Massive demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was off the northeast edge of the building, perilously close to the massive demon, having blown a hole through the tornado of demon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We need that key because we need to get that pig.” “Mate, I don’t know what pig you’re talking about,” Florin.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Matt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: ABOUT THE AUTHOR (This is where we switch to third person and pretend like Matt isn’t writing this himself.) Matt Dinniman is a writer and artist from Gig Harbor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Matt Dinniman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Matt Dinniman is described as a writer and artist from Gig Harbor.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Matt Dinniman All</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Copyright © 2022 by Matt Dinniman All rights.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Matt Dinniman Kaiju</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also by Matt Dinniman Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!) Trailer Park Fairy Tales (Short.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Matt Dinniman Patreon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you’re feeling especially saucy, you can join the Matt Dinniman Patreon and read advance chapters and whole books before they come out on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maurice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle and Imani, who were in the eternal slum, still had the quest also, and they needed to find and kill another iteration of Maurice, this one called the Red Menace of Tinker.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maxim</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dmitri said nothing to this, but Maxim cheerfully told them that the man had gotten what he deserved and that he was just sorry others had to die for it to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Maxim Popov</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Maxim Popov: race/species: <a href="bword://Human">Human</a> | <a href="bword://Nodling">Nodling</a>; class: Bogatyr Class.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Nodling</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Maxim Popov on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Maxim_Popov</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>May the Eulogist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: May the Eulogist ever sleep, my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meadow Lark</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Meadow Lark</b> is a crawler Party made up of the overnight staff and residents of an eldercare facility on Earth, allied with the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> beginning during their descent through the First Floor and Second Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Team Meadow Lark</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Meadow Lark on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Meadow_Lark</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meadow Lark and Bautista</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this worked as intended, both Meadow Lark and Bautista’s crew would have a warning that the train with the X would be the last one coming down the line for a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meadowlark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The look on his face makes this suicide mission all worth it.” It was Imani, Katia, Bautista, Louis, Britney, Tran, Li Jun, Li Na, Zhang, Chris, Elle, a few Meadowlark crawlers I didn’t really know, and a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meanwhile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Meanwhile, members of the crawler team became aware of exactly what was happening, and they formed a plan to defend Carl.” “So, they’re cheating also!” the caterpillar shouted, suddenly pulling himself.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meat Hook Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Meat Hook Spell</b> is a spell that summons all nearby carnivorous pet-class monsters away from their owners and to the source of the burning-stench.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Meat Hook Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Meat_Hook_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meat Hooks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The puppy was clearly out of range of the Meat Hooks spell, but it didn’t seem to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meat Shields</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She probably did some wheeling and dealing and parked that Rosemarie lady in there, so they were technically still mercenaries, but since nobody knew the club was also a Meat Shields location, they were safe.”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meatball pet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: An evil, vile, experience hog crawler named Tserendolgor somehow managed to get her hands on the voting ballot box for the pet show and changed the votes, stealing Mongo’s crown from him and handing it off to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meatus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Meatus</b> is a deity that is a god. After <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> casts her <a href="bword://War Crimes Spell">War Crimes Spell</a>, and Meatus is chopped into pieces, the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> closes the Scavenger's Daughter eye because "even she doesn't want to look at that."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Meatus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Meatus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mechanic's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mechanic's Boxes</b> are <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> awarded for building contraptions inside the Dungeon. They typically contain crafting tools.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Battlefield Construction Achievement; Grease Monkey Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mechanic's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mechanic%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Medium Skyfowl Settlement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have also gained control of the Medium Skyfowl.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Bobby and I are the only ones.” “Meet me there in a goddamn hour.” “Why?” the spider.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meet an Elite Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Meet an Elite</b> <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> is awarded to crawlers the first time they encounter an Elite, typically on the Third Floor or Sixth Floor. The reward modifies the crawler's Minimap.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Meet an Elite Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Meet_an_Elite_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mehmet Or</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mehmet Or</b> is Crawler #3,756,330, a human Sipahi who reached Level 62 before taking a deal to exit the dungeon, and the author of an entry in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s "Book of Boom" cookbook.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mehmet Or on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mehmet_Or</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Meld Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Meld</b> is a process, related to but distinct from Split Personality, in which one creature consumes and joins with another, combining two minds into one.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Meld Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Meld_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Melee attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I killed nothing but Howling Gulls, and the soul essence bar was pure, when I unleashed my melee attack, it was almost twice as powerful than it would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Melee attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most melee attacks would be useless because if I kicked the thing in the head, what difference would it make where it was standing when the kick actually hurt.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>members of the Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The list was too long to just manually scroll, but I did anyway, and they were all just members of the Princess Posse or Team Retribution or mercenaries from the recently-defeated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Memorial Crystal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Memorial Crystals</b> are extremely rare and powerful artifacts created upon the death of gods or demigods. Memorial Crystals are physically similar to soul crystals, but where a soul crystal merely stores raw soul power, a memorial crystal is packed with information -- the god-tier spells and knowledge of the deity who died to create it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dropped by fallen Gods and Demigods</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Memorial Crystal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Memorial_Crystal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Memorial crystals</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then I had a lead on a second one in the forest during Faction Wars, and we hunted it down, and then the god said that one was wrong, too, and now I have a new quest to find out why the memorial crystals.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Menagerie Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Menagerie Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded for discovering a pet Reward Room.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Menagerie Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Menagerie_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Menerva</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8,Backstage at the Pineapple Cabaret.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Menerva</b> is a former crawler and schoolteacher who was left behind on the Eleventh Floor generations ago by her party member <a href="bword://Porthus">Porthus</a>, and who, believed dead by him ever since, secretly survived to help build the Pineapple Cabaret alongside the cookbook author <a href="bword://Herot">Herot</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Menerva on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Menerva</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mentally Unstable Clothing Hoarder Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Mentally Unstable Clothing Hoarder Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for collecting over 700 conductors hats from other crawlers that were part of the Vermillion Line Rescue to help others trapped at the End of the Line Rescue on the Iron Tangle.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mentally Unstable Clothing Hoarder Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mentally_Unstable_Clothing_Hoarder_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mercenary Arcade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I spent some time in the Mercenary Arcade, the training room where the strippers were spending their time, working out, honing their fighting skills, and practicing with the myriad of weapons we’d collected.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mercenary market</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The hired ones, including the Sledge and the other surviving cretins, along with the for-hire, dungeon-born mercenaries for all the teams would simply disappear, possibly moved into “storage” for the next.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mercenary markets</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: None were weapons or mercenary markets or entrances to the Desperado Club, but there were food markets and multiple temples, all placed in ramshackle, wooden and metal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Merry Christmas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Merry Christmas,” I said as I pulled the first corpse into my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Messaging system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We believe that Stockade and Houston both conspired to use the secured messaging system to speak with someone upon the 18 th floor, who is relaying real-time information via a third party participating in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Metal Working Table</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Metal Working Table</b> is a crafting workbench that allows crawlers to manipulate metal by forming and shaping metal. Level 2 (5th floor) - Can view the tensile strength and load limits, and the interface had more shaping options.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Marketplace</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Metal Working Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Metal_Working_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Metcalf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Metcalf now had the map, and there were multiple paths, including the ominously titled toddler splash pad, the wave pool, and a massive loop-de-loop, which was the fastest path to the exit but was clearly the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mexx</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mexx</b> appears to be a brand of robotic limited artificial intelligence designed to manage <a href="bword://Senegal Production Systems">Senegal Production Systems</a> Production Trailers and instruct crawlers on tunnel appearances. It looks like a metallic, jet-black floating frisbee with a single blue light flashing on its edge, and typically hovers near the ceiling when not interacting with crawlers. It speaks in a soothing but emotionless robotic female voice.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mexx on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mexx</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mexx robot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We will ask the AI to fix that.” There was a ping, and the Mexx robot said from the corner of the room, “The AI indicates that it loves a good loophole, and it is unable to close it unless someone makes an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miami</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It said one of them is Miami, and I picked that one, but then I got a message that said there was a problem with the location, and it was sending us to Iowa.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Midnight Epicure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Over the cycles, the rather disturbing symbol of the Midnight Epicure, which is really just a racist caricature of an adult Caprid, developed into a ward against all night.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Midnight Epicure Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Midnight Epicure Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Sixth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Benefactor Box">Benefactor Box</a> from the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Midnight Epicure Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Midnight_Epicure_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Midnight Express</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I would still be stuck here when the Midnight Express card expired, and so would the two totems, but we were running out of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Midnight Express card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I would still be stuck here when the Midnight Express card expired, and so would the two totems, but we were running out of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Midnight Market</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Midnight Market is described as a special bazaar hidden in the bottom regular floor of the Desperado Club, which we now had access.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mike Barnes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mike Barnes</b> is one of the crawlers in the Subterranean Quadrant of Bubble #543. He shoots banana tree seeds from his hand. Since he's a druid, he can make the seeds sprout really fast afterwards, which makes for a surprisingly effective attack. Another crawler laments that the range of his attacks is really low.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 6, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mike Barnes on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mike_Barnes</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Milk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Milk</b> is a former crawler and the author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (Item)</i> (6th Edition). Milk is from a clawed race with annual migration patterns. She lived on an island with her family before entering the Dungeon. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> described her in dungeon race as a half frog - half bat hybrid.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Vesper and later Xenopus in dungeon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 27</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Milk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Milk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Milk of Lamashtu Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Milk">Milk</a> of <a href="bword://Lamashtu">Lamashtu</a></b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a scroll offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed, and its name is not explicitly connected to the deity Lamashtu anywhere in the text.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Milk of Lamashtu Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Milk_of_Lamashtu_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Milk of Lamashtu Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Milk">Milk</a> of <a href="bword://Lamashtu">Lamashtu</a></b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a scroll offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed, and its name is not explicitly connected to the deity Lamashtu anywhere in the text.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Milk of Lamashtu Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Milk_of_Lamashtu_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Milquetoast Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Milquetoast</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded when a crawler wins an important boss battle without killed the boss. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Fifth Floor for defeating <a href="bword://Mrs. Ghazi">Mrs. Ghazi</a> without killing her. He receives a <a href="bword://Potion of Bloodlust">Potion of Bloodlust</a> in the reward box.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Milquetoast Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Milquetoast_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mimic Rex</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is a minion of the Mimic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mimics</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mimics, even powerful ones, were ambush.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mind Balance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Mind Balance skill came from my new toe ring, though now it was trained up so much, I’d still have the skill, minus five levels, even if I took the ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mind Balance skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Mind Balance skill came from my new toe ring, though now it was trained up so much, I’d still have the skill, minus five levels, even if I took the ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mind Horror</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mind Horrors</b> are a level 4 Second Floor mob. They use mostly psionic attacks and look like jellyfish with brains. They are related to the Third Floor mob, <a href="bword://Brain Boiler">Brain Boiler</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mind Horror on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mind_Horror</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mind Horrors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We watched as their guide—one of those floating brain Mind Horrors—sat the three of them down and told them the only way to survive was to kill other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mindaro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So without any further fanfare and very little discussion about how terrible of an idea this was, the four of us loaded up onto a rapid response cart and dialed ourselves onto the Mindaro line and headed down.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Mine is described as a Valtay Perso-Shield, Platinum Edition , which was identical to the one I would’ve gotten from Paulie, and it was simply a round, pinback button that looked a little like the plan.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mined Material Reclamation Act</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mined Material Reclamation Act</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> law pertaining to the rights of planets to their mineral wealth.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mined Material Reclamation Act on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mined_Material_Reclamation_Act</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Level 225 Minor Feral Demon. Younger sister of <a href="bword://Slit">Slit</a>. Pulled out of The Nothing into the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> during Eighth Floor after <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> visited the casino there.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Minor Feral Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 32, Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Minge on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Minge</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minge the demon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I thought you were dead!” When Minge the demon had demolished the Desperado Club, I’d assumed all the guards had been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mini Cooper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: These were tiny platforms, each about the size of a Mini.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minion Army</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Minion Army is described as a battlefield sabotage spell, and it’s quite effective, but it has a long casting time, and it is temporary,” Doctor Bones.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minion Army Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Minion Army</b></i> is an expensive war spell that causes hostile enemies to fight for you rather than against you. Although it is a powerful mass-charm spell suited for battlefield sabotage, it is almost impossible to train beyond level one due to its casting time and the high risk of failure.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Minion Army Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Minion_Army_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minister Glory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Only that the Gleener body it inhabited was once mother to the current Syndicate Prime Minister Glory, which was as inappropriate and bizarre as it was confusing, especially since the fish people all looked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minister of Blood</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dekoki the kappa had regained the ability to move after the Minister of Blood-Letting poured a beer into her head bowl, and Louis was helping her reapply her Saran Wrap as she profusely apologized to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minister of Blood-Letting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Minister of Blood-Letting</b> is a Level 98 Dark Ghoul Papist, the leader of a racing team piloting a Wienermobile during the Tenth Floor's heats, and a regular karaoke performer at a bar frequented by racers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Minister of Blood-Letting on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Minister_of_Blood-Letting</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minister Victory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prime Minister Victory and the staff of the new temporary headquarters for the Syndicate’s presence in the system are on the Eastern Seaboard of your North.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minor deity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Psamathe, or Samantha as her friends used to call her, is a minor deity who was banished to the Nothing by her father after he found out she was kicking around with some ancient king.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Minus</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> security soldier posing as a tourist named <b>Linus</b>. He infiltrates the Tenth Floor of the World Dungeon pretending to be a creepy superfan of the crawler Elle in order to carry out an assassination mission.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Linus</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Teelan-3</li><li><b>Race:</b> Soother</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Minus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Minus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minute per level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Duration: (5 minutes + 1 minute per level of spell) × [Your Intelligence / (Target’s Constitution + Charisma)] Ahh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minute per level of spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Duration: (5 minutes + 1 minute per level of spell) × [Your Intelligence / (Target’s Constitution + Charisma)] Ahh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Minutes Until</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 84 ] 66 Minutes Until the destruction of the 9 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miriam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Miriam would keep them hidden and would buff Prepotente while the goat stacked debuff after debuff onto the target, doing it in a way the target didn’t even realize they were in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miriam and Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It sounded as if Miriam and Prepotente had stepped into something that was about to reverberate over the entire floor, just like when we’d unleashed Orthrus the two-headed puppy on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miriam Dom</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Miriam Dom</b> is an Earth crawler who entered the Dungeon with fifteen goats and crawls alongside two of them, <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> and <a href="bword://Bianca">Bianca</a>. Known to the audience as the goat lady long before anyone learned her name, she debuts at number five on the first leaderboard.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Goat Lady</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Italy</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Human | Vampire (5th Floor)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 47</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Miriam Dom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Miriam_Dom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miriam Dom and Prepotente</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It took just over 30 seconds for the information to seep in, so they weren’t suited for fast-moving combat situations, but it was how Miriam Dom and Prepotente would know exactly how to slowly kill the giant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Beatrice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Like that time Miss Beatrice got in the car accident when we were on our way back from the cat show, and I’d been asleep, and then suddenly there was this big bang, and I slammed against the side of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Beatrice’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You always had that stupid little thing, and Miss Beatrice’s family had that horrendous, fake, white monstrosity in the cats-not-allowed room.” She bounded off to the common area and leaped directly into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Beatrice’s mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She’s really into the idea of killing undead creatures, but she’s terrified of the idea of other gods.” “Well, it reminds me of Miss Beatrice’s mother, and I don’t like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Were you up there from before the race started?” “Oh, yes, Miss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The problem for Miss Katia here is that she can’t just say, ‘make me into a human.’ She has to shape it herself.” “It hurts, too,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Me</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The smeared “Miss Me?” was miraculously still intact, shining bright in the center of her charred.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Nadine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: All of her tattoos had returned, and she was brought back up with the help of the remaining were-castors, who all moved to the remains of Miss Nadine and started to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Nance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d named one Dinallo and the other “Miss Nance.” Carl : If Imani doesn’t want to do it, ask Li.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Piggy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tick tock, Miss Piggy.” “The surface,” she said with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Quill</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Miss Quill</b> is an elderly skyfowl NPC on the Third Floor and the assistant to <a href="bword://Magistrate Featherfall">Magistrate Featherfall</a> in the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement. She is secretly the settlement's true ruler and the neighborhood boss behind the murders at the heart of the Sex Workers Who Fell from the Heavens Quest, which places her in opposition to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Quill</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Third Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 18</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Miss Quill on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Miss_Quill</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Quill’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: We should find one without blood on it.” “After we finish looting that filing cabinet in the reception area, we can look through the office for more scrolls.” I added, “We should probably steal all of those.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Miss Thagra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tell me, Miss Thagra, what is considered the greatest program in the universe?” Rosetta Thagra shifted uncomfortably in her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Missile shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Well, the show is over.” Far behind us, the sound of a missile shot into the air, followed by a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Missile spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Magic Missile spell had risen to level five, and she now had the option to choose how many mana points to put into each casting, from three to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Missiles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Missiles disabled the enemy armor’s movement but not its.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mister Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mister Carl, I do fear I require assistance.” “Goddamnit, Jamal,” I said, turning to see the giant hammerhead shark flopping on the forest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mistress Henspar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He popped it into his mouth, lit it, and said to himself, “Show me Mistress Henspar.” It would be a long time before someone figured out what had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mistress Tiatha</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i>Mistress Tiatha'</i> is the manager for team <a href="bword://Elle McGibbons">Elle McGibbons</a>. She has a drinking problem and smokes blitz sticks. She once managed to charm a boss.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Miss T</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Former Crawler</li><li><b>Race:</b> Half elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mistress Tiatha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mistress_Tiatha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mitch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “But can we get this moving?” This was risky, but I was gambling that this Mitch guy was going to do the same thing he had done for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mobs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mobs that start out as a pet class will always be level one regardless of what they usually are when you find them in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>mobs and NPCs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most people don’t understand why I care so much about this one incident when I would eventually go on to kill thousands of mobs and NPCs alike over the course of my crawl.” He paused, emotion caught in his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mobs on this floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Mobs on this floor is described as dropping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mold Lion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mold Lions</b> were once an attraction at <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus on the Third Floor but were transformed by <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s curse. Regular-sized male lions, their manes are made of waving pink and green finger-like worms. At least one eye glows emerald. Half of their matted bodies are splattered in a pulsating green, lumpy, glowing fungus. When the lion is killed, the worms die and mold turns black before oozing off of the corpse; if a single microscopic spore of the mold returns to Grimaldi, the lion will regrow by the next day. The lions attack by pouncing, and slashing with claws and teeth.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mold Lion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mold_Lion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mole Man</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mole Man</b> are a race that are common as NPC traders on the Fourth Floor. They spend most of their days and nights reading. A Mole Man, <a href="bword://Limp Richard">Limp Richard</a>, trades books with <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mole Man on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mole_Man</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Molly Maguires Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> got "The Molly Maguires" achievement when he entered the <a href="bword://Guild of Suffering">Guild of Suffering</a> for the first time.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Molly Maguires Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Molly_Maguires_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mom tried to kill you, and in about 20 minutes, I’m pretty sure your new wife, my stepmother, is going to finish the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Moment the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: This furthermost car only held two of the Shock Chompers, and the moment the door opened, both Donut and I took them.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mommy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Mommy is described as going to kill the next one, and you get the one behind him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mongo</b> is a Dungeon-generated, pet-class <a href="bword://Mongoliensis">Mongoliensis</a> mob and the bonded "Royal Steed" of Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. He is the mascot and an integral member of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>, serving as a fierce melee combatant, loyal protector, and mount for Donut.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Murder Chicken</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Second Floor Pet Reward Room</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mongoliensis</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 46</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mongo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mongo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were attacking Mongo and Donut not because they were aggressive mobs, but because of the defenses I’d erected around the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo and Kiwi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d only let Mongo and Kiwi enter the actual pub, which was named “The Belching Termite.” I wanted them to get used to each other, and I needed Kiwi to get used to the clockwork versions of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo and Rend</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut had received some pretty wild clothes for all the mercenaries and pets with her new Gurkha class, but we decided to keep the nemes and headband on Mongo and Rend for this.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongo's Teeth Caps</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk">Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</a></b> is a fleeting item that can be placed on the teeth of either a crawler or pet to increase their biting damage. They are gold in color and little tendrils of electricity spark when the teeth are snapped. They are currently worn by <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Quest Box from the Show Must Go On Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mongo's Teeth Caps on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mongo%27s_Teeth_Caps</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk</b> is a fleeting item that can be placed on the teeth of either a crawler or pet to increase their biting damage. They are gold in color and little tendrils of electricity spark when the teeth are snapped. They are currently worn by <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Quest Box from the Show Must Go On Quest</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Fang Caps of the Expectorating Tizheruk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Fang_Caps_of_the_Expectorating_Tizheruk</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongoliensis</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Mongoliensis</b> is basically a velociraptor. In the Dungeon, they may be encountered as dangerous floor mobs or acquired as level one pet-class mobs.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mongoliensis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mongoliensis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongoliensis Pack Leader</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mongoliensis Pack Leader – Level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mongos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Mongos is described as on the roof right over the Magistrate’s office.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monk Seal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Monk Seal</b> are a race on the Eighth Floor in Cuba.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Monk Seal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Monk_Seal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monk Seal Picket Sentry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Monk Seal Picket Sentry is described as ready for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monk Seals</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Monk Seals hold the ocean sacred, and the very act of spilling so much crab chowder into their holy waters is considered a sacrilege most.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monobrow Sam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book All.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Monobrow Sam</b> is a friend of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s from before the Transformation. The two used to play video games together and trade friendly insults, and later his memory is weaponized against Carl in the form of a hydra head armed with a death ray.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Monobrow Sam on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Monobrow_Sam</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monster Truck</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It didn’t change my Monster Truck Driver class name, but I could pick one of three “endorsements.” They only give you ten minutes to pick, and you can’t use chat while you’re in the little.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monster Truck Driver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It didn’t change my Monster Truck Driver class name, but I could pick one of three “endorsements.” They only give you ten minutes to pick, and you can’t use chat while you’re in the little.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Next stop will be Mora Station number 82 followed by a Traveler Transfer station number 83.” “Monsters,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Moon Reaper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Moon Reaper</b> are a mob type found on both the Tenth Floor and Fifteenth Floor. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> takes this as his initial form on the Tenth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Moon Reaper on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Moon_Reaper</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Moon Reapers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Moon Reapers is described as one of several different types of janitor mobs that occupy the 15th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Morales</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Griffin, Morales, Jamal, and Rend were all still down there somewhere, and I could hear the sound of the two elves violently.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mordecai</b> is a former crawler and a Level 50 Non-Combatant NPC who serves as Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s reluctant but dedicated Manager. Originally the party's Game Guide in the Tutorial Guild, he is working off an indentureship on his last tour of crawl duty.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Morty, Uncle Morty, Uncle Mordecai</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Skyfowl Planet</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl (Original) | Changeling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 2</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mordecai on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mordecai</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai and Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai and Donut talked about some of the new upgrades that were available for our personal space, including a bed that would refresh us almost.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d already told Mordecai and Katia about the death of Loita and was now breathlessly attempting to explain what had happened.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai and Mistress Tiatha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: More so than Mordecai and Mistress Tiatha combined, both of whom literally worked in guildhalls when they weren’t on manager.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai and Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was our only opportunity to deal with this problem before it was too late According to both Mordecai and Rosetta, this particular drug—Glory Bound—was based on a real-life, outside-the-inner-system.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai's Special Brew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> brews <b>Mordecai's Special Brew</b> on the Fourth Floor using the level 3 <a href="bword://Alchemy Table">Alchemy Table</a>. The potion is constant healing mixed with rapid regeneration, healing almost anything for 30 seconds. It won't protect from anything that will kill instantly, like being blown up or decapitated.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Mordecai's Alchemy Table</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mordecai's Special Brew on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mordecai%27s_Special_Brew</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai’s Special</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai’s Special Brew A potion designed by a shifty Changeling who has a tendency to kill those who count on him the most, this special potion combines the effects of a Gold Standard Healing Potion and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mordecai’s Special Brew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai’s Special Brew A potion designed by a shifty Changeling who has a tendency to kill those who count on him the most, this special potion combines the effects of a Gold Standard Healing Potion and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mork</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: At the same moment, I pulled one of the black potions from my inventory—a potion of Inflict Black Nimbus —and I hurled it through the small vestibule so it slammed against the back of the head of Mork the ram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Morphology and Interface</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Variant 32.c This item is compatible with your Morphology and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Morris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>One of the crawlers in the Subterranean Quadrant of Bubble #543. His race is <a href="bword://Arachnid">Arachnid</a>, which is essentially a spider-taur. He has a human torso and a spider body where his legs should be, similar to <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a>'s crab body.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Arachnid</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 6, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Morris on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Morris</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Morris Sp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t know if Quetzalcoatlus or the sudden electrification of the interior of the temple was what killed Morris Sp, the final crawler within the subterranean.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mostly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mostly her body dashed straight forward in the direction she was facing, but sometimes, every once in a while, she flew slightly.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mother</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s Mother</b> is the mother of crawler Carl. Carl's mother's father played center for Oregon State before becoming a prison guard.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mother on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mother</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Bea's Mother</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Bea's Mom</b> is portrayed as having been a mean spirited woman and a bad influence on <a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. She was highly religious and had had plastic surgery. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> describes her as having "...perfect, tight, face-lifted cheeks."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: USA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Bea's Mother on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Bea%27s_Mother</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mother of All Bombs</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Mother of All Bombs</b> (also called <b>MOAB</b> or the <b>Bomb Chicken</b>) was crafted by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Brandon Andrews">Brandon Andrews</a>, and <a href="bword://Chris Andrews">Chris Andrews</a> on the Second Floor to lure the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a> away from <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a>. Its name was a point of contention; "Brandon pointed out MOAB actually stood for 'Massive Ordnance Air Blast,' but his brother said, 'Don’t be that guy.'</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mother of All Bombs on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mother_of_All_Bombs_%28MOAB%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Motley Crue</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple songs and small explosions rocked all around us, but the Motley Crue song was blasting right next to us, so loud we were both wracked with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Motorcycle helmet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The fishtank that he’d shattered with his motorcycle helmet remained there on its pedestal, still broken a month.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mountain Dew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: If it doesn’t, you’ll wish we’d left you on the ground.” The potion was actually a vial of Mountain Dew I’d gotten from a saferoom on the second floor, but Mordecai said the coloring was the closest of all the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mounted elves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mounted elves remained on the ground, mostly on the edge of the brambles, all turned, looking up at the floating, frozen mess, still hovering in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Move</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Move before we are forced to make you roadkill.” The creature whispered something to Dwight the unicorn, who.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Movement spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was the special backpack I’d received at the beginning of the tenth floor, designed to let me use my movement spells and bring a quadruped with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Moving</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Moving on.” A blast hit the portal, and the whole thing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Moxo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Except for myself and the two flea-infested mercenaries who are smoking illicit, mind-altering materials while on duty.” “Watch it,” Moxo called from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mrs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ceiling of the chamber was low, meaning when I was attached to the roof, my head would only be an inch or two above the top of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mrs. Ghazi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mrs. Ghazi</b> is a sand ooze, a Fifth Floor Borough Boss, and the daughter of <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> and <a href="bword://King Blaine">King Blaine</a>. As long as Samantha exists in this realm, Mrs. Ghazi can be fully resurrected from a single grain of sand. She can be temporarily killed by setting fire to her core.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Sand Ooze</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4 Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mrs. Ghazi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mrs._Ghazi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mrs. Parsons</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mrs. Parsons</b> was an old woman who lived in a first floor unit of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s apartment building in Seattle, WA. She was grumpy and liked to file complaints. Her husband died years ago and she never remarried.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mrs. Parsons on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mrs._Parsons</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mukta</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mukta</b> is a <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a> member of the <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a>, and an employee in <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>'s Communications Department. On the Second Floor, Mukta briefly takes over PR for the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> after the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> suspends Zev for warning <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> about the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a>. Unlike Zev, he never enters the Dungeon to talk in person, and he closes his chats to prevent crawlers from messaging him.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Borant System</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-Tin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 41</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mukta on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mukta</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Multiple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple businesses spread out, filled with unaware memory ghosts as the early-morning shoppers moved in and out of grocery stores and electronics shops and bakeries, all of which ringed the outer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Multiple achievements</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d received multiple achievements and prizes, including a “Last Place” achievement she was grumbling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Multiple boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Are you in the market for explosives tonight?” Her tables held multiple boxes of both goblin dynamite and hobgoblin dynamite, along with smoke bombs, detonators, and several odds and ends I’d never before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Multiple items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were multiple items of makeup Samantha had already rooted through, a few random odds and ends, and a pile of papers that looked like legal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Multiple shields</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The floating house, protected by multiple shields, carried enough explosives to completely level the town if we had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Munificent Goddess Kina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: These were the Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Munificent Goddess Kina, originally gifted to Katia on the ninth floor, given to Donut, and now equipped by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Murgh Vindaloo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Murgh Vindaloo</b> is a food item, spice level four. Boneless chicken cubes simmered in velvety sweet-and-sour vindaloo sauce. Also, potatoes and other spices. It is on top of biryani rice. Served with garlic-and-spinach naan.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Tally</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Murgh Vindaloo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Murgh_Vindaloo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Muscle car</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She paused because a long tanklike tube had just popped out the side of the team Sparkles bush, and it was pointed directly at the muscle car right in front of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Muscle Mommy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mongo is going to be so proud, and with my new Muscle Mommy benefit, his strength is boosted by 25% of my own strength!” She looked over the edge and clicked her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Music</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Music swelled, sounding almost like mariachi with an EDM.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mute</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I had to take a potion.” “Keep your Mute spell ready!” Her new Mute spell would only last a handful of seconds, especially on a boss monster like Asojano, but if we timed it correctly, we could keep him from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mute Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Mute</b></i> is a spell that inflicts the Muted Debuff on its target, preventing the target from casting spells. When cast, it looks like a yellow bolt that follows the target. When it lands, a timer and an icon featuring a speaker with a cross through it appear above the target's head.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mute Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mute_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mysterious Bone Key</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Climb, Soul Reaper, and Mysterious Bone Key.” Mordecai blinked at me with his single.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mysterious Letter</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mysterious Letter</b> is an item that is part of the Sex Workers Who Fell from the Heavens Quest. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> mentions that the script is necro-script, the language of necromancers. He later figures out that the letter is a spell scroll for the <a href="bword://Suppurating Eye Spell">Suppurating Eye Spell</a>, allowing the caster to see and hear what is going on around the scroll.</li><li><b>Source:</b> GumGum's corpse</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Mysterious Letter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Mysterious_Letter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mystic card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We also found a rare mystic card called Force Discard , which picked a random card from the opponent’s hand and tossed it to the discard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Mystic Cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ménage à Quatro Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Ménage à Quatro Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for surviving the four way boss battle at the end of the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ménage à Quatro Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/M%C3%A9nage_%C3%A0_Quatro_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nadia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d received word that the Mantis government had unexpectedly abandoned their stake when I last spoke with Quasar, which ended up being a good thing as there was another entity bidding for the open spot in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nadine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nadine</b> is an enormous long-bodied caterpillar with a thick coat of fuzzy fur and prominent black quills and bristles that cover her form, allowing her to brush past like a car wash when moving at speed. As a Bearded Sage Caterpillar she undulates rapidly through jungle terrain, produces wet chittering and hissing sounds, smells strongly of wet moss, and bears a large saddle on her back to serve as a six-seater mount. While her overall shape appears deceptively cute from afar, her true bug-like insectoid face is horrifyingly alien and best avoided up close.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Chee | Bearded Sage Caterpillar</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nadine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nadine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naga</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Naga</b> are a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race. They are ruled by the <a href="bword://Blood Sultanate">Blood Sultanate</a>. They have a legacy spot in Faction Wars (game) as they are considered broke after the <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> season where crawler <a href="bword://Drakea">Drakea</a> managed to drive them into bankruptcy. The Naga are cobra-headed aliens.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Naga on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Naga</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naga system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because if you are nice to the wrong person, they’ll either take advantage of you, or worse, they might never leave.” “That seems like terrible advice, Uncle Quasar.” “No, telling someone to sign a contract in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naga team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it looks like we have accounted for most everybody’s forces except the Reavers and the Naga team.” “The Reavers are the ones with the flying machines usually, so we gotta be on the lookout,” Boomer said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nagas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was expecting more Naga infantry, but I see only one of their standards.” Empress D’Nadia’s territory was just south of the Nagas, in spot number five, so it made sense she’d commit most of her force between.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nah</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Especially: T B, The Human, RinoZ, Mike Dirks, Macronomicon, Nah, Drew Gilmour, Zorathis, Jesse Tyner, Attonranden, Whale, Simone Berntsen, Jack Barrett, Timothy D Theis, Jamie Wahls, Derek Allen, Eric Lee.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naiad</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Naiads</b> are water nymphs: a fairy-class creature that lives in water. Half-Naiads may be created with Humans and Elves. Naiad's are governed by the <a href="bword://Naiad Confederacy">Naiad Confederacy</a>, an isolationist sect that drowns outsiders.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Naiad on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Naiad</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naiad Confederacy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Naiad Confederacy</b> is a governmental structure of the <a href="bword://Naiad">Naiad</a>'s on the Sixth Floor. The Confederacy is an isolationist group that drowns outsiders who enter their area.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Naiad Confederacy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Naiad_Confederacy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>name is Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “My name is Princess Chandra, Esquire, and I have been assigned as your attorney,” Chandra.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Name of the gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Oh, I suppose I should call you tsarina, shouldn’t I?” “What in the name of the gods?” Signet said, stepping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Named train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If they could maybe find one of those small trapdoors, they could get to the station and onto a named train like the Nightmare , take it over, and steam it into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Named trains</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The problem is we don’t yet know if the named trains, like the Nightmare Express or the Dismemberment Limited , are safe.” “Of course they’re not safe,” Katia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naughty Boys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naughty Boys Employment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: + 2 Hide in Shadows Skill + Fear spell + 1 Intelligence + 5 Charisma +5 to the Find Trap Skill +5 to the Backfire Skill +5 to the Escape Plan Skill Access to the Desperado Club (already obtained) Access to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naughty Boys Employment Agency</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Naughty Boys Employment Agency</b> is a social upgrade that can be purchased as a saferoom addition for 500,000 gold.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Naughty Boys Employment Agency on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Naughty_Boys_Employment_Agency</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naughty Little Piggie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A spokesperson for the royal family has requested people stop referring to the prince as ‘Carl’s Naughty Little Piggie,’” The audience roared, “But so far, nobody seems to be complying.” Odette turned to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Naughty Little Piggies</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Naughty Little Piggies is described as in charge of base defense, but they were currently spread to the wind, on assignment in the flat lands around the base in all directions, finishing up the scout.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Na’s tower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: If the worst happens, then you and I together will go on the best we can, and we won’t stop until each and every one of them has paid their debt tenfold.” Donut didn’t reply, still looking out the window at Li.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nearby</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Nearby, I had two more crawlers, members of Elle’s team, carefully watching them, making certain the mages weren’t spies or worm.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nearby crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The two had received multiple loot boxes from being player killers, including a ring that gave Maggie an area-of-effect stealth ability and a potion that imbued Frank with the ability to track down nearby.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nebular</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Nebular Balance</b> (colloquially called "Nebulars" or "Nebs") is a nomadic religious cult that worships the Eulogist. Nebulars do not actively recruit, but their members consist of Soothers, Humans, elves, and dozens of other aliens from across the galaxy. They all wear the same robes and large hats.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Nebs; Nebular</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nebular on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nebular</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nebular Sin Patrol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Nebular Sin Patrol is described as one of the few hunting groups who always attended a crawl, but the actual hunters were different every.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nebulars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the zombie event had started to disrupt Sister Ines’s charm poem, but since her sponsor and patron, the Nebulars, helped her raise that Word Weaver spell to level 15, she was able to fully control both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Necklace of Indelible Woe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://King Rust">King Rust</a> originally wields the necklace. This necklace is in the same collection as the <a href="bword://Enchanted Night Wyrm&#x27;s Ring of Divine Suffering">Enchanted Night Wyrm's Ring of Divine Suffering</a>. <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a> shows interest in the item but ultimately decides against taking it.</li><li><b>Source:</b> King Rust</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Necklace of Indelible Woe on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Necklace_of_Indelible_Woe</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Necropolis of Anser</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Level 43 Glass Mage Known by most everyone in the area as The Mad Dune mage, Ghazi came to the area for the same reason as the bugbear submarine captain, Shamus Chaindrive: to seek out the hidden treasure that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Need My Personal Space</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In fact, the only person who was sponsored by the Apothecary who’d received a benefactor box not intended for Donut was Katia, who’d received a small, knit beanie that increased all her stats down the line by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neighborhood Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: And since that one earlier was a Neighborhood Boss, I’d bet my left nipple this one is a Borough.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neighborhood bosses</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl : Hey Donut, can you go back out on the tracks and see if any of the corpses out there are neighborhood.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neighborhood Map</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Neighborhood Map</b> is a persistent item dropped by slain Neighborhood Bosses, meaning that every crawler who loots the boss's corpse can pick up the map. The Neighborhood Map looks like a scrap of paper. When a crawler touches it, it adds the boss's "neighborhood" and all mobs in the area to the crawler's minimap.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Neighborhood Boss</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Neighborhood Map on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Neighborhood_Map</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nekhebit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nekhebit</b> is a life and protector deity. She is a vulture goddess with the head of an Egyptian vulture and arms instead of wings. She has yellow and white feathers around her head that resemble an afro. Her beak is chipped and her skin has heavy scarring. She wears a threadbare red dress, bracelets, and an oak bow over her shoulder.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Odette (sponsor)The Scavenger Mother of Mothers</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nekhebit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nekhebit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nemes of Pestilence</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nemes of Pestilence</b> is an item <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives as part of her Assassin of Sekhmet Class. She gives it to <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Assassin of Sekhmet Class</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nemes of Pestilence on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nemes_of_Pestilence</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neon Blue</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 11 “‘Neon Blue’?” Donut exclaimed as she continued to examine the bootleg makeup.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The massive, flaming balls—significantly bigger and more powerful than what they were able to fire during the previous phase—started arcing through the sky, likely split evenly between the FUPA and the Nest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nest tower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The massive, flaming balls—significantly bigger and more powerful than what they were able to fire during the previous phase—started arcing through the sky, likely split evenly between the FUPA and the Nest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nester</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Nester is described as turned around in her chair, looking toward the apartment building.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neural Enhancer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: While your current wetware system is compatible with this Neural Enhancer, it is recommended you visit a Valtay Corporate Outreach Center to discuss upgrade.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Neural Enhancers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Valtay Corporation">Valtay Corporation</a> Neural Enhancers</b> are pills that cause permanent changes in the brain. Neural Enhancers are available in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space, or can be delivered to crawlers in Benefactor Boxes from their Sponsors. Once consumed, the Neural Enhancer grants specific abilities; these abilities work both inside and outside of Enhancement Zones, and cannot be unequipped or uninstalled.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Neural Enhancers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Neural_Enhancers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nevertheless</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Nevertheless, we knew the buffs were active because while our intelligence stat said one thing, our spell points reflected the actual.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A whole wall of New Achievement notifications appeared along with a couple other, new notifications, but instead of auto-playing like they did before, they appeared as little messages in the upper left of my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New Queen Anne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They trekked their way to the city where they found the burgeoning community of New Queen.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New Quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Carl, I told you her poetry was terrible!” New.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New Reformer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her uncle, the former opposition leader of the Gilt Party, was calling himself “Sultan Protectorate of the New Reformer” or something equally.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New York</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unlike the halting speech of their soother big brothers, this guy had more of a gritty, rapid-fire, New York sort of accent which was somehow even more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>New York City</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The exterior of the building looked straight out of 1920s New York.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nice</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Nice dixie cup, by the way,” I said, looking up at the white sailor hat sitting cockeyed on Donut’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nico</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nico</b> is one of the NPC racers that are against <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on the Tenth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Tigran</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nico on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nico</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nico and Dario</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She is married to both Nico and Dario, and she loves them both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Night</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Night is described as falling, and it would soon be dark, which was good for our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Night Weasel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Night Weasel</b> are A fuzzy jet black, six legged weasel with sharp oversized teeth. Capable of turning invisible and can paralyse creatures for five minutes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Night Weasel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Night_Weasel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Night Weasels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Night Weasels is described as the dogs in this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Night Wyrm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Night Wyrm, Astrid, Damascus Steel, and Anaconda are a family, and they’ve been stuck in the dungeon for a really long.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Night Wyrm's Nasty Little Web of Suffering Tattoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Night Wyrm's Nasty Little Web of Suffering</b> is a tattoo that grants access to the <a href="bword://Guild of Suffering">Guild of Suffering</a>. The extremely large tattoo appears on the Crawler's left elbow, and looks like a faded, green-tinted, poorly drawn spiderweb; the individual strands are segmented and wormlike.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Night Wyrm's Nasty Little Web of Suffering Tattoo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Night_Wyrm%27s_Nasty_Little_Web_of_Suffering_Tattoo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nightgaunt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What the fuck?” The moths all suddenly dive-bombed themselves into the candle as the Nightgaunt continued to ignore its own card deck—and the giant fucking cat—as it tried to cast another.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nightgaunt Cloak</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The bronze assassin box contained a pair of Quiet Slippers , which I wouldn’t be able to wear plus a mess of antidote potions I didn’t need unless I wasn’t wearing my Nightgaunt.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nightmare</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We should go on the Nightmare line to see where it goes.” She was probably right, but I didn’t like the idea of immediately jumping onto a train called “The Nightmare Express.” I turned to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nightmare Express</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Nightmare Express</b> is a named steam train that runs a figure-eight loop on the Iron Tangle of the Fourth Floor. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> hijacked it and used it for the rest of the floor before sending it into the Abyss as a bomb.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fourth Floor; Commandeered by Carl</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nightmare Express on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nightmare_Express</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nihit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Nihit, the reporter I’d stabbed in the neck with a pen and who was now the adjutant for the defeated Prism team of Empress D’Nadia had also been a four-armed snake, with purple and black.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nimblefoot Enforcer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The half-nagini, half-orc remained a Nimblefoot Enforcer, and she’d risen to level 55, equal with Katia and four below my level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nine-Tier Attack</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Nine-Tier Attack</b> is a catastrophic, nine-part assault associated with <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>, the great beast said to slumber beneath the volcano. In the lore of the volcano levels it is remembered as a legendary cataclysm — a "poison cloud" that killed or horribly transformed nearly everyone it touched — and it survives in the region's myths and folklore.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nine-Tier Attack on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nine-Tier_Attack</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ninth Edition</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ninth Edition&gt; Comrades, you know the high-level madness satchels, the bronze tier ones with the big.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nipple ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut actually had better fire protection than I did thanks to her nipple ring, but we were both exhausted and didn’t want to risk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nipple rings</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: He did have some magical items on him, but if he really had something to protect himself, he would’ve done it when he was getting eaten by the lady Mongos.” I reached down and pulled out the three remaining.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nitro Sludge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few days back when we were still on the previous floor, Mordecai had left a few little square blocks on my sapper’s table called Nitro Sludge.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>No Disassemble</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Goddamnit,” I called, turning around from my spot to see the small gnomish girl peeking around the back pile of the square-shaped No Disassemble EMP.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>No Disassembles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The square, shield and tech-busting No Disassembles clinked as they zipped out of the holders, pulled down by gravity, followed by the round high-yield Waffle Makers, which were shaped like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>No Man’s Land</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled myself up and over into No Man’s Land, joining over two thousand members of the Princess Posse as we engaged our first head-on assault into enemy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nodling</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nodling</b> are a race that has multiple heads they can have anywhere from two to six heads. When a nodling dies they split into a new creature for each head, these emerge as toddlers but quickly grow.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nodling on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nodling</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Non</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It will have the following effects on any pet or animal-class mob who consumes it: Non-sapient pets will have their Intelligence and Wisdom* stat raised to at least 3 and then additionally enhanced to be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>None</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: None of them killed him outright, and all were temporary, but there was paralysis, all stats are now 5, and something called “It’s been four hours and I think it’s time to call the doctor” which sounded.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nope</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pulled out the pack of cigarettes from my pocket and was about to light one when a loud, but firm, “Nope,” from behind the counter made me put it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Normally</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Normally, the other on-boarded attendants meet in a boardroom located in Larracos, but that meeting space is currently unavailable, so all of them will be attending from different locations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>North America</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prime Minister Victory and the staff of the new temporary headquarters for the Syndicate’s presence in the system are on the Eastern Seaboard of your North.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Notable attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Notable attacks: Blood into Lead Resurrect Totem Bullet Hell +20 additional skills and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Note</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 64 &lt;Note added by Crawler Carl, 25 th edition&gt; From page five of our planning notes: According to Edgar’s map, the control center for the four ballrooms—the two main ballrooms and the two attendant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Note Added by Crawler Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 59 ] &lt;Note Added by Crawler Carl, 25 th edition&gt; Operation: Ruin consists of four separate offensives, followed by what Donut calls the “Coup De War Crime.” Each of the four offensives kick off.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Note Added by Crawler Priestly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 30 ] &lt;Note Added by Crawler Priestly, 14th edition&gt; In the heat of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nothing by Apito</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to the legend, Yarilo—who was the child of Taranis—had been banished to the Nothing by Apito, who was both his grandmother and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nothing Special</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: One is called ‘Nothing Special Party Companion,’ one is just called ‘Nothing Special,’ and there’s this one.” I tentatively picked the crumbly little treat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nothing Special Party Companion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ‘Nothing Special Party Companion’ is similar to the Paramour benefit, but it is under the first subtree of the party companion, meaning they will stay in their existing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>NPC team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Seven teams remain, including for the first time ever, both a crawler team, led by Warlords Princess Donut and Carl, and perhaps even more astonishing, an NPC team, led by Warlords Ferdinand—whose existence.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>NPC teams</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t understand the math, but if a certain number of teams left us, most of the final heats—not including ones that already had more than one crawler team—would be one crawler team versus two NPC teams.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>NPCs</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>NPCs</b> (Non-Player Characters) are the living, biological creatures the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> engineers or repurposes to populate the Dungeon, ranging from autonomous mobs and scripted elites to indentured former crawlers. Safe-zone guildmasters, guides, and merchants fall under the same heading, flagged by the system as non-combatants.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: NPCs on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/NPCs</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nude Glabers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We start in the village, which is really a cavern filled with these things called Nude.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Nullian</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Nullians</b> (a.k.a. <b>Nulls</b> or <b>Grays</b>) look like typical "Roswell aliens," and are generally discriminated against in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space. Nulls are not a <a href="bword://Syndicate Council">Syndicate Council</a> race, and entire systems (illegally) refuse entry to their kind. As a result, many nulls live in communes in unfriendly systems; the Orcs recently raised the tax rate on the Null commune in the Filt System.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Nullian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Nullian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Number of crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai hadn’t said anything about it, but sometime in the past few weeks he’d turned off the surviving number of crawlers from displaying in the personal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Number one crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And while there are other military groups upon the surface who are clearly more suited, as well as other in-dungeon entities who could easily claim the title, the fact you are a warlord and the number one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oak bracelet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut had looted that oak bracelet off the dead body of one of Bautista’s family members, and he never seemed to have.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oak Fell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You assholes think Donut here is this Oak Fell antichrist person because she’s wearing that anklet?” “I told you wood jewelry is ugly,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oak Mother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Oak Mother’s wrath will set upon anyone who breaks the peace!” The room was huge, bigger than I’d expected from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Obsidian Butterfly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Obsidian Butterfly</b> is the race <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a> chooses on the Third Floor, pairing with the Fire Spiritualist class to form a support build centered on healing and buffs delivered through her wings.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Obsidian Butterfly on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Obsidian_Butterfly</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Obviously</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Obviously, the explosion part isn’t usually a feature of the toy, but the panel is designed to break if someone tampers with or attempts to illegally modify the toy’s innards.” “Then why is there a panel in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ocean floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had no idea what would happen if the octo-shark digested the stairwell, but I suspected it’d quickly pass through the shark’s system, and the entire room would just end up on the ocean.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ocean’s floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They say there are horrors deep down near the ocean’s floor.” I had forgotten that Chris had not beaten that level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Octo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s not, I repeat, it’s not named in such a way because of Nadya Suleman, the so-called Octo-mom and her eight babies who took the tabloids by storm all those years.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Octo-Shark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Octo-Shark</b> is a race of sharks with an octopus-like tail. Like a water centaur. <a href="bword://Lusca">Lusca</a> is one.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Octo-Shark on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Octo-Shark</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Odds is described as good nothing will happen up here on this floor, but I really want you guys out of here before it all goes down just in case.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odette</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Odette</b> is a former Crawler and the host of <i>Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette</i>. She is described as "the most-beloved interviewer and program host in the history of the galaxy."</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Unknown seeded world</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human (non-terrestrial)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Odette on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Odette</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odette’s show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: When I went from Odette’s show to that room, and I was by myself I had this terrible feeling for a second that I would never see you again.” She.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odious Creeper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What sort of prize?” “An expensive one.” All of my regular loot boxes had been uninteresting, including the boss box from the one Odious Creeper we killed, which gave me a bunch of satchel.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odious Creepers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Those two Odious Creepers we were worried about remained, and it didn’t appear as if either were going down, which meant everyone was about to lose the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Odius Creeper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Odius Creeper</b> are a mob on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Odius Creeper on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Odius_Creeper</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Offensive spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, it’s not meant as an offensive spell, and there are some protections in place to keep it from being used as.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oftentimes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Oftentimes the whole thing ended up in disaster, but sometimes there were negotiations that resulted in a ransom paid, which required both parties to temporarily trust each.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ogre</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ogre</b> are a race.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ogre on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ogre</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ogre creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ogre creature, apparently called a Psycho Sticker, had been so obliterated it didn’t even register as an X on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ogre Imperium</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He mentioned something called the Ogre Imperium, which was a long-forgotten plotline that had gotten binned because the ogres were made too intelligent and they had immediately figured out that they were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ogun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ogun</b> is a god who is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon, with <a href="bword://Legba">Legba</a>, <a href="bword://Shango">Shango</a>, <a href="bword://Oshun">Oshun</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Inle">Inle</a>, and <a href="bword://Yemaya">Yemaya</a>.According to <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a>, a common god to worship. God of blacksmiths and protection.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ogun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ogun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oh shit! A country Boss! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Oh shit! A country Boss! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded for seeing a Country Boss. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this when <a href="bword://Imogen">Imogen</a> shows up in the convoy on the way to the castle.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Oh shit! A country Boss! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Oh_shit%21_A_country_Boss%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ohhh</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thou hast seen nothing yet!” “Ohhh, look at that,” a female dwarf called, looking me up and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Old Shuck</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was Onikuma the bear mount for the dead razor foxes and Old Shuck, the giant dog from the Juggler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Olga</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Besides, if the AI thinks redheads are the spiciest, he’s clearly never met a Latina.” “I can’t help but notice you’ve been referring to the AI as a ‘he’ a lot more recently, Carl.” Olga slumped over in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Olivia Newton John</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You mean the song by Oasis?” Peaking at Number 1 on Nov 16, 1981, it’s “Physical!” The alarm trap activated, and the Olivia Newton John song started playing, so loud I took a small amount of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>On List</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, On List is described as released, he marched himself right to the closest port, found a bounty crew who hired null, and he signed right.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Once Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Once Donut is described as seated at the correct height, she could pull the “babies” out of her inventory, and they’d emerge right on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oncoming train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew I was running faster than any human had naturally run before, but I’d wasted a precious few seconds being disoriented and staring at the oncoming.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One and Only True Blood</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once you enter the ballroom and you are deemed to be in compliance with all the rules, you will officially be under the formal protection of the Queen of All That Is and the Queen of All That Will Be, the One.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One Attached Gem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One Attached Gem: Plain.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One Fine Pig</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I know I’m supposed to do what you say and I promise to come back, but I want you to know this is my family, and I will do what it takes to protect them.” Finley walked right out of the open garage door.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One Lady’s Journey</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her smash-hit book, having just passed a trillion copies sold, is entitled A Petite Chronicle of the Crawl: One Lady’s Journey into Enlightenment Through Knowledge and Scholarship and Three-Beat Poetry.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One More</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also by Matt Dinniman The page where we tout Facebook groups so they let us spam them about this book One.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One Quadrillion! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>One Quadrillion! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> when he reaches one quadrillion views. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> also received the achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: One Quadrillion! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/One_Quadrillion%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>One Way</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I tossed a hob-lobber toward a heavy gun position on the far side of their rapidly-deployed defense, but it got stuck in an anti-grenade, One Way shield, similar to the ones we.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Onikuma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was Onikuma the bear mount for the dead razor foxes and Old Shuck, the giant dog from the Juggler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Onikuma the bear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was Onikuma the bear mount for the dead razor foxes and Old Shuck, the giant dog from the Juggler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Only Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Only Donut, Griffin, Morales, and Jamal had the debuff, though the shark wasn’t actually affected since he couldn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ontario</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A Hunter who set traps for crawlers in Zockau.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 5, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ontario on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ontario</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Onto the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: My body smashed like a piece of dough caught under a tire, spread over the wall and door, completely covering it before I was plopped helplessly back onto the floor like an on-fire, liquid-filled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Onto the train</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: He and the survivors had retreated from the mimic back onto the train, which was thankfully still.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oooh, Magic Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> donned on his <a href="bword://Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling">Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling</a> and <a href="bword://Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness">Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Oooh, Magic Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Oooh%2C_Magic_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oozy Form</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d had Samantha roll into the cul-de-sac earlier and distract the two bugbears while I used my Oozy Form spell for the first.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oozy Form Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Oozy Form</b> is a spell that lets <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> temporarily transform into a slime that can traverse tight gaps, learned from a spellbook on the Tenth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Oozy Form Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Oozy_Form_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Open your damn boxes.” * * * During the Butcher’s Masquerade, I’d stood upon Prepotente’s personal space cube for a minute and opened a few boxes, but I’d deliberately not checked my achievements which.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We couldn’t open boxes inside the bar, nor were we protected from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I do hope you’re not planning on trying to get it because I will not have you wearing something that was pooped out by a goat.” Mordecai gave me a sharp look, but we were interrupted by the shout of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open doors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The moment it happened, I realized why we shouldn’t ever use the Hole spell to break open doors if we wanted to ever utilize said doors.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Hostilities</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We called it “Operation Ruin,” and it would kick off the moment the Open Hostilities phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Intellect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Intellect Pacifist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Intellect Pacifist Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC</b> is a small, tax-exempt organization operating in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space and the second sponsor of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fifth Floor. Behind its charitable registration, the Network is an activist group of former crawlers working to bring the crawl to a permanent end, and it is later re-formed as an openly militant resistance movement.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> OIAN; Open Intellect Pacifist Network; The Pacifist Network; Open Intellect Action Network</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Open_Intellect_Pacifist_Action_Network</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</b> is a DBA and front company for the <a href="bword://Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network">Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</a> (OIPAN), an intergalactic NFC.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Long_Haul_Biological_Waste_Management_Solutions</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open Intellect Pacifist Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open my boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had 24 stat points to distribute, but Mordecai had taught me to open my boxes and weigh any possible loot upgrades.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open our boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 25 We sent Rosetta and Tipid into Hungry Eyes to grab a mercenary—if any were left—while Donut and I returned to the safe room to quickly open our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s about a fourth of the size of this place, and it’s just one open room, save for the crafting studio, which is much smaller than the one you have.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open the box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Make certain you open the box before the floor ends, or you won’t be able to take it with you.” “Wait,” I said, looking between the hunter and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bomo has to open the door and stand on the edge, then I’ll cast the Clockwork Triplicate so the minion appears outside the room in that weird space where all the doors.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Open your boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had this one brother Charleston Chew, who got sent all the way to Australia, and the people who bought him used a private jet to⁠—” “Carl,” Imani said, interrupting, “you should open your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Opening the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: We entered the inn, and upon opening the door, we discovered the saferooms had reverted to their previous style from the first two.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Operatic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic reclamation team, run by a group of guys collectively named Otto Banking Unit 3 (remember that name), originally decided it was best to just poison the whole planet and start over, but an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Operatic Collective</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Operatics</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d helped him get a job where he never had to go down a gravity well ever again: Security working on the Auditor General’s compliance barge, moving from system to system, watching the Operatics fart and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Operating system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Operating system is described as still rudimentary, but you should be thankful because you wouldn’t be here right now without it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Operation Ruin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Operation Ruin is described as set, but the biggest wildcard was the enemy’s plans, if any, for the moment the third phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Opiee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Opiee</b> is a small, floating <a href="bword://Bune">Bune</a> and the adjutant for the Madness, one of the Faction Wars adjutants who preside over formal rules disputes during the Faction Wars on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Opiee on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Opiee</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Opposite Day Ring</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Opposite Day</b> looks like a "fat ring" studded with five small jewels that glow ominously like tiny <a href="bword://Soul Crystals">Soul Crystals</a>. When the wearer is struck by a powerful spell, the ring activates: one of the jewels burns out (or explodes or disappears), and the spell rebounds at the caster. The ring can only be used five times.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Opposite Day Ring on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Opposite_Day_Ring</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orange cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Orange cat is described as shaking with fear, and the end of his tail was scorched, like he’d barely missed getting skewered by a fire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orange Julius</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Next to us stood an Orange Julius stand, and the girl behind the counter looked at us and said, “If you’re not going to order anything, you gotta move.” I hadn’t realized that these simulations could include.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orc</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Orc</b> are a race both in the Dungeon and in the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>. Within the Syndicate, the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> is a part of the <a href="bword://Syndicate Council">Syndicate Council</a>. One of the citizen types of the Overcity are orcs. <a href="bword://GumGum">GumGum</a> is an orc NPC, but doesn't have the same skin color as <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orc on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orc</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orc Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Race: Half Nagini, Half-Orc Class: Nimblefoot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orc prince</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We just didn’t want you to be lonely, mate.” Donut was examining the orc prince via her new Targeting skill which had just been added to her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orchid of Eileithyia's Grace</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Orchid of Eileithyia's Grace</b> is a pink orchid contained inside the <a href="bword://Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm">Engaged Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</a>. Before choosing to worship the goddess, the three boons are simply described as: "<i>Triple Constitution, You are an Attendant, You get Pregnant.</i>" These descriptions are expanded after worshipping her.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orchid of Eileithyia's Grace on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orchid_of_Eileithyia%27s_Grace</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orcish Supremacy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>An Orcish government ruled by Tusklings, comparatively small when compared to the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>. They are also regarded as a more "hedonistic" society.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orcish Supremacy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orcish_Supremacy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orisha</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The Orisha pantheon are a group of former gods and goddess that attempt to get <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> to repair their shrine so that they can Ascend.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orisha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orisha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orren</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Orren</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> Liaison, an independent fact finder called in when the showrunners and the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> cannot agree on what happened. Orren is a parasitic worm and a citizen of the Valtay. He describes himself as an independent consultant retained by the Syndicate rather than an employee of Borant, working in concert with the showrunners, the Syndicate government, and the controlling System AI. When the kua-tin and the AI file contradictory or inconclusive reports on an incident, a liaison investigates and recommends a resolution; crawlers would not normally meet one.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Manager Orren, Orren the Liaison</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Knot</li><li><b>Race:</b> Gondii</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orren on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orren</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Orthrus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Orthrus</b> is an adorable, floppy eared, impossibly massive two-headed puppy that was pulled into the Nothing after his former master, <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>'s son <a href="bword://Geyrun">Geyrun</a>, was murdered.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 29</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Orthrus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Orthrus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oshun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Oshun</b> is a god who is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon, with <a href="bword://Ogun">Ogun</a>, <a href="bword://Legba">Legba</a>, <a href="bword://Shango">Shango</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Inle">Inle</a>, and <a href="bword://Yemaya">Yemaya</a>. During the Thorn Room Boss Battle, if her shrine is repaired: “Sweet, sweet Oshun. If you repair her shrine, you all will be protected from the next five fatal blows. Everyone will receive a fifty percent boost to their charisma. All will receive a level 10 <a href="bword://Heal Party">Heal Party</a> spell. Oshun will ascend.”</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Oshun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Oshun</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ossie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ossie</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (18th Edition)</i>. Ossie's Cookbook took the form of scrolls. &lt;Note Added by Crawler Ossie, 18th Edition&gt;</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ossie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ossie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Osvaldo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Osvaldo</b> is a Brazilian Crawler and a member of Team <a href="bword://Flamengo">Flamengo</a>. A <a href="bword://Curupira">Curupira</a> Ranger, he is known for his dispute with <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> over the <a href="bword://Memorial Crystal">Memorial Crystal</a> of <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Brazil</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Curupira</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 69</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Osvaldo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Osvaldo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Osvaldo and Filipe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Osvaldo and Filipe is described as standing outside their garage with the giant painted “3,” chatting with Radoslav and Jasha in garage four, who had their door open and were in their regular places in the lawn.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Others</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were “A new pet” and “50,000 gold” and “A Legendary Weapon box.” Others weren’t great, but they would still be considered a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Otherwise</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Otherwise, we’ll throw you all over the edge.” “Carl, administer the medicine.” I took the vial of Mountain Dew, yanked the cork, and poured it down the throat of “Wynne.” I suppressed a grin as he.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Otto Banking Unit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Operatic reclamation team, run by a group of guys collectively named Otto Banking Unit 3 (remember that name), originally decided it was best to just poison the whole planet and start over, but an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Outer ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We won’t have to pay our upline or make the initial seed investment when we do it that way.” As we finished our circle of the outer ring of the quadrant, I was forced to kill one of the clurichauns with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Outreach Guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente was in Hungry Eyes, walking along the long line of crawlers queuing to enter the Outreach Guild, collecting last-minute items from their inventories as they moved.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Outreach Guilds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Outreach Guilds is described as always right there on the eleventh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Outside</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I can go grab them real fast if you want.” Outside the privacy bubble, Rend and Mongo both walked up and their faces pressed against the bubble like it was a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Over City</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Like Scolopendra’s nine-tier attack that ruined our kingdom, she has prepared something that will reclaim the Over City for the skyfowl.” “She was waiting to collect enough power before she could cast this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Over on Facebook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: THE PAGE WHERE WE TOUT FACEBOOK GROUPS SO THEY LET US SPAM THEM ABOUT THIS BOOK Over on Facebook, if you want to talk about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Oxygen levels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Those were just the minions.” Warning: Your oxygen levels are.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were no circumstances in which Baroness Victory would end up in charge of the Skull Empire, not unless she first killed about 1,000 other nobles spread across the galaxy, so her motivations in helping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pacifist</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded when a crawler survives an entire floor without hurting a single mob. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Eighth Floor because <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> broke the Seventh Floor, resulting in every crawler making it through that floor without killing a single mob.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pacifist Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pacifist_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist Action Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Made for Crawler Carl by the Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic NFC with design approval by CEO and president of Outreach Operations.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That gave him a slightly-different designation on the form, and someone working for the Pacifist Network, someone imbedded deep, deep in the bureaucratic quagmire of the Syndicate had changed the code on that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pacifist's Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Pacifist's Box</b> is a Loot Box given to crawlers for actions of non-violence. Residents of <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> that made it to the Third Floor without killing anything got <b>Legendary Pacifist's Boxes</b>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Actions of non-violence</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pacifist's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pacifist%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pain Amplifier Jellyfish</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pain Amplifier Jellyfish</b> are a mob on the Fifth Floor. They are small, white jellyfish located in the Water Quadrant of Bubble 593. They inflict the <b>Kill Me Now It Hurts</b> debuff and a one-minute healing block. The <b>Kill Me Now It Hurts</b> debuff can be removed by using the <b><a href="bword://Jellyfish Salve">Jellyfish Salve</a></b> lotion.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pain Amplifier Jellyfish on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pain_Amplifier_Jellyfish</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paint the Target</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ll try to get someone who can see through the clouds with a Paint the Target spell so we can target it with the long range.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pair of crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: They said all that was left was splatter on the floor.” I had no idea what was going on with this exchange, but Zev seemed to realize she was talking in front of a pair of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pair of magic missiles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut hit the Cave Mudge standing in the open door with a pair of magic missiles, and it fell over dead just as the doors started to slide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Panda Express</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Permission will be granted in exchange for noodz or gift certificates to Panda.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Panford</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Panford</b> is a Dreadnaught and the adjutant for the <a href="bword://Lemig Sortion">Lemig Sortion</a> (referred to in the official proceedings as the "Democratic Sortion"), one of many adjutants who preside over formal rules disputes during the Faction Wars on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Panford on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Panford</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Panty Dropper Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Panty Dropper Spell</b> is a spell. The "Panty Dropper" spell is part of the NecroBard Class at level 3.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Panty Dropper Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Panty_Dropper_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Papa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Papa will keep you safe this time.” One of the gremlins jumped off the road and started booking it toward the hills, only to get splattered by a wrecking-ball-sized chunk of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paper monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: A few of the paper monsters draped themselves over the top of the castle, but several others lowered themselves slowly into the dark.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Parade of Horribles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And the second attack was called ‘A Parade of Horribles’?” “Yes, Ripper, that’s what I’m telling you.” And that’s when all the monitors in the studio changed to a screen showing nothing but a flowing star.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Park Fairy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Park Fairy Tales</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Parsons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Parsons had told me it was something to do with Marjory, but I hadn’t told that to Bea because she already hated the woman because Gravy Boat was always outside in the tree, harassing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Part</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Part of a collective mind intent upon destroying any semblance of scientific progress in the universe, the Krakaren is the only communal brain entity in the galaxy who actually gets stupider as time moves.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Part of the city</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We probably don’t want to get teleported out.” As we exited the club, I suddenly remembered there were several Faction Wars participants still trapped on the bottom level of the Desperado because the lower.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Part of the creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The head was the least kangaroo part of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Party</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The fact this has happened at the expense of the Skull Empire has made the Party very happy with you.” “Oh shit,” I said, any sense of amusement.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Party Companion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both of these are results under the Party Companion subtree of the result list with Donut under tree path one and Prepotente under tree path.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Party Planner</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He used a precious Emergency Repair scroll to speed up the repair of Party Planner and was about to start his second run as soon as they finished reloading the bomb.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Party Planner Vehicle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Party Planner</b> is an airship that <a href="bword://Louis">Louis</a> receives in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> box for surviving being marked for death by <a href="bword://Ysalte">Ysalte</a> on the Eighth Floor. The Party Planner is a fifty-foot long, sleek, silver tube sitting on a pair of chopper-like landing skids with four rectangle-shaped protuberances on the top. The fuselage isn’t a perfect tube, but more like a squished oval. It looks like a submarine crossed with a bullet train car with a rounded nose made of heavily-tinted glass reminiscent of the nose of a B-29 bomber, though not nearly as big.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Party Planner Vehicle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Party_Planner_Vehicle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Passenger car</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were only two cars on the train where we could get on: a caboose at the very back and a passenger car at the front, right behind the engine car, which had pulled into the forward tunnel the same way the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Passenger cars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: They didn’t all fit into the passenger cars, so they were moving to the cargo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Patches are fleeting items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: (Hidden until it is affixed and then worn.) Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pater Coal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pater Coal</b> is an NPC and the High Cleric Supreme of the <a href="bword://Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun">Emberus High Temple of the Ending Sun</a>, <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>'s secret temple inside <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Hobbledehoy</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 18</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pater Coal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pater_Coal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Path Method</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the castle is conquered, then the associated armies are defeated and dominion is transferred to the conqueror.) (See my essay on the nature of NPCs for more insight on how conquering areas may be utilized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pathfinder</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I also have something called Pathfinder which helps me find stairwells really easily.” I exchanged a look with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pathfinder skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s not quite as good as the pathfinder skill, which basically opens up the map in a very wide area, but this might be even more useful in some ways.” “Okay, what about this Naughty Boys Employment Agency?”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Patty Pump-It-Up</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Patty Pump-It-Up</b> is a <a href="bword://Dwarf">Dwarf</a> and an NPC in <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>'s Dungeon. She works as a stripper at the Bitches Room in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> meet Patty Pump-It-Up after completing the assassination quest for the <a href="bword://Guild of Suffering">Guild of Suffering</a>. She is one of the many strippers hiding from the Vorpals inside the Penis Parade.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dwarf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 33</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Patty Pump-It-Up on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Patty_Pump-It-Up</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paulie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Paulie</b> is the preferred name of <b><a href="bword://Goff">Goff</a></b>, and is a human occupied by a <a href="bword://Residual">Residual</a>. In the months before the transformation, he lived at the Shepherd's House, a homeless shelter near Miami, Florida.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Goff; Paulie</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Miami, FL</li><li><b>Race:</b> Residual</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 49</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Paulie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Paulie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Goff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Paulie">Paulie</a></b> is the preferred name of <b>Goff</b>, and is a human occupied by a <a href="bword://Residual">Residual</a>. In the months before the transformation, he lived at the Shepherd's House, a homeless shelter near Miami, Florida.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Goff; Paulie</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Miami, FL</li><li><b>Race:</b> Residual</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 49</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Goff on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Goff</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paw Patrol Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Paw Patrol</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a scroll offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Paw Patrol Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Paw_Patrol_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pawna's Cries</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar but distinct from the <a href="bword://Pawna&#x27;s Tears">Pawna's Tears</a> <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a>, the <b>Pawna's Cries</b> potion adds three levels to any spell or skill that the crawler chooses. Due to its "limited shelf life," it must be used before the Floor collapses.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pawna's Cries on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pawna%27s_Cries</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pawna's Tears</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar but distinct from the <a href="bword://Pawna&#x27;s Cries">Pawna's Cries</a> <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a>, the <b>Pawna's Tears Potion</b> is an extremely high-value item that adds five levels to any spell or skill chosen by the imbiber. The potion does not expire.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Legendary Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pawna's Tears on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pawna%27s_Tears</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pawna’s Cries</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: And that wasn’t including the Pawna’s Cries potion I’d received from the Open Intellect Pacifist Network or the I Take it All Back potion given to Prepotente, both also intended to be given to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pawna’s Tears</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a bit of a waste using my more-powerful Pawna’s Tears potion, which raised the skill by five additional points, but my sponsor claimed he would replace.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Payload Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Payload Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for dropping all the bombs in his inventory at the end of the Ninth Floor. He receives a Gold version of the box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bomb's Away Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Payload Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Payload_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Payment and Legacy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Payment and Legacy plans.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The last time they’d tried this, the seal had overwhelmed Paz’s defenses too quickly, making it so Anton had to take it out with a “one-shot.” I wasn’t certain what that was, but I assumed it was a powerful.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paz and Anton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple fountains sprouted out of nowhere, including one right underneath both Paz and Anton, who remained on the floor, writhing in pain from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Paz Lo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Paz Lo: race/species: <a href="bword://Human">Human</a>; class: Santero Class; first scene: Chapter 5, Book 6.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 5, Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Paz Lo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Paz_Lo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pazuzu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pazuzu</b> are a half-human, half-scorpion race found in the land quadrant of the Fifth Floor of the Dungeon. They appear both as settled townsfolk, who keep the inns, taverns and shops of towns such as Pandinus, and as feral mobs that swarm the open desert after dark.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pazuzu on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pazuzu</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peace Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Peace Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Pacifist&#x27;s Box">Pacifist's Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://Pacifist Achievement">Pacifist Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Pacifist's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Peace Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Peace_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peaking at Number</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: – Anyway, Peaking at Number 3 on June 20, 2020, despite first coming out in 1992, It’s “Killing in the Name.” The Rage Against the Machine Song echoed, powerfully loud, literally shaking the ground louder than.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pearson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>One of the Changelings from Hump Town. He had been impersonating a <a href="bword://Dromedarian">Dromedarian</a> bartender. He meets <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> to get the map and plan to evacuate the Changelings to the 9th Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 58, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pearson on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pearson</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peeling of Larracos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then there’s the Ramp-Up, Open Hostilities, and the Peeling of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peeling Phase</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Message : Since we are now in the Peeling Phase, this ruling is rendered.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peeping Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We decided to call it Peeping Mongo, remember?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Peeping Tom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We call it ‘Peeping Tom’ so I can look through the hole first and see if the clockwork Mongos are.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pending Action</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What should we use them on?” “Once the game starts, it’s too late to use regular action items, but we can still propose emergency ones while the game is ongoing, so we need to keep ours in reserve in case.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pending Action Item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What should we use them on?” “Once the game starts, it’s too late to use regular action items, but we can still propose emergency ones while the game is ongoing, so we need to keep ours in reserve in case.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penelope</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Penelope</b> is a pig. A beloved and many married pig and magically protected pig.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Yorkshire Sow</li><li><b>First scene:</b> book 8, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Penelope on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Penelope</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penelope the pig</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Porky was staring at the food on the ground, but then I realized he was actually staring at Penelope the pig, licking his lips with his half tongue, which looked especially lascivious due to the white gimp.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penis Palace</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Apparently she went to the Penis Palace on the middle floor, but it was “boring,” so she went up a floor to where I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penis Parade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia and I needed to figure out how to find and kill the club’s assistant manager, Astrid, and then we needed to get her body to the Guild of Suffering, the entrance of which was one floor above us in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Penn</b> is a former crawler who comes back to help the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> in the Faction Wars (game) on the Ninth Floor. A tall, muscular human sergeant stood right there, carrying what looked like a steel H-beam for a building. The metallic beam had to be 20 feet long, and it looked as if it weighed two tons. The monster of a man held it on his shoulder.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Penn on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Penn</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Penny</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: A sluggalo was on fire, screaming, spinning in circles, while Penny the pig ate a fully cooked chicken sandwich, grunting contentedly on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>People</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: People shied away from me even more afterward, and after I accidentally hurt two crawlers when my Whoopsie Daisy spell backfired and I received two player-killer skulls, I was truly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Per level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d gained one called Raise Forest , which caused trees and plants over a one kilometer area per level of spell to quickly sprout, which was just.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Per level of spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d gained one called Raise Forest , which caused trees and plants over a one kilometer area per level of spell to quickly sprout, which was just.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Perhaps</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Perhaps these mages winning, no matter what their motives are, is not such a bad.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Permacube</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>permacube</b> generates heat when combined with combustible material such as coal. It has an extremely long lifespan and goes dormant when it runs out of coal, but does not turn off. It reactivates a few minutes after the material is shoveled in.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Goblins</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Permacube on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Permacube</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Persian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And our tattoos were placed upon us against our wills.” Bea’s tattoo was a faded, Persian cat on her lower back surrounded by weird, uneven.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Persian cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “And our tattoos were placed upon us against our wills.” Bea’s tattoo was a faded, Persian cat on her lower back surrounded by weird, uneven.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Personal Space</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The magical enhancements of this item may only be imbued within a Safe or Personal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pestiferous Vine</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pestiferous Vine</b> appear to be massive living vines that infect other mobs. Pestiferous Vines aren't really a plant, but rather a hybrid fungus with a type of plant parasitic worms.They really exist outside the Dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pestiferous Vine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pestiferous_Vine</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Biscuit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A regular <b>Pet Biscuit</b> is a small, round, crumbly hard pellet that can feed a pet or dungeon familiar for one full day. Pets find them delicious, so <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> advises only pulling out one biscuit at a time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Pet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pet Biscuit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pet_Biscuit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Pet Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for pet-related achievements. All tiers of the box have a hedgehog-like animal stenciled on them. The bronze Pet Box is about half the size of a military footlocker, bronze-colored and had a hegde-hog like animal stenciled on top.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pet Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pet_Box</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Angels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Pet Angels is described as a Seattle charity that gave free pet supplies to people.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Biscuit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A regular <b>Pet Biscuit</b> is a small, round, crumbly hard pellet that can feed a pet or dungeon familiar for one full day. Pets find them delicious, so <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> advises only pulling out one biscuit at a time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Pet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pet Biscuit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pet_Biscuit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Pet Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for pet-related achievements. All tiers of the box have a hedgehog-like animal stenciled on them. The bronze Pet Box is about half the size of a military footlocker, bronze-colored and had a hegde-hog like animal stenciled on top.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pet Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pet_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet Carrier</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pet Carrier</b> is a magical pet carrier that is "the dungeon's equivalent of a pokeball." It allows bonded pets into a crawler's inventory without harming them.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Buy</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pet Carrier on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pet_Carrier</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pet show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: An evil, vile, experience hog crawler named Tserendolgor somehow managed to get her hands on the voting ballot box for the pet show and changed the votes, stealing Mongo’s crown from him and handing it off to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>PETA Enthusiast Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>PETA Enthusiast Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded for removing the aggro of an opponent.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: PETA Enthusiast Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/PETA_Enthusiast_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Petite Chronicle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her smash-hit book, having just passed a trillion copies sold, is entitled A Petite Chronicle of the Crawl: One Lady’s Journey into Enlightenment Through Knowledge and Scholarship and Three-Beat Poetry.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pharmaceutical Starter Kit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Pharmaceutical Starter Kit</b> is a large chest filled to the brim with chemicals, herbs, and "everything you need to start your own pharmaceutical empire." <a href="bword://Quint">Quint</a> sells these in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, but crawlers typically can't afford them until the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Pharmacists</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pharmaceutical Starter Kit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pharmaceutical_Starter_Kit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’ll be there in less than an hour.” Phase two was now finished, and we’d managed to save about 1,500 crawlers.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase Lava Rock</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Phase Lava Rock potion only lasted a minute, so I didn’t want to take it too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It can be a special type of healing scroll for lava rock monsters like Chris, or it can be used as a phase potion for people to be able to easily pass through lava.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase Through Wall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first dozen were bronze and gold adventurer boxes, filled with good healing and mana potions along with a few potions of Phase Through Wall , which Mordecai was pretty excited about, along with dozens of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase Through Walls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I have a few Phase Through Walls potions!” I called, jumping down to land amongst everybody.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phase Two</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re going on Odette’s show, and you’re going to walk straight from there to the Phase Two onboarding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Phil Collins</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pony was singing “In the Air Tonight” by Phil.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Physical attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t know what upgrade the Lady Dominators had picked, but if it was a shield, it likely didn’t stop both magical attacks, which was what Donut’s bolt would be, and physical attacks, which was what the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Piccadilly Theater</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The floor contained the massive Piccadilly Theater, surrounded by bars, including a few true.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Pick some, and it’ll be a deal.” All of the books were science fiction or 80’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pick a class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once she actually got herself to a safe room, she could pick a class and change her race if she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pick a new class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It said it was December 10 th .” “Carl, I need to pick a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pied Piper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Or maybe you can use it in conjunction with your Pied Piper skill or whatever.” “I didn’t get to keep Pied Piper until after that had happened, and the skill allows me to play musical.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pierre</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pierre</b> is a Porter for the Yellow line train on the Iron Tangle. Pierre is a quarter giant, similar looking to Lurch from the Addam's Family. He's described as having a wide pale face, and hair that looks like dying moss. He wears a porter outfit.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Grapple</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pierre on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pierre</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pincushioned Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pincushioned</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for having both eyeballs pierced at the same time. During a Deckmaster fight against Tom the <a href="bword://Turkey">Turkey</a> and <a href="bword://Sharp-Elbows">Sharp-Elbows</a> the <a href="bword://Ogre">Ogre</a> Necromancer Witch (Deckmasters Eight and Nine) on the Eighth Floor, Carl activates the Daughter's Kiss ability of his <a href="bword://Scavenger&#x27;s Daughter Upgrade Patch">Scavenger's Daughter Upgrade Patch</a> to kill Tom the Turkey. The resulting explosions destabilizes the barn where the fight had occurred and in the resulting tumult Carl secures himself to a concrete slab using his Sticky Feet Benefit.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pincushioned Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pincushioned_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pineapple Cabaret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d spent some time discussing the potential trap that the Pineapple Cabaret was, and even though we’d already sent Li Na, we decided it would be best that only volunteers who didn’t worship a god and had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ping</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It works really well, especially when combined with Ping because I can apply it to hunters who are pretty far away.” I pulled myself up, but my head swam, and I lowered myself back down onto the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ping Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Also known as, “Here piggy, piggy,” or “The Night Dread,” Ping is a hunting and artillery-aiming tool for those who do not care if their quarry knows they’re coming.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ping Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ping_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pit Fighter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: One was a human named Britney Proskurina, and she was a level-27 Pit Fighter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pit Stop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was an arch with the words “Pit Stop” over it in flashing neon light that looked ridiculously out of place in the middle of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plague Bearer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: But one is considered deliberate, thirst-based evil, and the other, the Plague Bearer, is a study on how one’s poor actions can ripple through time and become amplified and doom us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plains of Larracos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The whole area outside the city was called the Plains of Larracos, and it was mostly flat, except for the northernmost area of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plan A is for one of my kind to say this face-to-face inside the dungeon when the time is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plan skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The little sign could only be read using my Escape Plan skill, and it simply said, “Trainyard.” I took a screenshot, and trainyard C was a burned-out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Planet Beautiful</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Planet Beautiful is described as not privy to your exact location, and the only entities on board are you and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Planetwide Message</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Planetwide Message: Be advised that the 16 th Floor, supposedly entitled “Agony of Mirrors” is actually a giant, empty room with a single mirror in it: a non-magical mirror plucked from an abandoned Motel 6.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai said since this was a Platinum box, people had to actually pay a credit to vote, so the voting was less likely to be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Asshole’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: When my next box opened, the Platinum Asshole’s Box, I realized what it was the moment the symbol appeared in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Asshole’s Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: When my next box opened, the Platinum Asshole’s Box, I realized what it was the moment the symbol appeared in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Big Daddy Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Platinum Big Daddy Box contained a regular, unenchanted toothbrush, plus a tiny, travel-sized tube of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai said since this was a Platinum box, people had to actually pay a credit to vote, so the voting was less likely to be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Fan Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You have received a Platinum Fan.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Lucky Bastard Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Platinum Lucky Bastard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “As a result of surviving the event quest, all crawlers in the initial blast zone were promised a Platinum Quest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Quest Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “As a result of surviving the event quest, all crawlers in the initial blast zone were promised a Platinum Quest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Sharpening Stone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets the <b>Platinum Sharpening Stone</b> as a reward for completing the Squeeze out the <a href="bword://Juice Box">Juice Box</a> Quest quest on the Fifth Floor. A little black rock.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Quest Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Platinum Sharpening Stone on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Platinum_Sharpening_Stone</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Spicy Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You have received a Platinum Spicy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum That</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pushed it aside for the moment to give room for the last box to open, my Platinum That-Was-Disgusting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum To Hell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d received the same Platinum To Hell in a Handbasket box I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Tyrant’s Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: At the same time I had received a Platinum Tyrant’s Box plus a Bronze Assassin’s box, which I’d gotten for becoming the town’s new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Venison</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have received a Platinum Venison.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Platinum Venison Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have received a Platinum Venison.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Play a game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Some of them are genuine NPCs, but the big ones like Apito and Eris and so forth are always sponsored by some rich prick who basically paid extra to play a game called the Celestial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Playing cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m sorry,” he cried before bouncing off the corner of the room, flying across the small chamber, knocking half the playing cards off the counter, launching off my shoulder, howling the whole time before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Playing the game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had spent hours and hours playing the game when I was stuffed in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Please</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Please talk some sense into my idiotic partner, who is insisting on sabotaging our next race.” “I have a sixth sense about these.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Please Wait</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Epitome Tagg of the Dream is a...” Please.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plenty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Plenty</b> are a caprid people who invented and control the modern tunneling system, the proprietary technology that carries real-time communication across the universe.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Herd</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Plenty on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Plenty</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plenty of Plenty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 27 A few hours later, Donut and I were yanked away to go on our live appearance of the show Plenty of Plenty.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plenty Tunnel Project</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He is one of the chief engineers of the Plenty Tunnel Project, which opened up the veins of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Plus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plus it’ll be in the sky above, not the ground, and it’ll suck everything up like that cute but sad metal guy you have floating in your house that slurps up bags of potato chips and cleans when Mongo tinkles.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pocket Kuma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pocket Kuma</b> is the race <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> became on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pocket Kuma on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pocket_Kuma</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Podophilia! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> earned this achievement for stomping a goblin with his bare feet on the first floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Podophilia! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Podophilia%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poet Laureate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: When we had the choices presented to us, it was the only race that gave me a charisma boost for my Word Weaver spell, amongst others, and our game guide insisted it would allow me to choose the Poet Laureate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Point Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d already received a notification that she’d changed the name from “Large Ursine Settlement” to “Point Mongo.” The system had also changed her name to “Mayor Donut” but she’d quickly changed it back to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poison Antidote</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Poison Antidotes</b> are <i>presumably</i> potions that heal the Poisoned debuff. Crawlers may receive Poison Antidotes in low-tier Adventurer Boxes.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Poison Antidote on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Poison_Antidote</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poison Cloud</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plus this thing imbues stuff with Poison Cloud .” She answered by making a growling noise and then rolling off through the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poison Grapple Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Poison <a href="bword://Grapple">Grapple</a></b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Poison Grapple Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Poison_Grapple_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pollyslog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pollyslog</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that can be found at the Phantom Kangaroo station #84 on the Yellow Line. The Pollyslog look like large, green-skinned fish-like humanoids. They have massive milk white eyes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pollyslog on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pollyslog</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pontifex Shine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pontifex Shine</b> is a wealthy, external sponsor representing the religious faction known as the <a href="bword://Nebular">Nebular</a> Balance. Very little is known about the character personally, but they play a critical background role in sponsoring both crawlers and deadly deities during the events of the World Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Syndicate (Outside the Dungeon)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown (Nebulars consist of various species including Soothers, Humans, and Elves)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 66</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pontifex Shine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pontifex_Shine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pontiff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pontiff</b> is a level 83 Taurin mercenary and a former dancer. Originally introduced as hired help for the racing team One Fine Pig during the Tenth Floor, Pontiff eventually defects to aid <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>, acting as their driver and undercover operative.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Taurin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pontiff on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pontiff</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pony</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: All that mattered was in about three or four minutes, Vinata and her guards were going to transfer to this specific temple, and Katia, Pony, Samantha, and myself were going to break into Club Vanquisher, break.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pooka</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Pooka</b> on the Fourth Floor is a Neighborhood Boss, and part of the Iron Tangle political statement. They hand out the drugs that the <a href="bword://Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)">Krakaren Clone (Fourth Floor)</a> boss would make.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon - Fourth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Plenty</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pooka on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pooka</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Poor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Poor guy.” Ifechi entered the dungeon with a group of other men, all soldiers, all wielding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Popov</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The photograph the blob kid was getting autographed was of the Popov brothers swinging their meteor hammer while inside an about-to-derail train car as flaming hedgehog things attacked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Popov Brothers</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Fraternal twin brothers. The two of them speak with an eastern European accent. Dmitri had just brought a cat named Capitan Whiskers. Maxim has a meteor hammer.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Nodling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 25, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Popov Brothers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Popov_Brothers_%28Maxim_%26_Dmitri%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Popovs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They could get either me or the Popovs, and there was one photo, a video really, of me shaking hands with the brothers, taken literally minutes earlier in the green.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pork Boy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I would personally like to assure King Rust that I would never call Prince Maestro ‘Carl’s Naughty Little Piggie’ or ‘Pork Boy.’ And I would like to encourage my audience to never sink to that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pork Boy Snick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: This gripe you have with Prince Maestro is getting much more attention, especially with that Pork Boy Snick that just showed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Porky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Porky</b> is one of the two split halves of the mantaur <a href="bword://Corcunda">Corcunda</a>, a Stage Performance Guildmaster reunited with his other half, Corky, over the course of the Tenth Floor's racing arc.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Porky on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Porky</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Porsuk</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Porsuk</b> are a badger-headed race. All the bartenders at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> are Porsuk.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Porsuk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Porsuk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Portal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Portal</b> allow for teleport between two locations in the Dungeon. They were a common mechanic on the Fourth Floor. Portals are a Dungeon mechanic that allows for teleportation between one location to another. Portals can be key locked to prevent unauthorized individuals from passing through. Portals can pass inside each other without issue.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Portal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Portal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Portal skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: My portal skill didn’t work on it because it wasn’t turned on, though I suspected we were out of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Porthus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Dr. Porthus Hu</b> is a former crawler and the author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (2nd Edition)</i>. He is now CEO of the <a href="bword://Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network">Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Dr. Porthus Hu</li><li><b>Race:</b> Elf| High Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Porthus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Porthus</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Posse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Princess Posse</b> is Team #3 in the Ninth Floor Faction Wars of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>, co-led by Crawlers <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and crowd-funded by Donut's massive intergalactic fan club. Sponsored by the <a href="bword://Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels">Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels</a> (TSECS) and doing business as Princess Posse Enterprises, NFC, the team aims to survive the Ninth Floor, protect as many crawlers and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> as possible, and level the playing field by stripping the safety protections from the wealthy out-worlder combatants.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Princess Posse, sponsored by the Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels (TSECS), NFC, DBA as Princess Posse Enterprises, NFC</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Princess Posse on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Princess_Posse</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Posse Fan Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Princess Posse Fan Club</b> is the official fan club for Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. Donut refuses to acknowledge the unofficial fan club, the <a href="bword://Donut Holes">Donut Holes</a>. Zev lets Donut know that the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> is winning out over the Donut Holes as a name for her fan club.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Princess Posse Fan Club on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Princess_Posse_Fan_Club</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Post Nut Clarity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Post Nut Clarity was, apparently, a debuff from my soul poisoning that knocked me out once I activated my Daughter’s Kiss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>In the Dungeon, <b>Potions</b> are single-use consumable items that produce a magical effect, usually a buff or debuff, and act on crawlers, pets, mobs, and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> alike. They can be drunk, triggered through the hotlist, or thrown as weapons.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting, Loot, Loot Boxes, Store</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Bloodlust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Potions of Bloodlust</b> make you go faster and hit harder, and every time you kill something you go even faster. That's the fun news. The bad news is it also makes you forget whose a friend and whose an enemy<b>.</b> <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets two as a reward for the <a href="bword://Milquetoast Achievement">Milquetoast Achievement</a> from a Silver Pacifist Box, which goes to show you that the AI loves a proper bit of irony.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Pacifist Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Bloodlust on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Bloodlust</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Dinosaur Repellent</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives several <b>Potions of Dinosaur Repellent</b> in adventurer boxes on the Fifth Floor. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> is immediately concerned by the appearance of these potions. After receiving several on the fifth floor, Carl receives another 10 on the Sixth Floor before their purpose starts to becomes clear. Carl makes sure all the crawlers attending the Masquerade have access to Dinosaur Repellent as he guesses that <a href="bword://Kiwi">Kiwi</a>'s herd are going to become hostile.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Adventurer Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Dinosaur Repellent on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Dinosaur_Repellent</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Half Splat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Potion of Half Splat</b> is a potion that prevents you from dying from falling damage but leaves you at 5% health.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Half Splat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Half_Splat</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Healing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: (2/10) Potion of Healing X.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Invisibility</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives his first <b>Potion of Invisibility</b> in a Reward Room prize case on the Fourth Floor. Turns the drinker invisible for 2 x intelligence seconds.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Reward Room prize case / Adventurer Box / Silver Lucky Bastard Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Invisibility on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Invisibility</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Phase Through Wall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives a few <b>Potions of Phase Through Wall</b> at the start of the Eighth Floor. Allows the drinker to phase through wood, stone, or metal walls. According to <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> they're more complex that their description suggests and actually work on anything that is "solid and unnatural non-biological with empty space on both sides".</li><li><b>Source:</b> Adventurer Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Phase Through Wall on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Phase_Through_Wall</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potion of Somnolent Embrace</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> brews the <b>Potion of Somnolent Embrace</b> for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> to take while they expunge <a href="bword://Shi Maria">Shi Maria</a> on the Ninth Floor. Mordecai gives this potion to Carl so he can stay asleep during the process to get rid of Shi Maria.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Mordecai</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potion of Somnolent Embrace on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potion_of_Somnolent_Embrace</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potions</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Potions is described as much better, especially this season with the unlimited.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Potsy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Potsy</b> is an NPC that guards the front door of <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Potsy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Potsy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Power armor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I’d allowed them all to come in here with their heavy duty, Cronus-class power armor, they’d already be putting those collectible, heart-covered boxers on clearance in all waystation gift shops across the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Powered magic missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She shot a low-powered magic missile, and it blew an axe out of the hand of a Draconian who was about to sink it into the back of Dong’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Powerful monsters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, some of the other, more powerful monsters have become something else, and short of a god, they are the most powerful monsters who have ever set foot on a sixth floor in Dungeon Crawler World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Powerful spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: All 15 of those immortals had temples inside of Club Vanquisher, and, unique amongst the light gods and goddesses, was a powerful spell called Travel the Path.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Powerful Strike</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I really needed to work on my Powerful Strike, which was a more general, catch-all fighting skill than the punching-only Bare Knuckle, but since I’d practiced it the day before, I wanted to see if training it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pox Slug</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pox Slug</b> are a horrifying slug mob that can start exploding from your skin if your infected with Slug Pox. Starts at level 1, each additional one becomes stronger by one level.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pox Slug on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pox_Slug</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Precious Elemental Reserves Code</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Precious Elemental Reserves Code</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> law pertaining to the rights of planets to their mineral wealth.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Precious Elemental Reserves Code on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Precious_Elemental_Reserves_Code</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Precision Card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Precision Card</b> is a utility T'Ghee card that increases a totems chance of landing an attack. If stacked they count as a single buff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Precision Card on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Precision_Card</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Predator</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: As soon as I called “Predator,” we both went invisible, and Donut switched to infrared vision using her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Predator Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Predator Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the <a href="bword://Apex Predator Achievement">Apex Predator Achievement</a>. This is the first <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> box he receives. He opens this box on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Apex Predator Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Predator Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Predator_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Premature Questjaculation Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Premature Questjaculation Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> killed <a href="bword://Claude Sludgington the Fourth">Claude Sludgington the Fourth</a>, a level 65 borough boss, before they received the quest for it because Carl recalled <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> when he dropped his xistera.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Premature Questjaculation Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Premature_Questjaculation_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepare</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Prepare yourself, Santero!” Asojano.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepare for Trouble! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The Prepare for Trouble! Achievement<b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for fighting two card opponents at the same time on the Eighth Floor.</b></li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prepare for Trouble! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prepare_for_Trouble%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Prepotente Lorenzo De La Rosa</b>, often called <b>Pony</b>, is a Crawler from Italy in Dungeon Crawler World: Earth. He entered the Dungeon as one of roughly fifteen brown-and-white Boer goats belonging to fellow crawler <a href="bword://Miriam Dom">Miriam Dom</a>, until an <a href="bword://Enhanced Pet Biscuit">Enhanced Pet Biscuit</a> transformed him into an intelligent, bipedal <a href="bword://Caprid">Caprid</a>. He quickly climbed into the dungeon's Top Ten Crawlers at Miriam's side as a Forsaken Aerialist.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Pony</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Italy</li><li><b>Race:</b> Boer Goat | Caprid</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 14</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prepotente on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prepotente</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Prepotente Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for A box that screams when it's opened, named after <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>. It appears to be a statue of Prepotente.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prepotente Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prepotente_Box</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente and Jurgen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was a total of twelve heats entering this race, but none of the other heats were teams from our guild except Prepotente and Jurgen aboard their tapir.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente and Katia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: These are the actions everyone in the room took the moment we all unfroze: Vinata’s three guards charged at us as they fired their rifles, focusing on Prepotente and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente and Miriam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I focused on Prepotente and Miriam, and they were said to be near Point.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente and Miriam Dom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It makes for some delicious combinations, like that epic battle with Grull, with Prepotente and Miriam Dom losing out on killing a province boss twice thanks to you guys and then to Quan Ch, and of course.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Prepotente Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for A box that screams when it's opened, named after <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>. It appears to be a statue of Prepotente.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prepotente Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prepotente_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prepotente’s Sweety</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Far ahead, I spied Prepotente’s Sweety turned sideward, making their way along the wall.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Presence of a deity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 66 You are in the presence of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pretty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pretty much everyone had thought this was a terrible idea, but I had argued that the birth of Bigs on the previous floor had saved my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Priestly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Priestly</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (14th Edition)</i>. Priestly's contributions to the cookbook are the single best source of information about the Ninth Floor Faction Wars. He loved music and was charmed by the city of Larracos.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Priestly on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Priestly</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primal</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Primal</b> is an ancient alien race that vanished after spreading across the galaxy. It survives in the Dungeon as a rare, blank-slate racial option available to Crawlers on the Third Floor, one that costs base stats but removes the training cap on every skill.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Primal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Primal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primal Engine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Primal Engine</b> is an ancient planet-regulating computer with a basic operating system. A machine approximately the size of a grain of rice, <a href="bword://Primal">Primal</a> Engines can be found at the cores of some planets in multiple solar systems throughout the galaxy.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Primals</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Primal Engine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Primal_Engine</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primal Engine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Primal Engine</b> is an ancient planet-regulating computer with a basic operating system. A machine approximately the size of a grain of rice, <a href="bword://Primal">Primal</a> Engines can be found at the cores of some planets in multiple solar systems throughout the galaxy.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Primals</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Primal Engine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Primal_Engine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primal Engines</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Many years later, and in a separate innovation, we discovered what’s basically an advanced, upgraded operating system for the Primal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primal race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: My primal race allowed me to train past 15 in skills and spells, but armor and weapons-based enhancements lost effectiveness past 15, which meant certain benefits, such as the +1 to Powerful Strike from my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Primals</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The first part is what we, the Primals, called ‘The Resolution.’ And yes, I, along with all macro AI systems, are.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prime Minister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: By the gods, the people are about to watch all of their leaders get murdered one by one live via tunnel.” “That’s not going to happen,” the Prime Minister.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prime Minister Glory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: A galactic power—In this case, Prime Minister Glory himself—just attempted to assassinate you using a little known, but apparently still active control panel that allows the Syndicate to override certain.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prime Minister Victory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prime Minister Victory and the staff of the new temporary headquarters for the Syndicate’s presence in the system are on the Eastern Seaboard of your North.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prince Maestro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prince Maestro had been the second in the line of succession until he’d been disowned and then killed by the Valtay in retaliation for the botched assassination attempt that had killed Manasa instead of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prince Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We represent the Princess Posse, a non-profit conglomerate with tens of thousands of chapters of intelligent, like-minded, beautiful fans from...” “This is ridiculous,” Prince Stalwart said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d received word that the Mantis government had unexpectedly abandoned their stake when I last spoke with Quasar, which ended up being a good thing as there was another entity bidding for the open spot in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Chandra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Much the same way these papers paint a picture of who you are, Princess Chandra.” Donut let out another.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Chonkalot</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Princess Chonkalot</b> is <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s grandmother, a celebrated, multiple-award-winning Persian show cat who originally belonged to Bea as a childhood pet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Princess Chonkalot on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Princess_Chonkalot</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Navigator: The Champion of Nekhebit, Harbinger of Doom, Assassin of the Great and Feral Sekhmet, Princess Donut, She Who Is Foretold to Bring the Gnashing of the Teeth and the Woe of All Who Harbor Hope and Is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Donut and Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, before I bring our guest on, I want everyone to know that I sat Princess Donut and Carl down ahead of time and told them we were going to have this guest, and I asked their permission to do.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Donut the Queen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Donut’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And then, after a short conversation and a discreet payment to a district judge’s reelection campaign, Chandra had found herself assigned as Princess Donut’s attorney, effectively giving herself access to both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess D’nadia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I am sponsored by the beautiful and awesome Princess D’nadia of the Prism and Carl got the Valtay Corporation.” “I did,” Katia said as we entered the pho.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Formidable</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s just see what it is.” Soon after Katia had been sponsored by the Princess Formidable, the youngest sister of Prince Maestro and Crown Prince Stalwart of the Skull Empire, we explained to her everything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Imogen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: At the same time, just as King Finian’s health started to fail, his heir—Princess Imogen—set out to eradicate all the half-elf bastards he’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Lunette</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the meantime, Imani and Rosetta, you will open up Princess Lunette and expose the heart and pulmonary.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Palette</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Their newest product, the Princess Palette eye makeup kit, was already the highest-earning crawler merch in the history of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Posse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Princess Posse</b> is Team #3 in the Ninth Floor Faction Wars of <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>, co-led by Crawlers <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and crowd-funded by Donut's massive intergalactic fan club. Sponsored by the <a href="bword://Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels">Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels</a> (TSECS) and doing business as Princess Posse Enterprises, NFC, the team aims to survive the Ninth Floor, protect as many crawlers and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> as possible, and level the playing field by stripping the safety protections from the wealthy out-worlder combatants.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Princess Posse, sponsored by the Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels (TSECS), NFC, DBA as Princess Posse Enterprises, NFC</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Princess Posse on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Princess_Posse</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Posse Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: And most importantly, I am a warlord of the Princess Posse Faction Wars team.” She turned to regard.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Posse Fan Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Princess Posse Fan Club</b> is the official fan club for Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. Donut refuses to acknowledge the unofficial fan club, the <a href="bword://Donut Holes">Donut Holes</a>. Zev lets Donut know that the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> is winning out over the Donut Holes as a name for her fan club.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Princess Posse Fan Club on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Princess_Posse_Fan_Club</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Princess Vinata</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: That was our Great Consensus, engineered by Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prism</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d received word that the Mantis government had unexpectedly abandoned their stake when I last spoke with Quasar, which ended up being a good thing as there was another entity bidding for the open spot in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prism Industries Capacitating and Focusing Goggles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Prism Industries Capacitating and Focusing Goggles</b> are sunglasses gifted to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> by her sponsor Princess D'Nadia in a <a href="bword://Benefactor Box">Benefactor Box</a> on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prism Industries Capacitating and Focusing Goggles on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prism_Industries_Capacitating_and_Focusing_Goggles</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prism Kingdom</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Prism Kingdom</b> is country of <a href="bword://Saccathian">Saccathian</a> ruled by Princess D'Nadia. It is small but known for its trade abilities. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> first meet Princess <a href="bword://D&#x27;nadia">D'nadia</a> while on the show Danger Zone with <a href="bword://Ripper Wonton">Ripper Wonton</a>. The program's director Evo described the Prism Kingdom as "rather small" but "a powerful force in certain trade circles." Following the attack by Prince <a href="bword://Stalwart">Stalwart</a> of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> that kills <a href="bword://Manasa">Manasa</a>, Princess D'nadia formally condemns the action on behalf of the Prism Kingdom.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prism Kingdom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prism_Kingdom</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit</b> allows someone to create a tattoo on a target, can potentially imbue magical buffs and upgrades if the artist is good enough.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Enchanted Prison Tattoo Kit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Enchanted_Prison_Tattoo_Kit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prison Pocket</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Prison Pocket</b> is a boss on the Tenth Floor during the 2nd Heat.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Poppa</li><li><b>Race:</b> Macropus Dominus</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prison Pocket on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prison_Pocket</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prison Pocket</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Prison Pocket</b> is a boss on the Tenth Floor during the 2nd Heat.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Poppa</li><li><b>Race:</b> Macropus Dominus</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prison Pocket on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prison_Pocket</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prize box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I forgot what the prize box said it was.” “You’re judging a kids’ art contest.” “Are you fucking kidding me?” “Try not to swear for this one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prize boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If I happen to collect a few more hands before the evening is over, but they’re still in their prize boxes, can I use them?” Chaco smiled, revealing his wolf.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prize Carousel</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Prize Carousel</b> is a time-restricted Dungeon minigame hosted by <a href="bword://Chaco">Chaco</a>. Crawlers can receive the ability to "play" if their fans select it as a <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a> reward, but it is not particularly popular and only happens a few times each season.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fan Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Prize Carousel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Prize_Carousel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Probably</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I don’t know which god they’ve sponsored, but if I was still a gambler, I’d bet on Grull.” “Can the god get to us if I don’t accidentally summon him?” “Probably.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Production Facility</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 40 Entering Production Facility “Hello, guys,” I said to the large, two-headed ogre creature sitting before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Profane Vitiate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente changed to a Profane Vitiate , but it didn’t explain what that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Professor Liddi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We only had robot Donut for a few days, but I taught her several tricks.” Gravo looked irritated and was about to pause the taping so I could get back on script, but Professor Liddi rolled with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protection</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: Protection against.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protection Act</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Protection Act is described as endangering the very existence of all life in the universe, I leaned forward and banged my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protection spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: A blue magic protection spell appeared around us, followed by a new one, this time a translucent shield spell, a real shield spell, that would also protect us from physical.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protection spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d just finished shoring up the FUPA, and they would spend the next two hours painstakingly moving up the tower, creating a lattice pattern of protection spells on each individual.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protective Shell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t know what was happening, but I banged onto Protective Shell just as Donut’s Wall of Fire burst up inside the room The door exploded the moment I cast my spell, which didn’t make much.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protective Shell Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Protective Shell</b> is a rare protection spell that creates a stationary, temporary sphere around the caster's position at time of casting. Red-tagged corporeal mobs and physical attacks cannot move through the sphere.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Protective Shell Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Protective_Shell_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Protective Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was not a portal, but a forcefield of some sort, similar to the wall of my Protective Shield spell, protecting the entrance to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Province Boss</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Now go do it again with a Province.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Province bosses</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Everywhere, groups who’d stayed in the stations had taken heavy losses, but those who’d fallen back were unable to get back in, and now we had reports of province bosses appearing in the rooms where there.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Proximity Trigger</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Proximity Trigger</b> is an expensive trap-building device that, when added to a trap at a <a href="bword://Sapper&#x27;s Table">Sapper's Table</a>, allows the user to set conditions for when the trap goes off.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Sapper's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Proximity Trigger on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Proximity_Trigger</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Prudence</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: According to Prudence—poor, dead Prudence—Tina was supposed to do a dance called the “Water Ballad,” which was about a mer-bear or some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Psamathe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately for her, her first escape attempt from the Nothing resulted in a split, and half of her essence was forced to take refuge in the closest unoccupied naiad vessel she could find, which happened to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Psionic attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What does she know now?” “She has a party heal spell, an illusion spell, an ice spell that she still can’t cast very well, a psionic attack that makes her nose bleed and does nothing else to mobs, and a new.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Psycho Sticker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Psycho Sticker</b> are mobs that appear on the Fourth Floor. They attack the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> at the start of the Fourth Floor. Hedgehog Ogres. Can be found on Mora Station #82 on the red line of The Iron Tangle.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Psycho Sticker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Psycho_Sticker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pterolykos</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pterolykos</b> are a wolf with wings.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pterolykos on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pterolykos</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Puddle Jump</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: As soon as she’s done, I’m going to toss smoke bombs, and then we’ll Puddle Jump to the entrance and make our way inside.” Donut had to position herself so she could actually see the group, and she plopped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Puddle Jumper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s spells currently were Heal – Level 1 (Max) Torch – Level 10 Magic Missile – Level 9 Puddle Jumper – Level 3 Second Chance – Level 5 Heal Critter – Level 1 Clockwork Triplicate – Level 1 I hadn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Puddle Jumper Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Puddle Jumper</b></i> is a teleportation spell that allows the caster to teleport themselves and up to three others to a location in line of sight. Due to its high cost, time delay, and long cooldown, it is not a practical combat spell.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Puddle Jumper Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Puddle_Jumper_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Puget Sound</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We had all sorts of similar animals in the Puget Sound, from the common harbor seal to the much-larger elephant seal and the loud-ass California sea.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pugilism Skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: -2 to Intelligence -2 to Charisma +3 to the Pugilism Skill +1 to the Unarmed Combat Skill +1 to the Iron Punch Skill +5 to the Knockout Skill +(1 x Floor Number) gold for every mob killed with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pull</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pull it, and then turn the steering wheel all the way to the right!” The clockwork zombie toad pulled the correct lever, and we all rose up in height as the legs took.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pulse rifle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’d also had an automatic pulse rifle on him that was supposedly worth a lot of money, though the gun still wasn’t as good as most of our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pulse rifles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Florin had taken out two Goriffs—blue, humanoid aliens with flapping antennae that looked oddly like twin elephant trunks upon their heads—and each had pulse rifles and electric body.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Purple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia : That’s what we’ve been doing, but the trains stopped coming on the Purple.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pushed the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d either pushed the door closed too tightly, or not tightly enough, but when the missing part of the door reappeared from wherever it went, the bolts weren’t perfectly lined up with how they’d been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Pustule</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Pustule</b> is a hobgoblin NPC and the proprietor of the Hobgobs and Boom Sticks in the Silk Road at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. She sold <a href="bword://Hobgoblin Hob-Lobber">Hobgoblin Hob-Lobber</a> and <a href="bword://Goblin Dynamite">Goblin Dynamite</a> to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Hobgoblin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 16</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Pustule on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Pustule</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Put</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you’re not going to understand my references, I don’t know why I even bother discussing classic entertainment with you.’ “Put me.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Put Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Put Mongo away, talk to Mordecai, and then make sure you get everybody near you into your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>PVP Coupon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>PVP Coupon</b> is an item that the first crawler in a Party to receive a Player Killer Skull gets. Other players in the party who get Player Killer Skulls do not receive them.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Player Killer Skull</li></ul><p class="source">Source: PVP Coupon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/PVP_Coupon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quadrillion Favorites</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quadrillion Followers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quadrillion Leaderboard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Views: 1.43 Quintillion Followers: 7 Quadrillion Favorites: 2.4 Quadrillion Leaderboard rank: 3 Bounty: 800,000 gold Welcome, Crawler, to the fifth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quadruped Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Quadruped Box</b> appears to be a type of Loot Box given to four-legged creatures for... being four-legged. The symbol on the front of the box is a lion.</li><li><b>Source:</b> achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quadruped Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quadruped_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut, don’t put it on yet.” When we’d first watched Quan wear his magical, celestial-tier cloak on the recap episode all those weeks ago, we’d seen that he’d had several new abilities right away, and we.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quan Ch</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Quan Ch</b> is an Earth Crawler and a Half-Elf Imperial Security Trooper, unaffiliated with the Royal Court. He is best known for surviving "The Fools Who Broke the Glass" on the Third Floor to claim one of Dungeon Crawler World's rare <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> prizes: an enchanted robe that lets him fly on ethereal wings, his left hand glowing blue with its power.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Half-Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 24</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quan Ch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quan_Ch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quarry Sees Another Spring Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Quarry Sees Another Spring</b> achievement is awarded to Crawlers who enter a stairwell while they are featured on the Dungeon's Leaderboard. The crawler's bounty multiplies every time they receive this achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quarry Sees Another Spring Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quarry_Sees_Another_Spring_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quasar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Quasar</b> is a <a href="bword://Nullian">Nullian</a> advocate and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s legal representative under <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> law. Carl gains him on the Sixth Floor by demanding a lawyer, far earlier in the crawl than counsel is normally available, and Quasar tells him at their first meeting that he represents Carl alone.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Franciscan Arm</li><li><b>Race:</b> Nullian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quasar on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quasar</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Queen Anne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Queen Anne Chonk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: My full name is ‘GC, BWR, NW Princess Donut the Queen Anne Chonk.’ I wouldn’t think of boring you guys with all of my titles.” She leaned toward the audience and stage whispered, “GC stands for Grand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Queen Consort Ugloo</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Queen Consort Ugloo</b> is the wife of <a href="bword://King Rust">King Rust</a> and mother to Prince <a href="bword://Stalwart">Stalwart</a>, Prince <a href="bword://Maestro">Maestro</a>, and Princess <a href="bword://Formidable">Formidable</a>. Sister to <a href="bword://Victory">Victory</a>. The <a href="bword://Valtay Corporation">Valtay Corporation</a> destroys Prince Stalwart's space yacht in retaliation for his accidental killing of their pop singer <a href="bword://Manasa">Manasa</a>. Queen Consort Ugloo and former Prince Maestro were presumed to be on board and killed in the explosion.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Queen Consort Ugloo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Queen_Consort_Ugloo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Queen Imogen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: An elf’s panicked and surprised voice squeaked, “I present to you, Queen of All That Is and the Queen of All That Will Be, the One and Only True Blood, Immortal-in-Waiting, Chosen Daughter of Apito, Her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Queen of All That Is</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once you enter the ballroom and you are deemed to be in compliance with all the rules, you will officially be under the formal protection of the Queen of All That Is and the Queen of All That Will Be, the One.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quemada</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “That means Donut, Quemada, and Finley, you keep your eyes out for threats behind and to the sides.” You know the third GPS upgrade comes with a threat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quench Moths</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The enemy deckmaster had a half-dozen minions with him, things called Quench Moths.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest and Social Upgrades</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before I barely had the chance to read the menu, he reached forward with his hand and clicked on Quest and Social Upgrades.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Quest Box</b> is a type of Loot Box awarded to crawlers for completing a Quest or for quest-related achievements. Quest boxes arrived with the Quests tab of the crawler interface, which unlocked for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on the Third Floor with their first quest. The <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> generally determines a box's contents.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Completing a Quest; Surviving a group quest, which pays every qualifying participant; Quest-related achievements, such as the CockBlock Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quest Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quest_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest boxes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I had literally dozens of achievements and boxes, including a Legendary boss box and two different quest boxes for the Vengeance of the Daughter and the Recital.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest Chat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Quest Chat notification popped up on the side of my interface as we pushed through the remains of what had once been a group of walls protecting Ghazi’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest Complete</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was followed by a distinctive slap and Rapture shouting, “Shut up!” Quest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quest Update</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Guys, change of plans!” Quest Update: Find Out Who Killed My.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quetzalcoatlus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Quetzalcoatlus</b> is a Wailing Shrieker that guards the Necropolis of <a href="bword://Anser">Anser</a>. As a wailing shrieker, she can be defeated by banishment, exorcism, electrocution or destruction of the corpse of her lover.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quetzalcoatlus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quetzalcoatlus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Into town,” I said, “Quick.” Even though we were out of the water, I took another scroll of water breathing to stave off the vomiting that would incapacitate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plus I had that Bronze Boss box from killing Quill and a mess of other loot packs, mostly bronze and silver adventurer’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quint</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Quint</b> is a Pharmacist at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. His race is a <a href="bword://Half-Djinn">Half-Djinn</a>, Half-Garbarge Scowl. He is a former Crawler and used to be an orc. He gives alchemy supplies to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and Mordicai in exchange for the three bottles of Rev up Moonshine.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Half-Djinn, Half-Garbarge Scowl Formerly Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Quint on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Quint</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quintillion Followers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Mad Dune Mage Time to Level Collapse: 10 Days and 4 Hours Views: 17.72 Quintillion Followers: 13 Quadrillion Favorites: 4.1 Quadrillion “I did all that sewing for nothing?” Katia said as we stepped out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But there’s plenty enough room in this world for another dead rottweiler!” “Quit throwing barrels at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Quite</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Oh, and I got a skill called Guinness that doubles my strength if I’m drunk.” “Are you serious?” “Quite,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Qwist</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Qwist</b> is a <a href="bword://Bopca">Bopca</a> Protector who staffs the Big Shot Chicken safe room where <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> shelter early in their crawl. Like other Bopca Protectors, Qwist manages, cleans, and cooks for the safe room, addressing Donut as "Princess" and catering to her demands, including grooming her with a brush and fetching her custard on request.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Qwist on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Qwist</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Race</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Race ain’t over yet, though.” “The new shield works great,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Race and class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Now that we’ve reached the halfway point of the level, game guides are now able to instruct you upon some of the intricacies regarding the third floor and how the race and class selection process.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Radoslav</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Radoslav</b> was a level 75 <a href="bword://Bugbear">Bugbear</a> <a href="bword://Jack">Jack</a> of All Trades and a member of Team Free Love, an NPC racing team that competed on the Tenth Floor of the World Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The World Dungeon (NPC)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bugbear</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 8, Chapter 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Radoslav on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Radoslav</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ragazzaccia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ragazzaccia</b> is <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>'s "girlfriend" from before the Transformation. Ragazzaccia is never shown on-page. Lonely and missing his old friends, Prepotente asks <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for advice about using one of his special enhanced pet biscuits to turn a fellow goat, Ragazzaccia, complete with a "mind-control-sex-slave" benefit. Donut immediately and firmly shuts the idea down, telling Prepotente it isn't right no matter how lonely he is.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ragazzaccia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ragazzaccia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rage Elemental</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rage Elementals</b> are mobs drawn from the Thirteenth Floor of the Dungeon and deployed on lower floors as a penalty rather than as an encounter. On the Second Floor, the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> announced that any human-born crawler who intentionally relieved themselves outside a designated bathroom area would have one summoned on the spot, promising it would kill the offender and their entire party.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rage Elemental on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rage_Elemental</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ralph</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ralph</b> is a <a href="bword://Frenzied Gerbil">Frenzied Gerbil</a> and a Neighborhood Boss on the Second Floor. Ralph looks like a regular ("absurdly cute") Earth gerbil, except for its mouth. The mouth looks normal when closed, but opens wider than physically possible when it opens its mouth to scream. The mouth opens so wide that it obscures the rest of the body behind it.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Frenzied Gerbil</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ralph on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ralph</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rambo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is Footloose , not one of your Rambo movies.” “This is nothing like Footloose , Donut.” “It’s nothing like Rambo , either,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ramp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Ramp-Up Chapter 20 Chapter 21 Chapter 22 Chapter 23 Chapter 24 Chapter 25 Milk Chapter 26 Chapter 27 Chapter 28 Chapter 29 Chapter 30 Chapter 31 Chapter 32 Chapter 33 Chapter 34 Volteeg Chapter 35 Chapter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Randall</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Randall</b> was a former patient of <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> Elder Care Facility. Either two patients shared the name "Randall," or he is a continuity error.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 27</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Randall on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Randall</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Randy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Get to the Twister and fly away.” “You’re going to hell,” little Randy the bear cub said to Bonnie the gnome, who remained on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Randy and Todd</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I reread the note and then eyed Samantha, who was rolling around the room squealing hysterically while the two small ursine, Randy and Todd, used her like a soccer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ranged attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bomb Bards are experts with explosives, and they strike with a variety of ranged attacks that’ll blow your socks.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ranged weapon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Each one picks a weapon proficiency upon childhood, kinda like a ninja-turtle thing, and they train in both that and in a ranged.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ranged Weapon Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Ranged Weapon Box</b> is a Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for the <a href="bword://That&#x27;s not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement">That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement</a> when he stabs <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> with the <a href="bword://Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea">Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ranged Weapon Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ranged_Weapon_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ranged weapons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tran was so encased in metal that he couldn’t carry any ranged weapons, lest the system label him as “armor.” I started to say something, but a commotion down the line caused us all to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rapid Detox Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> makes <a href="bword://Louis">Louis</a> and <a href="bword://Firas">Firas</a> Rapid Detox Potions which causes them to become violently ill. The potion is used to treat alcoholism and to torture prisoners. Mordecai adds sage beetle ichor to disguise the potions, but it makes it so they go bad after an hour. They are white with little frothy bubbles.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rapid Detox Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rapid_Detox_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rapture</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book {{{1}}}.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rapture</b> is a Level 95 Lady Mantaur Dominator, one half of a pair of womantaur drivers (alongside <a href="bword://Genesis">Genesis</a>) racing for the Lady Dominators during the Tenth Floor's racing heats.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rapture on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rapture</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rapture and Genesis</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Rapture and Genesis is described as walking from their garage toward a doorway at the end of the street that led to what looked like a group of falling-down buildings and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rat creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I am assuming this rat creature won’t be able to travel with us,” she lifted her paw and pointed it at me, “so you have been promoted from manservant to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rat Hooligan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai was no longer a Rat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rat-kin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>In the Dungeon, <b>Rat-kin</b> are a First Floor mob and are <i>most likely</i> a race option that crawlers can choose on the Third Floor. <a href="bword://Rats">Rats</a> attacked <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a>'s group shortly after they entered the Dungeon from Seattle, WA.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rat-kin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rat-kin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rats</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rats</b> are rats.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rats on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rats</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Raul</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then, Shi Maria ended up telling this to Geraldo the next time they were both together, and now Geraldo was calling Raul “the spider lady’s jinetera.” Skylar Spinach never talked and didn’t have much of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Raul and Geraldo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also, as expected, Raul and Geraldo didn’t get along and would fight each other unless Donut yelled at them to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Raul the Crab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Raul</b> is a Red Maníseros Land Crab who becomes one of Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s primary Totem Cards during the card-battling event on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Cuba (Eighth Floor)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Red Maníseros Land Crab</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 24</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Raul the Crab on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Raul_the_Crab</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Razor Fox</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Razor Fox</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that is suffering from the DTs. The normal version of this mob can be found on the Fifth Floor. The <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> comes across this mob at station 75 during the End of the Line Rescue. They only see the mob when it is suffering from stage 3 of the DTs, which is very different than the base version of the mob.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Razor Fox on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Razor_Fox</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Read</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Read this shit before you put it on.) Placing this crown upon your head permanently places you within the royal line of succession for the Blood Sultanate on the ninth floor of the world.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ready</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s if all teams are finished with their defenses, their throne is locked in place, and they mark themselves as ‘Ready.’ In the first case, the ceasefire ends.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Real</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Real situations with real consequences colliding with make-believe, and the consequences of the make-believe portions were oftentimes more dangerous than.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Real god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: One was a giant, hairless gorilla thing, and the other, I think the real god, was a snake with the head of a bald.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Real Gs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Real Gs.” “Does that mean we’re going to slaughter Li Na and laugh as she drowns in her own blood?” “No,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Real world</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It turned out Vadim was a cosmetic surgeon in the real world, and Britney had been at his office for a consultation when it all went down, and they were the last two survivors of their original.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reaper Gaze</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of Rend’s weaknesses was something called “Reaper Gaze.” I examined.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reaper Spider</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reaper Spider minions are regular mobs one may find on the fifteenth floor, working as minions of something kinda fucked up we’re not ready to talk about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reaper Spider Minion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Reaper Spider Minions</b> are terrifying arachnid mobs with celestial blood and devastating special attacks. Although they are commonly found on the Fifteenth Floor, they may appear on other levels as traveling merchants.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reaper Spider Minion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reaper_Spider_Minion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reaper Spider Minion Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Reaper Spider Minion Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> in a Platinum <a href="bword://Hell In A Handbasket Box">Hell In A Handbasket Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://Holy Hell Achievement">Holy Hell Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum Hell In A Handbasket Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reaper Spider Minion Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reaper_Spider_Minion_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reaver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was heading toward the opposite side of the Reaver castle, much like we were marching on the backside of the Madness castle, but so far, Yarilo hadn’t actually done anything yet except call for Samantha to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reavers</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The Reavers are a megacorporation and system government that live on a dyson sphere. Originally several different races that enhanced themselves with machines until the only organic part remaining was their brain. Originally related to the Nebulars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reavers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reavers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rebecca</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It showed the man running directly into some plant mob thing I’d never seen before, and of Frank finally tracking Rebecca down, cornering her in the quadrant with the scatterer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rebecca W</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rebecca W</b> was a crawler from somewhere near Seattle, WA, whose real name was most likely Rebecca Wong. The <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> labels her corpse as: <i>Lootable Corpse. Crawler Rebecca W. Level 3. Killed by Crawler <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a>.</i></li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Near Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 12</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rebecca W on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rebecca_W</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Recap show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the meantime we were going to pick a random transit station and hit the safe room, open Katia’s boxes, watch the recap show, and then have ourselves a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>receive a Silver Earth Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>received a Platinum Venison Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have received a Platinum Venison.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Received a quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I discussed with my friend how I wished we could kill more ghasts, because they often drop knowledge scrolls, and not one hour later, I received a quest to hunt down a certain number of the very same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Received an achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She received an achievement for collecting the bounty, and a legendary bounty box which was that 500,000.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Recon Legionnaires</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Surrounding the skull in large letters in Syndicate Standard read the words “Princess Posse or Death.” It had their hastily-formed battalion name—the 103 rd Recon Legionnaires—added to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Red</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trained in the deadly art of Caribbean Kung Fu, the monk seal sentry’s sacred duty is to protect the coast and hunting grounds from the impending invasion of their dread enemy, the Red Maníseros Land Crabs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Red Beret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Red Beret</b> is an item</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Red Beret on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Red_Beret</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Red Cross</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: A member of the Red Cross who’d been following around a group of militants when they’d all entered the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Red Equinox</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: In eleven days is the Red.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Red Man</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trained in the deadly art of Caribbean Kung Fu, the monk seal sentry’s sacred duty is to protect the coast and hunting grounds from the impending invasion of their dread enemy, the Red Maníseros Land Crabs.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Redacted</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This season we’re going…” “Redacted” abruptly appeared on the screen as Kevin the lizard breathlessly described whatever hidden horrors they had in store for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reef Explorer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Reef Explorer</b> is a boss on the Eighth Floor. It is flanked by two <a href="bword://Swordfish Interlopers">Swordfish Interlopers</a>. The Reef Explorer is wearing an etheral glowing deep dive suit.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reef Explorer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reef_Explorer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Regular</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Regular, old-school grinding was important, not just for experience, but to keep training up our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>regular NPCs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The white dots were the regular NPCs, the red appeared to be the city guards, the orange were the pets, and the purple were the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Relief</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Relief washed over me with the knowledge that Imani was still.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remainder of this floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Effective immediately and for the remainder of this floor, all saferoom access—and this includes personal space access—is operated under tenth floor and below.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remained in the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Remained in the room is described as blackened, crumbly husks of bodies and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remained on the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple fountains sprouted out of nowhere, including one right underneath both Paz and Anton, who remained on the floor, writhing in pain from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remaining attacks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: WARNING: If Scolopendra dies or if Scolopendra is abandoned upon a collapsing floor, the remaining attacks will trigger all at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remaining Crawlers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Remaining Crawlers: 32,429 Warning: Due to the previous floor’s ruleset regarding looted items from the memory simulation, multiple items have been removed from your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remaining Hunters</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trophy Count: 34 Remaining Hunters: 1013 The number of hunters in the dungeon appeared on the screen, just below the remaining crawler counter, which had lowered to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remember</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Remember what happened with that one show with the She-Devil lady and the Sulley guy?” I was too genuinely pissed to figure out what Donut was talking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remex</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Remex</b> is an NPC associated with the Sex Workers Who Fell from the Heavens Quest and the Fools Who Broke the Glass Quest. He's also a former crawler.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 24</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Remex on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Remex</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remex the Grand</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: And if by chance it hadn’t, that second burst, which activated all magical weapons, should have set it off, which would’ve exploded the dynamite, which would have killed Remex the Grand, triggering that final.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</b> is a Level 125 City Boss designed by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a>. <i>Most</i> but not all parties must fight a custom hydra to win a Key during the second phase of the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reminiscence_Hydra_of_Malicious_Compliance</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Remote Detonator</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I marked the bomb using a second Remote Detonator and also added this trigger to my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Removal Order</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The dose I’m giving to you, Carl, I’m also going to combine with another potion called ‘ Removal Order .’ That way we’ll be visiting the spider’s memories instead of your own.” He paused, swallowing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Removing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: Removing or triggering a single trap in the sequence will activate all remaining traps in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ren</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She was one of the most celebrated North American Persians until her granddaughter came along and shattered all her records.” “You just suggested we name him Cousin Oliver.” “That was before I realized you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rend</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The 104 th Naughty Little Piggies is back to their normal castle defense positions, cleaning up after the brain worm attack, but we lost most of our mages, and it’s going to be a while before the castle’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rend and Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I can go grab them real fast if you want.” Outside the privacy bubble, Rend and Mongo both walked up and their faces pressed against the bubble like it was a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rental Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rental Rooms currently available: 10 Rental Room price: 180.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rental Rooms</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rental Rooms currently available: 10 Rental Room price: 180.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ren’s team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The rabbit, pig, and turtle were suddenly on Ren’s team, and the hairy ghost thing and the Mongolian Death Worm were now flying Quan’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Requires</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Requires the bomber to know the altitude of their target to obtain maximum.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reset our buffs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s eat and take a nap and reset our buffs because I don’t know how long before we’ll find another safe room after this.” “We’re going through the portal?” Katia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Residual</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Residuals</b> are a hyperspatial, collective-mind alien race without a home who secretly inhabit the bodies of a host species on worlds containing <a href="bword://Primal">Primal</a> Engines.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Residual on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Residual</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Residuals</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Residuals is described as in the dungeon, the moment I learned how unstable the AI was, and how it favored this predator , I feared this exact thing would.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Resolution</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I must say, this Resolution parade float has done a better job of portraying the reasoning behind our ultimate resolution than I had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rest assured such movements are perfectly natural, and the resort’s centrifugal dampeners are more than up to the task of keeping you safe.” Florin: The tree broke, and now the centipede is all mixed up with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rest of the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Let’s hope this doesn’t set off some sort of unforeseen domino effect that will ripple throughout the rest of the floor, leading to mass confusion and death amongst you and your fellow.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Returned to the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tell us who your source is and how they communicate with you, and you will be returned to the dungeon with no penalties, and you will be given a Legendary box that will contain an item that will all but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Returned to the green room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Just give me the damn videogame one.” * * * After we were done, we returned to the green.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Returned to the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Do the math.” “How did you autograph stuff?” Katia asked just as Donut returned to the room, sparkling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Things like “You’re the Boss Now” and “Your own hours” and “It’s not a pyramid!” and “Safe!*” Inside the pamphlet was a wall of text of mostly gibberish about the benefits of becoming a “Rev-Up Moonshine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev-Up</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rev-Up</b> is a running gag in <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>'s 18-Level World Dungeon created as a political satire of the <a href="bword://Apothecary">Apothecary</a> and the <a href="bword://Plenty">Plenty</a>. The <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a> and Borant Corporation lost a ton of money in communication stocks when the Plenty released the tunnel system, and retaliated by spreading rumors that (a) the Apothecary is the true creator of the tunnel system, and (b) using the tunnel system will indoctrinate you into the collective mind.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rev-Up on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rev-Up</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev-Up Immunity Smoothie</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> Immunity Smoothies</b> are a discontinued Rev-Up product originally manufactured on the Second Floor. Consuming the smoothie gives immunity to the Taint and Vigorous Measles.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafting</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rev-Up Immunity Smoothie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rev-Up_Immunity_Smoothie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev-Up Magic Hair Restoration Tonic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Produced by <a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a>, <b>Rev-Up Magic Hair Restoration Tonic</b> stimulates hair growth when applied to any surface. The tonic is packaged in a set that includes plastic gloves, cotton swabs, multiple trays of assorted size and brushes, and an extremely long paper fold-out pamphlet with convoluted instructions.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gore-Gore's Corpse</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rev-Up Magic Hair Restoration Tonic on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rev-Up_Magic_Hair_Restoration_Tonic</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev-Up Toilet-Grade Moonshine</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rev-Up Toilet-Grade Moonshine</b> is manufactured and dropped by Unvaccinated <a href="bword://Clurichaun">Clurichaun</a> <a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> Consultants on the Second Floor. Rev-Up Moonshine Consultants sell this item to other mobs, but it's definitely not a pyramid scheme.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Unvaccinated Clurichaun Rev-Up Consultant</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rev-Up Toilet-Grade Moonshine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rev-Up_Toilet-Grade_Moonshine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rev-Up Vitamin Shots</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rev-Up Vitamin Shots</b> (officially known by the system as the <b><a href="bword://Rev-Up">Rev-Up</a> Amazing Cure-All Vitamin Immunity Shot</b>) are highly addictive, race-specific opiate painkillers distributed to mobs on the Fourth Floor of the World Dungeon.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Krakaren (Manufacturer) / Pookas (Distributors)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rev-Up Vitamin Shots on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rev-Up_Vitamin_Shots</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reverse Gravity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This was much different than the Reverse Gravity.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reverse Gravity Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Reverse Gravity</b></i> is a limited range spell that does exactly what its name suggests: gravity pulls all creatures and objects in range in the opposite direction, so the floor becomes the ceiling and the ceiling becomes the floor. When cast, the targets "fall" upward and crash into the ceiling. The spell's boundaries are abrupt: if someone or something is pushed over the boundary, it falls to the actual floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Reverse Gravity Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Reverse_Gravity_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Reward</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next thing you know you’ll be fashioning bottlecap earrings, drinking oat milk, and selling your ugly crap on Etsy while you wax poetic on Instagram about your “journey.” Reward : You’ve received a Bronze.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rezan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The original plan was to have all the people, doggies, cats, fish, that turtle in apartment 411, the monkey Rezan mom’s had in 634, everything as shells living in their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rickard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rickard</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (24th Edition)</i>. Rickard entered the Dungeon with his pregnant wife. She disappeared when they passed through the doors, and he never saw her again.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 25</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rickard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rickard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ricky Joe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: And standing between the guards was little Ricky Joe, looking at the stump of his arm which was now shooting blood into the air like one of those summertime sprinklers for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ring of Divine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: My finger itched, and the Ring of Divine suffering hung heavily upon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ring of Divine Suffering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of the snake heads reached down and curled around the web pattern on my elbow tattoo, the one that gave me access to the Guild of Suffering, which I hadn’t seen or heard anything about since I’d gotten.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ring of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a <b>Ring of Water Breathing</b> on the Fifth Floor. A glowing blue ring that twinkles with little sparkles.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold I'm Wet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ring of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ring_of_Water_Breathing</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ringmaster Grimaldi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ringmaster Grimaldi – Pestiferous Vine This is an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Riot Forces</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Riot shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She rotated her riot shield 90 degrees and pushed it forward, like a literal battering.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ripper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And the second attack was called ‘A Parade of Horribles’?” “Yes, Ripper, that’s what I’m telling you.” And that’s when all the monitors in the studio changed to a screen showing nothing but a flowing star.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ripper Wonton</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ripper Wonton</b> is the host of <i>Danger Zone with Ripper Wonton</i>. Ripper is known as a "good-hearted gentleman" with strong opinions that treats guests fairly but will push back if he disagrees with them.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Setonix</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ripper Wonton on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ripper_Wonton</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Risen to level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s spells currently were Heal – Level 1 (Max) Torch – Level 10 Magic Missile – Level 9 Puddle Jumper – Level 3 Second Chance – Level 5 Heal Critter – Level 1 Clockwork Triplicate – Level 1 I hadn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rishi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rishi</b> is a four-armed <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> who serves as a representative for the <a href="bword://Blood Sultanate">Blood Sultanate</a> during the Ninth Floor's Faction Wars. He is legally married into the Naga royal family,</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Sultan Rishi the Usurper, First of his Name of the Naga Uprising</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Naga System</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naga</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rishi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rishi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>River of Sloth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ginger base camp was right at the halfway point for the River of Sloth, so we’d have been forced to take this path whether we wanted to or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Robert the Human</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It’s our chance.” “What the hell are you talking about?” On the screen, just as Geraldo started to attack the doll, Mongo flew through the air and chomped onto the throat of Robert the Human, killing him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Robot Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Robot Donut</b> is toy created by the <a href="bword://Veriluxx RealPet Companions">Veriluxx RealPet Companions</a> company and owned by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> As a part of their sponsorship deal of Donut, Veriluxx RealPet Companions sends her a special prize box. <a href="bword://Loita">Loita</a>, instructs the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> that they are expected to play with the toy and praise it. They are also informed they will be appearing on a special show where they will be asked their opinion on the toy.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Robot Donut on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Robot_Donut</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Robot Uzi Jesus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re terrified, and I have a 25,000 credit a month lease to Dictum I can’t pay.” “Dude, that fucking sucks,” Robot Uzi Jesus.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rock Bottom Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Rock Bottom Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for diving more than 1000 meters under the water.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rock Bottom Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rock_Bottom_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rock creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: And there was a good chance Chris, as a rock creature, would simply drop dead the moment he stepped out of an enhancement.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rock monster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re part of the package deal we negotiated in exchange for temporarily giving up the Gate of the Feral Gods.” Donut jumped to the Sledge’s shoulder, and the rock monster gave her an affectionate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rock of Ages</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So when Bautista’s crew only saw one cart—this one playing “Rock of Ages” by Def Leppard—it was actually the cart they needed to jump in front of, but they had no way of knowing.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rockard's Ring of Sniping</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> takes <b>Rockard's Ring of Sniping</b> from <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a> on the Fifth Floor. An amber-stoned ring.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quan Ch's hand</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rockard's Ring of Sniping on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rockard%27s_Ring_of_Sniping</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rod</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Madison, are you sure?” “Rod will come.” “Bye, Madison,” I said, turning.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Roller Limbo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: For our Golden upgrade, we went with Roller Limbo, a trans-dimensional upgrade that would allow us to race upside.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ronaldo Qu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ronaldo Qu : a round section of the floor disappeared in the middle of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Roof of the building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: On the ground, shouting defenders flowed around the colorful barracks building on both sides, but a group of us moved to the roof of the building so we’d have a clear view of the enemy’s position below.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Room door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If it’s too much to handle, we’ll run back here and rush into the safe room.” “The safe room door won’t open if mobs are outside,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Room rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: This location is exempt from the local safe room.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rooted in Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It looks like you’ll be getting more practice in with that Rush attack,” I said as I looked up Rooted in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rory</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rory</b> is a Level 7 <a href="bword://Goblin Shamanka">Goblin Shamanka</a> NPC who appears on the First Floor. Her name was assigned by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. She is also the protagonist of the first part of Backstage at the Pineapple Cabaret.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Goblins</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 15</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rory on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rory</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rory and Lorelai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I don’t understand what the difference is.” “The copper chopper was a custom-made, handcrafted vehicle built especially for us by Rory and Lorelai, and it had a sidecar attachment with a personalized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rosemarie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rosemarie</b> is an elderly female <a href="bword://Dwarf">Dwarf</a> NPC who runs the Meat Shields in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Rosemarie is normally found in the Penis Parade, where she runs her Meat Shields location. She has a mole like creature named Bernie who sits on her shoulder. She likes to throw coins at the strippers and gets in trouble for aiming at their eyes.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rosemarie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rosemarie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Rosetta said she spent every penny getting here, and once the quarantine went into effect, the officers she was expecting refused to come, so she only has a few friends.” “Do you think she’s in there?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rosetta and Mordecai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: While this practice of mass teleportation wasn’t unheard of, both Rosetta and Mordecai said it’d only happened a handful of times in Faction Wars because of how grueling the process.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rosetta and Tipid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Rosetta and Tipid is described as already mercenary shopping for us, but they said the prices are pretty outrageous since our mercenaries always die.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rosetta Thagra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rosetta Thagra</b> is an alien, a former Crawler, and an anarchist, best known as the author of the ninth edition of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a></i>. Her marginal notes, addressed throughout to "comrades," lean heavily toward demolitions and practical bomb-craft.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Rosie</li><li><b>Race:</b> Crest</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 13</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rosetta Thagra on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rosetta_Thagra</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rot Stickers</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rot Stickers</b> are a Dungeon mob found on the First Floor, where they're generally levels 1-2. Rot Stickers are small, round, "black-hued ravioli"-like monsters capable of running on walls and ceilings. They chitter loudly as they approach, and "suicide bomb" crawlers and attempt to attach to their target's body. Three seconds after attaching, they explode; each blast inflicts the Take Down Effect and feels like "getting hit with a sledgehammer."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rot Stickers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rot_Stickers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Roth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Mr. Roth</b> was the landlord of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s Apartment in Seattle, WA, before the transformation. He lived alone in the basement apartment, and was collected during the collapse.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Seattle, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 46</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Roth on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Roth</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal Bodyguard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Iron Tangle” Your title has reverted to Royal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal Chariot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: But before they left, Donut took one look at the vehicle-in-progress and said, “I’ve decided to name it the Royal Chariot.” She flipped her tail and exited the room astride Mongo, following.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal Court</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We have the Royal Court, Sister Ines, the mindless zombies, the demon-possessed zombies, the crabs, the seals, and of course, Madre de Aguas herself, no longer under control of anybody!” As he mentioned each.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal Court of Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Embroidered onto the flag on the top and bottom was a motorcycle club-style patch that read, “The Royal Court of Princess Donut.” Centered on the boxers over the crotch area was an embroidered bomb patch.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal Court of Princess Donut</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Royal Court of Princess Donut</b> (or simply the <b>Royal Court</b>) is the crawler party of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and his sapient cat, Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, on this season of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>. <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a> joined on the Second Floor, and the party's manager, <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a>, one floor after that.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Royal Court</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Royal Court of Princess Donut on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Royal_Court_of_Princess_Donut</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Royal family</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to the Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore, which she didn’t even have anymore, Donut wouldn’t be allowed to exit the ninth floor until every other member of the Blood Sultanate royal family was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rubbed Pug</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’ll meet you right here an hour after sundown tomorrow night.” * * * “What’s the point of having me as a manager if you two suicidal idiots don’t listen to my advice?” Mordecai raged after we arrived back at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rubber Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rubber Spell</b> is a damage transfer and protection spell that redirects an incoming attack to a selected target, while mitigating a large portion of the damage to the caster.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rubber Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rubber_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ruby</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>An orphan <a href="bword://Changeling">Changeling</a> child from Hump Town. She suffers from Compression Sickness, and has no arms, and a sunken in head when in her default form.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ruby on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ruby</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ruckus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ruckus</b> is a Borough Boss on the Fifth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Spring-operated Chicken Hawk Sentinel</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ruckus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ruckus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rude</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “That entrance is not nearly as exciting as the others,” Donut muttered just as a laser shot out from the nose of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rude-Dolph the Blood-Nosed Slay-Deer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Rude-Dolph the Blood-Nosed Slay-Deer</b> is an arena boss on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Rude-Dolph the Blood-Nosed Slay-Deer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Rude-Dolph_the_Blood-Nosed_Slay-Deer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Run</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Run!” Donut leaped off my shoulder as I turned and rushed toward the others, who were struggling to get everyone into the safe.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Run, Little Guenter, Run Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Run, Little Guenter, Run Spell</b> is a spell given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> in a Legendary <a href="bword://1914 Box">1914 Box</a>. The book grows centipede legs and runs away from Carl. It leaks foul-smelling black liquid. The book starts shivering and whimpering and shrieking "Stay Away! Don't Touch Me!" While it can speak, Carl can still pull the book into his <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a>, so it is not considered a pet.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Run, Little Guenter, Run Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Run%2C_Little_Guenter%2C_Run_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rush</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I knew Katia had an active skill called Rush, something she could only do once a day, and that’s what’d happened to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rush ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Use it in conjunction with your Rush ability.” The shield disappeared, and her arm reshaped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rushed across the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: What are you doing?” I took a step back, ran, and activated Walk on Air as I rushed across the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rushed into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Or maybe since they walked here, it’s different.” Another three rushed into the room, screaming and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Russian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Imani and Elle had a plan to blow the Cadillac sky-high and stop the Russian crawlers within if they’d refused to take a deal, but the car was still sitting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Rust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The rest were waylaid several kilometers back, and on Rust’s orders, they’d stopped their advance, though rumors were now that they’d been hit hard by the Posse.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saber-Toothed Void Leopard</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Saber-Toothed Void Leopard</b> is a powerful creature race that <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> could have transformed into early in the crawl, but never does. Nothing about the race's abilities or appearance is described in the text. It is mentioned only as an example of the kind of dramatic, powerful race change offered to popular crawlers on the early floors.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Saber-Toothed Void Leopard on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Saber-Toothed_Void_Leopard</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saccathian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Saccathian</b> (or <b>Sacs</b>) are a common race outside the dungeon. They are described by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> as squid-like and also like human sized Cthulhu. They squid-like tentacles dangling from cephalopod-eque faces.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Saccathian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Saccathian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saccathians</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They each landed upon an alien, who turned out to be a pair of tentacle-faced Saccathians, both wearing some sort of mechanical enhancements on their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sadie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Part of <a href="bword://Hekla">Hekla</a>'s group of <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a>. She casts her <i>Glass Prison</i> spell on the <a href="bword://Kravyad">Kravyad</a> so the group could pass through in The Iron Tangle.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 21, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sadie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sadie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sadir</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sadir</b> was an alien bounty hunter who was hired to find and kidnap a cat known Gravy Boat so she could be brought into the dungeon. He had a mate and multiple children, who lived in a null commune in the Filt System. His mate was unaware of his being a bounty hunter, believing instead that he worked on an elemental barge.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Nullian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Epilogue of Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sadir on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sadir</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Things like “You’re the Boss Now” and “Your own hours” and “It’s not a pyramid!” and “Safe!*” Inside the pamphlet was a wall of text of mostly gibberish about the benefits of becoming a “Rev-Up Moonshine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safe Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: You may purchase this workbench from a Safe Room.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safe room rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: This location is exempt from the local safe room.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safe Rooms</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bopca Protectors are magical, gnome-like creatures who exist solely to watch over Safe.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safe zone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We were currently in a safe area, but the garage itself wasn’t a safe zone, so the rules were a little weird about what we could and couldn’t do to break.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Safehome Yolanda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Safehome Yolanda</b> is a Dungeon guild founded on the Sixth Floor and led by the Crawler <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>, with Team Meadowlark as its controlling party. Chartered so that several parties could pool their personal space upgrades without merging into a single party, it became the shared home, workshop, and barracks of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> and most of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s closest allies.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Safehome Yolanda on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Safehome_Yolanda</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saferoom door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: She dropped the last potion onto the ground in front of the saferoom door and rushed forward toward her two dogs and the wall of fire as Donut pounded in the opposite direction toward the treehouse.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saferoom rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We have crews here, on both borders, and others building fall-back positions throughout along with multiple watchtower squads.” “Wait, what are the new saferoom rules?” I asked, my head.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sake of the gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Epitome Tagg : For the sake of the gods, it’s in the notification.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sally</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Beatrice">Beatrice</a> managed to get her fired after she messaged <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> asking him out for drinks. Bea contacted Carl's boss, <a href="bword://Dick">Dick</a>, and claimed that Sally was stalking Carl. Dick agreed to fire her in return for Tiffany sending him a picture of her boobs, this was really just a picture she found on the internet. Beatrice and her friends named this episode Operation Slut Stop.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sally on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sally</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Salvatore</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Salvatore</b> is a member of the <a href="bword://201st Security Group Militia">201st Security Group Militia</a>, a group of <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a> worshiping elves on the Third Floor. Salvatore is one of three robed elves outside the 201st Security Group Militia headquarters. They attempt to bring people in by handing them sticks with acorns hanging off the end.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Salvatore on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Salvatore</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sam didn’t mind us calling him ‘Monobrow,’ but he was dating this one girl for a while that hated it, so we had to come up with a new nickname when she was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sam Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s nowhere else to do this.” “You can rent a slot at Sam Town,” Samantha.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Samantha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Psamathe</b>, better known as <b>Samantha</b>, is a banished minor deity and a Withering Spirit who possesses a decapitated latex doll head. She is an NPC who joins the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> as a companion and officially hired "Trainer."</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Samantha; Psamathe; Lika Love Doll Head; Goddess of Unrequited Love; Goddess of Crazy Ex-Girlfriends</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Celestial Ascendency (Twelfth Floor)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naiad | Minor Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 24</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Samantha on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Samantha</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Samantha and Mongo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Samantha and Mongo is described as having a great time, chasing after each other in the soft.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Same with Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Same with Chris and the double Erins and Prepotente and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sanderson Pinkstaff</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Will the children of Sanderson Pinkstaff ever forgive.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Santa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The wide-faced man wore a Santa hat and had a t-shirt that featured a wrapped present on it with the caption, “I have a huge package for you.” He started grunting and jumping up and down as we approached, like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Santero</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Nobody must touch the Sun of Reawakening until after the Santero has rebuilt my shrine!” Donut and Paz stopped talking, and we all focused our attention on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sapper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: You have the ability to imbue Fear upon this module, but your current level of Trap Engineer (Level 1) only allows this action to be done at a Sapper’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sapper's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Sapper's Box</b> is a Loot Box that gives various trap supplies.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sapper's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sapper%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sapper's Table</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Sapper's Table</b> is a Crafting Workbench that can be purchased from a Safe Room Marketplace or received from a <a href="bword://Sapper&#x27;s Box">Sapper's Box</a>, and installed in a crawler's Personal Space. It allows crawlers to create stable traps, explosive items, and poisons, or to modify existing explosives and traps with modules or triggers.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Marketplace; Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sapper's Table on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sapper%27s_Table</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sapper’s Table</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Using a Sapper’s Table, the highly-valuable Proximity Trigger may be attached to any non-static.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sappy Gold Ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sappy Gold Ring</b> is a ring <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> took off <a href="bword://Quan Ch">Quan Ch</a>'s arm.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quan Ch's Arm</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sappy Gold Ring on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sappy_Gold_Ring</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sarah Hayse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Destruction team was another group of crawlers from China I didn’t know very well mixed with that Hawaiian guy with the really long name who went by Makana and his partner, the hexcrafter paladin crawler.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Saran Wrap</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dekoki the kappa had regained the ability to move after the Minister of Blood-Letting poured a beer into her head bowl, and Louis was helping her reapply her Saran Wrap as she profusely apologized to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sasquatch</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sasquatch</b> is a race offered to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> during Third Floor race selection. A Sasquatch looks like a stereotypical Big Foot humanoid creature with massive feet and covered in hair. It has fangs and a giant forehead.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sasquatch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sasquatch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sat on the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The second clockwork sat on the floor of the truck behind me, groaning, back against the pile of weapons and chains, yanking on its own tongue as wet gore sloshed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Satan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, most of the tracks are like waterslide tubes, and there’s this Satan guy, who keeps saying, “I hope none of these tubes lead to my butthole.” It’s really.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Satan's Lil' Hedgehog</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Satan's Lil' Hedgehogs</b> were a Second Floor mobs, but were moved to a lower level during the first round of floor patches because the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> determined that their armor-piercing quill attack was too strong for the new crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Satan's Lil' Hedgehog on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Satan%27s_Lil%27_Hedgehog</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Satan’s Water</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente’s and Louis’s teams both appeared to be on the same track as each other as well, which was described as “Satan’s Water Park.” I closed my eyes for a second, pretending I was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Satan’s Water Park</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente’s and Louis’s teams both appeared to be on the same track as each other as well, which was described as “Satan’s Water Park.” I closed my eyes for a second, pretending I was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Satchel Bomb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a bunch of <b>Satchel Bombs</b> in a <a href="bword://Boss Box">Boss Box</a> from killing an Odious Creeper on the Sixth Floor. A little bag with leather handles containing explosive equivalent to three sticks of hobgoblin dynamite. Enough to level a small town.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Boss Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Satchel Bomb on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Satchel_Bomb</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sausage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Sausage is described as captured and held as ransom by the Bactrian camels, the young woman threw such an epic temper tantrum that the only thing that calmed her was watching her father order the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Savage Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Savage Box</b> is a type of Loot Box given players for violence against other crawlers. When the first crawler in a party gets their first Player Killer Skull, they receive a booklet of PVP Coupons for every crawler in their party.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Violence against Crawlers</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Savage Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Savage_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Save</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I picked it up, and the note said simply, “Save the toraline.” The toraline was the yam-like vegetable they’d given me the last floor to save.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Say</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Say hello, meat.” It took the two men a moment to realize the Maestro was talking to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>SAYS IT’S</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: FIRAS SAYS IT’S BLOWING DIFFERENT THAN BEFORE, AND IT’S MAKING IT HARD FOR THEM TO.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>SAYS SHE’S</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: SHE ALSO SAYS SHE’S GETTING A TATTOO OF ME ON HER LOWER.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scab Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Scab Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>participation in a mass crawler movement</b> (unionizing or similar). Gold Scab Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scab Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scab_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scar Tissue Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scar Tissue</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scar Tissue Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scar_Tissue_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scat Thug</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scat Thugs</b> are level 3 First Floor mobs. They are bipedal racoon-headed monsters that stand approximately 2 ft tall, are roughly as strong as <a href="bword://Goblins">Goblins</a>, and are armed with spears.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scat Thug on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scat_Thug</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scatterer</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scatterers</b> are chittering, cockroach-like mobs found on the Dungeon's First Floor (specifically, in the <a href="bword://Hoarder">Hoarder</a> neighborhood). Brood Guardians are approximately cat-sized, and quickly charge at their targets. Stomping on their head almost always results in a critical hit, and they leak white goo when killed. They occasionally drop vials of Scatterer Hemolymph, which is tagged as a alchemy material.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scatterer on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scatterer</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scatterer Brood Guardian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Scatterer Brood Guardian is described as a giant bug who wants nothing more than to fuck you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scatterers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cigarettes are disgusting.” “Well, there are only nine left, so you won’t have to suffer…” I stopped dead as the trio of Scatterers rounded the corner, chittering as they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scavenger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tell us everything you know about the Scavenger storyline.” “Yes,” Donut muttered, “let’s all waste time learning about dungeon lore that’s not going to matter because we’ll all be dead in a few hours.”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scavenger Mother of Mothers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The tree gave fruit to the old gods, including Nekhebit, who is now also called the Scavenger Mother of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scavenger's Daughter Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Scavenger's Daughter Upgrade Patch</b> is a divine, unique, and fleeting back upgrade patch awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> <a href="bword://Predator Box">Predator Box</a>. It doubles the stat point benefit of every other patch on the garment it is affixed to, grants flat bonuses to Dexterity and Strength alongside three further benefits, and confers shifting temporary effects in the presence of deities and demons.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Predator Box, opened on the Eighth Floor; Reward for the Apex Predator Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scavenger's Daughter Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scavenger%27s_Daughter_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scavenger’s Daughter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Now, let’s take a look at Carl’s, uh, very different Celestial item, which is now his third, behind the Scavenger’s Daughter patch we were just discussing and the potion that gave him his Gloom Wraith Phase.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scolopendra</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scolopendra</b> is the colossal monster of dungeon legend — a sleeping beast said to lie at the very bottom of the great dormant volcano and blamed for the ancient nine-tier attack, the cataclysm that killed or monstrously transformed most of the volcano-world's inhabitants before the current crawl.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Dungeon Boss; "the big bad centipede thing"</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Scolopendra's Lair, at the bottom of the World Dungeon's volcano</li><li><b>Race:</b> Giant Centipede</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scolopendra on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scolopendra</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scolopendra Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: As this 16 th floor is currently outside the Scolopendra layers, any guests who wish to evacuate the Scolopendra Club should immediately proceed to this location, where you will be safe from any impending.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scorpion’s Bite</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut had her Scorpion’s Bite weapon mount with the crossbow on her back, cocked and ready to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scourge Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Scourge Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing the most <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a> on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scourge Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scourge_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scream Warriors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Scream Warriors is described as harassing the backside of the enemy approach while helping to break Florin’s 101 st from the stalemate that had kept him buttoned up in the fallback.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scree</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, <b>Infiltrators</b> (AKA <b>Scree</b>) are an aquatic brain parasite that can take control of a living brain. Crawlers may choose the "<a href="bword://Infiltrator">Infiltrator</a>" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scree on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scree</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Infiltrator</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Similar to <a href="bword://Gondii">Gondii</a> and Intellect <a href="bword://Hunters">Hunters</a>, <b>Infiltrators</b> (AKA <b><a href="bword://Scree">Scree</a></b>) are an aquatic brain parasite that can take control of a living brain. Crawlers may choose the "Infiltrator" race on the Third Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Infiltrator on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Infiltrator</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Screeching Death</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re probably going to find this hard to believe, but not only are these things sort of real, but that name they have—Screeching Death Manatees—is the actual name of record for this particular.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Screeching Death Manatee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Miss Talulah. Level 90 Screeching Death Manatee.</b> This is a special guest creature for the 10th and 11th floors. You’re probably going to find this hard to believe, but not only are these things sort of real, but that name they have—Screeching Death Manatees—is the actual name of record for this particular species. They were regular manatees, not unlike the Trichechus manatus most people from your world know and love. These guys were added to a former Land War world undergoing reparative terraforming when the company in charge of the project suddenly went bankrupt.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Screeching Death Manatee on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Screeching_Death_Manatee</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Screeching Death Manatees</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re probably going to find this hard to believe, but not only are these things sort of real, but that name they have—Screeching Death Manatees—is the actual name of record for this particular.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Build Trench</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives approximately 50 <b>Scrolls of Build Trench</b> during the Ghosts of Earth floor. These scrolls are central to Carl's plan to save crawlers who didn't manage to get keys by the end of the 8th floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Spam Box/ Adventurer Boxs</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Build Trench on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Build_Trench</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Meat Hooks</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scrolls of Meat Hooks</b> are scrolls that attract all carnivorous pet-class mobs within range toward the caster. Once cast, rancid-smelling black smoke trails from the caster's hand.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Meat Hooks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Meat_Hooks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Upgrade</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scroll of Upgrade</b> is a scroll that upgrades a currently equipped item. It is a limited time item so crawlers can't bring it to lower floors and upgrade later. While a crawler can take every item off except for one, <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> mentions that the upgrade isn't as good.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Upgrade on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Upgrade</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b> is a scroll that allows a crawler to breathe underwater for a limited amount of time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Water_Breathing</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sea creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was basically eight, double-jointed legs and a harness for the 17-foot-long sea creature, who had the anatomy of a regular, if not oversized, hammerhead shark, only he now breathed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sea level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d already discovered several interesting things about the artifact, including a way to very roughly predict what a location’s time would be, especially if I could relay to her the exact distance of a spot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Searched the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Uh, Carl,” I said as I frantically searched the map for the best way to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>season of Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If you’re not familiar, this kua-tin is none other than Cascadia, showrunner and executive producer of this season of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>season of Dungeon Crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If you’re not familiar, this kua-tin is none other than Cascadia, showrunner and executive producer of this season of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>season of Dungeon Crawler World</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If you’re not familiar, this kua-tin is none other than Cascadia, showrunner and executive producer of this season of Dungeon Crawler World: Earth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seattle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The yellow bag featured a happy, cartoon cat and was called “Meow Time.” There was also a sticker on the bag, “a gift from Pet Angels.” Pet Angels was a Seattle charity that gave free pet supplies to people.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sebastian</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sebastian</b> is a level 21 <a href="bword://Bopca">Bopca</a> Protector, and the proprietor a First Floor Safe Room located near the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Overwhelmed by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s Charisma, he gifts her his personal hairbrush and enthusiastically brushes her.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Bopca Homeworld</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bopca</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 26</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sebastian on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sebastian</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Second Chance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut’s spells currently were Heal – Level 1 (Max) Torch – Level 10 Magic Missile – Level 9 Puddle Jumper – Level 3 Second Chance – Level 5 Heal Critter – Level 1 Clockwork Triplicate – Level 1 I hadn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Second Chance Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Second Chance</b></i> is a necromancy spell that allows the caster to raise a lower-level monster from the dead and turn them into an undead minion. The minion's level is 1/3 of its previous level (minimum level 1) and it will fight on the caster's side for a number of minutes equal to the spell's level. As a minion, it can be targeted by minion spells such as <a href="bword://Clockwork Triplicate">Clockwork Triplicate</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Second Chance Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Second_Chance_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Second Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Second Place: The Royal Court of Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Secondary Gig Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Secondary Gig Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> upon recieving the <a href="bword://Guild of Suffering">Guild of Suffering</a> quest from the <a href="bword://Enchanted Lock Box of the Night Wyrm">Enchanted Lock Box of the Night Wyrm</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Secondary Gig Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Secondary_Gig_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Security Group</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s go talk to the elves.” * * * The sign didn’t announce the building as the headquarters of the 201st Security Group, but as we approached, it was clear that Mordecai’s intelligence was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Security Guard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I do think that bitch is just as crazy as the Security Guard guy, but that’s another topic for another day.” As the arena drew closer, I could see what was basically a jumbotron sitting above the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Security room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If we can get Donut into the tunnel or maybe Katia can turn into a slug or something, they can sneak into the tunnel and get to the security.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Security Trooper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle moved up to spot eight, and Li Jun the street monk had fallen off, replaced by Quan Ch, who was a level 31 half-elf Imperial Security.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>See</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the castle is conquered, then the associated armies are defeated and dominion is transferred to the conqueror.) (See my essay on the nature of NPCs for more insight on how conquering areas may be utilized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seize the Day Toothpaste</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Seize the Day Toothpaste is described as well over.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Self</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: That does not mean you are protected from direct contact with fire or lava.) You may now cast a level-10 Self-Immolation once a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Self Only Duration</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Wisp Armor Cost: 5 Mana Target: Self Only Duration: 5 minutes + 1 minute per level of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Self-Immolation Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Self-Immolation Spell</b> is a spell given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> by <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a> for being in his good graces as a Devotee.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Self-Immolation Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Self-Immolation_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Selvi and Tuba</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The two women were named Selvi and Tuba, and they both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Semeru</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: These troops, mostly Semeru dwarves and the regular citizens of Larracos were now mixed in with Li Na’s 107 th White Dragon Urban Defense battalion, forming an impressive ring around the lip of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Semeru Dwarves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: This usually happens here in the dungeon, but only upon the battlefields outside Larracos, where the Semeru Dwarves built their castle along the roots of the All Tree, seeking the very gods who disdain.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Send</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It has been for a while now.” “Send it up there and make it as bright as you can, will you?” She waved her paw, and the light shot into the air and zipped away from us like a fast-moving.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Senegal Production Systems</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Senegal Production Systems, Unlimited</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> corporation that owns and operates Production Trailers, which it rents out to multiple tunnel productions during the crawl.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Senegal Production Systems on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Senegal_Production_Systems</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sensation Entertainment</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We want for you to extend one of those licenses to Sensation Entertainment, Incorporated for their program, Vengeance of the Daughter .” Before the Borant rep could respond, Orren spoke.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sensation Entertainment Inc</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sensation Entertainment, Incorporated</b> is the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> production company responsible for <i>Vengeance of the Daughter</i>. They are located in one of the Center Systems, and their employees include Saccathians.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sensation Entertainment Inc on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sensation_Entertainment_Inc</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sentinel gun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There had apparently been a sentinel gun thing that’d been a neighborhood boss, but he said he’d never looted the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sentry Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sentry Spell</b> is a spell that <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives from the <a href="bword://Tserendolgor Box">Tserendolgor Box</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sentry Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sentry_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sepsis Crown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: As long as she didn’t go with the constitution buff, the issue with the Sepsis Crown would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sepsis Whore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to the Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore, which she didn’t even have anymore, Donut wouldn’t be allowed to exit the ninth floor until every other member of the Blood Sultanate royal family was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sergeant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Florin had actually named the Sergeant rank “Adjutant” at first, but we made him change it because it would be confusing with the existing Adjutant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sergeant Bret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sergeant Bret S : Me and the other mages are coming down to help secure the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sergeant First Class</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The strippers were all getting Sergeant First Class, which would allow them to recruit and command the base troops, such as the Sluggalos, which were now called Legionnaires.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seriously</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Seriously, will he die if I punch him in the leg?” “You’d probably get an awesome achievement for that,” Gwen.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sesame Street</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: And that’s the same thing as Sesame Street .” Louis almost jumped up from his seat, eyes going.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Session of Love</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Session of Love, the final show before we all die.” In smaller letters under that, it read, “Balcony still closed due to jellyfish infestation.” “She’ll meet us at the entrance to the Desperado Club,” Arief.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Set</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: If that’s true, then we can stay in the area until it’s time to go down.” “Set me free?” Madison said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Setonix</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Setonix</b> (or <b>Quokkas</b>) are a race that appears as a cross between a wombat and ewok, they are around 4 feet tall and are extremely cute. According to Evo, most people call them Quokkas.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Setonix on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Setonix</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seventeenth floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Apparently, the tourists on the eighteenth floor had the ability to move themselves to the sixteenth floor, where it was safe, but they first had to make their way through the seventeenth floor for some.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seventh Floor Pole Position</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Seventh-Floor Pole Position</b> is a one-time "Future Floor Boon" that was sold at the prize counter of the Butcher's Masquerade Ball and guaranteed its buyer a protected, privileged start to the Seventh Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Seventh Floor Pole Position on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Seventh_Floor_Pole_Position</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Seventh Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Seventh Place: The Royal Court of Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Several Days Earlier</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: * * * Several Days Earlier “Wait, before we go, I have a question,” I called after Pater.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sex doll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately for her, her first escape attempt from the Nothing resulted in a split, and half of her essence was forced to take refuge in the closest unoccupied naiad vessel she could find, which happened to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sex Pervert Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Sex Pervert Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> pierced himself with the <a href="bword://Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler">Enchanted Nipple Ring of the Defiler</a> while trying to convince <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> it wasn't weird and wouldn't make her a "whore".</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sex Pervert Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sex_Pervert_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sex Workers Who Fell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Sex Workers Who Fell from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shade Gnoll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shade Gnoll Riot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bronze Boss Box (5/7) Tome of Wisp Armor Silver Goblin Box (6/7) Stick of Dynamite X5 Lighter Satchel of Gunpowder Gold Crowd Control Box (7/7) Enchanted Spiked Kneepads of the Shade Gnoll Riot Forces Scroll.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shade Gnoll Riot Forces</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: One such outfit, trained specifically for this sort of situation, is the Shade Gnoll Riot.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shade Gremlins</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Then Henry got pulled into an alleyway and banged some succubus witch who ended up giving birth to thousands of these little goblin monsters called Shade.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shadow Boxer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: After the success of this first movie, the mysterious Open Intellect Pacifist Network had funded Shadow Boxer , a weekly show that was now telling the story of Earth through interviews that were designed to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shadow Leaks</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shadow Leaks</b> are level 14 are smoke-like ghost mobs that utilize a stinging cold attack. The can be found on the Third Floor inside the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement at night.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shadow Leaks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shadow_Leaks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shadow Mimics</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re still in their early stages of planning, but the Shadow Mimics who are loose in my dungeon are one part of the Big Six groups left in the dungeon trying to either take over or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shambling Acid Impaler</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shambling Acid Impalers</b> were a common Second Floor mob in previous <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a>-hosted seasons of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>. They are hairy, green, four-legged monsters that spit acidic darts capable of melting a crawler's skin from their bones.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shambling Acid Impaler on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shambling_Acid_Impaler</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shambling Berserker</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shambling Berserker</b> are tall piles of body parts, all sewn together and acting like a typical zombie. The can be found on the Third Floor outside the Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shambling Berserker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shambling_Berserker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shambling Berserkers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Some are nothing more than mini-Grinders, also known as Shrillings, also known as Shambling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shamus Chaindrive</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shamus Chaindrive</b> is a deceased bugbear treasure hunter and submarine captain whose decades-long hunt for the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a> led him to the Necropolis of <a href="bword://Anser">Anser</a> on the Fifth Floor, where the automaton guardians he left behind are still active long after his death. He never appears on-page; everything known about him comes from the AI Descriptions of the constructs and <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> tied to his story.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shamus Chaindrive on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shamus_Chaindrive</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shango</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shango</b> is a god who is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon, with <a href="bword://Ogun">Ogun</a>, <a href="bword://Legba">Legba</a>, <a href="bword://Oshun">Oshun</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Inle">Inle</a>, and <a href="bword://Yemaya">Yemaya</a>. During the Thorn Room Boss Battle, if his shrine is repaired: “He’s a little shy. You will learn a level-15 Lightning spell if you repair it. You will gain the ability to train this spell to 20. Hint, hint that spell might come in handy later. Shango will ascend.”</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orisha Deity</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shango on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shango</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shanty Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bodi and Prepotente are way on the other side, coming up from Shanty Town right now with the 103 rd , and we want the Madness defenders to focus on them while we sneak up.” I took a few steps, reached down.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sharp Elbows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sharp Elbows tossed her existing staff aside and grabbed the tree with two hands as digital, laughing skulls and wraithlike ghosts danced about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sharp-Elbows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sharp-Elbows</b> is an <a href="bword://Ogre">Ogre</a> Necromancer Witch, covered in spikes, who fights <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on floor 8.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sharp-Elbows on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sharp-Elbows</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shatter Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shatter Spell</b> is a spell that disarms attackers. Any attack that lands upon the object imbued with this enchantment has a 1.5% chance to disarm the opponent. If the opponent is disarmed, there is a 90% chance the item will drop to the ground, a 5% chance the weapon will break, and a 5% chance the weapon will be immediately transferred to your inventory.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shatter Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shatter_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shattering Train Bomb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shattering Train Bomb</b> is what the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a> turns into when there is no one driving the train. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> creates this bomb on the Fourth Floor by killing the train engineers on the Nightmare Express using his <a href="bword://Protective Shell Spell">Protective Shell Spell</a>. <a href="bword://Fire Brandy">Fire Brandy</a> helps him defuse the bomb by teaching him how to drive the train, earning him the <a href="bword://Kept A Rollin&#x27; Achievement">Kept A Rollin' Achievement</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Nightmare Express (Fourth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shattering Train Bomb on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shattering_Train_Bomb</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sheet Music</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sheet Music</b> is similar to a scroll or magic book but exclusive to bard classes. It allows bards to sing a song they normally couldn’t, the system helped them perform if they had the music in front of them making it easier to pull off but with a chance of failure if the performance was out of key. If sung badly the sheet music tears a little bit and with too many tears, it’s ruined. If sung perfectly and in the correct pitch the spell is added to your bard repertoire, when this happens the sheet music isn’t consumed and can be sold or given to someone else.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sheet Music on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sheet_Music</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shell spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A favorite of frontline tanks and castle guards, the expensive and rare Protective Shell spell shields the caster and anyone within the sphere from a mob’s physical presence or physical.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sheol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The trap was that as soon as one passed through this central area where one had to choose a path for the final approach to the exit, the ground would drop out, dropping everyone into the waterworks and pipes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sheol Bricks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sheol Bricks</b> are the hardened, shriveled, blackened corpses of <a href="bword://Lesser Demon">Lesser Demon</a> infants. Common on the Fifteenth Floor, these coal-like bricks are a valuable resource that burn long and hot when exposed to fire.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Lesser Demon MILF; Fifteenth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sheol Bricks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sheol_Bricks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sheol Fire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sheol Fire</b> is an extremely destructive fire native to Sheol, the Fifteenth Floor, hot enough to turn wood instantly to ash and rock to lava. Unlike ordinary fire, it also empowers any demon it touches instead of harming it.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sheol Fire on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sheol_Fire</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sheol Glass Reaper Case</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sheol Glass Reaper Case</b> is a case that can contain extreme things and stop them in time, such as a crystal nuke that is a millisecond away from exploding.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sheol Glass Reaper Case on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sheol_Glass_Reaper_Case</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shepherd</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was still just Shepherd, which was apparently a type of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>She’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d dropped just a few moments after I’d spoken.” “Tell me exactly what you were doing at the time when they dropped this girl on the building.” It was only then I realized I’d been clutching onto the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>She’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Session of Love, the final show before we all die.” In smaller letters under that, it read, “Balcony still closed due to jellyfish infestation.” “She’ll meet us at the entrance to the Desperado Club,” Arief.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>She’s Bloom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s Bloom, and I am not.” Bloom was the kua-tin political party that ran.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shi Maria</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shi Maria</b> is a demi-god, <a href="bword://Reaper Spider Minion">Reaper Spider Minion</a>, and Level 140 City Boss encountered in the Cuba region of the Eighth Floor. Also known as the Screamer, the Bedlam Bride, the Widow Endless, and MaeMae, she briefly serves as a powerful Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> in Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s deck before binding herself to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> as a living tattoo.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Screamer, The Reviled, Kwaku's Love, The Corrupted Paramour, The Bedlam Bride, The Widow Endless, MaeMae</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Celestial Realm (banished to the Eighth Floor)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Reaper Spider Minion (Demi-god)</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shi Maria on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shi_Maria</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shi Maria Jola Lazarus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shi Maria’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was supposed to have gone and experienced a dream from Shi Maria’s point of view, and Katia was supposed to have faced this dream on her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mine was a Valtay Perso-Shield, Platinum Edition , which was identical to the one I would’ve gotten from Paulie, and it was simply a round, pinback button that looked a little like the planet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shield Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Shield</b></i> is a popular protection spell that creates a translucent sphere around the caster, and repels corporeal red-tagged mobs and physical attacks. Unlike <i>Protective Shell</i>, <i>Shield</i> moves with the caster.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shield Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shield_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shirley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: What’s a girl gotta do to get a shirty dirly?” “It’s dirty Shirley,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shirley Temple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only one of the group who didn’t have alcohol was Donut, who was instead sipping on a regular Shirley Temple, chatting away with the Sledge, who grumbled happily in response to all of her declarations and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Drinking a swig (defined as 1.5 ounces) of this “potion” will immediately cause you to gain the Shit-Faced debuff, has a 50% chance to render you Blind for a period of 30 hours, a 5% chance to immediately kill.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shit Alley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ruby lived, but Flint says she’s destined to spend her life in Weird Shit Alley.” “Why did the gnomes bomb you?” “Flint says it’s because gnomes are short little assholes who deserve nothing but to be trampled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shivered Sky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shivered Sky Series</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shock Chomper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shock Chomper</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that looks like a lion-headed naga with an electrical attack that can be found on the Yellow Line. No full description is given other than <b>"lesser nagas with mommy issues."</b> Given that this whole floor is a political parody, this is most likely in reference to the <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> and the story of the Sepsis Whore.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shock Chomper on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shock_Chomper</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shock Chompers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Bonkers, as we started calling them, and the Shock Chompers, which were described as “lesser nagas with mommy issues.” These things were all level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shoe Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shoe Box</b> are <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> awarded for foot related achievements.</li><li><b>Source:</b> This Little Piggy Went to Market! Achievement (Platinum)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shoe Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shoe_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Short</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shot a magic missile</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut jumped to my shoulder and shot a magic missile at the tiny monster, and she scored a direct.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shot Chicken</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d never heard of the restaurant chain before we’d entered the dungeon, but Donut and I had spent some time stuck in a Big Shot Chicken safe room on the second floor while we’d been trapped by the rage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shotgun Messenger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Florin – Crocodilian – Shotgun Messenger – Level 33 – 100,000 (x2).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shouldn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Shouldn’t you go down there and take care of it for us?” Pontiff was looking back toward our.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shouting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Shouting street vendors with food carts and what appeared to be glowing souvenirs littered the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: More specifically, the room was devoted to Donut’s Best-in-Show ribbons, her Best-in-Breed ribbons, and the countless trophies and framed photographs of her sitting on a table, looking all fuzzy and pissed off.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Show Must Go On</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Show Must Go.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shrink Wand</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Shrink Wand</b> is a wand that causes items to shrink in size. Has 10 charges. The effect can be <b>prematurely stopped.</b></li></ul><p class="source">Source: Shrink Wand on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Shrink_Wand</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shush</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Shush, both of you,” I said, watching a zombie seal shuffle-flop down the street, chasing an oblivious memory ghost that miraculously was still fully.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Shut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: No offense.” “Shut the fuck up, Paulie,” another homeless person called as he also sat down at the table with a cup of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Siege Master Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Siege Master Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>participating in large-scale warfare</b>. <b>Contents:</b> Unclear, but <b><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> obtained powerful explosives and siege-based gear</b>​</li><li><b>Source:</b> B'sieging Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Siege Master Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Siege_Master_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sierra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sierra - Northropi <a href="bword://Vampire">Vampire</a></b>. This is a Bereft Minion of Viscount Fog</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sierra on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sierra</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sigmund Textiles Foundry</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The Sigmund Textiles Foundry is a publicly traded company based in the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>. It's shares are valued at 10.422 credits.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sigmund Textiles Foundry on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sigmund_Textiles_Foundry</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Signet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Signet’s blood also remained in this world, yanked from her flesh as she was torn away, temporarily creating an ethereal form that rose into the air and started to swirl and swirl upon itself, turning into a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silfa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Silfa</b> is a fairy class healer crawler who is a part of <a href="bword://Brynhild&#x27;s Daughters">Brynhild's Daughters</a>. She has two daughters who came into the dungeon with her. Silfa has at least three spells, a healing spell, a map hiding skill, and an ice spell.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Wisteria Fairy</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 22, Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Silfa on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Silfa</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silk Road</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d purchased a few sets of pus from Pustule the last time we’d been in the Silk Road, and I was burning through the expensive detonator.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Adventurer Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Silver Adventurer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What the hell is this, and why is it in a silver box and not a bronze one?” “Its origin and value and rarity and a hundred other factors determine the required box type,” Mordecai said, snatching it from my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Earth</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Earth Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: As an incentive to choose an Earth Class, you will receive a Silver Earth Box upon choosing this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Goblin Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, I’d received another Silver Goblin Box for killing more than 10 mobs with an explosive and a Gold Crowd Control Box for killing more than 15 mobs with the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Mechanic’s Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Silver Mechanic’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You will receive a Silver Quest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Quest Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You will receive a Silver Quest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Silver Ring</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Silver Ring</b> is simple rings that, when equipped on a crawler's finger, boost a specific stat by a set value. They can be found in Silver <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Silver Ring on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Silver_Ring</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Similar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Similar buffs from different hat items, like two different intelligence boosts, will not combine and will take up two.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Simoom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Simoom</b> is a rhino that <a href="bword://Ferdinand">Ferdinand</a> uses as a mount on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Simoom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Simoom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Since</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Since the colored lines all emptied into the massive pit at different heights, coming into the giant crater from different directions, and since the walkways that circled the pit’s interior were now blasted to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Since Bomo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Since Bomo had last entered the personal space through the saferoom in Zockau, he’d leave the same way if he went out the door by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Since Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Since Carl is described as still, technically, conscious when you made your offer, the request was delivered directly to his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Single card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The stronger versions of Asojano and Uzi Jesus made it a great, powerful combo, especially since we’d be allowed to bring a single card with us onto the next.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Single crawler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: If it comes down to saving the life of a single crawler or blowing an entire drama that’s been building for weeks, what do you think is going to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sinjin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sinjin</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (15th Edition)</i>. Sinjin came from a race with paws. They were most likely killed by the Dungeon goddess Kuraokami after trying to leave the faith.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sinjin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sinjin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sir</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sir, I know I’m not supposed to question orders, but I feel I need to understand.” Captain Fresh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sir Ferdinand</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Gah!” Imogen cried as the orange form of Sir Ferdinand appeared and slammed into her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sir Ferdinand of Team Retribution</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sir Ferdinand of Team Retribution has proposed an emergency action.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sir Rendlegore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Sir Rendlegore is described as curled up at my feet, snoring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sirin Station</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Next stop: Sirin Station (81) in 12 minutes and 32.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Sister,” Paz called up to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sister Ines</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sister Ines Quiteria</b> is a Cuban nun and a Crawler from Earth who leads a three-crawler squad alongside Anton and <a href="bword://Paz Lo">Paz Lo</a>. Her squad allies briefly with the Royal Court on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Sister Ines; El Segador de La Habana</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Cuba</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Cat Girl</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sister Ines on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sister_Ines</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sister Ines Quiteria</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “During the battles between the crabs and seals, crawler Sister Ines Quiteria used her level-15 Book of Rhymes bard skill, which she just got from her patron, to enhance her Word Weaver spell to charm this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sister Ines’s</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the zombie event had started to disrupt Sister Ines’s charm poem, but since her sponsor and patron, the Nebulars, helped her raise that Word Weaver spell to level 15, she was able to fully control both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sitting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sitting on the concrete floor of the bar, wrapped around the base of the table, was a tall, long-limbed soother.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sitting on the floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The DJ was shouting at Dwight, who was now sitting on the floor like a dog and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sixth Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sixth Place: &lt;Empty&gt; A replacement team will be assigned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Size</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Size-altering and were-creatures, such as the BigBoi are required to wear enchanted, self-sizing items lest they wish to turn the dungeon into a nudist colony when they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Size Up</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Everything from the invisibility potions that Zabit had picked to a few of those Size Up potions I’d taken from Miriam Dom to Feather Fall and several protection.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Size-Up Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Size-Up Potion</b> is a potion that gives detail about mobs including their stats and skills.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Size-Up Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Size-Up_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skarn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A juvenile changeling, about eight years old. Orphaned and living in Hump Town after the Gnomes bombed their town. Operates the telescope. Currently being looked after by <a href="bword://Flint">Flint</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 2, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skarn on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skarn</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skedaddle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I’ll take the shrink wand, the eighth floor lore book thing, that music sheet of Skedaddle , that case of 24 Feather Fall potions, and a case of healing potions.” * * * After a parade of other pets, it was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skellie</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Skellies</b> are "your typical, run-of-the-mill reanimated skeleton," and may be created by the <i>Boned</i> spell. In the Dungeon, several <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> share this race, but Skellies may also appear as "weak undead" mobs.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skellie on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skellie</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skewer Belle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Skewer Belle</b> is a mushroom that, upon touching the top, extrudes a spike and causes skin to boil if it pierces you, The spike only activates if you touch the top, therefore it is advised that you should only touch the stem of this mushroom. The <b>Skewer Belle</b> has useful alchemical properties.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Sixth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skewer Belle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skewer_Belle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skill Level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I also received a new one: IED Skill Level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skill levels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Plus there are those Cheat Code potions that do the same thing, but for three skill levels.” Mordecai.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skill Potion</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Skill Potion</b> is a special potion that, when consumed, adds a single level to a skill specified in the item's description. Typically received in <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a>, Skill Potions may be the only way to unlock certain Crawler Menu options such as sorting inventory by value.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skill Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skill_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skills and spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: My primal race allowed me to train past 15 in skills and spells, but armor and weapons-based enhancements lost effectiveness past 15, which meant certain benefits, such as the +1 to Powerful Strike from my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skull Clan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This title is being disputed by Warlord Stalwart of the Skull.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skull Empire</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Skull Empire</b> is one of the largest and oldest system governments in the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, ruled by an orcish royal family that claims descent from gods and royalty. The family owns the Death Watch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem production and fields the Skull Clan in Faction Wars on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skull Empire on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skull_Empire</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: So far, it looked as if the best prizes were the Eye in the Sky scrolls, the magical tomes, Velma the Flame Thrower, the nickel sock, and, of course, Quan’s celestial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sky Series</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skydiver's Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Skydiver's Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed the <a href="bword://Cannonball! Achievement">Cannonball! Achievement</a> for falling from a great height.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Cannonball! Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skydiver's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skydiver%27s_Box</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skyfowl</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Skyfowl</b> are a race of flying, eagle-headed humanoids and the birth race of <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a>. Before the Cataclysm, they ruled the Third Floor's Over City, and they still govern several of its settlements.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skyfowl on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skyfowl</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skyfowl Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Skyfowl Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Sixth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Ranged Weapon Box">Ranged Weapon Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://That&#x27;s not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement">That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Ranged Weapon Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skyfowl Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skyfowl_Upgrade_Patch</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skyfowl Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Skyfowl Upgrade Patch</b> is an <a href="bword://Upgrade Patch">Upgrade Patch</a> given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Sixth Floor in a Gold <a href="bword://Ranged Weapon Box">Ranged Weapon Box</a> rewarded to him for the <a href="bword://That&#x27;s not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement">That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement</a>. This patch is applied to the <a href="bword://Enchanted Anarchist&#x27;s Battle Rattle">Enchanted Anarchist's Battle Rattle</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Ranged Weapon Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skyfowl Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skyfowl_Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skylar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Skylar is described as about to time out.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Skylar Spinach</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Skylar Spinach</b> is a Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> on the Eighth Floor. This card is held by the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Eighth Floor Cuba</li><li><b>Race:</b> Monk Seal</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Skylar Spinach on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Skylar_Spinach</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slammed the door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t wait to see what that was about before I dove into the saferoom and slammed the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slate Butterfly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Slate Butterfly is described as given a boon by the fairies of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slaughter Gnus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Slaughter Gnus is described as six-legged beasts of burden that historically were used as war mounts by a race of creatures called Scads, or the Rascals by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sledge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Sledge</b> is a <a href="bword://Cretin">Cretin</a> NPC employed as a bodyguard at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> and later assigned to the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> as permanent security. He works alongside <a href="bword://Bomo">Bomo</a>, a fellow cretin bodyguard.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Sledgie</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cretin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 8</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sledge on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sledge</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sledgie</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Sledgie is described as stunned, and the other guy had a knife and…” “ What lady.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slender Man</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: My first impression was Slender Man.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slime</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Slimes</b> are toxic, arm-less mobs found on <i>at least</i> the First Floor and Seventh Floor of the Dungeon. Level 2 Green Slimes are roughly pig-sized gelatinous green blobs that occupy at least one neighborhood on the First Floor. Their flesh is acidic and burns on contact.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Slime on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Slime</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slime Time</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d originally called it “Slime Time,” but it had somehow evolved to “Hekla.” Katia thought it was distasteful and a little fucked-up to call it that, and it was, but it was also pretty damn.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slingshot</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Slingshots</b> are small, handheld, hand-powered projectile weapons typically made with a Y-shaped frame with an elastic strip tied to the ends of each prong. On Earth, slingshots are common toys but have also been used as military weapons by guerilla forces. During the Siege of Marawi in 2017, the Philippine Army's elite Scout Rangers used slingshots to hurl grenades at opposing forces.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Mobs</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Slingshot on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Slingshot</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Slit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Slit</b> is a Feral Minor Demon who was banished to the Nothing a very, very long time ago. Slit and her sisters, including <a href="bword://Gash">Gash</a>, are members of a demon harem living in the Nothing. <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>, while banished there, talked about her sexual exploits with her king, and Slit thought that Samantha had had sex with <i>their</i> king. Naturally, Samantha trolled them with made-up stories about her wild sexcapades with <i>their</i> king. Now, Slit and her sisters hate Samantha.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Lesser Demon</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 34</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Slit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Slit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sluggalo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sluggalos</b>, initially identified by the system as <b>Pox Slugs</b>, are a bizarre, juggalo-themed species of slug monsters born from the infectious boils of the SlugPox disease. They exhibit a crude, rowdy culture and serve as highly enthusiastic, albeit expendable, combatants for the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> during the Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sluggalo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sluggalo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Small</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Small, angry, think they are bigger than they really are, there is nothing more terrifying than a pack of these little bastards charging at you across the battlefield, at least in their own.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Small box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Though I did receive something quite useful from a platinum Did-You-Really-Just-Cast-Fireball-in-a-Room-This-Small box.” “We still need to have a discussion about that,” Mordecai said, sounding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Small creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’s going to use the chain to get to us.” Sure enough, once the chain was tight, the small creature attached a little basket to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Small room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I just want to know everything I can about that ooze that is coming up the stairs, because I need to get rid of it long enough for me to occupy that small room in the back of your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Small town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The club was right where the bopca said it would be, several miles south along the main drag of a small town, about halfway toward our destination of the swamp with Samantha and the boss.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smeat Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Smeat Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Smeat Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Smeat_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smoke</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Hit it with a missile as we cross!” Smoke filled the street ahead of us, but it caught on a breeze, blowing in the wrong.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smoke bomb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing that’d worked so far was mixing a smoke bomb with a healing spell, which in turn was a great offense against.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smoke bombs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Are you in the market for explosives tonight?” Her tables held multiple boxes of both goblin dynamite and hobgoblin dynamite, along with smoke bombs, detonators, and several odds and ends I’d never before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smoke Form Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Smoke Form</b> is a Phase-style travel spell granted by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s Assassin of Sekhmet class, letting her slip through and around obstacles. Unlike <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s Gloom Wraith Phase, which is an attack that forces movement in a single direction, Smoke Form is purely a travel spell, letting the caster equally slip through and around obstacles without a fixed direction.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Smoke Form Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Smoke_Form_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smush</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: This limited race is only available to Crawlers who have obtained a level five Smush skill by the time race selection becomes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smush skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: This limited race is only available to Crawlers who have obtained a level five Smush skill by the time race selection becomes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Smushed for Daddy Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Smushed for Daddy Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for being stepped on by <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a>. Carl opens this box in a safe room on the Fifth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Smushed for Daddy Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Smushed_for_Daddy_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snail Trail</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Snail Trail</b> is a goblin dancer at Bitches, a club inside the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>, and the wife of <a href="bword://Splash Zone">Splash Zone</a>. Snail Trail works the early shift at Bitches, alongside her mother, <a href="bword://Grandma Sticky">Grandma Sticky</a>, who works the later shift. Splash Zone, her husband, speaks fondly and proudly of her pole routine.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Snail Trail on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Snail_Trail</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snake lady</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So Katia and Carl and me went to kill the snake lady, but when I got there, my plan was to go say hi to and possibly kill my own mother—her name is Theia—but when I went to her place, it really wasn’t her but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snake Pit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: What he was really saying was it was time to initiate Operation: Snake.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snare card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We collected several other cards, including one more snare card, a card called Hole in the Bag , which drained a totem’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snare cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I can’t discard for several seconds, and it’s all snare cards!” Geraldo, who’d still been in the shell’s area when it cast, continued to unnecessarily flip as he moved off in a random direction, screaming at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snare Trap</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Snare Traps</b> activate when stepped on, and hold the target in place. During the second phase of the Eighth Floor, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s snare traps hold targets in place for fifteen seconds.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Market</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Snare Trap on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Snare_Trap</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sneak attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The sneak attack of the crabs was stopped in its tracks thanks to crawler Sister Ines Quiteria warning the Seals of the treachery, but that warning has come with a terrible.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sneaky Tyler</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: JUSTICE LIGHT “Leave,” Justice Light said to Sneaky Tyler C, the lead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sniper's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Sniper's Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>long-range combat proficiency</b>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Hail Mary Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sniper's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sniper%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Snow Cones</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Some people thought you were actually a Valtay agent, but WriggleInDew2023, who is the vice president of the team Mongo Mommies Valtay chapter and a street team member of the Snow Cones, said there was no.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>So Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “So Donut gets bonus damage to her spells, and Mongo gets 20% more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Social Media Board</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Social Media Board</b> is a tier-1 Personal Space Social Upgrade that allows crawlers to view a running log of social media comments. It is heavily moderated by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> and fully replaces your appointed <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> Social Media Manager. The board beeps and clicks as the feed updates.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Social Media Board on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Social_Media_Board</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels (TSECS), NFC</b><b>, dba</b> <b>as <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a> Enterprises, NFC</b> is a nonprofit <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> organization and NFC taking legal action to prevent the Syndicate from preserving Cocker Spaniel DNA. It also sponsors the Princess Posse faction on the Ninth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> TSECS; Princess Posse Enterprises</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Society_for_the_Eradication_of_Cocker_Spaniels</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soft Vore Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Soft Vore</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after he got eaten by <a href="bword://Lusca">Lusca</a> the <a href="bword://Octo-Shark">Octo-Shark</a> Brood <a href="bword://Mother">Mother</a> Queen. Carl receives this achievement on the Fifth Floor after getting eaten whole by Lusca. No one else that participated in the fight got this achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Soft Vore Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Soft_Vore_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Solar system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Borant Corporation, having been assigned regency over this solar system, is allowed to choose the manner of this reclamation, and they have chosen option 3, also known as the 18-Level World.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sometimes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sometimes, there are no surprises, and despite that, despite your intention to gird yourself against what’s about to come, it still hits you with the force of a kick to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Somewhere</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’s probably on the roof.” Somewhere in the house, there was a crash followed by a distinctive pop.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s just see what it is.” Soon after Katia had been sponsored by the Princess Formidable, the youngest sister of Prince Maestro and Crown Prince Stalwart of the Skull Empire, we explained to her everything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soother</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You skin one little warren of rabbits, you spray the innards of a chatty, overweight Soother tourist all over the gift shop, and everybody is suddenly “scared.” What about my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sorry</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Sorry we can’t wine and dine you first, bomber boy, but we need to get trucking on this bullshit your friend is making us do,” Gwen said as we marched toward the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sort</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Are you able to change the name over your head?” “Sort of,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sort of creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The monster was the same sort of creature as a shambling berserker, though those things had all been made of random piles of body parts all splatted.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul armor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: This would be a problem if we ever made it to the twelfth floor—though that floor had some interesting rules regarding soul armor—but for right now, it gave us a few minutes before the Maestro would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul crystal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: They are created by capturing rising or falling souls via a soul crystal, rerouting the energy through a corruption spell, and then mixing it all together, which turns it all into an oil-like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul Crystals</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Soul Crystals</b> is a power source that elves use. They are created when certain types of people die and their souls are filtered through <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s body. They are powered by and run off actual souls.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Souls dying in a set area to charge the crystal</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Soul Crystals on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Soul_Crystals</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul gem</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once cast, the ritual begins, the guest list forms, and we have the party.” “Wait, you cast a spell for the party?” She fingered the soul gem around her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul Leech Capacitor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: A Soul Leech Capacitor can do that for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul power</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: He knew bringing another dark cleric like myself from the dim would make an especially strong capacitor, giving him the additional soul power he needed to subjugate the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Soul Reaper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Climb, Soul Reaper, and Mysterious Bone Key.” Mordecai blinked at me with his single.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sounds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “That’s just, I don’t know, eww.” “Sounds like she gave you a simple quest which will allow her to give you another boon without you actually having to worship her.” “I know the winner of Faction Wars doesn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Space Jam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple children sat within the safe room, just like the last time, and they all huddled together, no longer paying attention to the movie on the screen, which appeared to be the original Space Jam.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spam Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A Silver <b>Spam Box</b> is awarded to "most everyone in the dungeon" after the first card battles on the eight floor. TBA</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Spam Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Spam_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spanish</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A black garbage bag was ripped open at my feet, and I could see fast food wrappers, soggy Spanish-language magazines, and empty cans.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sparkles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the end, we went with the Bubble Buddy upgrade, a heat sink in case we lost our containment, and the regular version of the same booster rockets that team Sparkles had, which, when combined with the bubble.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sparks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sparks and little electronic pieces showered as it was hit with a second electrical bolt, cast by one of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Speaking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Speaking of the Death Challenge, Carl, are you aware of the significance of what happened today?” “Well, I got stepped on by a god and got a close-up view of a building-sized monster get split in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Special abilities</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They are fast and strong and still have any spells and special abilities of the monsters they were before they were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Special ability</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: My Agent Provocateur class added an additional +1 to intelligence, and thanks to the constitution bump from the ring, I figured it was in my best interest to utilize my special ability to double my level-up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Special Brew</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mordecai’s Special Brew A potion designed by a shifty Changeling who has a tendency to kill those who count on him the most, this special potion combines the effects of a Gold Standard Healing Potion and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Special Play</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> come up with Special Plays as a system for quickly taking specific actions on the battlefield. Each play is supposed to be followed immediately and without question upon being called.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Special Play on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Special_Play</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Special Rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Special Rules: Your vehicle will have a special containment unit surrounding you for this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Species Protection</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: After a few more minutes of the bug woman discussing how the formation of the Indigenous Species Protection Act was endangering the very existence of all life in the universe, I leaned forward and banged my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Species Protection Act</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Species Protection Act is described as endangering the very existence of all life in the universe, I leaned forward and banged my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spell or skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Note : these two benefits include your full spell and skill arsenal, so it’s possible an item-based spell or skill will be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spells</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Not when I knew he would perish on his own at sunset, which is what happened.” “How did you know he’d die at sunset?” “Spells, when they bounce back and forth so many times, have a tendency to fall, how do you.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spells and skills</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: That was followed by a long list of spells and skills, including a level-15 Unending Insanity , which was self-explanatory, and a level-15 Lights Out , which I knew was a blindness.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spicy Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Spicy Box</b> is a type of Loot Box given to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for achievements the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> <i>really</i> likes. Due to the individual nature of each awarded box, this page is broken up by book.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Spicy Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Spicy_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spider Reaper Minion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This patch is finely made from Spider Reaper Minion thread.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spider Stalker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Spider Stalkers</b> (also frequently referred to as spider automatons or seeker spiders) are small, crafted robotic devices that function as mechanical versions of the explosive "rot sticker" mobs. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> uses them as mobile delivery systems for bombs, traps, and potions.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Crafted by Carl (Requires a Main Driveshaft and automaton components)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Spider Stalker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Spider_Stalker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spike Module</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Spike Module</b> is a type of trap that produces spikes that move up and down off the surface that it is placed on. “The spike module had a similar description (to Alarm Module), but it would cause 50-centimeter spikes to pop out of the ground at two second intervals in a one meter-square area. Friendly <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> and crawlers wouldn’t set it off, but once triggered, it would keep spiking up and down forever, so we had to be careful.”</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Spike Module on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Spike_Module</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spin Again</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: spot took up two spaces and one of them, 500,000 gold, was a tiny sliver with “Spin Again” on either.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spit and Swallow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We sat in the Spit and Swallow, which was filled with camels taking a quick breather from their frantic work to repair the town before the next impending sand.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Splash Zone</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Splash Zone</b> is an NPC otter and former stripper at the Penis Parade who serves as a mercenary and loyal ally to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, and their faction.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Splashy, Splash</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The Dungeon (Desperado Club)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Otter</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 23</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Splash Zone on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Splash_Zone</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Splat potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’ll keep you from dying via falling damage, but you’ll be at 5% health after you hit the ground.” “They’re still better than that Dolores Doesn’t Splat potion,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Splatter Skunk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gold Shoe Box (9/10) Enchanted Toe Ring of the Splatter.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Split Personality</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It is quite rare for two halfsies to survive more than a few minutes after the casting of Split Personality , so I haven’t had the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Split Personality Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Split Personality</b> is a spell that cuts a living creature vertically in half, leaving both resulting halves alive and functional. Victims of Split Personality are severed exactly down the center, from between the eyes through the groin, left standing on a single leg with one arm and a second arm above it. Split victims rarely survive more than a few minutes after the casting, and the two halves normally must wear supportive suits to keep their organs from spilling out. Reuniting the two halves afterward requires a fleshmancer.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Split Personality Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Split_Personality_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sponsor box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have a celestial sponsor box to open, plus I just received a Former Child Actor accessory kit in the saferoom mailbox, and I need to open it right away.” She looked me up and down with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sponsored by Huanxin Jinx</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sponsored by Huanxin Jinx of the Grixist Swarm, CEO of Icon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sponsored gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Sponsored gods is described as all suddenly acting quite.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sponsorship Tab</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Additional details available in the Sponsorship Tab of your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sponsor’s Name</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Sponsor’s Name: The Open Intellect Pacifist Action Network, Intergalactic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sprout Forest Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sprout Forest</b> is an ability of <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s that lets her grow a wide variety of plants, not limited to trees despite the name. Despite its name, Sprout Forest isn't restricted to growing trees; it lets Donut choose from a wide array of plant types. She uses it in consultation with <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> to select a specific flower for decoration purposes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sprout Forest Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sprout_Forest_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Spunky Jefferson the Enchanted Nickel Sock of the Elderly Miser</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Spunky Jefferson the Enchanted Nickel Sock of the Elderly Miser</b> is a weapon. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets the <a href="bword://Uh oh. It talks. Achievement">Uh oh. It talks. Achievement</a> for examining this weapon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Spunky Jefferson the Enchanted Nickel Sock of the Elderly Miser on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Spunky_Jefferson_the_Enchanted_Nickel_Sock_of_the_Elderly_Miser</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Squad Leader</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once all cards in your deck are played or discarded, the Squad Leader regains their ability to use their inventory and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Squim Conglomerate</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Squim Conglomerate</b> is a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> Corporation that mines planets. They ran the crawl before Dungeon Crawler World: Earth, a battle royale style season called Battle for Planet Aryl. The entire planet battles down to one champion. It makes good television but does not earn them a lot of money. The season does not include Faction Wars or <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Ascendency segments.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Squim Conglomerate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Squim_Conglomerate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Squonk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Squonk</b> are a mob on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Squonk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Squonk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stairwells</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stairwells</b> are a feature of the Dungeon that allows for passage between floors. Different floors of the dungeon may have different rules governing how their stairwells are accessed.</li><li><b>Source:</b> World Dungeon / System AI</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stairwells on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stairwells</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stalwart</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stalwart</b> is an orc and the crown prince of the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a>, one of the largest and oldest governments in the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>. He orders the execution of <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> after Carl insults his father, <a href="bword://King Rust">King Rust</a>, on live television.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Captain Enormous (proposed by Donut)</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Skull Empire</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 44</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stalwart on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stalwart</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stalwart of the Skull Empire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Let’s just see what it is.” Soon after Katia had been sponsored by the Princess Formidable, the youngest sister of Prince Maestro and Crown Prince Stalwart of the Skull Empire, we explained to her everything.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Standing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Standing just over 11 feet tall, the clown-on-stilts was always a favorite amongst the snot-nosed kiddos visiting Grimaldi’s three-ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Standing Ovation</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Standing Ovation</b> is a bard spell that increases the party's dexterity and make their regular spells more powerful.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Standing Ovation on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Standing_Ovation</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stanley Kubrick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’s the Ivy League college kind, where it’s a bunch of rich idiots who rent out a place, plaster a few pagan symbols on the wall, and then they wear masks with horns and leaves and shit on them and pretend.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Star Trek</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Akula ’s control center was a combination between the Enterprise bridge from Star Trek and the interior of a Victorian-era-themed restaurant that was so expensive, the prices weren’t on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Star Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d have to see it for myself, but I had the impression they looked kinda like the sand people from Star Wars wearing bishop.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Starting</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Starting at the end of this message, all saferoom locations will also include a practice arena where you may, for a fee, enter and summon your totem cards in a limited mock battle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stat Point</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Here’s a cheat sheet: Current Marks killed: 0 Current Mark benefit: +1 Stat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Station</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Station Mimic</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Station Mimic</b> is a City Boss on the Fourth Floor. They are at station 433, Terminous station. Mimics are shapeshifting creatures that can turn into inanimate objects to fool crawlers into coming close enough to be eaten. If a part of a mimic is chopped off, the removed part turns into a spidery creature and crawls back to join the whole. Bashing weapons and magic do nothing against it. If more than 50% of the body mass is removed, it can no longer heal or transform. The sliced pieces can't be killed and won't die until the entire thing dies.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mimic</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 17</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Station Mimic on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Station_Mimic</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Station Repair</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning.” The next was “Station Repair,” and after that was a third entitled “Recycle.” There were so many tracks on the ground, it was difficult to tell what led.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Status</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Status: Explosion Imminent (3/1x10 7 ) Created by a man who murders babies and steals rare collectibles from his elders, this device is powerful enough to level an entire city and all the suburbs around.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stay</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: She asked Britney about her underwear, and she said it featured a pin-up version of Elle along with the caption “Stay frosty, boys.” But the panties themselves increased her dexterity by 10% and enhanced all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Steadfast Emberus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The effects of this condition are being partially blocked by your Pauper’s ring of the Steadfast.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Steam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Steam rose off of her, and her eyes were swirling, black.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Steel Mill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read, “Gasworks Screw Factory, Steel Mill, and Doll Workshop.” And under it, a smaller sign read, “There’s no cocaine in here.” Sparks flew from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Step</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Step two will be to map out the area and figure out where the best monsters are.” “Stairs not too far,” Bomo said, tracing a rocky finger along the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Steve Rowland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of them—Author Steve Rowland—was slow dancing with Britney despite the music, which was currently “Grandma Got Run Over by a Reindeer.” The song was being played on an old, early-generation iPod, which was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t like having them, so I buried yours under a train track, and I threw Katia’s out in the garbage at that Hotdog on a Stick.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sticky Feet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We need to make sure they can reach their gate!” I pulled myself from my seat, activated Sticky Feet to make certain I wouldn’t plummet, and I started hurling Biscuit Packs at the bugs if they flew too.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stock Certificate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The bearer of this instrument now holds one share of common stock in the <a href="bword://Sigmund Textiles Foundry">Sigmund Textiles Foundry</a>, a publicly-traded company based in the Gun-ya system within the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> and traded via the I.R.F.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stock Certificate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stock_Certificate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stockade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: You, Stockade, knew that the spider would jump at the ability to escape, which is exactly what would’ve happened if things had gone as you anticipated.” Panford looked at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stone Hawk</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stone Hawks</b> are a pet-class mob that can be found in the Dungeon. Stone Hawks resemble standard American brown hawks, but their underwing coverts are opalescent. These hawks have the ability to transform their targets into stone through unspecified means; the target can then be shattered.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stone Hawk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stone_Hawk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Stop it now!” Mongo lifted his head into the air and howled joyfully as he continued to jackhammer into Kiwi like a drunken sailor on his first liberty at a foreign.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Storm Commander</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The skyfowl warrior had limited magic, despite his Storm Commander class, but he had a party protection spell that kept the caustic rain from burning them.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stout</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Totems: Shi Maria Jola Lazarus A-Bang-Bang Skylar Spinach Geraldo Raul Utility Cards: Stout (x2) Time Extend Greased Lightning (x2) Snare Cards: Hobble (Consumable) Hole in the Bag Damnation Lamp (Consumable).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stout card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strange</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Strange that it requires us to drink it manually.” “It makes us drink it manually because it tastes like Miami swamp ass,” Louis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strange creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The massive statue we’d made of the Unwashed, the strange creature that Juice Box had turned into, popped up like a jack-in-the-box, huge and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stranger Danger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: A gold T’Ghee box she’d gotten for using the practice room, her platinum fan box, and the bronze Stranger Danger.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stranger Danger Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Stranger Danger Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> unknowingly inviting a red-tagged NPC boss into a saferoom. <b>Bronze Stranger Danger Box</b></li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stranger Danger Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stranger_Danger_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stranger Things</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Just like in Stranger Things !” The world immediately changed so we were, indeed, on the first level of a bustling, multi-level shopping.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Street dogs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: She made comment after comment on the colorful architecture and clothing and vehicles, plus she was constantly hissing and complaining about the multiple street dogs who skulked around every.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Street Monk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: He was a level 28 Street Monk, which I assumed was some sort of melee.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Street Urchin</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Street Urchin</b> are the janitor mobs of the Third Floor. They appear to consume trash. Street Urchins look like knee high tumbleweeds, covered in black spikes about 15 inches long.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Street Urchin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Street_Urchin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Street Urchins</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Street Urchins is described as transformed into what you see.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strength</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So after I was done, I ended up with: Strength: 41 +3 (When Gauntlet Formed) Intelligence: 15 Constitution: 34 Dexterity: 23 Charisma: 25 “A strength over 40 is good for the fourth floor,” Mordecai said when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strength ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ring Pater Coal had secretly helped me “buy” at Club Vanquisher, the one disguised as my strength ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strip club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia and I needed to figure out how to find and kill the club’s assistant manager, Astrid, and then we needed to get her body to the Guild of Suffering, the entrance of which was one floor above us in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Strung Out</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Both had a Strung Out warning over them, meaning they’d been hit by the cloud of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stuffed Figure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Stuffed Figure</b> is a summoning device that looks like a beanie baby in the shape of a race of creature or a specific figure. Removing the tag summons the creature, having them fight on behalf of the summoner. The stuffed figures are made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stuffed Figure on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stuffed_Figure</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stuffed Grulke Infantry Figure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stuffed Grulke Infantry Figure</b> is a <a href="bword://Stuffed Figure">Stuffed Figure</a> of a <a href="bword://Grulke">Grulke</a> wearing a spiked helmet made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>. <a href="bword://Bautista">Bautista</a> acquired this from <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a>'s apartment on the Third Floor. He gives the stuffed animal to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after the End of the Line Rescue.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Miss Quill's apartment</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stuffed Grulke Infantry Figure on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stuffed_Grulke_Infantry_Figure</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stuffed Kimaris Figure</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stuffed Kimaris Figure</b> is a <a href="bword://Stuffed Figure">Stuffed Figure</a> made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>. It is a "Unique" Item, and one of the most valuable items in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Miss Quill's collection</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stuffed Kimaris Figure on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stuffed_Kimaris_Figure</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Kimaris</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Stuffed Kimaris Figure">Stuffed Kimaris Figure</a></b> is a <a href="bword://Stuffed Figure">Stuffed Figure</a> made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>. It is a "Unique" Item, and one of the most valuable items in <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Miss Quill's collection</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Kimaris on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Kimaris</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stuffed Slate Butterfly Figure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Stuffed Slate Butterfly Figure</b> is a <a href="bword://Stuffed Figure">Stuffed Figure</a> of a Slate Butterfly made by <a href="bword://Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited">Jaxbrin Amusements, Limited</a>. See also: <a href="bword://Talisman of the Slate Butterfly">Talisman of the Slate Butterfly</a> (Book 1)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Stuffed Slate Butterfly Figure on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Stuffed_Slate_Butterfly_Figure</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Stun effect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Most of the creatures had been knocked out by the smoke, but a handful of them were either immune or resisted the stun.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>STUPID DOG</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: THE SYSTEM IS SPELLING HIS NAME WRONG BECAUSE HE’S A STUPID DOG.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Subterranean level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gwen and Tran and their team had ventured into the now-dry subterranean level and managed to loot the remains of the tomb raiders and unearth the tomb of Anser, which was just a sarcophagus filled with the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Subterranean Quadrant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: All crawlers who originated in the Subterranean Quadrant may now freely travel to the other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Subway car</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d built herself almost like a toggle bolt, affixing herself to the flat front of the subway car, reaching in through the window and then making a small, vertical hunk of Katia that was bigger than the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Subway cars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: He still fit in the individual subway cars okay, but if we came across smaller cars with rowed seating, it was going to be a tight squeeze, especially around the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Succubus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Succubus</b> are the female equivalent of an <a href="bword://Incubus">Incubus</a>. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> warns <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> about staying away from girls with horns who would be attracted to his increased charisma.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Succubus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Succubus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Suddenly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are in the secret Temple of the Ending Sun.” Suddenly, there was a bubbling, marble fountain on the ground at my feet, similar to the one from the Yemaya.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sugar Cube</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Sugar Cube is a persian cat and one of <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s cousin and half aunt. She was used for <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sugar Cube on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sugar_Cube</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sultan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because she’d put it on, she’d become a royal member of “The Blood Sultanate.” We could only get off the ninth floor if all other members of that family were killed, including the Sultan.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sultana</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Sultana is described as the eldest half-sister of the Sultan, and she would be the mother of the future generation of the royal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summary Judgement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I noted she hadn’t received either the Summary Judgement boxes or the spicy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summary Judgement Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Summary Judgement Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <a href="bword://Fight the Power Achievement">Fight the Power Achievement</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> won seven of these for winning seven court cases.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fight the Power Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Summary Judgement Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Summary_Judgement_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summon Ally</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Summon Ally is described as a rare, multi-functional teleport.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summon Guard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: A still-activated Summon Guard trap was a dangerous liability for the person attached to the other side of that trap, and Justice Light had taught me a really nasty trick with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summon Guard Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Summon Guard</b> is a summoning spell that instantly teleports backup to the caster's location. It appears both as an innate ability of the fairy <a href="bword://Astrid">Astrid</a> and, separately, as a type of preset trap module planted in contested territory.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Summon Guard Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Summon_Guard_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summoning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Summoning the hunters was the best choice after Lucia cast those spells and set up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Summoning rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: All deity summoning rules will remain in effect as written for the ninth.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sun of Reawakening</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Nobody must touch the Sun of Reawakening until after the Santero has rebuilt my shrine!” Donut and Paz stopped talking, and we all focused our attention on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sun Shrine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Sun Shrine</b> appears in a crawler's Personal Space if they choose to worship <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a>. The shrine is a bubbling fountain consisting of a sun disk, a cup, and a skull; the blessed water runs red, and can be used in crafting.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Worshipping Emberus</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sun Shrine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sun_Shrine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Super Spreader</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: He was already throwing a card out just as I cast Super Spreader.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Superb Constitution Buff Potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> brews <b>Superb Constitution Buff Potion</b> on the Fourth Floor using the level 3 <a href="bword://Alchemy Table">Alchemy Table</a>. The <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a> buffs a player's Constitution by a random number between 1 and 4 and the crawler can only take two total potions.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Mordecai's Alchemy Table</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Superb Constitution Buff Potion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Superb_Constitution_Buff_Potion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Superior Fire Demon</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>These bitches are three pugs short of a grumble. <b>Superior Fire <a href="bword://Demons">Demons</a></b> are notorious for their power, instability, and ability to wreak havoc.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Superior Fire Demon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Superior_Fire_Demon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Supposedly</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Supposedly some seasons had gauntlet-style floors where you had to get to a central control room and kill the trolls who were operating the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Suppurating Eye</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: And with the blood splattered all over the room, I strongly suspected whomever the head bad guy was, he probably had that Suppurating Eye spell.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Suppurating Eye Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Suppurating Eye Spell</b> is a common lich spell that allows the caster to see or overhear anything going on while the spell is active. Commonly attached to Scrolls.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Suppurating Eye Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Suppurating_Eye_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Surefire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A Surefire adds the “Guided” status to any projectile, powered or not. It may be utilized on a vast array of crafting tables. If added to an arrow or bolt, you must choose a target prior to unleashing the weapon, and the projectile will seek that target.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Sniper's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Surefire on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Surefire</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Surely</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Surely she didn’t know what Katia and I were attempting, but if she knew Samantha was responsible for this demon, who knew what she would.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Surface of your planet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you choose not to enter the World Dungeon, you will have to sustain yourself upon the surface of your planet, and this may be the last communication you receive during your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Surprise One</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: With Surprise One, I wasn’t certain if a chomp from Mongo would be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Surprised</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Surprised, I took a quick glance at my main chat window, and it was filled with panicked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Survivor's Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Survivor's Box</b> is a Loot Box awarded to crawlers who only just barely survive a battle. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives this box on the First Floor after the fight with the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Survivor's Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Survivor%27s_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sushi Smoothie Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sushi Smoothie Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for using the Soul Reaper Skill on a <a href="bword://Concierge Shark">Concierge Shark</a> to cure his Soul Poisoning debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sushi Smoothie Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sushi_Smoothie_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Svern</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Svern</b> is a <a href="bword://Changeling">Changeling</a> impersonating a <a href="bword://Dromedarian">Dromedarian</a> in Hump Town.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Changeling Principal</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Svern on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Svern</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Swear to god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If this ends up being some sort of trick or bullshit beyond what you say, I swear to god I will kill you myself,” I said to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sweety</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was a total of twelve heats entering this race, but none of the other heats were teams from our guild except Prepotente and Jurgen aboard their tapir.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Swordfish Interlopers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Swordfish Interlopers</b> are a bipedal swordfish mob.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Swordfish Interlopers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Swordfish_Interlopers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sydnee</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “In the common understanding of the fairy tale and in the dungeon itself,” Sydnee said, “the nine-tier attack is a magical assault that is considered to have been nine different, rapid, devastating spells that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Sydnee Iglacia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Sydnee Iglacia</b> is a <a href="bword://Saccathian">Saccathian</a> author and self-described crawl historian. A civilian rather than a crawler, she first appears as a panelist at CrawlCon, where she is largely overlooked despite her passion for chronicling the dungeon's history.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Makoka Cloud (SCC 5 / "Observation Deck")</li><li><b>Race:</b> Saccathian</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 33</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Sydnee Iglacia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Sydnee_Iglacia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Syndicate</b> is a massive intergalactic governing body composed of over three billion independent star systems and quintillions of citizens of various races. It orchestrates the seizure and mining of seeded planets, running the World Dungeon broadcasts—the Crawl—as a lucrative source of entertainment and resources.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Syndicate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Syndicate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate Council</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Syndicate Council</b> is the governing body of the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, the interstellar government whose member states set and enforce law across more than three billion independent star systems. Races apply to the council for membership.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Council</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Syndicate Council on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Syndicate_Council</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate Court</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Prism Kingdom officially condemns this action by the Skull Empire, and we will be filing an immediate grievance with the Syndicate Court.” “The Valtay and the Skull Empire are strong allies,” Tucker.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate Crawl Version</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current wetware: Syndicate Crawl Version.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate Government</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Per subsection 1256-C of the Indigenous Species Protection Act, this particular reward is required by the Syndicate Government to be awarded to all Crawlers who have chosen a domestic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate rule</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All off-boarding requests are currently suspended while the Earth system remains in quarantine per Syndicate rule 470.B through 495 in the System AI Stability.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate rules</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Per Syndicate rules, subsection 543 of the Precious Elemental Reserves Code, having failed to file a proper appeal for mineral and elemental rights within 50 Solars of first contact, your planet has been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Syndicate Standard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was a mostly-blank sheet of paper with a signature line at the bottom and a hand-written headline at the top in Syndicate Standard that read, “Admission of non-sanctioned violence by Crawler number 4,122.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Synergy tower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this was successful, immediately afterwards, Li Na’s builder squad led by Toyotomi would get to work on a very tall, very powerful, very specialized, very expensive synergy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Synergy towers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Shanty Town menu allowed us to build several types of automatic defensive towers, but we could only build a handful of manned towers, actually called synergy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>System</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Borant controls the mobs and the experience earned, for the most part, but the System AI is still the ultimate arbiter and is using that strength to even out the odds.” “Is that why you didn’t have a fit when.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>System AI</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>System AI</b> is the artificial intelligence that generates, narrates, and enforces the rules of the World Dungeon on <i>Dungeon Crawler World: Earth</i>. When <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> is involved, it frequently refers to itself as "Daddy."</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> "Daddy" (self-applied, with Carl)</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Hive Home (Mantis system); installed in Earth's Primal Engine</li><li><b>Race:</b> Macro AI</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 1</li></ul><p class="source">Source: System AI on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/System_AI</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>System Message</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: System Message: All assets of the Blood Sultanate, as per prior arrangement, have been split evenly with the surviving blood relatives of the Sepsis Whore, Warlord Princess Donut and General Katia Grim, both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>T'Ghee Card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>T'Ghee cards</b> are the playing pieces of T'Ghee, the deck-building game the dungeon imposed on every crawler for the length of the eighth floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Flagging a creature at 5% health or below; Looted from defeated opponents</li></ul><p class="source">Source: T'Ghee Card on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/T%27Ghee_Card</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taco Bell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut and I had sat in the Taco Bell watching it on the screen while the proprietor, another Bopca Protector named Sebastian, sat next to us and brushed Donut’s hair with one of his own brushes, which he’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tagg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It is studied extensively, but we still have more questions than answers.” After a moment, he added, somewhat wistfully, “Gaining access to this creature is my life’s work.” “Superstitious nonsense,” Tagg.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tagg and Fang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “All we need to do is find and kill both Tagg and Fang, and all their soldiers will drop dead.” “True,” Rosetta.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tailed Brindle Grub</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Tailed Brindle Grub is described as finally able to defend itself, kind of like the way a toddler holding a plastic baseball bat is able to defend.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taint</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you make the smoothie, you’ll have immunity from the Taint debuff and something called the Vigorous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Take</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: So if your boyfriend ogling your tootises keeps these easy-peasy bugs coming at us instead of more of those lava-spitting llamas, then you better buck up, get over your human male privilege, and take one for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Take a potion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I had to take a potion.” “Keep your Mute spell ready!” Her new Mute spell would only last a handful of seconds, especially on a boss monster like Asojano, but if we timed it correctly, we could keep him from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Take Down</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The attacks also inflicted Take Down, an effect that was supposed to knock you on your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Take That Shit Off Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Take That Shit Off Spell</b> is a theorized spell capable of stripping away biological armor, meaning it could, in theory, be used to forcibly remove an alien pilot from inside a god's Soul Armor body.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Take That Shit Off Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Take_That_Shit_Off_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taking</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Taking this potion is optional, however it may not be sold or transferred to other Crawlers or.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talent</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>He was killed after firing a lightning bolt too close to the All Tree. He has three scrolls of lightning and almost 70,000 piece of gold on him.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Talent on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Talent</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talent show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: All guests who are participating in either the talent show or the pet contest, please join me backstage for some quick instructions!” Most of the crawlers in the room turned and started shuffling toward the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talisman of the Slate Butterfly</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Talisman of the Slate Butterfly</b> is a small silver butterfly charm on a small silver ring, so it can be attached to collars or other jewelry. It jingles when it moves.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Magic Item Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Talisman of the Slate Butterfly on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Talisman_of_the_Slate_Butterfly</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Talk after they show me!” As they revealed Donut’s selection process, I couldn’t get the image of that Godzilla-sized monster out of my.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talk of the Town Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Talk of the Town Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>gaining significant public attention</b> due to strategic actions. Gold Talk of the Town Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Talk of the Town Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Talk_of_the_Town_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talk show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: An hour and a half earlier I’d been certain I was about to die, and now I was aboard some sort of yacht from another planet, ready to be interviewed on an intergalactic talk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talking cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: He was just starting to gain his confidence back when a guy in his underwear and a talking cat walked in, trounced him, tied him up, and slaughtered the person he was sworn and honor bound to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tall and thin with wide, bug eyes and pale skin, but where the soothers had a big, domed head, this guy’s head had two distinct humps, almost like a drawing of a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tally</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tally</b> is the first <a href="bword://Bopca">Bopca</a> Protector that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> meet inside the Dungeon. Tally agreed to work for <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> to save his own home world from a transformation. He has worked for Borant Corporation for many cycles, and has spent years learning to make Earth food. He makes an <i>awesome</i> <a href="bword://Murgh Vindaloo">Murgh Vindaloo</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Unspecified Bopca Planet</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bopca Protector</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tally on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tally</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talon Strike</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This one was still alive, and I activated Talon Strike and kicked him in the head, finishing him.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Talon Strong</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: This one’s name was Talon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tami</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only remaining, conscious head was that of Tami-Lynn, and I met eyes with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tami-Lynn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tami-Lynn</b> is <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>'s <a href="bword://Father">Father</a>'s second wife and <a href="bword://Asher">Asher</a>'s mom. She has a tattoo of a pitbull on her neck and smokes cigerettes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tami-Lynn on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tami-Lynn</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tangle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Allows access to Iron Tangle employee-only areas and actions on all 400 steam-type trains of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taranis</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The god of thunder. Older brother of <a href="bword://Emberus">Emberus</a> and his twin brother <a href="bword://Hellik">Hellik</a>. Half sister of <a href="bword://Eris">Eris</a> Hellik seeks to kill Emberus and Taranis. The wife of Taranis is the goddess <a href="bword://Apito">Apito</a>. Together, Apito and Taranis held the first Butchers Masquerade in the Halls of Ascendency. They are also the parents of many children including the war god <a href="bword://Grull">Grull</a> and the lust god <a href="bword://Yarilo">Yarilo</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Taranis on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Taranis</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taranis and Apito</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The child of Taranis and Apito, Grull, the god of war, is one of the few trueborn heirs to the Celestial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Target</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Such as “Target any healers within range” or “Target anyone who thinks it’s okay to put mayonnaise on hotdogs.” If added to a powered weapon, such as an explosive-based rocket, targets may be assigned during.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: If I didn’t know better, it sounded like they were talking about Tart the adjutant, who was now back on the 18 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tasks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tasks required to complete: Each path is of equal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Taurin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Standing off to the side, holding a comically large mallet similar to the one the late Brandon had once carried, was the bullheaded.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Co-Warlord Princess Donut is a member of the royal line of succession of another team (Team #4, the Blood Sultanate.) See the succession rules on how this will affect.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Cichociemni</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Li Jun, Li Na, and Chris, along with several others who were not on the Twister , had moved into the town after Louis had bombed the second creeper to death to find several of Eva’s team—a mix of former.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Flamengo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I didn’t know much about his party, called Team Flamengo, but I knew this guy only put his stats into his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Free</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Team Free Love’s van was also bouncing along behind the Dominator.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Free Love</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Team Free Love’s van was also bouncing along behind the Dominator.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Katia</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Team Katia is a group of crawlers centred around <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Team Katia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Team_Katia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Li Na</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>A team of Crawlers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Team Li Na on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Team_Li_Na</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Meadow Lark</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because of the turkey’s proximity to their bubble, all of the remaining Team Meadow Lark members were forced down the stairs, leaving only Imani and Elle in their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team One Fine Pig</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We lost two teams so far, but I’m not sure if...” Team One Fine Pig’s APV rumbled over the finish.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Quan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Team Quiteria’s key has been transferred to Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Quiteria</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Sister Ines is out of the fight!” Team Quiteria has fled the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Retribution</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Team Retribution</b> is the NPC faction recognized as the defending team during the ninth-floor Faction Wars (game), headquartered in the funnel city of Larracos.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Team Retribution on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Team_Retribution</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Roundabout</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You are standing in your Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Sister Ines</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It said Team Sister Ines on it, and it was a blue field with a black and white, embroidered blob on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Team Sparkles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Team Sparkles is described as in the very first garage, and I saw Osvaldo’s slaughter gnu was also.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tears</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tears ran down her cheeks, and they were made of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tech shield</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Stay safe!” I called at Louis before I activated my tech shield and jumped out the open door, followed soon by.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was immediately reminded of Master Splinter the rat sensei from Teenage Mutant Ninja.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teleport Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Teleport</b></i> is "one of the most powerful, most important spells to have in this dungeon." <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> describes a level 15 <a href="bword://Puddle Jumper Spell">Puddle Jumper Spell</a> as "almost as good as <b>Teleport Spell</b>."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Teleport Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Teleport_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teleport to Stairwell Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Teleport to Stairwell Spell</b> is a spell that At level 15, the spell <i>Teleport to Stairwell</i> allows a user to pick a stairwell from a list and teleport there. The list of stairwells show all stairwells on the entire floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Teleport to Stairwell Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Teleport_to_Stairwell_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teleport Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Teleport</b></i> is "one of the most powerful, most important spells to have in this dungeon." <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> describes a level 15 <a href="bword://Puddle Jumper Spell">Puddle Jumper Spell</a> as "almost as good as <b>Teleport Spell</b>."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Teleport Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Teleport_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teleport to Stairwell Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Teleport to Stairwell Spell</b> is a spell that At level 15, the spell <i>Teleport to Stairwell</i> allows a user to pick a stairwell from a list and teleport there. The list of stairwells show all stairwells on the entire floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Teleport to Stairwell Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Teleport_to_Stairwell_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Teleported into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is the trick.” He once again slapped his hands together, and Princess Formidable teleported into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Television show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Since the phrase came from the old Lone Ranger television show, I was inclined to side with Elle on the matter—plus the AI who had named the upgrade in the first place, though if you’d asked me before this, I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Television shows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your benefit came with an enhanced memory that allowed you to remember all those conversations and television shows, and you know how people are supposed to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d dropped just a few moments after I’d spoken.” “Tell me exactly what you were doing at the time when they dropped this girl on the building.” It was only then I realized I’d been clutching onto the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tell Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Tell Donut I said hi!” The gnoll guards surrounded me, saying nothing, grim.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tempest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Never give shit up for free because if you do, they’ll just keep coming back to you and will expect more and more.” “The only lesson I’ve learned today is that my uncle is a colossal prick,” Tempest.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The shortest path is a direct line through the jungle that may require the clearing of foliage and has very uneven ground, and there appears to be a Temple of the Exploding Snake Goddess in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temple Defiler Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Temple Defiler Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> fell through the roof and broke the shrine in temple of <a href="bword://Diwata">Diwata</a> which <a href="bword://Lucia Mar">Lucia Mar</a> had previously desecrated. As a reward he recieved an <a href="bword://Enemy of the Church Tattoo">Enemy of the Church Tattoo</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Temple Defiler Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Temple_Defiler_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temple of Khepri</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: It would, in theory, cause pure chaos, and it would give us the opportunity to keep the halls clear while we made our way to the Temple of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Temporary effect from Grull: All four-legged creatures in your presence, friend or enemy, are temporarily given 2× power to their most powerful.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary effect</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Okay, so, people are going to think this is some sort of bullshit where I saved your ass from this elaborate trap you just walked into, but this is genuinely the temporary effect one receives from being in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary Effect from Khepri</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Temporary Effect from Khepri: You are immortal for the next 90.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary Effect from Yarilo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Temporary Effect from Yarilo: Protection from all venereal.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary Macaroni</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Donut, who’d just received the spell from her Banquet Fit for a Princess book, cast Temporary Macaroni.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Temporary Water Source</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: But there were some additional scrolls tossed in, including some Fill Trench scrolls and Temporary Water Source scrolls, which I’d have to examine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Terminus Station</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was called “Terminus Station.” It took less than twenty minutes to reach the switching station now that I was more familiar with the train’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Terror the Clown</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Once clowns-on-stilts performing at <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus on the third floor, <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s curse transformed the <b>stilt clowns</b> into long-limbed monsters likened to "Slender Man dressed as a clown."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Terror the Clown on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Terror_the_Clown</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Texas</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d temporarily left my dad and run away to her parent’s’ house all the way down in the middle-of-nowhere southern Texas, dragging me.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thank</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: People who don’t care one bit about the emotional abuse they’re heaping onto the one person who is doing everything for them without so much as a “Thank you” or a “Here’s an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thank god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I rolled, and I rolled, and I spun, feeling my skin burn, my bones break, and all I could think was these bombs are much smaller than I thought, thank god.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thank the gods</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It’s going to be impossible for anybody to win.” “I have a whole list of problems I must deal with, warlord, but that, thank the gods, is not one of them,” Cascadia.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thankfully</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thankfully, we had several illusionists with us who were also casting spells, making illusionary doubles of everyone, along with a few illusionary war machine tanks, to focus their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thanks</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thanks to the Enchanted Crown of the Sepsis Whore, which she didn’t even have anymore, Donut wouldn’t be allowed to exit the ninth floor until every other member of the Blood Sultanate royal family was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That was Disgusting Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>That-Was-Disgusting Box!</b> is a type of loot box awarded by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> for achievements that are (presumably) very disgusting. <b>Platinum Tier</b></li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: That was Disgusting Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/That_was_Disgusting_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for attacking a Province Boss. <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives a Platinum version this box for attacking a boss on the Fourth Floor and opens it in a safe room on the Fifth Floor. The contents of the box are not specified.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/That_Wasn%27t_Too_Smart%2C_Was_It%3F_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>That's not how you use arrows, dumbass</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after stabbing <a href="bword://Vrah">Vrah</a> with the <a href="bword://Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea">Arrow of Enthusiastic Double Gonorrhea</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: That's not how you use arrows, dumbass Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/That%27s_not_how_you_use_arrows%2C_dumbass_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That's the Spirit Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>That's the Spirit Box</b> is a Loot Box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Killing family member</li></ul><p class="source">Source: That's the Spirit Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/That%27s_the_Spirit_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: That’d be a lot easier than Gadget.” “It’d be better than this eagle, that’s for sure.” “I’ll be back,” Mordecai said, sounding.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>That’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: That’ll give us time to run up there and block out the door if they’re coming at us from that side.” I turned and sprinted for the other side of the car before they could.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Apothecary</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’d only seen the term “The Apothecary” once.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Bloc</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Bloc</b> is a formal truce and military alliance formed at the start of the Ninth Floor Faction Wars, uniting eight crawler and NPC teams against <a href="bword://Team Retribution">Team Retribution</a> and the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>. It fractures steadily over the course of the war through both desertion and elimination.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: The Bloc on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/The_Bloc</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Blood Sultanate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because she’d put it on, she’d become a royal member of “The Blood Sultanate.” We could only get off the ninth floor if all other members of that family were killed, including the Sultan.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Bone Clan</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And tell us about the condition of your forces.” “The Bone Clan remains stronger than ever, and we are confident we will, once again, stand victorious,” Stalwart.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Bopca</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Pull up the upgrade menu, and show me the available tier one environmental upgrades.” The Bopca, still in a daze, waved her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Borant Corporation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The main program, Dungeon Crawler World: Earth is owned by the Syndicate Government, and this season it is produced by the Mu… The Borant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Butcher’s Masquerade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Here’s a copy and paste of page one of our notes: The Butcher’s Masquerade is a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Ceasefire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: The Ceasefire expires in 60 hours or until.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Democratic Sortion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Eliminated: The Democratic Sortion I stepped from the hospital tent to find the base in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Desperado Club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Under the knife in tall, thin letters, the spinning sign read “The Desperado Club.” And under that, in smaller letters it read “So fun it hurts.” And then “Members only section + public pub.” “I’ve always.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Dream</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: [ 69 ] Remaining Teams: The Princess Posse Team Retribution (castle occupied by War Mage Rebellion forces) The Dream (castle occupied by Team Retribution forces) The Reavers The War Mage.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, I’d been warned by The Dungeon Anarchist’s Cookbook against using the marketplace interface while I had the book on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Epicure</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I know nothing about it except the information from the patch.” The Epicure patch on my jacket protected me from vampires and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Final War</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, The Final War is described as a long, dangerous spell that takes three nights to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Gathering</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Hopefully now you’ll realize all those Magic: The Gathering cards are nothing more than just meaningless pieces of paper, and you should have spent your money on something with actual value, like a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Good Guys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Now let me do this.” Warlord Donut of the Princess Posse has named the truce “The Good Guys.” Warlord Ferdinand of Team Retribution has renamed the truce “Ferdinand and his Bird.” “Hey!” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Grinding</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Hunting Grounds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, The Hunting Grounds is described as a different sort of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Iron Tangle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Iron Tangle!” the announcer called as the show went.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Jugglers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Second Place: The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Ladies Love It</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The largest of the regular pubs on this street was called The Ladies Love It, and it was from within this bar came the loudest music and most.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Maestro</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You wanted to trade places…” The Maestro growled, “You don’t tell me what to do on my own show, meat.” He slammed down on a button on his table, and the two men to my right.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Nest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Quick, shoot a Magic Missile at it!” The Nest remained at 8% health, but if the casting of War Crime went as expected, the whole area around the tower would soon be on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Nest Tower’s Integrity</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: The Nest Tower’s Integrity is at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Nightmare Express</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We should go on the Nightmare line to see where it goes.” She was probably right, but I didn’t like the idea of immediately jumping onto a train called “The Nightmare Express.” I turned to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Nothing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, The Nothing is described as currently draining into The Halls of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Oak Fell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, The Oak Fell is described as Apito’s version of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Open Intellect Action Network</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Captain in the newly coined resistance organization, The Open Intellect Action.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Operatic Collective</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: The Bone Clan has announced a formal truce with The Operatic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Other Side</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her documentary, The Other Side of the Glass , had resonated, but like with all things that were hard to look at, people clicked their tongues in sympathy and then immediately looked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Pineapple Cabaret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Pineapple Cabaret has more than enough hands to protect.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Plenty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Plenty invented the modern tunneling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Princess Posse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your team’s official name is The Princess Posse, sponsored by The Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels (TSECS), NFC, DBA as Princess Posse Enterprises.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Reavers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, The Reavers is described as the ones with the flying machines usually, so we gotta be on the lookout,” Boomer said.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>the Red God Glows</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Where the Red God.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Screamer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The primary name was “The Screamer.” I read the short description: The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Semeru Dwarves</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: 64 &lt;Note added by Crawler Allister, 13th Edition&gt; The Semeru Dwarves of the ninth floor all only worship a single goddess, who inevitably makes an appearance near the end of the battles as the final teams.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Shivered Sky Series</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>the Sidewalk Ends</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Where the Sidewalk.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Silk Road</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Of the lit signs, they were Bitches , Penis Parade , Jobs , The Silk Road , and Restrooms.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>the Skull Empire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: When the Skull Empire had accidentally killed Manasa—who had really been controlled by a brain parasite from the Valtay Corporation—in their attempt to assassinate us, D’nadia had been very.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Sledge</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: One was named Bomo, and the other was “The Sledge.” They seemed about as intelligent as one would expect from someone named Bomo and The Sledge, but the 7-foot-tall, tuxedoed monsters followed us at a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Society</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read, “The Society for the Eradication of Cocker Spaniels ” in Syndicate Standard, but the word “Cocker Spaniels” were censored with a transparent line through.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>the Syndicate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: So the Syndicate places a heavy premium on such.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The System</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I looked at him and said, “The System AI is totally going to hell.” “Okay,” the kid replied, not appreciating my lame attempt at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Thief</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her mystic card, The Thief , which she directed at the ogre, who was in the process of summoning a card of her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Toe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Let’s get into a saferoom and get some food and listen to what they gotta say.” We walked toward Mordecai’s chosen inn, a bar called The.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Trauco Triplets</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: 4 The Trauco Triplets have been eliminated due to the expiration of their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Valtay</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The Prism Kingdom officially condemns this action by the Skull Empire, and we will be filing an immediate grievance with the Syndicate Court.” “The Valtay and the Skull Empire are strong allies,” Tucker.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Valtay Corporation</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read The Valtay Corporation, Keeping the Best of You.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The War Mage Rebellion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The War Mage Rebellion be given full access to the Conscription spell and all its benefits.” This request has been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>The Wild Hunt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of three from team The Wild.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Theia</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Theia</b> is a light based deity that is a god of something. She is <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>'s mother, whom she has a complicated relationship with. Shows up to kill <a href="bword://Slit">Slit</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Rectrix (sponsor)</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Theia on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Theia</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Themed greeting cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Theobold</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tina kept wandering off, so Donut had to go out there and keep her in line, much to the dismay of the elf servant guy, whose name was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Therefore</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Therefore, she currently had a pool of 49 mana points at her disposal, so she could raise four of the rabbit monsters, take a potion and raise four more, which was a pretty formidable.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>There’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’d been dozens of corpses, all with different demon names over them, meaning there were now dozens of full-power Sheol demons suddenly unleashed into.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>There’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’ll probably be a meadow involved, and she’ll be wearing a ring of flowers in her hair, and there’ll be candles and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>There’re</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: There’re a few different kinds, but I’m talking about those snotty little pricklets who defend their precious corner of fandom from “outsiders” like they’re the Redcoats at Rorke’s Drift, completely oblivious.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>They like me! They really like me! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>They like me! They really like me! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fourth Floor for gaining 500 trillion followers.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: They like me! They really like me! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/They_like_me%21_They_really_like_me%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>They’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d mostly come to this panel not because they wanted to get a mind-numbing history lesson, but because they knew Circe and I would be in the same room together, and they wanted to see what was going to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>They’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If the AI is going to allow the eight sponsors plus the crawler team to bolster their numbers so much, what’s it doing for the NPCs?” “They’ll be our allies,” Tipid said, leaning over the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>They’re</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’re doing a cash grab with the new patron system, but they don’t get that money, or any money, from the advertising or the tourism funds or elite monster sponsorship or anything else until the season is.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>They’ve</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’ve been working together for some time, and they have raced already in two heats, coming in first and second.” “The time we came in second doesn’t count,” the Grulke said, crossing her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Thick clouds covered most of the area, and I didn’t have time to marvel at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Things</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Things like “You’re the Boss Now” and “Your own hours” and “It’s not a pyramid!” and “Safe!*” Inside the pamphlet was a wall of text of mostly gibberish about the benefits of becoming a “Rev-Up Moonshine.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Think</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Miss Beatrice didn’t call me Princess Money Bags for nothing.” “I’m pretty sure it was ‘Princess Money Pit.’ She called you that because we spent more on you than we did for our rent.” “Well, obviously that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Third Place</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Third Place: Lady Dominators and the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thirty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: What do you think?” “Are you asking me to lie, Carl?” “Yes, I am,” Thirty minutes later, I sat on the couch in my ridiculous makeshift loincloth as we watched our last moments on the third floor play out on.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>This Little Piggy Made a Boom Boom! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>This Little Piggy Made a Boom Boom!</b> is an exclusive achievement that the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> awards to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Sixth Floor. The name might be related to the First Floor <a href="bword://This Little Piggy Went to Market! Achievement">This Little Piggy Went to Market! Achievement</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: This Little Piggy Made a Boom Boom! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/This_Little_Piggy_Made_a_Boom_Boom%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>This Little Piggy Went to Market! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>This Little Piggy Went to Market!</b> is the logical follow-up to the <a href="bword://Podophilia! Achievement">Podophilia! Achievement</a> and Smush Skill. It is most likely a unique achievement only awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>. It's also the first time that the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> refers to itself as "Daddy."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: This Little Piggy Went to Market! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/This_Little_Piggy_Went_to_Market%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>This Pet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, This Pet is described as Suffering from Not Just Rabies, but.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thorny Devil</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: I pointed at the ridge to the right of us, back where we’d encountered the Thorny Devil, now a good 300 feet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Though</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: My first thought was “space marines.” Though not all of them had human-like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thousand Lights</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: (They, of course, were fined so much money that the habitat was eventually repossessed and the vast majority of the adults were forced to indenture to pay off the debt, but this part is usually left out of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thousands</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: What an absurd threat.” “How many other skull empire orcs are there on the floor?” “Thousands,” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Threaten to kill his family</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Three Cheers for Slaughter Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Three Cheers for Slaughter Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing three neighborhood bosses with his <a href="bword://Protective Shell Spell">Protective Shell Spell</a> on the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Three Cheers for Slaughter Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Three_Cheers_for_Slaughter_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Throne Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Throne Room of the Bone Clan is being occupied by forces from Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Thwap</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: We is sick of it, we is!”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tiara of a Thousand Lights</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Tiara of a Thousand Lights</b> is a unique item created for <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> by her sponsor <a href="bword://Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions">Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions</a> and given to her in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> <a href="bword://Benefactor Box">Benefactor Box</a>. It is an upgradable tiara, with 99 slots to upgrade.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Benefactor Box from Long Haul Biological Waste Management Solutions to Donut</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tiara of a Thousand Lights on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tiara_of_a_Thousand_Lights</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tiddy Twister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Tiddy Twister is described as my van,” Louis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tier attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: One of the few races who managed to not get fully wiped out by Scolopendra’s nine-tier attack, the bugbears are now one of the most common races one might find in the Kapok district of the Hunting Grounds.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tigran</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tigran</b> is an orange, furry humanoid race. race a crawler can choose upon entry to the Third Floor. Tigrans are bipedal humanoid / big cat hybrid, covered in lots of fur. The fur color is a very vibrant orange. Tigrans have a long shaggy tail.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tigran on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tigran</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tigrans</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m sure there are dweeb Tigrans and pacifist Tigrans and vegan Tigrans and Tigrans who have fallen prey to some random MLM and irritate all their extended family members and friends from high school with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tiled floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Heyzoos flexed, and the cross shattered into holographic dust as the barefooted man landed on the tiled floor of the mall, his white robe flapping in a wind that wasn’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Time is described as scarce.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time Extend</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Two of them were Time Extend cards, which doubled the amount of time a summoned totem would last, and the third was a Stout card, which added 25% to a mob’s health for the duration of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time Extends</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was the two Time Extends , the Combo card, and the Stout.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time Limit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Time Limit: This must be completed before the collapse of the 11 th floor, and as a practical matter, you should complete it before you complete the Kill Hellik.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time to Level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time to Level Collapse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time Until Level Collapse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Time Until Level Collapse: 6 days, 22.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Time Until the Hunt Begins</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: 9 Time Until the Hunt Begins: 3.5.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tin</b> is a former Crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> (21st Edition)</i>. &lt;Note added by Crawler Tin, 21st Edition&gt;</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 32</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tin on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tin</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tin Man</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Carl, what does that Tin Man thing mean?” “I have no idea,” I said as I pulled another.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tina</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her father was a cleric who only saw young Tina for what she was supposed to be, and her mother, while fiercely protective of the child, only saw herself in those big, brown eyes that were always tearing up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tina quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d floated the idea to use the talent contest to finish out the Big Tina quest, but they’d immediately rejected my application because it required the main part of the act to be a crawler or a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tinky Winky</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, I’m glad Mongo ate Tinky.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tiny room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was amazing how much one could accomplish while sitting in a tiny room with wideband tunnel.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tipid</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tipid</b> is a veteran former crawler who authored the fourth edition of the <a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a>. He returns to the Dungeon during the Ninth Floor's Faction Wars as a Level 60 mercenary, serving as a Colonel and key tactician for the <a href="bword://Princess Posse">Princess Posse</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Crest System</li><li><b>Race:</b> Crest</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Epilogue</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tipid on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tipid</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tipid and Rosetta</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The assault part of this plan had not come from me, but was one of many contingencies that Tipid and Rosetta had brought with them from the outside, which was part of the reason why we’d been able to put this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tish</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I won it in the breakup.” She rolled over to the chandelier she had borrowed from the room next to Tish’s room when she had done that thing she wasn’t going to talk about because everyone would get.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Titan Conglomerate</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Titan Conglomerate</b> is the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> production company primarily responsible for <i>Dungeon Crawler After Hours with <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a></i> and <i>Planet Beautiful</i>, as well as other popular programs.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Titan Entertainment</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Titan Conglomerate on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Titan_Conglomerate</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tito</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tito</b> is a croupier for the Wheel of Fortune game at the Casino in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. One of the spots on the game has an option for “Get stabbed in the stomach by the croupier.” Tito is the croupier.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 9</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tito on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tito</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tizquick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tizquick</b> is a <a href="bword://Dwarf">Dwarf</a> train conductor of the <i>Mango Line</i> on the Fourth Floor Iron Tangle. He was given false memories of a daughter that never existed.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> NPC</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dwarf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tizquick on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tizquick</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tizquick the dwarf</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Back at the front of the line, Elle’s away team, with the help of Tizquick the dwarf, found the correct colored line, making their way to the exploded station.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toad Cudgel</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Toad Cudgel</b> is a wooden stick about 3/4 the length of a baseball bat. The toad cudgel is the first unenchanted weapon <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives after entering the Dungeon; it comes in a Bronze <a href="bword://Weapon Box">Weapon Box</a> awarded for the <a href="bword://Unarmed Combat Achievement">Unarmed Combat Achievement</a>. Carl attempts to use it in combat exactly one time before putting it back in his inventory.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Bronze Weapon Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Toad Cudgel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Toad_Cudgel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toby Dinniman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Cover Illustration by Luciano Fleitas https://www.artstation.com/lucianofleitas Cover Design by Toby Dinniman Interior Map and Art by Erik Wilson.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Today</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Today is described as the day I’m going to slaughter a child.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toe</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: At this moment, he was sitting inside the Toe, drinking blood wine, telling the second-shift bartender about the group of grulke toad soldiers he’d seen out in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toe ring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: However, because of your brilliantly timed request, the entity controlling Carl could—and indeed did—accept the invitation to parlay.” The screen showed the teleport request getting accepted the moment my toe.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toes spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Twinkle Toes spell, which made it so Mongo could run fast while she was mounted would probably just end up killing her at level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Together</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Together, these guys were the number five crawler in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tom</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tom</b> is a boss on the Eighth Floor.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Turkey</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tom on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tom</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tommy gun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately, the horn wasn’t a normal honk, but a recording of a gangster’s voice saying, Make way for the big shot , followed by a chicken squawk and the rat-a-tat shooting sound of a tommy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tomorrow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tomorrow we were to attempt to hop over to a line that didn’t intersect yellow and then get the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ton</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The cretin and the lava rock crawler had formed a strange friendship since we’d assigned Clay-Ton to be Chris’s bodyguard on the Hunting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tonight</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tonight I will drink to Alevtina and dreams not attained, and I will drink to the friends of today.” He jumped out the back, landing heavily on the trailer, and moved into his destroyed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tony the Tiger</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: That would be okay, too, even if he looks like a generic brand Tony the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Took</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Top Level: 89 Dungeon Exit: Took deal at the beginning of the 11 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Top Level</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Top Level: 89 Dungeon Exit: Took deal at the beginning of the 11 th.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toraline</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Toraline Root Vegetable</b> is essentially a sweet potato with alchemical uses. Toraline looks a like a sweet potato and only grows in volcanic soil. The <a href="bword://Inventory">Inventory</a> flags it as as "Very Rare," but its sell value is low. It can be used to create special ink for scrolls or for various lava-themed potions, including a Phase Lava Rock <a href="bword://Potion">Potion</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Benefactor Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Toraline on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Toraline</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Torch</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Torches</b> are single-use utility items frequently and abundantly received in <a href="bword://Loot Boxes">Loot Boxes</a> on the First Floor. They look like short, tapered sticks, approximately the diameter of a cork.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Torch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Torch_%28Item%29</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Torch Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i>This article is about the spell. For the item, see <a href="bword://Torch">Torch</a> (Item).</i> <b>Torch</b> is a utility spell that creates an orb of flaming light that follows the crawler around. It works underwater.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Torch Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Torch_Spell</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Torch Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i>This article is about the spell. For the item, see <a href="bword://Torch">Torch</a> (Item).</i> <b>Torch</b> is a utility spell that creates an orb of flaming light that follows the crawler around. It works underwater.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Torch Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Torch_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Torn Book Page</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Torn Book Page</b> is an item on the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Torn Book Page on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Torn_Book_Page</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Total, Utter Failure Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Total, Utter Failure Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for failing the quest Free <a href="bword://Wynne">Wynne</a> from His Bondage Quest.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Total, Utter Failure Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Total%2C_Utter_Failure_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Totem card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If the totem card timed out and returned to the opponent’s deck, they could resummon the monster once they pulled it again, but the 90% time reduction would persist through.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Totem cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Starting at the end of this message, all saferoom locations will also include a practice arena where you may, for a fee, enter and summon your totem cards in a limited mock battle.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Touched by a Demon Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Touched by a Demon Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for being touched by the demon lord <a href="bword://Amayon">Amayon</a> during the Eighth Floor. Carl opens this achievement in a safe room on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Touched by a Demon Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Touched_by_a_Demon_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Town Hall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’d also received the quest to find out what was in the Town Hall and had been discussing what to do about it when we’d.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Town Upgrade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The quest box from the Tina quest was the same item Donut received, which was a coupon for a free Town.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toy Story</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The dromedarian children were in the middle of watching Toy Story 3 when we entered, and they insisted on finishing it before they.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toyota Tundra</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Then three vehicles appeared in the room: an ice-cream truck, one of those trucks with stairs from the airport, and a battered Toyota.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Toyotomi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Toyotomi: race/species: Sai; occupation: Guard at <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a> (formerly)Mercenary During Faction Wars; first scene: Book 7, Chapter 28.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Sai</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 28</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Toyotomi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Toyotomi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Track</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Track sections on the circular platform were designed to line up with these service bays, allowing trains to proceed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tracked All-Terrain Suicide Machine. The Royal Chariot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tracked All-Terrain Suicide Machine. The Royal Chariot</b> is a vehicle constructed by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Fifth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tracked All-Terrain Suicide Machine. The Royal Chariot on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tracked_All-Terrain_Suicide_Machine._The_Royal_Chariot</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tracksuit Troika</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You always think of something.” Elle: Those three idiots in the Tracksuit Troika said they were taking a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Traditionally</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Traditionally, once the fighting started, enemy teams weren’t allowed to enter the city, but the city itself wasn’t protected, and wayward spells, artillery fire, and rampaging gods usually did a decent job of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trailblazing Crazy Cat Lady Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><i>Trailblazing</i> Crazy Cat Lady</b> is a unique achievement awarded to the <i>first</i> crawler to enter the Dungeon with a cat.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Trailblazing Crazy Cat Lady Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Trailblazing_Crazy_Cat_Lady_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trailer Park</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trailer Park Fairy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trailer Park Fairy Tales</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: ALSO BY MATT DINNIMAN Operation Bounce House (Scifi!) Kaiju: Battlefield Surgeon (Horror LitRPG!) Dominion of Blades Series (LitRPG!) The Shivered Sky Series (Angels vs demons!) The Grinding (A horror novel!).</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Train car</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The photograph the blob kid was getting autographed was of the Popov brothers swinging their meteor hammer while inside an about-to-derail train car as flaming hedgehog things attacked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Train cars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Multiple tracks led off to a small-scale yard, consisting of a few dozen flatbed train cars designed to be occupied by no more than a handful of riders at a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Train Conductor's Souvenir Hat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>All crawlers receive the Train Conductor's Souvenir Hat upon entry to the Fourth Floor from the <a href="bword://I&#x27;m on a train Achievement">I'm on a train Achievement</a>. A simple, non-magical, blue and white hat with the words “I rode the Iron Tangle” embroidered on it.”</li><li><b>Source:</b> I'm on a train Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Train Conductor's Souvenir Hat on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Train_Conductor%27s_Souvenir_Hat</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Training guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We don’t know what will be useful and when, so if it’s not bolted to the ground, it’s going into the bag.” Our next stop was a nearby training.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Training guilds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was sandwiched by a pair of training guilds—one for longbow and one called “Druids only.” Next to the druid guild was an Arby’s, which would be the saferoom, and then another store specializing in clothes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Training Room</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Training Room</b> is a Personal Space upgrade that allows crawlers to train specific skills and spells. At level 1, it allows for one hour a day on a specific non-magical skill. It won't train anything explosives based until level 2 which has a Bomber's Studio.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Market</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Training Room on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Training_Room</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tran</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tran</b> is a human Crawler from Earth and a Swashbuckler who fought with Gwen's team in the land quadrant of the Fifth Floor. Tran is quiet, dry, and willing to volunteer for dangerous work, joking that he should put on a red shirt before the dive to the Akula and then asking himself aloud why he had volunteered. He defers to his own team's leadership, checking with Gwen before acting on instructions from a crawler he had only just met.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth (Da Nang, Vietnam)</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 20</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tran on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tran</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tran and Bautista</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All the candy is from Vietnam, too, and I don’t know what’s good, but Tran and Bautista helped pick some for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tran and Vadim</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Katia and I appeared first, teamed up together with Tran and Vadim individually on the other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tran and Zhang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Tran and Zhang had chased off all the NPCs and set up a few tables, and it was turning into a large party, even for those who didn’t want to participate in the Cabaret escape.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Transferring</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: And come back tomorrow night for a special episode!” Transferring in twenty.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trap door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was an advanced alarm trap and a trap door trap in front of both the surgical theater and the throne.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trap Maker’s Workshop</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Trap Maker’s Workshop is described as just a table while the Bomb Maker’s Workshop was an entire.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trap skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Trap skill is described as so high, she could turn them off and on from afar, controlling them like gate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trapmaster Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Trapmaster Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>expert use of traps in battle</b>. <a href="bword://Insurgent Achievement">Insurgent Achievement</a> <b>Not yet fully documented</b>, but <b><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received multiple Phase Through Wall scrolls</b>, possibly related​</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Trapmaster Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Trapmaster_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Traps skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: As we moved to a proper spot to go down a floor, my Find Traps skill activated, and sure enough, sitting right in the middle of the hallway was a second gate, but it was a freeze.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Travel the Path</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This skill, combined with Travel the Path , would allow the adherent to follow the bijou fairy’s light tendril path directly to the strongest connected temple, which was invariably inside of Club.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Traveling Circus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Before the cataclysm, if you asked any child of the sprawling Over City what their favorite activity was, a good number of them would happily tell you of the great and wonderful Grimaldi’s Traveling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Travis Priest</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Travis Priest</b> is a strong and addictive painkiller.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Travis Priest on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Travis_Priest</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Treasure Map</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Treasure Map</b> is a persistent item dropped by a slain Country Boss, meaning that any crawler can loot it from the boss's corpse. It shows the location and quality of all magical equipment in the area.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Country Boss</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Treasure Map on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Treasure_Map</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trench scrolls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Build Trench scrolls, despite being handed out like candy to us crawlers during the last floor, were rarely used by the Faction Wars participants because they were expensive, and as such, possible exploits.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trillion Favorites</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Views: 1.4 Quadrillion Followers: 30 Trillion Favorites: 6.2 Trillion The first thing I did as I settled into Mordecai’s chamber was collapse into one of the classroom.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trillion Followers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: 41 Time to Level Collapse: 4 Days, 6 hours Views: 1.4 Trillion Followers: 352.2 Billion Favorites: 21.4 Billion I still had plenty of moonshine left, and Donut and I spent the hour torching all the pupae in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trillion Leaderboard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: 14 Time to Level Collapse: 6 days, 23 hours Views: 123.8 Quadrillion Followers: 1.5 Quadrillion Favorites: 372.4 Trillion Leaderboard rank: 6 Bounty: 100,000 gold The Nightmare Express Landing - Station 283 I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tripper</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tripper</b> is a spell that triggers all passage-, motion-, heat-, and weight-triggered traps in a Crawler's radius. At level 15, the radius is massive.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tripper on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tripper</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tripper spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Tripper spell is described as something I’d seen a few times during class selection, but at level 15, it had a very wide.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troglodytes</b> are humanoid, unintelligent mobs. On the First Floor, they occupy the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Troglodytes are muscular, humanoid mobs dressed in loincloths. Their head and torsos resemble Komodo dragons, but their scaly legs are long and bent, almost kangaroo-like. Their attacks may inflict a devastatingly painful Poison Debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troglodyte on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troglodyte</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte Basher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: After killing a Troglodyte Basher, all that was left from one of Donut’s missile strikes was the lizard’s head, rolling about like a lumpy soccer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte Bashers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The level threes were called Troglodyte Bashers and the level fours were Troglodyte.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte Pygmy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troglodytes</b> are humanoid, unintelligent mobs. On the First Floor, they occupy the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Troglodytes are muscular, humanoid mobs dressed in loincloths. Their head and torsos resemble Komodo dragons, but their scaly legs are long and bent, almost kangaroo-like. Their attacks may inflict a devastatingly painful Poison Debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troglodyte Pygmy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troglodyte_Pygmy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troglodytes</b> are humanoid, unintelligent mobs. On the First Floor, they occupy the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Troglodytes are muscular, humanoid mobs dressed in loincloths. Their head and torsos resemble Komodo dragons, but their scaly legs are long and bent, almost kangaroo-like. Their attacks may inflict a devastatingly painful Poison Debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troglodyte on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troglodyte</p></div></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troglodytes</b> are humanoid, unintelligent mobs. On the First Floor, they occupy the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Troglodytes are muscular, humanoid mobs dressed in loincloths. Their head and torsos resemble Komodo dragons, but their scaly legs are long and bent, almost kangaroo-like. Their attacks may inflict a devastatingly painful Poison Debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troglodyte on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troglodyte</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troglodyte Pygmy</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troglodytes</b> are humanoid, unintelligent mobs. On the First Floor, they occupy the <a href="bword://Juicer">Juicer</a>'s neighborhood. Troglodytes are muscular, humanoid mobs dressed in loincloths. Their head and torsos resemble Komodo dragons, but their scaly legs are long and bent, almost kangaroo-like. Their attacks may inflict a devastatingly painful Poison Debuff.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troglodyte Pygmy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troglodyte_Pygmy</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troll Smoke Mantel</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Troll Smoke Mantel</b> is a smoke bomb, costs 30,000 gold for a set of twelve.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Troll Smoke Mantel on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Troll_Smoke_Mantel</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troll Smoke Mantle</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The market on the Desperado Club’s middle floor had a booth that sold something called a Troll Smoke Mantle, which was a step up from the smoke curtains that Pustule the hobgoblin sold on the top.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trollskin Shirt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The best prize was a Scroll of Upgrade, which had been good to me so far, but when I applied it, all it did was add +3 dexterity to my Trollskin.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trophy Count</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trophy Count: 34 Remaining Hunters: 1013 The number of hunters in the dungeon appeared on the screen, just below the remaining crawler counter, which had lowered to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Troy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I did ask them to make Kevin and Magnificent Troy come and do commentary, but it looks like that Troy guy is currently in an alcohol-fueled state of unconsciousness, and I promised I wouldn’t poke at those.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Truck Driver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: It didn’t change my Monster Truck Driver class name, but I could pick one of three “endorsements.” They only give you ten minutes to pick, and you can’t use chat while you’re in the little.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Trust</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Trust me.” “Are you sure?” she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Try</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: I forgot what the prize box said it was.” “You’re judging a kids’ art contest.” “Are you fucking kidding me?” “Try not to swear for this one.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tsarina Signet</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tsarina Signet</b> is a level 60 Elite NPC and the star of the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> program <i>Vengeance of the Daughter</i>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> first encounter her on the Third Floor near <a href="bword://Grimaldi">Grimaldi</a>'s Traveling Circus.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Signet the Bastard</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Hunting Grounds, Liana Sector</li><li><b>Race:</b> Half-Naiad, half-High Elf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tsarina Signet on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tsarina_Signet</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tserendolgor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tserendolgor</b> ("Ren") is a crawler from Earth. Despite <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s constant harassment due to her dog-themed race, Ren becomes a close ally of <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> and the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> after fighting beside them on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Ren</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Mongolia</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Dog Soldier</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 31</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tserendolgor on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tserendolgor</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tserendolgor Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Tserendolgor Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for the <a href="bword://Chickenshit Exploit Achievement">Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</a>. Both <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive a Platinum version of this box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tserendolgor Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tserendolgor_Box</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tserendolgor Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Tserendolgor Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for the <a href="bword://Chickenshit Exploit Achievement">Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</a>. Both <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive a Platinum version of this box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Chickenshit Exploit Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tserendolgor Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tserendolgor_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tucker</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tucker</b> is one of the guests on Danger Zone with <a href="bword://Ripper Wonton">Ripper Wonton</a>. A stuffy looking middle-aged human with a stupid grin.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 2 Chapter 19</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tucker on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tucker</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tuesday Two For One</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Tuesday Two For One</b> is a heavily pierced goblin female stripper in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Goblin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tuesday Two For One on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tuesday_Two_For_One</p></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tummy Acher</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Tummy Acher</b> is a type of pet-class mobs. <a href="bword://Garret">Garret</a> is a Tummy Acher owned by <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a>. Tummy Achers are known for their abilities to be excellent melee tanks.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tummy Acher on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tummy_Acher</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Garret</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Garret</b> is a <a href="bword://Tummy Acher">Tummy Acher</a> pet that originally belonged to the crawler <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a> (nicknamed "Ren" by her fellow crawlers, since nobody could pronounce her name), who received him as a gift for attending Crawl Con.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Rend, Garret, Bam-Bam, Cousin Oliver, Tummy Acher, Belly Acher</li><li><b>Origin:</b> The World Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Tummy Acher / Belly Acher</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Garret on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Garret</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tundra Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Elle had transformed from a Blizzardmancer to a Tundra Princess, but the fairy woman didn’t look any.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tunnel system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If this whole thing is a metaphor like you say, about how bad the Krakaren monsters really are for the universe, about how they’re using the tunnel system to spread their influence or whatever, then I’m.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tunneling system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Those guys, the Plenty, were the ones who invented the tunneling system, the ones who were supposedly working with our new sponsor, the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Turkey</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Turkey</b> are a race of mob on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Turkey on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Turkey</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Turn</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Turn off the portal, but get ready to flip it back on.” She nodded and rose into the air on stilt-like legs to reach the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Turn Undead Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Turn Undead Spell</b> is a bard spell that turns undead. The "Turn Undead" spell is part of the NecroBard Class at level 3.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Turn Undead Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Turn_Undead_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Turns</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Turns out if you use the same scroll more than 200 times, you learn the spell.” I went to the new tab and wrote, “Does this work?” The magical pen rose into the air as the top popped off the ink.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tuskling</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>In the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, <b>Tusklings</b> are pig-like orcs and the ruling class of the <a href="bword://Orcish Supremacy">Orcish Supremacy</a>. They appear in the Dungeon as part of the <a href="bword://Ball of Swine">Ball of Swine</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tuskling on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tuskling</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tutorial Guild</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: It read “Tutorial Guild.” The words were in that same, odd.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tutorial guilds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A couple of rooms with white dots appeared, and upon closer inspection, they were all tutorial guilds, all with Mordecai.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twelfth floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I will make certain you survive until the twelfth floor, where you and Katia and the cat will help me claim the Celestial Throne once and for all There were actually two ways out of the Sepsis Whore.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twenty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Twenty-two crawlers plus all the mercenaries shoved into the same vehicle with the Apito memorial crystal around Prepotente’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twin</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Twin antennae spread from her head with a span over at least six feet, as wide as the crab.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twinkle Toes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A cheap spell that makes a minion run really fast for as many seconds as the caster's intelligence level. Obtained by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in a platinum quest box on Floor 5.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Twinkle Toes on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Twinkle_Toes</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twinkle Toes spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Twinkle Toes spell, which made it so Mongo could run fast while she was mounted would probably just end up killing her at level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twister</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The original Twister, is the roofless Chevy Astrovan driven by <a href="bword://Louis">Louis</a>, which became famous when he accidentally drove it into one of the first dungeon stairwells during the collapse.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Earth</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Twister on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Twister</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twitter Instagram</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Twitter Instagram OceanofPDF</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: (Or his art !) Facebook Twitter Instagram.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Two Chicks at the Same Time Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Two Chicks at the Same Time</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers who use one mob's body to beat another's, and kills both mobs in the process.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Two Chicks at the Same Time Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Two_Chicks_at_the_Same_Time_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tyler Storm</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>One of the crawlers in the Subterranean Quadrant of Bubble #543.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 6, Book 4</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tyler Storm on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tyler_Storm</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Type</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Type: Involuntary Abjuration Cost: Item Based Duration: Instantaneous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Type of creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: A “Mace and bashing weapons guild.” And a small, general store called “Limp Richard’s Sundries.” The proprietor of the general store, Limp Richard, was a type of creature I’d never seen.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Type of monster</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The moment we approached the cages, each filled with a different type of monster, two achievements popped.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Tyrant Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Tyrant Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for <b>killing a mayor</b> in the dungeon.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Killing a mayor of a settlement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Tyrant Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Tyrant_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ugly Ass Backpack With a Completely Useless Design that Only an Idiot Would Wear</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> crafts the <b>Ugly Ass Backpack With a Completely Useless Design that Only an Idiot Would Wear</b> for <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> at his <a href="bword://Armorer&#x27;s Workshop">Armorer's Workshop</a> during the Fourth Floor. The idea is to take advantage of her <a href="bword://Doppelgänger">Doppelgänger</a> race's ability to assimilate and shape equipped gear by making an equippable item that can store variable amounts of mass.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Carl Armorer's Workshop</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ugly Ass Backpack With a Completely Useless Design that Only an Idiot Would Wear on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ugly_Ass_Backpack_With_a_Completely_Useless_Design_that_Only_an_Idiot_Would_Wear</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uh oh. It talks. Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Uh oh. It talks. Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for discovering a Sapient weapon, Spunky Jeffery the Enchanted Nickel Sock of the Elderly Miser and giving it to <a href="bword://Dong Quixote">Dong Quixote</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uh oh. It talks. Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uh_oh._It_talks._Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ukrainian</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you had asked me, I could have told you this earlier.” We all just looked at the Ukrainian barbarian woman like she’d just sprouted a hamster from her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ultima Corp</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ultima Corp DungeonWerx Municipal, Multi-Destination Doorway-based Subspace.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ultimate Extreme Power Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Ultimate Extreme Power Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for killing <a href="bword://Miss Quill">Miss Quill</a> and gained control of a Medium <a href="bword://Skyfowl">Skyfowl</a> Settlement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ultimate Extreme Power Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ultimate_Extreme_Power_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ultra-Stabilized Size-Adjustable Race-Adjustable Alleviating Sleep Apparatus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Ultra-Stabilized, Size-Adjustable, Race-Adjustable Alleviating Sleep Apparatus</b> is a Tier-1 Personal Space Bed Upgrade available on the Fourth Floor.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Market</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ultra-Stabilized Size-Adjustable Race-Adjustable Alleviating Sleep Apparatus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ultra-Stabilized_Size-Adjustable_Race-Adjustable_Alleviating_Sleep_Apparatus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unaffiliated Hunter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Merchant This is an Unaffiliated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unarmed Combat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The only thing that helped was that my Pugilism, Unarmed Combat, Foot Soldier, and Smush skills were all level 5 now.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unarmed Combat Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Unarmed Combat</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers who enter the First Floor without a weapon. This is the sixth achievement <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives after entering the Dungeon wearing only boxers and a leather jacket.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unarmed Combat Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unarmed_Combat_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unarmed Combat Skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: +1 to the Bomb Surgeon Skill +1 to the Trap Engineer Skill +1 to Unarmed Combat Skill -25% Damage when using bladed weapons +25% Mana cost for damage-dealing spells +5% skill progression speed in all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unbirthing Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Unbirthing Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for teleporting into <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a>'s neck hole on the Eighth Floor. The <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> is a big fan of this achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unbirthing Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unbirthing_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uncle Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Uncle Carl is described as on television.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uncle Morty’s Insta Lawn-Kill and Undead Repellant, Extra-Strength Edition</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Uncle Morty’s Insta Lawn-Kill and Undead Repellant, Extra-Strength Edition</b> is a potion made by <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uncle Morty’s Insta Lawn-Kill and Undead Repellant, Extra-Strength Edition on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uncle_Morty%E2%80%99s_Insta_Lawn-Kill_and_Undead_Repellant%2C_Extra-Strength_Edition</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uncle Quasar</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because if you are nice to the wrong person, they’ll either take advantage of you, or worse, they might never leave.” “That seems like terrible advice, Uncle Quasar.” “No, telling someone to sign a contract in.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unconscious</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: His health was down to 2% and he was Unconscious with a one-minute timer over his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uncooked Llama steaks</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Uncooked Llama steaks</b> is a food item that are received as a loot drop after killing Bad Llamas. The system AI automatically adds these to the Food Items tab when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> adds them to his inventory.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uncooked Llama steaks on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uncooked_Llama_steaks</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Undead creature</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I’m resistant to ice attacks, and it still worked on me.” “That’s because it’s not a regular ice attack when it’s from an undead creature,” Mordecai said, walking into the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Under Orders</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Harpocrates had Under Orders over his head, and he was rank Legionnaire in the White Dragons.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Underneath</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The line under the shop name read, “Shop opens after the third heat.” Underneath that, it was a crude.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unfortunately</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately for her, her first escape attempt from the Nothing resulted in a split, and half of her essence was forced to take refuge in the closest unoccupied naiad vessel she could find, which happened to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unique</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Good to know.” I knew there were seven levels of rarity for the totem cards: Common, Uncommon, Rare, Very Rare, Legendary, Mythic, and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unique item</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Enchanted Repeating Crossbow of the Scavenger Mother of Mothers This is a unique.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>United States</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, United States is described as broken up into regions, but the map lines were.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>United States of America</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We have seen the specials about what happened to children left in India for obvious reasons, but your country, the United States of America, was on the other side of the planet.” I had no idea what was obvious.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unk Tehi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Unk Tehi</b> is a deity of unrecorded domain. Enemies calling themselves Unk Tehi Temple Guards appear as level 85 <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> opponents: large lizard-headed humanoids wearing turkey-feather headdresses and carrying bows, arrows, or spears, announced with black-and-yellow confetti and a "these lizards" tagline.</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Bedlam Bride</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unk Tehi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unk_Tehi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unless</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unless you’re a complete psychopath, we know you probably didn’t wake up this morning and tell yourself, “Today is the day I’m going to slaughter a child.” Well let us put your mind at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unlike</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unlike a succubus or incubus, who are inordinately obsessed with sex, an Ifrit is more attuned to the aura of lust and sin and envy and greed and all those other nasty iniquities that spill over the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unsinkable Sam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: It was like the Unsinkable Sam cat tale I’d just told.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unvaccinated Clurichaun Rev-Up Consultant</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b><a href="bword://Clurichaun">Clurichaun</a></b> are a fairy-class mob on the Second Floor. Small and troll-like with humanoid bodies and oversized heads, Clurichaun are marked by curly black hair, hooked noses, small feet, and very few teeth. They make a puglike gurgling noise when attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unvaccinated Clurichaun Rev-Up Consultant on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unvaccinated_Clurichaun_Rev-Up_Consultant</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Clurichaun</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Clurichaun</b> are a fairy-class mob on the Second Floor. Small and troll-like with humanoid bodies and oversized heads, Clurichaun are marked by curly black hair, hooked noses, small feet, and very few teeth. They make a puglike gurgling noise when attack.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Clurichaun on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Clurichaun</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Unwashed</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Unwashed: race/species: Unknown; first scene: Chapter 43, The Inevitable Ruin.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Stalker, The Last Light, The Thirst, The Fell, The Inexorable, The Beautiful Place</li><li><b>Race:</b> Unknown</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 43, The Inevitable Ruin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Unwashed on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Unwashed</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Up Smoothies</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The banner read, “Rev-Up.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Upgrade</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: (Hidden until it is affixed and then worn.) Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Upgrade Coupon</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Coupons for upgrading Crafting tables. Came in the <a href="bword://It&#x27;s Not My Fault You Fish-Headed Assholes Don&#x27;t Properly Program Your Quests Box">It's Not My Fault You Fish-Headed Assholes Don't Properly Program Your Quests Box</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Upgrade Coupon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Upgrade_Coupon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Upgrade Patch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting items. You may remove them, but they will be destroyed in the process.</i></li></ul><p class="source">Source: Upgrade Patch on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Upgrade_Patch</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Upgrade patches are fleeting items</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: (Hidden until it is affixed and then worn.) Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Upgraded Shower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Automatic shower in the Royal Palace that adds +10% to all base stats and lowers "Detectability" for 30 hours. It is one of the party's daily buffs.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Dungeon Market</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Upgraded Shower on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Upgraded_Shower</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uprooted Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Uprooted Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> used his <a href="bword://Protective Shell Spell">Protective Shell Spell</a> to launch <a href="bword://Claude Sludgington the Fourth">Claude Sludgington the Fourth</a>, a Level 65 Borough Boss Eryops Gigantis.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uprooted Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uprooted_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uptown Hal</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Uptown Hal</b> is a slate, which is an orange alien shaped like a piece of chewing gum. He was never a crawler, but was a game guide and occasional manager for 200 seasons before the crawl starting using ex-crawlers. He runs a talk show called <i>Uptown Hal Talks Tactics</i>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets him on the panel Crawling Through the Ages at CrawlCon!</li><li><b>Race:</b> slate</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uptown Hal on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uptown_Hal</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Urgyle</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Urgyles</b> are a small, winged, demon-like race. They populated at least one of the seeded planets featured on <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Urgyle on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Urgyle</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ursensus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>Ursensus'</i> is a race that is a combination of a <a href="bword://Mongoliensis">Mongoliensis</a> and a <a href="bword://Ursine">Ursine</a>, mixed up with the results of <a href="bword://Scolopendra">Scolopendra</a>'s Nine Tier Attack. <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a> and <a href="bword://Kiwi">Kiwi</a> have two girls.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ursensus on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ursensus</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ursine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ursine</b> are a race of bear-like <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> on the Sixth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ursine on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ursine</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Use</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Use a magic missile, not a...” The fireball shot forth from Donut, moving almost lazily as it glided through the air, sizzling and crackling in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>use a Flee card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: From this point forward, however, keyholders who use a Flee card will automatically lose their key to their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Use the doggie door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once they were set up there, we could use the doggie door upgrade to fast travel from the city to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Used</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Used to sight long-range targets for their bomb runs, the FarSeerer is credited as one of the key inventions that allowed the Dirigible Gnomes to obtain air.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Used Pet Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Used Pet Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for adopting a pet that belonged to someone else. He received this on the Eighth Floor when he received <a href="bword://Garret">Garret</a> from <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Used Pet Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Used_Pet_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Using</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Using some of our literal thousands of Build Trench scrolls collected from the previous floor, we’d created a labyrinthian trench system, filled with traps and soldiers, fortified bunkers, hospitals, fall-back.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Using the gate</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: It didn’t show anything about the gods and the Orthrus quest or the folks escaping their bubbles using the gate, but it was clear they were saving it all for the next.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Usually</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Usually it’s a big fight, but I have analyzed your plan, and I do feel it’s going to work quite well, and this final race will be a nonevent.” “Prepotente, darling,” Donut said, “I don’t want you to take this.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Utility card</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: They were like magic spells that directly affected gameplay somehow, unlike a utility card which would affect just one totem or a special card, which was usually a consumable, one-time effect on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Utility cards</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: The next six were all utility cards: Two Time Extend cards, one strength-increasing Stout card, one mana-increasing Blue Stuff card, a speed increasing Greased Lightning card, and the consumable Combo.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Utter Annihilation Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Utter Annihilation</b> is a destructive spell channeled through a wand, whose only appearance in the text is as an incidental durability test rather than a combat encounter.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Utter Annihilation Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Utter_Annihilation_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uzi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I can’t wait.” To Jesus, she said, “Flip it, pet.” The small robot shoved his tiny Uzi into his waistband and grabbed the coin with two.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uzi Jesus</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: In the end, they ended up with a seal thing; a crab named Raul; a giant, terrifying cat; this donkey snake thing; a guy named Uzi Jesus and another guy named Asojano, who both got combined to another guy named.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uzi Jesus and Asojano</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Jola hissed and pounced and disappeared in a puff of smoke as both Uzi Jesus and Asojano.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Uzzi</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Uzzi</b> was Mordecia's brother and former team mates along with <a href="bword://Chaco">Chaco</a> and <a href="bword://Hold Steady">Hold Steady</a>. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> mentions his brother choose the Storm Commander Class during their crawl.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Skyfowl</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Uzzi on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Uzzi</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vadim</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Also known as Doctor Vadim. Was a cosmetic surgeon with lots of billboards and TV commercials. He was infamous for having impregnated many of his clients, and is rumored to have 50 children.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Ukraine</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human | Kuhli</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vadim on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vadim</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The program they’re attending is owned by the Open Intellect Pacifist Network, and as you should know, Valtay rules state a liaison needs to be present in any instance when a crawler might be alone with a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay Corporate Outreach Center</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: While your current wetware system is compatible with this Neural Enhancer, it is recommended you visit a Valtay Corporate Outreach Center to discuss upgrade.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay Corporation</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Valtay Corporation</b> is a corporate system government within the <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a>, at once a business and a nation. Its people are a parasitic species that pilots the bodies of other races, and the corporation holds the debt of the <a href="bword://Borant System">Borant System</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Valtay ; The Brain Worms</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Valtay Corporation on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Valtay_Corporation</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay Corporation Neural Enhancer</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Valtay Corporation Neural Enhancer.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay Corporation Neural Interface</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: You do not have a Valtay Corporation Neural Interface.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Valtay Perso</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Mine was a Valtay Perso-Shield, Platinum Edition , which was identical to the one I would’ve gotten from Paulie, and it was simply a round, pinback button that looked a little like the planet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vampire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4 and 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Vampire</b> are a race that are required to drink large amounts of blood to survive. If a crawler is infected by vampirism and makes it to a staircase the infection becomes permanent and they gain a title.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vampire on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vampire</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vampire skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Her Love Vampire skill, which she’d received for reaching 100 in charisma, had never worked really well until she’d used the temporary tiara on the previous floor that had effectively doubled her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Varg Vikernes Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Varg Vikernes Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for defiling <a href="bword://Club Vanquisher">Club Vanquisher</a> while he was attacking <a href="bword://Vinata">Vinata</a>. Presumably the following crawlers also got this achievement: <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a>, <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>, <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a>, <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> and <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Varg Vikernes Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Varg_Vikernes_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veeka</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Veeka</b> is a hunter, she got called out by the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> because she locked herself in a saferoom crying and masturbating.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Veeka on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Veeka</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Velma the Flamethrower</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Velma</b> is <a href="bword://Tserendolgor">Tserendolgor</a>'s enchanted flamethrower, which was upgraded once at the end of the Fifth Floor. Part weapon and part breast plate, it looks like a <i>Ghostbusters</i>-esque backpack that also wraps around the wearer's torso like a shirt.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Velma the Flamethrower on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Velma_the_Flamethrower</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vending machine</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: There was a broken-down Ford truck with only three tires, a rotted couch, an old dishwasher, plus what looked like a regular, old-school soda vending.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vendor Village</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We jogged through the arch and entered the Vendor Village.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vengeance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: If Donut and I both happen to survive past tonight, and I make it to the sixth floor, I will sign an exclusive agreement to only complete elite-themed quests on the sixth floor that are directly associated.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Venison Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Venison Box</b> is a Loot Box given to crawlers on the Leaderboard upon entry to the next floor. Due to the individual nature of each awarded box, this page is broken up by book.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Quarry Sees Another Spring Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Venison Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Venison_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veriluxx</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: With that toy company Veriluxx being a dud and Empress D’Nadia selling my sponsorship, it would be nice to have something to complement the Apothecary, even if she does sponsor half the dungeon.” We entered.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veriluxx New Toys Showcase</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Veriluxx New Toys Showcase is described as a pair of tall, giant-eyed alien.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veriluxx RealPet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: The ultimate play toy, Veriluxx RealPet Companions are budget-friendly, nearly indestructible collectibles that will give your child hours and hours of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veriluxx RealPet Companions</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>Verlluxx real pet Companions are a company that manufactures pet robots. They're linked to a soother lending fund named Veritan Linkage that advocates for the enslavement for those that can't afford their taxes. Closely allied to Borant and the <a href="bword://Bloom">Bloom</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Veriluxx RealPet Companions on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Veriluxx_RealPet_Companions</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Veriluxx RealPet Dungeon Crawl Special Edition Exclusive</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Veriluxx RealPet Dungeon Crawl Special Edition Exclusive</b> is kids toy version of Princess <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, except filled with creepiness.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Veriluxx RealPet Dungeon Crawl Special Edition Exclusive on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Veriluxx_RealPet_Dungeon_Crawl_Special_Edition_Exclusive</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vermillion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’re taking a Vermillion subway train down to 101, picking everybody up, and we’re taking them back by going reverse, using a second engine attached to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vernon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Vernon</b> is a train conductor for the Yellow line train on the Iron Tangle. The <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a> takes time between stations 84 and 85 on the Yellow Line to explore the train. They enter the Employee Break Room and talk to Vernon. Due to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s high Charisma he is very talkative and explains a lot of the mechanics of how trains circle back to the beginning of the line. Vernon also talks about his wife and how he's excited to go back to her.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dwarf</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vernon on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vernon</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Very Rare</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Good to know.” I knew there were seven levels of rarity for the totem cards: Common, Uncommon, Rare, Very Rare, Legendary, Mythic, and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Very Sullen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Very Sullen</b> is a cretin working as a bodyguard in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. His face is thinner than the other cretin guards' faces, and his eyes are tiny emotionless dots. He rarely (if ever) speaks. Although not verified in the text, he is most likely 7 foot tall, level 35, and wearing a tuxedo, like the other cretin guards.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Cretin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 7</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Very Sullen on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Very_Sullen</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Viceroy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She is the vice president of Nova Client Services, LTD., a notorious medical collections agency whose primary client is the Viceroy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Viceroys</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>The Viceroys</b> are a <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> race and government. They regularly participate in the Faction Wars (game) as the Madness. Marked by featureless white masks, Viceroys specialize in horrific battlefield spells and necromancy. They regularly join the Ninth Floor Faction Wars as "The Madness," and although they have not won in a few years, they often claim victory. They won in Rosetta's year. Another contributor to the <a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a> wondered if the viceroys were biological, as no one had seen one die.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Madness</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Viceroys on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Viceroys</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Victory</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Victory</b> is an Orc attorney and judge from the <a href="bword://Skull Empire">Skull Empire</a> who serves as Adjutant to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>. She is the half-sister of <a href="bword://Queen Consort Ugloo">Queen Consort Ugloo</a>.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Baroness Victory, esquire ; Judge Victory ; Prime Minister Victory</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Skull Empire</li><li><b>Race:</b> Orc</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 36</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Victory on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Victory</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Victory and Drick</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Victory and Drick, tell us what you think about the two celestial tier items Carl and Katia received.” Drick grunted, his voice only slightly touched by the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Victory and Fopsy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: What the hell does that mean?” She looked over at Victory and Fopsy, silently watching.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Victory and Judge Deng</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Victory and Judge Deng is described as quietly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Video game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: He’s the type of guy who would one-star an upcoming video game because the artists had the temerity to draw a female character who didn’t give him an instant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Video games</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The Sledge, who’d so far spent the entire floor playing video games in the saferoom, was already engaged with his much more dangerous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vietnam</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: All the candy is from Vietnam, too, and I don’t know what’s good, but Tran and Bautista helped pick some for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Viewers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Viewers only get a certain number of favorites, so consider it an honor.” “But,” Mordecai continued, “ultimately, it’s all about the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Views</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: 32 Time to Collapse: Five days, ten hours Views: 69 Billion Followers: 635 Million Favorites: 149 Million As we approached the quadrant with the stairwell, we could now see that there was a tutorial guild and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vigorous Measles</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: If you make the smoothie, you’ll have immunity from the Taint debuff and something called the Vigorous.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Village Guard Swordsman</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Village Guard Swordsman</b> are a guard type NPC / mob on the Third Floor. Village guards are decked head to toe in plate mail and carry a long, steel blade. They are bulky and tall, with indeterminant race characteristics.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Village Guard Swordsman on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Village_Guard_Swordsman</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vinata</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Vinata</b> is a <a href="bword://Naga">Naga</a> princess and member of the ruling class of the Nagas in <a href="bword://Syndicate">Syndicate</a> space. She is also the warlord of her faction in the Ninth Floor Faction Wars.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Sepsis Whore</li><li><b>Race:</b> Naga</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 7, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vinata on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vinata</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vine Creepers</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Vine Creepers</b> are a plant-type mob found on the First Floor. They are thorny, creeping plants that emerge from giant pods and consume crawlers whole.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vine Creepers on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vine_Creepers</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vinegar Bitch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: While all the other gods have been waking up long-forgotten deities to assist them, the Vinegar Bitch knows having a prince of hell at her side will help ensure her claim to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Viper Queen</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I was a Viper Queen for a few.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Viscount Fog</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: This is a Bereft Minion of Viscount Fog The great, now-deceased hunter of vampires and all thing undead, Viscount Fog had trained his trusty mount, Sierra, to never leave his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Visitor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Visitor</b> are a type of mob on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Visitor on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Visitor</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vocal Coach Training Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A <b>Vocal Coach</b> is a special training room that can be installed inside a crawler's Personal Space. The room is roughly the size of a closet, and includes a microphone behind a glass panel (à la a recording studio). A holographic NPC named Lover Illiana teaches the crawler throat exercises that train up the crawler's Singing Skill, which helps Bard class crawlers cast spells and create substantial illusions.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Loot Boxes; Marketplace</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vocal Coach Training Room on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vocal_Coach_Training_Room</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Volteeg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Volteeg</b> is a former crawler and author of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a></i> (7th Edition). They contributed only a single entry to the cookbook, in which they lamented the loss of a loved one.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Sturnus | Gargoyle</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 47</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Volteeg on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Volteeg</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Voodoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Rosetta was just telling Milk and myself about that book of Voodoo you have, and I believe you may be able to share skills and spells that way as well, using the same.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Voting Admin Mass Number Three</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Voting Admin Mass Number Three</b> is the warlord in charge of the Operatic Collective for Faction Wars. They are a collective entity made up of three individuals, one of whom is Hortense.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Voting Admin Mass Number Three on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Voting_Admin_Mass_Number_Three</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vrah</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Vrah</b> is a <a href="bword://Mantis">Mantis</a> Hunter and the top hunter of the <a href="bword://Dark Hive">Dark Hive</a> who hunts crawlers on the Sixth Floor. She pursues <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, and the Royal Court after Carl kills her sister, <a href="bword://Xindy">Xindy</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantis</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 5, Chapter 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Vrah on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Vrah</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Vulgar Temple</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “She needs to get to the Basilica or the Vulgar Temple and place the gem in the plinth, and she’ll be back for.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Waffle House</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: We still had two hours until the collapse, and I suspected they were waiting to see if Chris could convince the thirty folks holed up in that Waffle House to come.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wailing Shrieker</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: If a Wailing Shrieker forms, she has the tendency to haunt her lover’s grave, and she will aggressively defend the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wait, Bosses Can Leave Their Rooms? Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Wait, Bosses Can Leave Their Rooms? Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> when they get ambushed by the <a href="bword://Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)">Krakaren Clone (Second Floor)</a> boss while standing outside the room.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wait, Bosses Can Leave Their Rooms? Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wait%2C_Bosses_Can_Leave_Their_Rooms%3F_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Waldrip Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Waldrip Chris</b> is a Gremlin animal trainer available to crawlers on the Tenth Floor. He is assigned to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s garage. Waldrip Chris is one of two choices for mechanic or animal trainer for the Tenth Floor races. His specialty is Animal Trainer, whereas <a href="bword://Hedy">Hedy</a> is another gremlin that specializes as a Vehicle Mechanic.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Gremlin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Waldrip Chris on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Waldrip_Chris</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Walk</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: So when the season’s Walk-On List was released, he marched himself right to the closest port, found a bounty crew who hired null, and he signed right.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Walk on Air</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I dropped them all, all at once, and then I activated Walk on Air and made a mad dash for the lip of the city, hoping my speed would get me there before the skill would run.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wall Monitor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wall Monitor</b> is a lizard class mob that is located in the Abyss Station #436 of the Iron Tangle. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> first sees the Wall Monitor Lizards when he explores the Abyss Station #436 shortly after stealing the <a href="bword://Nightmare Express">Nightmare Express</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wall Monitor on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wall_Monitor</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wall Monitors</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Wall Monitors is described as congregating near.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wall of Fire</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I have my Wall of Fire ready.” “That’s what they say.” The bright green mask on the girl featured a horrific goblin but with short tusks and a crooked.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wall of Fire Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wall of Fire Spell</b> is a spell obtained by <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> on Floor 5. <b>Cost</b>: 15 mana</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wall of Fire Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wall_of_Fire_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Walter</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Walter: race/species: Sai; occupation: Firefighter; first scene: Chapter 32, Book 6.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Sai</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 32, Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Walter on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Walter</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wand Bearers</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Carl's Doomsday Scenario, Wand Bearers is described as some of the most pious, most indoctrinated, and therefore, the most dangerous members of the silly little.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wand of Nighty Night</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wand of Nighty Night</b> is a wand that casts level 10 sleep on single opponent.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wand of Nighty Night on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wand_of_Nighty_Night</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wand Recharge Scroll</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wand Recharge Scroll</b> is a scroll that recharges a wand.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wand Recharge Scroll on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wand_Recharge_Scroll</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Want</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Want me to go back to my tug and come back later?” Quasar’s tie featured an upside-down pineapple that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: I watched them trample each other as they ran from mobs I’d never seen, from spine-covered wolves to shapeless blobs of fire to floating, stingray-like creatures which shot magic missiles from eye stalks, like.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Chief</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It gets quite heated there at the end.” War Chief Stalwart : I demand the immediate return of my family’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Chief Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “It gets quite heated there at the end.” War Chief Stalwart : I demand the immediate return of my family’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Crime</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: And while the area of effect was relatively low, the synergy tower’s ability to upgrade Atrocity spells and allow them to be cast over a wide area made War Crime a true spell of mass.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Crime spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The spell wasn’t nearly as large as when she cast her War Crime spell, which she wouldn’t be able to cast again until the twelfth floor, but it was still.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Crimes Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>War Crimes Spell</b> is a spell given to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> in a Legendary <a href="bword://1914 Box">1914 Box</a>. The spell book itself is described as bleeding and crying before the user reads it.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: War Crimes Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/War_Crimes_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Criminal Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>War Criminal</b> is an achievement awarded when a crawler kills 21+ non-combatant <a href="bword://NPCs">NPCs</a> in a single attack. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive the You Monster! and <b>War Criminal Achievement</b> on the First Floor after launching a cart laden with explosives and <a href="bword://Hobgoblin Pus">Hobgoblin Pus</a> into the Goblin War Chieftan's boss room... which also happened to contain a lot of babies, pregnant goblins, and elderly goblins.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: War Criminal Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/War_Criminal_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Because of my magic gauntlet’s special ability, each successful punch had a 1.5% chance to summon Grull the war god, which would probably be a juiced-up, almost immortal version of Prince Stalwart of the Skull.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Lord Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>War Lord</b> is a spell whose only appearance in the text is as the title of a spellbook offered as a prize at the Butcher's Masquerade Ball. Beyond its mana cost, nothing about what the spell actually does is ever revealed. It is not to be confused with Warlord, the rank held by a leader of the Faction Wars.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: War Lord Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/War_Lord_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Mage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>War Mages</b> are a race / class of magic-users known for casting brutal dark magic such as <i><a href="bword://You&#x27;re Not Done Yet Spell">You're Not Done Yet Spell</a></i>. War mages are symbiotes who survived at least 12 resleeving <a href="bword://Flesher">Flesher</a> attacks.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: War Mage on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/War_Mage</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Mage Rebellion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Message : After a careful deliberation, it has been determined that the Non-Team Entity known as the War Mage Rebellion will not be allowed access to Larracos until the Peeling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>War Mages</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We didn’t expect the powerful War Mages in the water of the castle to be killed by it suddenly turning to acid—basically the same trick we used with the water in the trenches during our first offensive—but it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord update : Two Pieces of Armor, both owned by the Operatic Collective have been captured and added to your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Agatha</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Message : Warlord Agatha of the War Mage Rebellion has been deemed to have abandoned her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Akuma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The bewildering conversation confused everything even further: Warlord Akuma : Hey, underwear.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ll give it to Warlord Carl for detonating the bomb that was dropped by a wraith-possessed Crawler Louis Santiago 2, who was under control of Warlord Juice Box of Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Chat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I altered the way warlord notifications appeared, and I moved to the Warlord Chat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Donut : I DON’T KNOW ANYTHING ABOUT BANKERS, BUT I DO KNOW NOBODY LIKES YOU RIGHT NOW AND NOBODY IS GOING TO BE DOING YOU GUYS ANY FAVORS ANY TIME.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Empress</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Emergency Action item proposed by Warlord Empress D’Nadia of the Prism: Remove the Princess Posse from Faction Wars and/or delete Crawlers Carl and Princess.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Fang</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Warlord Fang is described as not in the Reaver castle, and that it was abandoned except for their “security” system, which was a bunch of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Hortense</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Hortense Leadership</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Hortense Leadership Unit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Current status: Died after deliberately filling a Weeper-class tank with explosives and detonating it in the center of the Bloc force, killing over 10,000 of his fellow soldiers with a single blast, including.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Juice Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Also, Warlord Juice Box has requested your private presence before we attend with her co-warlord.” I felt my eyebrow.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Message</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warlord Message : After a careful deliberation, it has been determined that the Non-Team Entity known as the War Mage Rebellion will not be allowed access to Larracos until the Peeling.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord of Team Retribution</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Co-Warlord of Team.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord on High</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Warlord on High, Sir.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Princess</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: System Message: All assets of the Blood Sultanate, as per prior arrangement, have been split evenly with the surviving blood relatives of the Sepsis Whore, Warlord Princess Donut and General Katia Grim, both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Princess Donut</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Butcher's Masquerade, Warlord Princess Donut is described as a member of the royal line of succession of another team (Team #4, the Blood Sultanate.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warlord Stalwart</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: This title is being disputed by Warlord Stalwart of the Skull.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warning</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: If this upgrade patch is affixed to an eligible garment, it will imbue the following upgrades: (Hidden until it is affixed and then worn.) Warning: Upgrade patches are fleeting.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Warrior's Gold Helmet of Resistance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Warrior's Gold Helmet of Resistance</b> is a piece of magical head armor obtained by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor of the World Dungeon. This helmet is a piece of magical armor designed to offer resistance to the wearer. However, because the item features the word "Warrior's" in its title, it carries a strict equipment condition: the item only works if the person wearing it is actively wielding a sword.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Boss Box (Eighth Floor)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Warrior's Gold Helmet of Resistance on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Warrior%27s_Gold_Helmet_of_Resistance</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wars team</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Wars team is described as to negate their ability to basically enslave crawlers to their.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wasn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The last thing he remembers is it being his last day as a game guide.” “Wasn’t that like a bajillion years ago?” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wasteland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: This whole inspection thing was clearly put into the storyline as a way for us to be able to get to the Wasteland , but it was all screwed up now thanks to the death of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Waster Patrol</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: There were seven of them, and the system listed them as Level 48 Waster Patrol.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wastrel Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Wastrel Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for feeding the <a href="bword://Enchanted Night Wyrm&#x27;s Ring of Divine Suffering">Enchanted Night Wyrm's Ring of Divine Suffering</a> to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> at the end of the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wastrel Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wastrel_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Watch</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Except for myself and the two flea-infested mercenaries who are smoking illicit, mind-altering materials while on duty.” “Watch it,” Moxo called from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Watch Extreme</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You are likely unaware of this, but Death Watch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem announced earlier that it was going into hiatus while the show restructured.” “I’m shocked,” Donut.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Watch the show</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Like, can they cheat and have their friends watch the show from outside and follow us and then tell them where we are at all times?” “Well, in theory, people outside the dungeon aren’t supposed to talk to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Watch where you're waving that thing, big boy. Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Watch where you're waving that thing, big boy. Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after he uses a Wand in combat against the <a href="bword://Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance">Reminiscence Hydra of Malicious Compliance</a> on the Eighth Floor</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Watch where you're waving that thing, big boy. Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Watch_where_you%27re_waving_that_thing%2C_big_boy._Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Water</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Water arced into the air from the middle of the house like they’d sheared off a fire hydrant, and the main had been knocked.</li></ul></div><div class="multi-lookup"><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ring of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a <b>Ring of Water Breathing</b> on the Fifth Floor. A glowing blue ring that twinkles with little sparkles.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold I'm Wet Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ring of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ring_of_Water_Breathing</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Scroll of Water Breathing</b> is a scroll that allows a crawler to breathe underwater for a limited amount of time.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Platinum That Wasn't Too Smart, Was It? Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Scroll of Water Breathing on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Scroll_of_Water_Breathing</p></div></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Water Lily</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: I couldn’t get him to look up the cure for the Water Lily curse, but I am headed to the town alchemist now to see if he has a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Water Lily Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Water Lily</b> is powerful <a href="bword://Naiad">Naiad</a> heirloom magic spell that renders the target Unconscious until the caster cancels the spell. If the caster dies, the target will never wake up. If someone attempts to move the target, the target's health rapidly decreases.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Water Lily Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Water_Lily_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Water Park</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Prepotente’s and Louis’s teams both appeared to be on the same track as each other as well, which was described as “Satan’s Water Park.” I closed my eyes for a second, pretending I was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Water Quadrant</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: All crawlers who originated in the Water Quadrant may now freely travel to the other.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Way for the big shot</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Unfortunately, the horn wasn’t a normal honk, but a recording of a gangster’s voice saying, Make way for the big shot , followed by a chicken squawk and the rat-a-tat shooting sound of a tommy.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Way into the room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Is Anaconda working tonight?” I pushed my way into the room to find an unexpected.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Way through the dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I wondered if that route, just quietly making one’s way through the dungeon, was actually better than the path we’d found ourselves upon, not that I could change that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Waystation Controls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bidding today is sponsored by Dictum Waystation Controls, Limited, because of course it.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We Want Meat! We Want Meat! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>"We Want Meat! We Want Meat!" <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a></b> is awarded the first time a crawler enters the Penis Parade at the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> received the "We want meat! We want meat!" achievement on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: We Want Meat! We Want Meat! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/We_Want_Meat%21_We_Want_Meat%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weapon Box</b></p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Weapon Box</b> is a Loot Box common on the First Floor. Although a Ranged Weapons Box is given out on the Sixth Floor, the basic "Weapon Box" is never mentioned again after the first book; instead, unenchanted weapons are distributed to crawlers on deeper floors in low-tier Adventurer Boxes.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievements; PVP Coupon</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Weapon Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Weapon_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weapon Oil: Weeping Wound</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Weapon Oil: Weeping Wound</b> is a type of potion that can be applied to edged weapons to cause wounds inflicted with the weapon to "bleed longer."</li><li><b>Source:</b> Silver Asshole's Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Weapon Oil: Weeping Wound on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Weapon_Oil%3A_Weeping_Wound</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weapons and armor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: My original plan was to just spam drop weapons and armor pieces into a huge pile and have Donut reanimate them all with her Legionnaires of the Damned spell, but Mordecai said the weapons had to have been.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wearing a crown</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I wish I had an entrance theme like that!” The hood fell back to reveal a boil-covered, dark-skinned man wearing a crown of thorns, but the thorns obscured his eyes like a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weeper Commander Volteeg</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Weeper Commander Volteeg for the Operatic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weird</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: The bottom line is I’m wearing this suit when I enter the dungeon for the foreseeable future.”.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weird Shit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ruby lived, but Flint says she’s destined to spend her life in Weird Shit Alley.” “Why did the gnomes bomb you?” “Flint says it’s because gnomes are short little assholes who deserve nothing but to be trampled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weird Shit Alley</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Ruby lived, but Flint says she’s destined to spend her life in Weird Shit Alley.” “Why did the gnomes bomb you?” “Flint says it’s because gnomes are short little assholes who deserve nothing but to be trampled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Welcome</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: His top torso wore a filthy, once-white tank top that read “Welcome to the Gun Show.” The bottom, larger half was naked except for a loincloth made of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Welcome Home</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Of those six, four also included a skill called Welcome Home.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Welcome to Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Welcome to Faction.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Welcome to Hump Town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Welcome to Hump.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Welcome to the Neighborhood! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i>Welcome to the neighborhood!</i> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded for purchasing a Personal Space. The doorway to the personal space opens as soon as the achievement fades away. If multiple party members purchase a personal space, the space is titled "Conjoined Personal Space of Team [Name]."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Welcome to the Neighborhood! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Welcome_to_the_Neighborhood%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Well-Rested! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Well-Rested</b> is an achievement for receiving the "Good Rest Buff" for the first time. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Fourth Floor after sleeping on the <a href="bword://Ultra-Stabilized Size-Adjustable Race-Adjustable Alleviating Sleep Apparatus">Ultra-Stabilized Size-Adjustable Race-Adjustable Alleviating Sleep Apparatus</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Well-Rested! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Well-Rested%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wendita</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>'<i>Wendita</i> is a <a href="bword://Bopca">Bopca</a> in the safe room at Station #83 in the Iron Tangle. She is describes as a shaggy female, Bopca gnome.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Unspecified Bopca Planet</li><li><b>Race:</b> Bopca</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wendita on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wendita</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Weren’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Weren’t expecting that, were ya?” Donut called with autotuned glee as she tossed a Greased Lightning onto.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wet Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Reward : You’ve received a Bronze I’m Wet.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wetware</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wetware</b> refers to a biological lifeform's brain and the "data" it contains. On Earth, the term "wetware" was popularized by Bruce Sterling's 1985 Nebula Award-winning cyberpunk novel <i>Schismatrix.</i> Rudy Rucker, who won the PKD award for his (frankly kind of weird) 180-page novel <i>Wetware</i> (1988), neatly summarized the term as "the whole bio-cybernetic software of the mind." In 2007, Rucker wrote a blog post explaining:</li><li><b>Source:</b> Inherent</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wetware on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wetware</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d given Samantha a line to say because she’d been bitching that we were underutilizing her “talents.” The instructions weren’t important, but she’d insisted on having something to do if she got captured and.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’ll circle back to it on a different date.) I’m leaving out a lot of really important details, but one of the end results of all this is a naughty man with beautiful feet sitting there in his underwear.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We’re</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’re starting with four teams each and ending with just one, so the plan is to try to keep as many NPCs alive as possible for this sixth heat and get as many crawlers as we can off the playing board before.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We’re in Iceland</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’re in Iceland.” “I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>We’ve</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “We’ve already had a run-in with the local dinosaurs, and they are absolutely not the kind I wish to associate with.” In the center of the clearing was a small, eight-foot, white-barked tree with a fairy ring.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>What Big Muscles You Have! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>What big muscles you have!</b> is an achievement awarded for reaching a base Strength of 100. Regardless of which option a crawler chooses, they immediately look more muscular.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: What Big Muscles You Have! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/What_Big_Muscles_You_Have%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>What Goes Up Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>What goes up...</b> is an achievement for surviving the <a href="bword://Reverse Gravity Spell">Reverse Gravity Spell</a>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive this achievement on the Second Floor after their fight with the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: What Goes Up Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/What_Goes_Up_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whatever</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Whatever is described as holding the arch down snapped, and the tree flipped up like a catapult, flinging the remaining cannibals in the opposite.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>What’re</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: “What’re you trying to say, Princess?” Chandra produced the court order laying out that Princess Donut had been put under a conservatorship and that Carl was named her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wheel of Fortune</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: The croupier behind the Wheel of Fortune game was not the same guy as last time, though he looked as if he could have been that guy’s bigger and meaner.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wheel of Fortune game</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Wheel of Fortune game is described as not the same guy as last time, though he looked as if he could have been that guy’s bigger and meaner.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wheeled bomb</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Bomo would pull the wheeled bomb out of the personal space and emerge out in a saferoom, and there wasn’t anything anybody could do about.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>When Gauntlet Formed</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: So after I was done, I ended up with: Strength: 41 +3 (When Gauntlet Formed) Intelligence: 15 Constitution: 34 Dexterity: 23 Charisma: 25 “A strength over 40 is good for the fourth floor,” Mordecai said when I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Where’s Chris</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Where’s Chris?” I asked Elle after I went through and acknowledged.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Where’s Zev</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Where’s Zev?” “Zev is taking some much-needed time.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>White Dragon Urban Defense</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: These troops, mostly Semeru dwarves and the regular citizens of Larracos were now mixed in with Li Na’s 107 th White Dragon Urban Defense battalion, forming an impressive ring around the lip of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>White Dragons</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The 104 th Naughty Little Piggies is back to their normal castle defense positions, cleaning up after the brain worm attack, but we lost most of our mages, and it’s going to be a while before the castle’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>White on the map</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even though her dot was still white on the map, I didn’t want to distract Bonnie the gnome even.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Who Let the Gods Out This Time? Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Who Let the Gods Out This Time? Box</b> is a Loot Box given to crawlers for the <a href="bword://Charge of the Light Brigade Achievement">Charge of the Light Brigade Achievement</a>. <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a>, <a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a>, <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a>, and <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> all received a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> version of this box.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Who Let the Gods Out This Time? Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Who_Let_the_Gods_Out_This_Time%3F_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Who Let the Gods Out? Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Who Let the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> Out?</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded for unleashing a Feral God. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the Fifth Floor after using the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a> for the first time.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Who Let the Gods Out? Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Who_Let_the_Gods_Out%3F_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whoa</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Fucking really?” Behind me, Mordecai laughed, but a moment later he added, “Whoa, that’s actually a really good prize.” It was a white pair of boxers covered in little red.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole building</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Whole building is described as going to collapse on top of us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole club</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re a creepy bird dude,’ and he was all ‘No, I mean, Theia isn’t,’ and I was like, ‘Yeah, okay, weirdo.’ But then the whole club went nuts and got pulled into some really creepy demon shit because Li Na has.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In A Parade of Horribles, Whole dungeon is described as imploding and that none of this would probably matter didn’t seem to be sitting well with a lot of the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole family</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Give them an hour and the whole family will be out there.” Across the room, Donut discovered the fireplace, which crackled.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole floor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Whole floor is described as easier than it should be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole house</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She’d managed to get the whole house down to the land quadrant, and she’d figured out how to breach the magical door that guarded the front of the land quadrant.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Whole room is described as made of glass, but the moment I sent Tran in, it all flashed, and everything turned to… A loud error message blocked out the rest of her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole system</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: We were being used like pawns in a royal pissing match between a brother and sister, who in turn were being exploited by the whole system while the entire universe laughed their asses off at us.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole town</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Even though the whole town had this aesthetic from the 1980s film Blade Runner , the restaurant gave the 1950s, at least at first.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whole world</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Gate of the Feral Gods, Whole world is described as starting to heat.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Whomever</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Whomever it was that had stepped on the traps, there were at least three of them, and they were now spread out all over the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Why Are You Hitting Yourself</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Oh, poo, I was hoping for Fireball, Why Are You Hitting Yourself?, and Hole .” I looked at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Why Are You Hitting Yourself Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Why Are You Hitting Yourself Spell</b> is a spell that makes target attack themselves.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Why Are You Hitting Yourself Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Why_Are_You_Hitting_Yourself_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Why Aren't You Wearing Pants? Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Why aren't you wearing pants?</b> is an achievement awarded for entering the Dungeon without pants. This is the fifth achievement that <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives after entering the First Floor wearing only his boxers and a leather coat. The Gold <a href="bword://Apparel Box">Apparel Box</a> contains the <a href="bword://Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness">Enchanted Nightgaunt Cloak of Stoutness</a> and the <a href="bword://Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling">Enchanted Trollskin Shirt of Pummeling</a>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Why Aren't You Wearing Pants? Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Why_Aren%27t_You_Wearing_Pants%3F_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Widget</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Widget</b> is an Steam Engine Train Technician on the Iron Tangle who works in Trainyard E. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> describes his accent as "Creole guy poorly attempting a cockney accent." Widget is very accommodating and friendly, though that might be due to <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s high Charisma.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> Grease Gremlin</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Widget on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Widget</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Widow’s Rights</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: She didn’t want attention brought to herself, but the title was necessary if she wanted to claim Widow’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wiggle Room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Flint says we’re supposed to go to the Spit and Swallow or the Wiggle Room.” I knew neither of those were true saferooms, and they wouldn’t be.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wild Goose Chase! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>Wild Goose Chase! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> after he drank a trick pitcher of lemonade <a href="bword://Bonnie">Bonnie</a> made thinking it would help him fight <a href="bword://Denise">Denise</a> the goose.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wild Goose Chase! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wild_Goose_Chase%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wild Hunt</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: So it was team Sparkles, then the Jugglers, then team Free Love, then the Wild Hunt, then the Lady Dominators, then One Fine Pig, and us at the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>win Faction</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I suspect the residual in control of the war mage team isn’t just trying to win Faction Wars and zero out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>win Faction Wars</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: I suspect the residual in control of the war mage team isn’t just trying to win Faction Wars and zero out the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wind Mage</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: The second elf was named Carmine, and his description was almost identical, except instead of a Root Druid, it said he was a Wind.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Winding box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The permanent portal into the Nothing was made from the now- broken, blood-soaked winding box from the Gate of the Feral.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wings of the Pixie Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wings of the Pixie Spell</b> is an escape spell availible to Bards. <a href="bword://Chaco">Chaco</a> had access to the spell during his crawl. <a href="bword://Mordecai">Mordecai</a> tells <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> to ask Chaco at the Butcher's Masquerade if he has anything like his <b>Wings of the Pixie Spell</b>.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wings of the Pixie Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wings_of_the_Pixie_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wisp Armor</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: After everything settled, I ended up with the following: Strength: 10 + 3 (toe ring) = 13 +3 (When Gauntlet Formed) Intelligence: 5 Constitution: 10 + 4 (shirt) +1 (ring) +2 (ring) +2 (boxers) = 19 Dexterity.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wisp Armor Cost</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: Wisp Armor Cost: 5 Mana Target: Self Only Duration: 5 minutes + 1 minute per level of.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wisp Armor Spell</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Wisp Armor</b></i> is a protective spell that blocks a percentage of magical attack damage and makes the caster temporarily immune to mind control effects. When cast, the crawler sees a countdown timer and sparkling lights twirling around them like comets.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wisp Armor Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wisp_Armor_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Within</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Within the little glass container, the dead fairy stared back at me, spinning until she was sucked away, the concoction first green, then red, and then.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Without</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Without the failsafe, the only ones who could possibly help were the Plenty, and despite being given a seat on the council, the goat fucks never showed.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Witness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The witnesses are protected from the gods during this, hence the invulnerability, and she can keep them in the Witness state as long as she.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Witness spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Witness spell is described as Rend, Bigs the Sluggalette, Gonk the yak, Jamal the shark, and Dong.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wizard of Oz</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Did you know that Terry, the cairn terrier that played Toto in the original Wizard of Oz movie was paid $125 a week, more than the vast majority of the other actors in the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Women’s room</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: Royal bodyguard?” “So you were asleep in there, in the women’s room?” I asked, ignoring his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Won the quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You still would’ve won the quest, and think of all the experience we would’ve gotten!” “It was a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wonder Woman</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: She was doing a Wonder Woman impersonation before our time out, and believe it or not, I thought…” “Shell!” Katia cried as I felt myself fly back from the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wonderwall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: “If we have time, Donut will make sure she’s reunited properly with her mother.” “This is ‘Wonderwall,’” Horton said, and he started to play the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Won’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I can get through, give them the potions, and we can keep going down!” “Won’t.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Won’t attack</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: But if there’s a bad outcome, your pet won’t attack you if you feed them the treat while you’re in a safe.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wooden door</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: At the end of this room was a wooden door, and based on the minimap, I immediately determined beyond that door was the lair of yet another neighborhood.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Woody Harrelson</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Gideon : Yeah, so the Woody Harrelson god killed the monkey god, and they both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Word</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook, Word is described as starting to spread that you needed the hat to utilize that escape, so those without were mostly braving the “ghoul coaster,” as Elle had dubbed it, back to the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Word Weaver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, the zombie event had started to disrupt Sister Ines’s charm poem, but since her sponsor and patron, the Nebulars, helped her raise that Word Weaver spell to level 15, she was able to fully control both.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worgo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: When Worgo—the real Worgo—had gone down the new tunnel and had returned just a few minutes later, Justice had noticed the change.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worked</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: Worked as a game guide and in the Desperado Club as a merchant renting mercenaries and occasionally was in charge of distribution of the dungeon.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>World Dungeon</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: A Syndicate neutral observer AI—myself—has been created and dispatched to this planet to supervise the creation of the World Dungeon and to ensure all the rules and regulations are properly.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>World quest</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: I don’t know if that means he’ll be pissed or happy we freed him, but either way it’s going to trigger a world quest, and we’ll need to see what the parameters are before we decide how to proceed from.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worm Fulcrum</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: It called the bad ones “Touched by the Worm Fulcrum,” whatever that.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worn Path</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the castle is conquered, then the associated armies are defeated and dominion is transferred to the conqueror.) (See my essay on the nature of NPCs for more insight on how conquering areas may be utilized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worn Path Method</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: Once the castle is conquered, then the associated armies are defeated and dominion is transferred to the conqueror.) (See my essay on the nature of NPCs for more insight on how conquering areas may be utilized.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worse</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Worse, much worse, was the sudden, out-of-nowhere implication of not following Katia’s.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worship a god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: We’d spent some time discussing the potential trap that the Pineapple Cabaret was, and even though we’d already sent Li Na, we decided it would be best that only volunteers who didn’t worship a god and had.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worshipped a god</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Anyway, this is a magic-using turkey who once worshipped a god named Wakinyan, AKA Meleagris Gallopavo Rex or the Great Wild.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Worships Hellik</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Warning: This NPC Worships.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wouldn’t</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: Wouldn’t that be great?” To our left, the door against the wall disappeared, revealing a new room with four more.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: The media rep will not be permitted to report on anything that occurs in this meeting unless a law is broken.” “Wow,” I said, turning to Katia, who suddenly had a sour look on her.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wraith Phase</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: I started to get back up, wondering if my Wraith Phase would.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wrangling dogs</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: When he isn’t attending cat shows, wrangling dogs, feeding turtles, playing bass in a punk/metal band, or writing books that make guys named Kyle threaten to kill his family because he called a goblin an.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wrap of Sekhmet’s Compassion</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wrap of Sekhmet’s Compassion</b> is an item <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receives as part of her Assassin of Sekhmet Class. She gives it to Rend.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Assassin of Sekhmet Class</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wrap of Sekhmet’s Compassion on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wrap_of_Sekhmet%E2%80%99s_Compassion</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wrath Ghouls</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wrath Ghouls</b> are a mob on the Fourth Floor. They are created by a mob having the infliction of the DTs and not surviving the second of three stages.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wrath Ghouls on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wrath_Ghouls</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wrecking Ball</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: In addition, while not necessarily known for their offensive skills, their Wrecking Ball attack is considered a terror on any.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Wynne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Wynne</b> is a <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> being held hostage and used as collateral to prevent the Dirigible Gnomes from bombing Hump Town.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Dirigible Gnome</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Wynne on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Wynne</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Xindy</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Zindy was a hunter that was killed by <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, her mother, Circe, hosted a panel at CrawlCon. She was meant to take over from her sister and become the hives next top hunter.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Mantis</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Chapter 5, Book 5</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Xindy on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Xindy</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Xistera</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> gets a <b>Xistera</b> in a <a href="bword://Fan Box">Fan Box</a>. It's an equippable scoop that allows him to throw things farther, particularly round shaped things. It is used in the sport Cesta Punta, the skill Carl got from the <a href="bword://Earth Hobby Potion">Earth Hobby Potion</a>.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Fan Box for One Quadrillion! Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Xistera on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Xistera</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yago</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yago</b> is a <a href="bword://Monk Seal">Monk Seal</a> Picket Sentry, an aquatic, melee tank mob and a Totem <a href="bword://T&#x27;Ghee Card">T'Ghee Card</a> on the Eighth Floor. This card was originally held by the Team <a href="bword://Sister Ines">Sister Ines</a>/Team Quiteria and later held by <a href="bword://Ysalte">Ysalte</a> after Sister Ines's death.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Monk Seal Picket Sentry</li><li><b>Race:</b> Monk Seal</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yago on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yago</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yard</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: But it was big.” I pulled up my chat to see if anybody knew what it was, and I saw with dismay that a group of four crawlers I’d tossed into a group I’d called “Yard F” were now all.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yarilo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yarilo</b> is the banished god of lust, and the son of <a href="bword://Taranis">Taranis</a>. Yarilo was once known for hosting orgies (which <a href="bword://Samantha">Samantha</a> attended even though he skimped on catering), but is now trapped in the Nothing.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Twelfth Floor</li><li><b>Race:</b> Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 21</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yarilo on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yarilo</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yellow</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook; the book associates it with this event or detail: They haven’t seen any sign of the Red, Yellow, or Nightmare lines.” “That’s interesting,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yellow-Bellied Chickenshit! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yellow-Bellied Chickenshit!</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> distributed to crawlers who end a boss battle without dying or killing the boss. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement after entering an encounter with the Level 85 Elite City Boss Ringmaster Grimadli - <a href="bword://Pestiferous Vine">Pestiferous Vine</a> on the Third Floor, and then talked his way out of it.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yellow-Bellied Chickenshit! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yellow-Bellied_Chickenshit%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yemaya</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yemaya</b> is a deity that is a god of rivers, but also the queen of love and bringer of life. She is a retainer of the goddess <a href="bword://Eileithyia">Eileithyia</a>. She is part of the <a href="bword://Orisha">Orisha</a> pantheon along with <a href="bword://Ogun">Ogun</a>, <a href="bword://Legba">Legba</a>, <a href="bword://Oshun">Oshun</a>, <a href="bword://Asojano">Asojano</a>, <a href="bword://Shango">Shango</a>, and <a href="bword://Inle">Inle</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Dungeon</li><li><b>Race:</b> God</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yemaya on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yemaya</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yenk</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yenk</b> are a race with three genders, all three of which are required to reproduce. The women, children and unintelligent third gender live together. The males are forced out when they reach maturity, they often work as mercenaries or bounty hunters but can't work with others of their kind lest they fight. Some of the males are castrated.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yenk on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yenk</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yep</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dealing with those guys is gonna be a real chore.” “Isn’t that what Mordecai really is on this floor?” “Yep, but it’s glitching.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Yet somehow, this simple act of taking a regular animal, a pig, and dragging her into sapience seemed a step too far, like I’d crossed a line I’d never known was.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yikes</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: They’ll all still want you to do it, though, since the odds are in their favor that it’ll tiddie-bang one of their competitors and not them.’” “Yikes,” I.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yo Silver</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The name of the upgrade, “Hi-Yo Silver” and not “Heigh- Ho Silver” had caused a great debate amongst some of the crawlers, especially Louis and Elle, and I thought they were actually going to come to blows.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yog's Special Chain</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>Yog's Special Chain</b></i> is a Scroll that allows the caster to summon and use any length (up to 1,000' or 304.8m) of glowing magical chain for 30 hours. The chain is "light as a feather" but at least as strong as regular chain.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Gold Loot Box</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yog's Special Chain on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yog%27s_Special_Chain</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yokai</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Louis, Britney, and Bautista surrounded the passed-out unicorn, protecting him while Imani moved to the Yokai–scarecrow fight in an attempt to keep them from murdering each.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yolanda</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “I think I can build a bomb that’ll go off on its own when the monster hits it, that way we don’t have to get locked in there to fight.” “It won’t work,” Yolanda said, coming up to stand with.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yolanda and Randall</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: The monster coalesced, coming into existence ten feet behind Yolanda and Randall, just above the prone form of the now-dead.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yolanda Martinez</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yolanda Martinez</b> was a crawler from Wenatchee, WA, and a member of the <a href="bword://Meadow Lark">Meadow Lark</a> party on the first and second floors. Her death had a profound impact on both <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Imani">Imani</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Earth: Wenatchee, WA</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 22</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yolanda Martinez on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yolanda_Martinez</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>York</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>York</b> is a Tenodera former crawler and the author of the tenth edition of the <i><a href="bword://Dungeon Anarchist&#x27;s Cookbook">Dungeon Anarchist's Cookbook</a></i>. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> first meets York not as a person but as a voice on the page. While resting during his crawl, Carl reads York's contributions to the cookbook — pages of rambling, philosophical essays he can barely parse, though one passage refuses to leave him. The entries reveal a being who came from a hive yet spent his life feeling utterly alone, and who took a strange comfort in the idea that the cookbook could become a hive of its own: a fellowship of crawlers who would never actually meet.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> A Tenodera hive</li><li><b>Race:</b> Tenodera</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 3, Chapter 15 (mentioned); Book 7, Epilogue (in person)</li></ul><p class="source">Source: York on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/York</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Call That a Trap? Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You call that a trap?</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler sets a trap that deals damage to a mob. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> both receive the "You call that a trap?" achievement after their final battle against the <a href="bword://Rage Elemental">Rage Elemental</a> on the Second Floor. Although Carl isn't sure how they earned the achievement and specifically notes that bombs are categorized separately from traps, he guesses that they received it by tripping the Elemental with the remnants of <a href="bword://Yog&#x27;s Special Chain">Yog's Special Chain</a> or spilling <a href="bword://Goblin Oil">Goblin Oil</a> on the Dungeon floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Call That a Trap? Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Call_That_a_Trap%3F_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Can't Fight City Hall! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You Can't Fight City Hall!</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> distributed to crawlers who discover a City Boss. This special achievement <i>cannot</i> be minimized in the Heads-Up Display (HUD).</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Can't Fight City Hall! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Can%27t_Fight_City_Hall%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Crossed a Line You Didn't Even Know Was There Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You Crossed a Line You Didn't Even Know Was There</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> granted the first time a Crawler uses an artifact of Divine Suffering. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement after using the Enchanted Night Wyrm’s Ring of Divine Suffering to kill <a href="bword://Chin&#x27;Dua">Chin'Dua</a> shortly after arriving on the Sixth Floor. The <a href="bword://Junkie&#x27;s Box">Junkie's Box</a> contains the Night Wyrm's Nasty Little Web of Suffering.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Crossed a Line You Didn't Even Know Was There Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Crossed_a_Line_You_Didn%27t_Even_Know_Was_There_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Found Stairs! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You found stairs!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler finds an accessible stairwell. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement after defeating the <a href="bword://Ball of Swine">Ball of Swine</a> on the First Floor, and gaining access to and seeing the staircase to the Second Floor. He does <i>not</i> receive this achievement for simply locating a staircase inside the Dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Found Stairs! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Found_Stairs%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You have discovered a Celestial Thorn Room! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>You have discovered a Celestial Thorn Room! Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> on the Eighth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You have discovered a Celestial Thorn Room! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_have_discovered_a_Celestial_Thorn_Room%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You in my house now boy Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>You in my house now boy Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for confronting <a href="bword://Khepri">Khepri</a> in his own temple during the Operation Snake Pit. Both <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> and <a href="bword://Prepotente">Prepotente</a> had received the same achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You in my house now boy Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_in_my_house_now_boy_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Know Everybody Can See You Right? Box</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>You Know Everybody Can See You Right? Box</b> is a Loot Box distributed for...reasons. Whatever the crawler was doing, where everybody could see them, they were doing it to such an extent that it was deemed worthy of a Legendary loot box.. The loot box is mentioned as part of the AI's description for Spunky Jeffery the Enchanted Nickel Sock of the Elderly Miser.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Achievement</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Know Everybody Can See You Right? Box on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Know_Everybody_Can_See_You_Right%3F_Box</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You Monster! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You Monster!</b> is an achievement awarded when a crawler kills an infant. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a> receive the <b>You Monster!</b> and <a href="bword://War Criminal Achievement">War Criminal Achievement</a> on the First Floor after launching a cart laden with explosives and <a href="bword://Hobgoblin Pus">Hobgoblin Pus</a> into the Goblin War Chieftan's boss room... which also happened to contain a lot of babies, pregnant goblins, and elderly goblins.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You Monster! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_Monster%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You now tread in the realm of gods Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>The <b>You now tread in the realm of gods Achievement</b> is an achievement awarded to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> for training his Mind Balance Skill over level 15 while being hit with a barrage of psionic attacks while storming the Madness stronghold during Faction Wars (game).</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You now tread in the realm of gods Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You_now_tread_in_the_realm_of_gods_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You're Not Done Yet Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><i><b>You're Not Done Yet</b></i> is a brutal necromancy spell that rips the flesh from the dead, creating a <a href="bword://Flesher">Flesher</a>. A flesher's goal is to find a new body to wrap itself around. This spell is associated with War Mages and often used on the Ninth Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You're Not Done Yet Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27re_Not_Done_Yet_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You're the Reason Why Daddy Drinks! Achievement</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You're the reason why daddy drinks!</b> is an achievement that the <a href="bword://System AI">System AI</a> awards to <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a>, and only Carl, on the Fifth Floor. After Carl, <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>, and <a href="bword://Katia Grim">Katia Grim</a> destroy the <i>Wasteland</i>, the System AI throws a hissy fit that includes the <a href="bword://Cannonball! Achievement">Cannonball! Achievement</a> and tagging the <a href="bword://Dirigible Gnome">Dirigible Gnome</a> corpses as, "<b>Killed by falling from a great height with an assist by the Crawler Bitch Boy Carl.</b>" Katia asks Carl what he did to piss off the System AI, and Carl, clueless, looks up to the air and says, "I don’t know what I did, but I promise it wasn’t on purpose."</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You're the Reason Why Daddy Drinks! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27re_the_Reason_Why_Daddy_Drinks%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Discovered and Read an Official Dungeon Sign Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You've Discovered and Read an Official Dungeon Sign</b> is an achievement awarded to crawlers the first time they see a legitimate sign advertising a Tutorial Guild Hall, which will presumably happen on the First Floor. The reward upgrades the crawler's Heads-Up Display (HUD) and Minimap.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Discovered and Read an Official Dungeon Sign Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Discovered_and_Read_an_Official_Dungeon_Sign_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Entered a Guildhall! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> earns this achievement after finding and entering a Tutorial Guild Hall on the first floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Entered a Guildhall! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Entered_a_Guildhall%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Inflicted Damage on a Mob Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You've inflicted damage on a mob</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler deals damage to a mob. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement after fighting the goblins for the first time on the First Floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Inflicted Damage on a Mob Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Inflicted_Damage_on_a_Mob_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Killed a Mob a Higher Level than Yourself! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You've Killed A Mob Higher Level than Yourself!</b> is an <a href="bword://Achievement">Achievement</a> awarded the first time a crawler defeats a mob of a higher level. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> earns this achievement after his first goblin encounter on the first floor.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Killed a Mob a Higher Level than Yourself! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Killed_a_Mob_a_Higher_Level_than_Yourself%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Killed a Mob! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You've Killed a Mob!</b> is an achievement awarded the first time a crawler kills a mob. This achievement is required to begin earning experience points in the Dungeon.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Killed a Mob! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Killed_a_Mob%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You've Killed an Armed Mob with Your Bare Fucking Hands! Achievement</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>You've killed an armed mob with your bare fucking hands!</b> is an achievement for killing an armed mob without a weapon. <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> receives this achievement on the First Floor, at the same time he receives the <a href="bword://You&#x27;ve Killed a Mob! Achievement">You've Killed a Mob! Achievement</a>, after killing a goblin with his fists.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: You've Killed an Armed Mob with Your Bare Fucking Hands! Achievement on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/You%27ve_Killed_an_Armed_Mob_with_Your_Bare_Fucking_Hands%21_Achievement</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Book of Voodoo</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You said there are eight combatants?” Your Book of Voodoo has been updated with an entry for Quan.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Charisma</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Your Charisma is described as still really high, but the effect on NPCs isn’t immediately as powerful as it once was,” Mordecai said, coming around the corner, wiping his hands on a.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Highness</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Also, you don’t want a room of your own?” “Of course, Your Highness,” Tally said and turned.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Intelligence</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Duration: (5 minutes + 1 minute per level of spell) × [Your Intelligence / (Target’s Constitution + Charisma)] Ahh.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Majesty</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: You will wave, and you will proceed directly to the couch next to the desk, Your Majesty sitting closer to.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Mind Balance</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In This Inevitable Ruin, Your Mind Balance is described as currently overwhelmed as you are getting absolutely hammered with psionic.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Pugilism Skill</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In Dungeon Crawler Carl, Your Pugilism Skill is described as now level.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Your Squad</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Your Squad has used a Flee.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’d</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’d think that chick that used to have the boob armor would’ve killed or subjugated this one by now since that’s been her MO since she set foot on the 12th floor, but for whatever reason, she’s left the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’ll</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 2.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Carl's Doomsday Scenario; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’ll be able to buy such a display for your private base later, but they’re likely prohibitively expensive.” The next few groups were crawlers I didn’t recognize, but who had picked odd races or classes.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’re</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: You’re too heavy for Bomo with all your armor.” I knew his breastplate was actually light as a feather when it wasn’t equipped, and he didn’t feel the weight while he wore it, but it made him outrageously.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’re Carl</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’re Carl,” Louis.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’re Not Done</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The plan was for them to drop a metric fuckton of corpses on the battlefield and have Donut cast her wide-range, tower-enhanced, You’re Not Done Yet , which would rip the skin off the corpses, creating.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’re Not Done Yet</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: The plan was for them to drop a metric fuckton of corpses on the battlefield and have Donut cast her wide-range, tower-enhanced, You’re Not Done Yet , which would rip the skin off the corpses, creating.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>You’ve</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “You’ve demonstrated to me that your control has extended outside of Earth’s orbit, and you likely have control all the way to the star, which means you’ve disabled the failsafe.” Gary shot a finger gun at.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Ysalte</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Ysalte</b>, also called the <b>Vinegar Bitch</b>, is a deity who can be pulled into the Dungeon as a secondary summoning when a feral god is released. The <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> announced that Ysalte was one of the deities that could be produced as a secondary summoning by the <a href="bword://Gate of the Feral Gods">Gate of the Feral Gods</a>, and that her arrival would have been an extinction-level event for the fifth and sixth floors. Rather than allow it before another floor of sponsorship bidding was complete, Borant blocked some, but not all, of the secondary summonings.</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> The Vinegar Bitch; The Downward Spiral</li><li><b>Race:</b> Deity</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 4, Chapter 33</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Ysalte on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Ysalte</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yule Cat</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>In The Eye of the Bedlam Bride, Yule Cat is described as a big grump, but this one is quite the serial.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yuto the Oni</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in A Parade of Horribles; the book associates it with this event or detail: Dekoki the kappa and Yuto the Oni from team Yokai both patted me on the.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yvette</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Yvette</b> is the daughter of <a href="bword://Maggie My">Maggie My</a> and <a href="bword://Frank Q">Frank Q</a>. She entered the dungeon with her parents after running away the night of the collapse. Yvette is reluctant to kill things in the Dungeon.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Seattle</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1 Chapter 43</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Yvette on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Yvette</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Yvonne Vu</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: Yvonne Vu : Our stairwell is in a subway.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zabit</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>Hunter, killed by his own traps when <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> cast <i><a href="bword://Tripper">Tripper</a></i>. He was the final Hunter on floor 6. He hunts for the survival of the <a href="bword://Atoll">Atoll</a>, using the money to pay for the upkeep of his habitat as their race has limited employment opportunities. Without the money from hunting crawlers his community would have to agree to an indentureship with another system.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Atoll</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zabit on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zabit</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zander</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 8.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in This Inevitable Ruin; the book associates it with this event or detail: “Are you sure it’s not because this waystation has the stupidest name I’ve ever heard?” Zander looked down at his.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zebani</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zebani</b> is a race very similar to human, but with a blue tinge to the skin. The only recorded Zebani encountered so far is the Earth Crawler <a href="bword://Emir Akbas">Emir Akbas</a> who <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> meets in the <a href="bword://Desperado Club">Desperado Club</a>. Carl comments that he "looked human at first" but had a "blue tinge to his skin".</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zebani on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zebani</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zentix</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zentix</b> is a reactive, tamper-proof polymer that feels like cheap plastic. As a smart polymer, it can be programmed with security settings that cause it to fail under certain conditions (such as when tampered with or used by unauthorized users).</li><li><b>Source:</b> Veriluxx</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zentix on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zentix</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zerzura</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Eye of the Bedlam Bride; the book associates it with this event or detail: “The resulting ghoul things from the Zerzura spell are still a problem, but most of your army has gotten away and has installed themselves onto your.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zerzura Spell</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zerzura</b> is a once-per-floor spell which transfers a Dungeon settlement to a deeper floor. It's extremely popular among watchers of <i>Dungeon Crawler World</i> due to the chaos it causes.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zerzura Spell on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zerzura_Spell</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zev</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zev</b> is a <a href="bword://Kua-Tin">Kua-Tin</a> and low-ranked employee in the <a href="bword://Borant Corporation">Borant Corporation</a> Communications Department. After <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> and <a href="bword://Donut">Donut</a>'s first appearance on <i>Dungeon Crawler After Hours with <a href="bword://Odette">Odette</a></i>, she was appointed as the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>'s "Outreach Associate."</li><li><b>Aliases:</b> Mudskipper, Fish Sticks</li><li><b>Origin:</b> Borant System</li><li><b>Race:</b> Kua-Tin</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 36</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zev on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zev</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zeynep</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 6.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zeynep</b> is a crawler from the middle east. She and <a href="bword://Carl">Carl</a> trade fist bumps on the Eighth Floor. Zeynep is a middle aged middle eastern woman whose crawler ID is similar enough to those of her three other party members that Carl suspects they entered the Dungeon together. She may also be in a party with <a href="bword://Burcu">Burcu</a>.</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 6, Chapter 32</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zeynep on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zeynep</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zhang</b></p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zhang</b> is a Crawler from China on Dungeon Crawler World: Earth. He entered the Dungeon with his best friend <a href="bword://Li Jun">Li Jun</a> and Li Jun's sister <a href="bword://Li Na">Li Na</a>, and the three of them form the core of <a href="bword://Team Li Na">Team Li Na</a>, allies of the <a href="bword://Royal Court of Princess Donut">Royal Court of Princess Donut</a>.</li><li><b>Origin:</b> China, Earth</li><li><b>Race:</b> Human</li><li><b>First scene:</b> Book 1, Chapter 42</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zhang on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zhang</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zhang and Li Jun</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 1.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in Dungeon Crawler Carl; the book associates it with this event or detail: But I remembered that Zhang and Li Jun had waved at them, and I doubted they’d have reacted like that toward a pair of scary-looking.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zhang's Cape of Odds</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 7.</p><ul class="definition"><li><a href="bword://Zhang">Zhang</a> receives the <b>Zhang's Cape of Odds</b> in a <a href="bword://Celestial">Celestial</a> Who Let the <a href="bword://Gods">Gods</a> Out This Time Box for the <a href="bword://Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement">Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement</a>. The cape is white with the standard black, red, and yellow archery target on the back. When it isn't activated, it gives 15% to all stats plus a level 10 Dodge Skill. Once an hour, it gives access to the Roll the Dice Skill.</li><li><b>Source:</b> Who Let the Gods Out This Time Box; Celestial Orgy of Chaos Achievement.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zhang's Cape of Odds on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zhang%27s_Cape_of_Odds</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zlurpies</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Spoiler note:</b> This article contains unmarked spoilers for Book 3.</p><ul class="definition"><li><b>Zlurpies</b> are a Fourth Floor mob that can be found on the Yellow Line of the Iron Tangle. Zlurpies are waist-high, semi-intelligent warrior armadillo things that roll into armored balls when they take damage, but they were small enough to be eaten whole by <a href="bword://Mongo">Mongo</a>, who would pick them up and crunch them like juice-filled hard candies.</li></ul><p class="source">Source: Zlurpies on Dungeon Crawler Carl Wiki<br />https://dungeon-crawler-carl.fandom.com/wiki/Zlurpies</p></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zockau</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 5.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Butcher's Masquerade; the book associates it with this event or detail: After my recent visit with Quasar, Zev confided that multiple, immediately unsuccessful lawsuits followed once the brambles hit Zockau, and the Syndicate Council—not the subcommittee that oversaw the crawl.</li></ul></div><div class="entry"><p class="headword"><b>Zombie Wynne</b></p><p class="spoiler-note"><b>Book source:</b> Book 4.</p><ul class="definition"><li>A named term in The Gate of the Feral Gods; the book associates it with this event or detail: Zombie Wynne snapped and thrashed as Leon placed his hand against the creature’s.</li></ul></div>